window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"hildegard-o-vis-aeternitatis":["A responsory: a soloist sings, the choir answers, a verse follows, and the answer returns shortened. One line of melody, no harmony written. What marks it out is the reach — the tune climbs and plunges across more than two octaves, opening out into long garlands of notes on single syllables, where ordinary plainchant would sit within a fifth or a sixth. Many performers add a low sustained drone underneath, which turns the leaps into something closer to soaring.","Hildegard ran her own community of nuns at Rupertsberg, near Bingen on the Rhine, and wrote both the words and the music of her songs — rare in a century where chant was inherited, not invented. She described the melodies as heard rather than composed, part of the same visionary experience that produced her theological books. The daily round of the Divine Office gave her singers eight services to fill, and her songs were made for those hours.","This is the piece that opens her song collection in both surviving manuscripts, the Dendermonde codex and the Riesencodex — a deliberate placement, since the text addresses the eternal power through which all things were made. Listen for how the melody keeps returning to the same three or four pivot notes, so that even the wildest leaps land somewhere the ear already knows.","Her music was effectively unheard for eight centuries. Scholarly editions appeared only from the 1960s, and a 1982 recording by Gothic Voices put her on record shop shelves. Performance is largely conjecture: the notation gives pitch clearly but says nothing about rhythm, tempo, drones or instruments, so every recording is an argument. Some ensembles sing it bare and slow, others build a shimmering accompaniment around it.","Play thirty seconds of ordinary Gregorian chant first, then this: the difference in range is immediate. Follow one long melisma and notice how far it travels before the syllable changes. Sequentia and Gothic Voices take opposite approaches to the drone question, and hearing both is the fastest way to understand how much of this music is a modern decision."],"hildegard-ordo-virtutum":["A sung drama in about eighty short melodies, running roughly eighty minutes. A Soul, drawn away from the Virtues by worldly promises, falls, returns wounded, and is received back; sixteen Virtues — Humility as their queen, plus Charity, Chastity, Obedience and the rest — sing her home while the Devil interrupts. The vocal writing is Hildegard's own, soaring and melismatic, but the drama is carried by contrast: individual voices against the massed sound of the Virtues.","It was probably made for Hildegard's own nuns at the Rupertsberg convent, perhaps around its dedication near 1151, with the community taking the Virtues' parts. Her secretary Volmar may have taken the Devil. Liturgical plays existed, but nothing of this scale with newly composed music survives from before it. A version of the text also closes her visionary book Scivias, so the drama grew directly out of her theology.","The Devil never sings. He shouts, barks and argues in speech alone, because in Hildegard's theology harmony is divine and he is cut off from it. Once you notice this, the whole piece reorganises itself: the argument between good and evil is staged as an argument between music and noise, and the Soul's recovery is literally her recovery of the ability to sing.","It is usually called the earliest surviving morality play, and the earliest music drama with a named composer. Whether it was fully staged, semi-staged, or read aloud in the convent is unknown. Modern performance began in the 1980s, with Sequentia's recordings the reference point; groups differ over whether to use instruments and over how theatrical to make the Devil's interruptions.","Skip to the Devil's first entry — the shock of a speaking voice tearing through the singing is the work's central effect. Then find the Soul's exhausted return, where the melody drops. It runs long, so treat it as a play rather than an album, and try Sequentia, who recorded it twice with different casts."],"hildegard-columba-aspexit":["A sequence, which means paired verses: each new melody is sung twice, to two different lines of text, and the pairs mount steadily higher before the tune settles. Six or so minutes of single-line melody, decorated at the ends of phrases. The images are sensory rather than doctrinal — a dove looking in through the lattice of a window, balsam dripping down a shining mountain, light on stone — and the melody rises at exactly the moments the text opens out.","The text honours Maximin, a fourth-century bishop of Trier. Hildegard corresponded with the monks of the abbeys at Trier and had close ties to that community, and songs for their patron saints belonged to the ordinary traffic of monastic life. She wrote her lyrics in a private, image-crowded Latin that startled her contemporaries as much as her melodies did, and she assembled the songs into a collection with a title of its own.","Her favourite word, viriditas — greenness, sap, the living force in plants and souls — runs through her writing and shapes the imagery here: growth, moisture, perfume, light. She uses the same small stock of pictures across dozens of songs, so the melodies feel like variations on one continuous vision rather than separate pieces.","Gothic Voices recorded it in 1982 on the album that carried her name to a general audience, and for many listeners it remains the piece by which Hildegard is known. That very success drew arguments: whether the sweet, drone-supported, all-female sound of those recordings reflects twelfth-century practice or a modern taste for the ethereal. Nothing in the manuscripts settles it.","Track the paired verses — hear a melody, then hear it again with new words — and you have the form in two minutes. Listen for the moment the tune reaches its highest point and refuses to come down straight away. Compare a version with a drone against one without; the difference in mood is startling."],"hildegard-o-euchari":["A long sequence in paired verses for Eucharius, held to be the first bishop of Trier. It is one of her more expansive songs: seven minutes or so of unbroken single-line melody that keeps finding new territory rather than circling one idea. The text moves from the saint's walking with Christ, through his preaching, to the community that grew from him, and the melody widens as the story does, opening into long runs of notes at the phrase ends.","Trier was a major ecclesiastical centre a short journey from Hildegard's Rhine convent, and she preached publicly there — an extraordinary thing for a woman of the twelfth century. Songs for a house's patron saint were practical objects: they gave a community something of its own to sing on its own feast day, and this one probably reached the monks of St Eucharius that way.","The sequence form lets Hildegard write far longer melodies than an antiphon allows, and she uses the space to build. Follow the highest note each verse reaches: it creeps upward across the piece, so the climax arrives by accumulation rather than by a single gesture. She also breaks the strict verse-pairing here and there, which suggests the text came first and the tune followed it.","Less recorded than her few celebrated pieces, partly because of its length. Editors disagree on where the verse pairs divide, and the manuscripts are not identical. Its interest for scholars is biographical as much as musical: it connects her to a specific network of houses and shows her writing to order for people she knew, rather than only from vision.","Give it the whole seven minutes and listen for the climb rather than for any single tune. Compare it with the shorter Columba aspexit, written in the same form, to hear how much difference length makes. An ensemble that keeps the tempo moving, rather than floating it, holds the shape together best."],"hildegard-caritas-abundat":["An antiphon, and one of her shortest songs: three minutes, a single melodic line, no repeats. Charity, the text says, abounds towards all things, from the depths of the earth up beyond the highest stars, and is loving towards all — and the melody does exactly that, opening with a leap of an octave and then working its way down through the range it has just claimed. The economy is the point: one idea, stated once, unhurried.","Antiphons frame the psalms at the daily services, so they are the most frequently sung music in a convent's life and the most tightly bound to the clock. Hildegard wrote dozens. Where a plainchant antiphon is usually brief and unassuming, hers make a small event of the moment, which meant her nuns sang something recognisably theirs several times a day.","The octave leap at the start is not decoration. It maps the text's geography — from the depths to the stars — onto the singer's body in the first three seconds, before a word of the meaning can register. She does this repeatedly: a picture of height in the words gets height in the melody, so the tune is a kind of illustration.","Its brevity has made it the most-covered Hildegard piece of the last twenty years, arranged for choirs, string groups and ambient records. Purists object that the arrangements smooth away the modal edges; others argue that a piece surviving only as a pitch outline was always going to be finished by its performers. Both positions accept that we do not know how it originally sounded.","Three minutes, one hearing, no preparation needed: catch the leap in the opening phrase and follow the melody down. Then compare it with a long sequence like Columba aspexit to feel the difference between her miniature and her expansive modes. Arvo Pärt makes a useful modern companion — the same trust in very few notes."],"leonin-viderunt-omnes-organum-duplum":["The gradual for Christmas Day, set in alternation. Where the old chant had a soloist, two singers take over: the lower one holds a single syllable of the melody for astonishing stretches — twenty, thirty seconds at a time — while the upper voice spins free, unmeasured decoration above it. Where the chant had the choir, the choir simply sings plainchant as before. In between come passages where both voices move together in a springing, measured rhythm.","Notre-Dame de Paris was rising from 1163, and its clergy wanted music to match. A collection known as the Magnus liber organi gathered two-voice settings for the solo portions of the great feasts across the church year. Nearly everything we believe about its authorship comes from a single English student, known only as Anonymous IV, writing in the 1270s, who named Leoninus as the best composer of organum and the book's maker.","This is not a fixed piece. The measured sections, called clausulae, were interchangeable modules — later musicians wrote replacements and slotted them in. Then someone added words to those replacement sections, and the detached, texted clausula became the motet, the most important form of the next two hundred years. The genre that dominated medieval music was, in effect, a spare part.","Everything rests on one late witness. Some scholars doubt whether Leoninus was a single composer rather than a name attached to a repertory, and the surviving manuscripts, copied decades later in Florence and Germany, disagree with one another. The largest argument is rhythmic: whether the flowing upper voice should be sung freely or fitted to the strict patterns theorists later described.","Ignore the top voice at first and listen only to the bottom. Try to count how long one syllable lasts — that suspended, immobile bass is the sound of Western harmony being invented. Then hear Pérotin's four-voice setting of the same chant to feel what the next generation did with it. The Hilliard Ensemble and Ensemble Organum take very different rhythmic positions."],"leonin-alleluia-pascha-nostrum":["The Easter Alleluia, whose verse announces that Christ our Passover has been sacrificed. It works like the Christmas gradual: the soloists' portions become two-voice organum, with the lower voice freezing on a note while the upper decorates, and the choir's portions stay in plainchant. The alternation makes a strong effect — long stretches of suspended, glittering elaboration, then the whole choir arriving in unison, plain and immediate.","Easter Day was the summit of the church year, and the Magnus liber organi covered the solo chants of the highest feasts. The music was for a small group of trained singers, probably standing at a lectern in the middle of the choir, with the rest of the clergy answering. The polyphony was a mark of rank: only the greatest days got it.","One measured section of this Alleluia, on the word beginning Latus, became the single most recycled passage in medieval music. Later composers rewrote it, added Latin and then French words to it, and spun dozens of motets from it, some of them love songs. A few seconds of Easter chant seeded an entire secular repertory.","Reconstruction is contested. The three great sources — a Florentine manuscript and two German ones, one copied in Scotland — differ in which clausulae they include and in how much is written out. Editors must choose a version, and the choice changes the piece's length substantially. As with all this music, the rhythm of the upper voice remains a matter of interpretation.","Listen for the switch: the strange, hovering solo polyphony against the clean unison of the choir. It repeats several times, so you will start anticipating it. Follow it with the Christmas gradual to hear the same technique applied to a different chant, and try an ensemble that keeps the upper voice supple rather than metrical."],"perotin-viderunt-omnes":["The same Christmas gradual, now in four parts. The bottom voice takes one syllable of the chant and holds it, sometimes for well over a minute, while three upper voices trade short rhythmic cells above it in a springing triple pulse, passing identical phrases between them. The harmony changes only when the held note finally moves, which happens rarely, so the effect is of vast slow blocks with intricate machinery running on top. Then the choir returns to plain unison chant.","In 1198 the Bishop of Paris, Odo of Sully, issued statutes that permitted — and by implication encouraged — organum in three and four voices at the highest feasts, apparently to reform rowdy Christmas customs. This setting is generally connected to that moment. The same English writer known as Anonymous IV, a century later, named Perotinus the finest composer of measured polyphony and credited him with the four-voice Viderunt and Sederunt.","The three upper voices frequently swap phrases outright: voice one sings a figure, then voice two sings it while voice one takes what voice two had. The ear registers repetition and pattern but cannot tell who is singing what. And the chant underneath is the same melody Leonin set — the older piece is buried inside this one, stretched almost past recognition.","Twentieth-century listeners heard it as a prophecy of their own music. Steve Reich named Perotin openly as an influence, and the piece is now a fixture of both early-music and minimalism arguments. Almost every practical decision — pitch level, tempo, tuning, whether to use voices only — is a modern reconstruction. The Hilliard Ensemble's recording set one influential template; Ensemble Organum's is far rougher.","Listen to the bottom note first and forget the surface entirely; then, on a second hearing, follow only the top voices. The gap between what the bass is doing and what the trebles are doing is the whole experience. Afterwards play Leonin's two-voice version, then the opening of Reich's Music for 18 Musicians."],"perotin-sederunt-principes":["The gradual for St Stephen's Day, 26 December, and the companion piece to the four-voice Viderunt omnes. Its opening is the most extreme gesture in medieval music: the lowest voice sounds the first syllable and simply stays there for around two minutes while the three upper voices build interlocking patterns over it. Nothing in the harmony moves. When the note finally changes, the effect is close to physical. Roughly twelve minutes in all, with the choir's plainchant framing it.","St Stephen's Day belonged to the deacons, one of the feasts around Christmas when the junior clergy were licensed to celebrate — sometimes riotously. Odo of Sully's 1198 statutes tied the new four-voice polyphony to exactly these days, which suggests the grandest music was partly a device for keeping order. The text, about princes sitting in judgement and speaking against the innocent, suits a martyr's feast.","The three upper voices work from a small stock of rhythmic cells, circulating them and exchanging them so that the same material is constantly redistributed. That is why the surface sounds both busy and static. And there is a Scottish connection: one of the two main German-held manuscripts was copied at St Andrews, proof that the Paris repertory travelled to the edge of Europe within decades.","Modern audiences generally find this the more overwhelming of the two quadrupla, and it has attracted composers and listeners from outside early music entirely. Scholars caution that the 1199 date is inference, not record. Tempo is the great practical dispute: taken briskly it dances, taken slowly it becomes a drone piece, and recordings differ by several minutes.","Start a stopwatch at the first note and watch how long that bass syllable lasts. The moment it finally moves is the payoff, so resist skipping. Then hear the four-voice Viderunt omnes for comparison, and Ligeti's Lux Aeterna to hear a modern composer doing the same trick with sustained sound."],"perotin-alleluia-nativitas":["A three-voice setting for the feast of the Virgin's Nativity, lighter and faster on its feet than the four-voice giants. Two upper voices move in a lilting triple swing above a held tenor, but here the held notes change more often, so the harmony turns over at a walking pace rather than a geological one. Six minutes, with the choir taking the plainchant portions. It is the most immediately enjoyable piece in the Notre-Dame repertory.","Three-voice organum sat between the older two-voice settings and the spectacular quadrupla, and there is far more of it. It was the practical everyday grandeur of the Paris chapels, used across the feast calendar rather than reserved for two days of the year. The rhythmic patterns it uses — long-short groupings later codified as the rhythmic modes — were the first system for writing down duration in Western music.","Whole phrases recur at different pitch levels, so the piece proceeds by sequence: a shape stated, then the same shape a step or two away. That is a compositional habit we associate with much later music, and it is one reason this piece sounds so much more familiar to modern ears than the quadrupla do. Follow the top voice and the repetitions become obvious.","Attribution is again from Anonymous IV, who mentions Perotinus's three-voice settings by name. This one is less debated than the quadrupla and less recorded, which has kept it fresher. Performers generally agree on brisk rhythmic realisation here, since the modal patterns are clearer in the sources, so recordings vary less than for the four-voice works.","Take this one first if the quadrupla feel forbidding — the pulse is easy to catch and the piece is short. Listen for a phrase in the top voice, then wait for it to come back higher or lower. Follow with Leonin's Easter Alleluia to hear the same feast-day logic in two voices."],"perotin-beata-viscera":["A conductus: a Latin sacred song for one voice, strophic, with several stanzas sung to the same graceful melody. There is no polyphony and no borrowed chant — just a tune, elegantly shaped, returning again and again as the words change. The text praises the blessed womb of the Virgin. After the immensity of the four-voice organa, its plainness is disarming, and the melody has the swing of a good song rather than the solemnity of liturgy.","The words are attributed to Philip the Chancellor of Notre-Dame, who died in 1236 and was the outstanding Latin poet of the Paris schools. Conductus was sung in procession — during the movement of clergy from one place to another — which freed it from the fixed chants of the service. That freedom is why it could be newly composed from top to bottom.","This is the crucial point and it is easy to miss: nothing here is inherited. Organum decorates an existing chant, but the conductus melody is invention all the way down, words and music together. It is arguably the first repertory of freely composed Western song, and the reason it sounds like a song rather than an elaboration.","The ascription to Perotin comes from Anonymous IV and is one of the few specific work attributions he gives. Performances vary enormously in how many stanzas they include and in whether to add drones, harp or vielle; some singers vary the tune slightly each time, on the model of later strophic song. The manuscript gives no guidance either way.","Let the first stanza go by, then notice that the second uses exactly the same tune. Count the stanzas and hear how a performer keeps them from becoming monotonous. Follow it with a Machaut virelai two centuries later to see how little the basic idea of the strophic song had to change."],"vitry-garrit-gallus-nova-fert":["Three minutes, three voices, and three different things happening at once. The top voice races through a Latin poem in short notes; the middle voice sings a second, unrelated Latin poem more slowly; the bottom voice has no words at all, only a fragment of chant laid out in a rhythmic pattern that repeats like a stencil. The texture is dense to the point of illegibility, and deliberately so. Nothing before it packs this much argument into so little time.","It survives inside the Roman de Fauvel, a satirical French poem about a horse-like beast, Fauvel, whose name spells out the vices and who is groomed by the whole court. A luxurious Paris manuscript of around 1317 interpolated some 130 pieces of music into the poem. The imagery here is political: a chattering cock for France, a blind lion for the king, a cunning fox stripping the flock.","The bottom voice carries two independent cycles — a rhythmic pattern and a melody of different lengths — so they go out of phase and only realign after several repetitions. That device, later called isorhythm, organised serious composition for the next hundred and fifty years. The middle voice also opens by quoting the first line of Ovid's Metamorphoses, on bodies changed into new forms.","Vitry's authorship is traditional rather than documented: only a handful of works can be securely assigned to him, and the Ars nova treatise that names the movement survives only in later reworkings, so even the period label is partly a modern construction. What is not in doubt is that the Fauvel motets mark the moment when rhythm became a compositional structure rather than a way of moving.","Do not chase the words on a first hearing — no medieval listener could follow all three either. Instead listen only to the lowest voice and count how long its pattern takes to come round. Then listen again for the top voice's speed against it. Ensembles like the Orlando Consort keep the lines separable."],"vitry-tribum-que-non-abhorruit":["Another three-voice motet from the same manuscript and built the same way: a fast upper poem, a slower middle one, and a wordless chant tenor stamped out in a repeating rhythmic pattern. The two texts are about corruption and its punishment, and the music matches them with a hard, clipped surface. Three minutes, no lyricism, cadences that arrive as if closing a case. It is Latin invective set as a machine.","Enguerrand de Marigny, the chief minister of Philip IV of France, was arrested after the king's death and hanged in 1315. The Fauvel manuscript's political motets circle that scandal and the wider theme of favourites who rise and fall. Whoever compiled the book was close enough to the court to be dangerous, and the music was a way of saying in Latin what could not be said openly in French.","The tenor's borrowed chant carries a text of its own that the singers knew even though it is not sung out: it translates as we suffer these things deservedly. That silent phrase is the verdict on the two poems above it — the piece's punchline is in the part nobody hears as words.","Like its companion, it is assigned to Vitry on stylistic and circumstantial grounds. Historians value the Fauvel motets as evidence that music was doing political work in the fourteenth century, though how many people could have decoded the layered Latin at speed is doubtful. Recordings are few, and the piece is usually programmed alongside Garrit Gallus.","Read a translation of both poems before listening — the pleasure here is in the venom, and you will not catch it in real time. Then listen for the bass pattern repeating and note how the upper voices' cadences fall in with it. Pair it with Verdi's chorus of exiles for a very different politics in music."],"machaut-messe-nostre-dame-kyrie":["Four voices, where the century's norm was three: two upper parts and, below, a tenor with a new companion that crosses beneath it, so the lowest sound keeps changing owner. The Kyrie takes an existing plainchant Kyrie and stretches it into long held notes in the tenor, over which the other parts move in strict repeating rhythmic patterns, jabbing at each other with sudden broken-off notes. Five minutes of hard, bright, faintly metallic sound, ending in open, hollow chords.","Machaut and his brother Jean were both canons of Reims Cathedral, and they endowed a Mass to be sung every Saturday at an altar in the nave in honour of the Virgin — and, after their deaths, for their souls. The music was probably made for that endowment. Reims had lived through the Black Death of 1349 and English raids; Machaut had earlier served John of Luxembourg, the blind king killed at Crecy.","The four parts are written so the two lowest constantly cross, which means the bass line as you hear it is passed back and forth between two singers rather than owned by one. Machaut also varies his technique movement by movement: the Kyrie and Agnus are built on strict rhythmic patterns, while the wordier Gloria and Credo are set almost syllabically, at speed.","It is routinely called the first complete polyphonic setting of the Mass ordinary by one composer, and that holds with caveats — earlier cycles like the Tournai Mass are compilations by several hands. A persistent story that it was written for the coronation of Charles V in 1364 has no evidence behind it. Live arguments concern unwritten sharps and flats, pitch level, and whether instruments doubled the voices.","Begin here rather than with the Gloria: the Kyrie shows the method at its clearest. Listen for the hollow, open sound at the cadences, which is the period's signature and jars modern ears at first. Then go to the Agnus Dei. The Hilliard Ensemble sing it lean and high; Ensemble Musica Nova take it lower and slower."],"machaut-douce-dame-jolie":["A virelai for a single voice, with no harmony written at all. The form is a loop: a refrain, then two matching verse-halves to a new tune, then a third section that borrows the refrain's melody, then the refrain again — and the whole cycle repeats for each stanza, so the refrain comes round five times. The tune itself is direct and shapely, rising on the opening address to the lady and settling back, more folk-song than court ceremonial.","Machaut was poet first and composer second in his own estimation, and this is courtly love lyric of the standard kind: a lover begging for pity from a woman who will not give it. He also did something new for a musician — he supervised the copying of his own complete works into large illuminated manuscripts, arranging his output as an author's oeuvre rather than letting it scatter.","The form is the argument. Because the refrain returns after every verse, the plea is made over and over with nothing changed, and the lover ends exactly where he began. Machaut wrote plenty of polyphonic songs; choosing a bare single line here was a deliberate archaism, a way of sounding like the trouveres of the previous century.","It is the most recorded piece of medieval secular music, which has given it a strange double life: austere in early-music performances, and rearranged with percussion and drones by folk and neo-medieval groups. Scholarship has moved against the heavy instrumental accompaniments of mid-century recordings, since there is no evidence for them, but the popular versions keep coming.","Count the returns of the refrain — five in most performances — and notice you are hearing the same music the fifth time with a completely different feeling. Then compare a solo unaccompanied version with a fully arranged one to see how much a performer adds. Landini's ballate make the obvious next stop."],"machaut-ma-fin-est-mon-commencement":["A three-voice rondeau of about four minutes whose text states its own construction: my end is my beginning, and my beginning my end. The tenor is the top voice sung backwards. The third part is a palindrome, running forward to its midpoint and then retracing itself note for note. What you actually hear is a slow, sweet, slightly stiff song; the machinery is invisible, which is part of the joke.","The fourteenth-century court prized cleverness as a form of luxury: puzzle canons, acrostics, poems that could be read several ways. Machaut circulated the piece in his own complete-works manuscripts, where scribes wrote it out with instructions rather than in full, leaving the singers to work out what to do. Solving it was part of performing it.","Because the notation is a set of directions rather than a score, performers must decide which line is retrograded and by whom, and there are rival realisations in print. Whether the middle voice or the top is the one reversed changes the sound noticeably. The listener hears none of this — the piece keeps its secret unless you are told.","Opinion divides sharply. Some hear a dry stunt whose interest is entirely on paper; others treat it as the ancestor of Bach's mirror canons and Webern's palindromes, and it is the most anthologised medieval song for exactly that reason. Recent scholarship reads it less as a trick than as a meditation on time, in a text about ending and beginning.","Listen once knowing nothing, and enjoy a graceful, slightly strange song. Then read what the voices are doing and listen again, following the tenor. The second hearing is a different piece. Ockeghem's canon Mass is the place to go next, and after that any Bach canon."],"machaut-rose-liz-printemps-verdure":["One of the largest songs of the century: a rondeau in four parts running to five or six minutes. The text piles up images of sweetness — rose, lily, spring, greenery, flower, balm, scent — only to declare that the lady surpasses them all. The top line is ornate and restless, running long decorated phrases; the lower parts move slowly beneath, so the song feels suspended rather than driven. The refrain scheme brings whole sections back unchanged.","Rondeaux were the most tightly patterned of the fixed forms, with a scheme of repeats fixed in advance and the poet working inside it. Machaut wrote in all of them and effectively completed their development; after him the forms were inherited rather than invented. This one comes down in his own supervised manuscripts, which give the songs in careful order by genre.","Four-voice songs are rare in the fourteenth century, and the evidence suggests the extra part here was added to an existing three-voice piece — the song works, and is sometimes performed, without it. That makes it a small window on how this music was revised and expanded after the fact rather than fixed at birth.","Performers disagree about whether to sing all the parts or give the lower lines to vielle and harp, and about tempo: taken slowly it becomes hypnotic, taken briskly it becomes a courtly compliment. Its length has kept it out of anthologies in favour of Douce dame jolie, but musicians often name it the finer piece.","Follow the top voice alone for the first minute and let the rest become a background wash; the ornament is where the expression lives. Then listen for the refrain returning intact and notice how the piece resists moving forward. Compare it with a Landini ballata for the smoother, warmer Italian alternative."],"machaut-puis-qu-en-oubli":["A short three-voice rondeau — barely two minutes and only eight lines of poetry. Since I am forgotten by you, sweet friend, the singer says, I take my leave of a life of love. The setting is unusually plain for Machaut: the lines move together without much decoration, saving ornament for the cadences, and the texture is thin enough that you can hear every part. Then the opening refrain returns and closes the door.","It belongs with a group of songs attached to Machaut's long narrative poem about an old poet and a young admirer, the Voir Dit, which mixes verse, letters and music into something like an autobiography. Whether the relationship it describes was real has been argued for a century; the songs are real enough, and this one is the farewell.","The refrain is the last line of the poem as well as the first, so the leave-taking is spoken twice, and the song ends on the same words it opened with. Machaut strips the decoration precisely where a courtly song would normally display it — the plainness is the expressive decision, not a lapse.","It has become a favourite of singers looking for a way into Machaut without the density of the four-voice songs, and it turns up in film and on compilation albums. Musicologists use it to argue about the Voir Dit's reliability: how much of the poem is a fictional frame and how much a record of events.","Two minutes, so play it twice. The first time listen to the words, the second to the two lower voices, which are simple enough to follow individually. Then hear Dowland's Flow My Tears and note how little the vocabulary of musical grief had to change in two hundred and fifty years."],"machaut-messe-nostre-dame-agnus-dei":["The Mass's closing plea, in three invocations: the first and third share their music, the second is set apart, so the movement is an arch. As in the Kyrie, four voices work over a plainchant tenor stretched into long notes and organised by a repeating rhythmic pattern. The upper parts break into hocket — a rapid alternation where one voice sings while another rests, the line stuttering between them — and the whole thing closes on a bare open chord.","The words ask the Lamb of God for mercy and finally for peace, and the Mass was endowed for the souls of Machaut and his brother at Reims, so the request is personal as well as liturgical. France in the 1360s had lost two great battles and its king had been taken prisoner at Poitiers; the plea for peace was not abstract.","Only the movements with long, wordless chant melismas — Kyrie, Sanctus, Agnus and the closing Ite missa est — are built on strict rhythmic patterns; the text-heavy Gloria and Credo are set in a fast, chordal, syllabic style instead. The construction follows the shape of the words, not a uniform scheme. Setting the Ite missa est at all was almost unheard of.","Modern choirs usually transpose the Mass upward to suit mixed voices, which lightens a sound the sources suggest was low and hard. Hocket divides listeners more than any other feature: some hear a nervous, sobbing effect entirely suited to the text, others hear a mannerism. There is no consensus on tempo, and recordings vary by half again in length.","Listen for the hocket — the moment the melody starts jumping between singers, so no one voice has it. Then hear the first and third invocations back to back and confirm they are the same music. Byrd's Agnus Dei from the Mass for Four Voices makes a striking companion across two centuries."],"landini-ecco-primavera":["Under two minutes, two voices, and an unmistakable lift in the pulse. Here is spring, the text announces, the season that calls us to love — and the music dances. The form is a ballata: an opening refrain, two matched verse sections, a final section that returns to the refrain's tune, then the refrain again. The two lines move together in easy parallel motion, sweet where French music of the same decade is angular, and the whole thing is over almost before it starts.","Florence in the decades after the Black Death, where Landini was the city's celebrated organist, blind from childhood smallpox, and a public figure crowned with laurel in Venice. The ballata was originally a dance song, and this one keeps the tread. Nearly all Landini's surviving music comes from one source, the Squarcialupi Codex, copied around 1410 to 1415 and opening with his portrait at a small portable organ.","The ornament that bears his name is all over this song: approaching the final note of a phrase, the top voice dips to the step below the leading note before rising to the goal. It gives Italian music of the period its particular softness at every cadence, and once you have heard it here you will hear it everywhere in the Trecento.","Trecento music vanished almost entirely until the nineteenth century brought the Squarcialupi Codex back into view. Performance is contested in the usual ways: whether the lower voice was sung or played on vielle or organetto, whether percussion belongs anywhere near it. Landini's reputation has risen steadily as recordings have multiplied, though he is still known for a handful of songs out of one hundred and fifty.","Tap the pulse from the first bar; the swing is the point. Listen for the little dip before each phrase-ending, then try any other Landini ballata and count how often it recurs. A version with organetto on the lower line gives the closest guess at what Florence actually heard."],"landini-questa-fanciulla":["A three-voice ballata in which the singer asks Love to make a girl merciful towards him, since her beauty has set him alight. The top line carries the melody in flowing, decorated phrases while two lower parts move underneath, often in parallel, giving the warm full sound that separates Italian song of the period from French. The ballata form — refrain, two verse halves, a closing section on the refrain's tune, refrain again — runs its course in about three minutes.","Landini worked at the church of San Lorenzo in Florence and moved in the city's literary circles; a contemporary dialogue by Giovanni da Prato describes him playing the organetto in a garden while the birds fall silent to listen. Ballate were sung in exactly such settings — private, sociable, among people who knew the poems and often knew the poet.","Watch the two lower voices: they frequently move in the same direction at the same distance, which by strict French standards of the day was a fault. Italian composers kept doing it because it sounds good, and the resulting sweetness is what northerners later travelled south to learn.","It is one of the ballate most often programmed by modern ensembles, partly because the three-voice texture works with any combination of singers and instruments. Scholarly interest centres less on the individual song than on the Squarcialupi Codex itself, whose lavish illumination raises the question of whether it was a working book or a monument to a lost generation.","Listen once for the top voice as a tune, then again for the two beneath it as a pair. Notice how the phrase-endings soften. Play it next to a Machaut rondeau of the same years and the contrast between Italian sweetness and French edge is immediate."],"landini-angelica-bilta":["A longer and more elaborate ballata than his dance-like miniatures: about four minutes, three voices, and a top line spun out in extended garlands of notes that hold up the words rather than serve them. The text praises an angelic beauty in the conventional terms of the age, but the setting treats each phrase as an occasion for melodic expansion. The lower parts move slowly, so the song hangs in the air, closing each section with the familiar softened cadence.","By the 1380s Landini was the most admired musician in Florence, and the ballata had grown from a dance song into a display piece for professional singers. He was blind, composed and taught by ear, and reportedly tuned and built instruments; his epitaph slab, showing him with his organetto, still survives in the city.","The elaboration is concentrated on a few key words, and the syllables underneath those long runs are almost always the ones carrying the poem's central image. The decoration is rhetorical, not decorative — Landini is underlining, in a music that has no other way to do it.","Songs like this complicate the popular picture of Landini as a purveyor of charming spring songs; the later ballate are ambitious, sometimes severe. Because the Squarcialupi Codex gives no dates, the chronology of his output rests entirely on style, so any ordering of the songs is an argument rather than a fact.","Follow one long melisma from its first note to the change of syllable and hear how far the singer travels. Compare it with Ecco la primavera to see the same composer at opposite extremes of scale. Machaut's four-voice rondeau makes the natural French counterpart."],"landini-ochi-dolenti-mie":["My grieving eyes, the singer begins, addressing the part of himself that did the falling in love. It is a ballata in three voices, four minutes or so, and the mood is darker than his spring songs: slower motion, more falling phrases, more weight on the lower parts. The refrain returns after the verses as the form demands, but here the repetition reads as brooding rather than as dance, and the closing cadence sounds more like resignation than sweetness.","Grief over love was the standard subject of the age's lyric, in Florence as in Paris, and the ballata could carry it as readily as it carried spring. Landini set his own texts and those of Florentine poets in his circle; the vocabulary of tearful eyes and merciless ladies came straight out of Petrarch, whose influence over Italian verse was by then total.","Notice how much of the melody moves downwards. The song keeps starting a phrase high and letting it sink, so the shape of the line does the mourning; when the refrain returns it starts high again, and the whole descent has to be made once more.","Modern taste has favoured the bright Landini over the melancholy one, and this song is far less recorded than Ecco la primavera. Ensembles who take it slowly find something close to the later madrigal in it; taken at a walking pace it remains firmly a dance-form song. There is no evidence to settle which is right.","Listen for the direction of the melody rather than for the tune: count how many phrases fall. Then play Machaut's Puis qu'en oubli, another short farewell to love from the same decades, and hear two different languages saying the same thing."],"dunstaple-quam-pulchra-es":["Four minutes for three voices, and the striking thing is that they mostly sing the same words at the same moment. Instead of independent lines tangling around each other, this is a declaimed setting in chords, the text audible throughout — how beautiful you are, my love, from the Song of Songs. The chords themselves are full of thirds and sixths, which give a warmth that continental music of 1430 largely lacked. Only at the closing Alleluia do the voices break into decoration.","The Song of Songs was read as a love poem addressed to the Virgin, so an erotic Hebrew text became Marian devotion. Dunstaple served John, Duke of Bedford, the English regent in France during the last phase of the Hundred Years War, and English chapels travelling with the occupation are the likeliest route by which this style reached Burgundian and French musicians.","There is no borrowed chant anywhere. The piece is freely composed, words and music together, at a time when almost all sacred polyphony was built on an inherited melody. That freedom is what lets the text govern the rhythm: the music moves when the words move, and stops when they stop.","Around 1440 the poet Martin le Franc wrote that Du Fay and Binchois had taken up the English manner, the contenance angloise, following Dunstaple — and this piece is the standard illustration of what he meant. English attributions are unstable, and some sources give works of this kind to Leonel Power instead. The style's ultimate origin, English or continental, is still argued.","Listen for the sweetness of the chords and try to say why they sound modern next to Machaut: it is the thirds. Follow the words, which for once you can. The Hilliard Ensemble and the Orlando Consort both record it; a small group of solo voices suits it far better than a choir."],"dunstaple-veni-sancte-spiritus":["A large four-voice motet for Pentecost, running about seven minutes in four sections. Two texts sound at once: the sequence Veni Sancte Spiritus in the upper voices, and the hymn Veni creator spiritus carried by the tenor beneath. Each section covers the same borrowed melody, but the note values shrink as the piece goes on, so the music accelerates while the singers keep an even beat. It ends far faster and brighter than it began.","Pentecost was one of the great feasts, and the layered-text motet was the most prestigious form a composer could attempt: it announced learning as well as devotion. Dunstaple's works survive largely in continental manuscripts, above all the Trent Codices copied in the Alps, because the English Reformation destroyed most of the choirbooks at home.","The acceleration is written into the notation, not the tempo. Each section reduces the tenor's values by a fixed proportion, so the last runs at three times the speed of the first from the same written notes. The singers do nothing different; the mathematics does it. This is isorhythm at its most audible, and the reason to hear the piece whole.","It is generally regarded as the finest English isorhythmic motet and the summit of a tradition that ended within a generation, as the freer, chord-based style of Quam pulchra es took over. Editors disagree over section boundaries and over which of two surviving text layouts is original, so recordings differ in shape.","Do not skip ahead: the entire effect depends on hearing the four sections in order and feeling the surface tighten. Time the first section and the last. Then hear Du Fay's Nuper rosarum flores, which does something similar with proportions on the other side of the Channel."],"dunstaple-missa-rex-seculorum":["A complete Mass ordinary in three voices, roughly twenty-five minutes, held together by two devices: every movement is built over the same borrowed chant melody in the tenor, and every movement opens with the same short phrase in the top voice. The result is a set of pieces that clearly belong to one another, sung across a service they would never be heard together in. The texture is smooth, the dissonance mild, the lines long and unhurried.","The unified Mass cycle was an English invention of the 1420s and 1430s, and it changed what a composer could aim at: not a movement but a work in four parts spread across an hour of liturgy. English singers and manuscripts reached the continent through the occupation of northern France and through the Council of Basel, and the idea took root there permanently.","The opening phrase shared by all the movements — the head-motif — is doing more work than the chant tenor, because it is the thing an ordinary listener actually notices returning. The chant is buried in long notes; the motif is on the surface. English composers understood early that unity has to be audible to count.","The Mass is attributed to Dunstaple in some sources and to Leonel Power in others, and the question is unresolved; the two men's styles are close enough that scholars have argued both ways for a century. It survives in the Trent Codices, so what we have is an Alpine copy of English music that was itself later destroyed.","Listen to the first ten seconds of each movement in turn and you will hear the shared opening at once — that is the invention this Mass exists to demonstrate. Then hear Du Fay's Missa Se la face ay pale to see what the continent did with the idea within fifteen years."],"dunstaple-salve-scema-sanctitatis":["A four-voice isorhythmic motet in honour of St Katherine, about six minutes long and laid out in strictly proportioned sections. Two upper voices declaim an elaborate Latin poem of praise while the lower parts carry a borrowed melody in a repeating rhythmic frame. It is the severe side of Dunstaple: less of the sweetness that made him famous abroad, more of the numerical rigour that English composers had inherited from the previous century.","St Katherine of Alexandria was among the most popular saints in late medieval England, with guilds, chapels and a huge devotional literature attached to her. A motet on this scale was written for a specific occasion — a patronal feast, a foundation, a great chapel's celebration — and the poem's elaborate praise suggests a commission rather than routine use.","The sections are laid out in exact numerical proportion to one another, a habit that medieval writers connected to the harmony of creation itself. Number schemes of this kind were meant to be admired on the page as much as heard, so part of the piece was addressed to a reader rather than a listener.","It is far less recorded than Quam pulchra es and shows Dunstaple in the older manner, which has made it useful for scholars arguing that the contenance angloise was one option among several rather than a wholesale conversion. Text and structure both survive imperfectly, and editions differ.","Listen for the lower voices' pattern coming round rather than for the melody, and let the Latin wash past. Then play Quam pulchra es straight afterwards: the same composer, ten years or so apart, sounding like two different centuries."],"dufay-nuper-rosarum-flores":["Four voices in four sections, each opening the same way: two upper voices in a free, soaring duet, alone, for a good while — and then, underneath them, two tenors enter together with a plainchant melody a fifth apart, in canon. That entry, repeated four times with different music above it, is the event of the piece. The sections run at different speeds, so the same material feels stretched and compressed as it goes. Seven minutes, and it moves like architecture being raised.","It was sung on 25 March 1436 at the consecration of Florence Cathedral by Pope Eugene IV, with Brunelleschi's dome newly closed over the crossing. Du Fay was in the papal chapel, which had been resident in Florence during the pope's exile from Rome. The text refers to a golden rose the pope had presented to the city, and the chant the tenors sing is Terribilis est locus iste — awesome is this place.","The four sections stand in the proportions 6:4:2:3. In 1973 Charles Warren claimed these matched the dimensions of Brunelleschi's dome, and the story spread everywhere. Craig Wright later argued the numbers correspond instead to the Temple of Solomon as given in the Book of Kings — a building the consecration liturgy explicitly invokes. The proportions are certainly deliberate; what they encode is still argued.","A Florentine eyewitness, Giannozzo Manetti, described the sound filling the church so completely that it seemed to come from heaven, which is why we know how it landed. The dome theory made the motet famous with general readers and is now largely rejected by specialists — a rare case where a discredited idea did a piece of music a great favour.","Wait for the tenors. Each section begins with the duet alone, and the moment the low voices enter beneath it is the one to listen for; there are four chances. A small ensemble in a resonant stone building comes closest to the original conditions, and the piece rewards volume."],"dufay-missa-se-face-ay-pale-kyrie":["Four voices, and underneath them the tune of a love song stretched into enormous slow notes. Du Fay took the tenor of his own ballade — if my face is pale, love is the cause — and made it the foundation of an entire Mass. In the Kyrie the borrowed melody unfolds once, so slowly that it registers as a series of held pitches rather than a tune, while the other three voices weave freely above and around it. Three sections, about five minutes.","The Mass was probably written for the chapel of the Duke of Savoy in the 1450s, possibly for a ducal wedding, and it is the first Mass by a leading composer to build every movement on a secular song. Du Fay by then had been a papal singer, a canon of Cambrai, and the most sought-after composer in Europe for thirty years.","In the Gloria and Credo the borrowed tenor is sung three times over, at three different speeds, and the singers had to work out those speeds from a written instruction rather than read them as notes. The tenor part was, in effect, a puzzle with a solution — one line of music doing three jobs.","Building sacred music on a love song looks scandalous now and was entirely normal then; the objections came a century later, from the Council of Trent. The Savoy occasion is inference from circumstantial evidence. Performance debates concern whether the tenor should be sung or played on a slide trumpet, which some sources suggest for exactly this kind of long-note line.","Find a recording of the ballade first — it takes two minutes — then listen to the Kyrie and try to pick the tune out of the slow-moving middle. You probably will not manage it, and that is the discovery. Ockeghem and Josquin both take the technique further."],"dufay-ave-maris-stella":["A Vespers hymn to the Virgin, sung in alternation: odd verses in plainchant, even verses in three voices, so the ear keeps swinging between bare unison and full harmony. The polyphonic verses are simple by design — the chant sits in the top voice, decorated a little, with a bottom voice moving mostly in parallel a sixth below and a third part filling the middle. The sound is rich, sweet and almost entirely made of chords in motion.","Du Fay wrote a set of hymns for the papal chapel covering the church year, giving singers polyphonic alternatives for the verses they sang every week. It was practical repertory rather than a showpiece, and it spread fast because it solved a problem: how to make an ordinary service sound splendid without rehearsing anything difficult.","One of the three voices is not written down. The manuscript gives two parts and the word fauxbourdon, which instructs a singer to double the top line a fourth below, by ear, in real time. So the score is deliberately incomplete, and the full harmony only exists in performance — written composition and improvisation fused in one piece.","Whether fauxbourdon began in England, where singers had long improvised in parallel thirds and sixths, or on the continent, has been argued for a hundred years without resolution. What is clear is that it made the new sweet harmony available to any chapel with three competent singers, which is why the style conquered Europe so quickly.","Listen for the alternation and notice how much brighter the harmonised verses feel arriving after plainchant. Then try to pick out the middle voice, the one that was improvised. Any ensemble that keeps a real chant choir for the odd verses gives the right effect."],"dufay-missa-l-homme-arme-kyrie":["Four voices over a popular tune. The armed man is to be feared, the song warns, everyone should arm himself with a coat of mail — and Du Fay lays that melody in long notes in the tenor and builds a Kyrie over it in three sections. The upper voices are busy and rhythmically springy; the tenor moves like a slow clock beneath them. Five minutes, and unusually martial in feel for a Mass movement, with sharply cut phrases and a hard rhythmic tread.","The L'homme armé tune attracted something like forty Mass settings over a hundred and fifty years, an unparalleled tradition. Its popularity has been linked to the Order of the Golden Fleece at the Burgundian court and to crusading rhetoric after Constantinople fell to the Ottomans in 1453. Du Fay spent his last decades as a canon at Cambrai, at the centre of that northern musical world.","At the close of the Mass, in the final Agnus Dei, the tenor is instructed to sing its tune backwards — the direction in the manuscript tells the singer to let the crab walk full but return half. It is a rare piece of wit inside a Mass, and a demonstration that the borrowed tune was material to be worked rather than a quotation to be respected.","The attribution to Du Fay was doubted in the past and is now generally accepted. The tradition itself remains the puzzle: nobody has explained convincingly why one secular tune, and a militant one, became the standard test piece for sacred composition across five generations, or where the melody came from.","Learn the tune first — it is short, blunt and instantly memorable — then hunt for it in the slow middle of the texture. Follow with Josquin's Agnus Dei on the same melody to hear what the next generation added. Recordings by the Binchois Consort and the Tallis Scholars take contrasting approaches."],"dufay-flos-florum":["A short Marian motet in three voices, and quite unlike the massive ceremonial pieces Du Fay became known for. The top voice carries an ornate, song-like melody; two slower parts support it underneath. There is no borrowed chant and no rhythmic scaffolding — this is a prayer written in the shape of a love song, four minutes long, ending in a long spun-out Amen where the melody finally lets go and runs.","Du Fay spent his twenties in Italy, in the orbit of the Malatesta family and then the papal chapel, and this belongs to those early years. The style, called cantilena, treats the top line as a soloist and the lower parts as accompaniment — the format of secular song, borrowed for devotion, and the most personal-sounding music of the period.","Everything expressive happens in the final Amen. The words end, and the melody keeps going for a long stretch on a single syllable, decorating with nothing left to say. For a fifteenth-century composer, ornament in a wordless space was the closest thing available to speaking in one's own voice.","Early Du Fay is dated almost entirely by style and by which manuscripts contain it, so a range of years is the most anyone can honestly claim. Pieces like this have been used to argue that his Italian period, not his later Cambrai decades, produced his most immediately appealing music.","Skip to the last minute first and listen to the Amen; then start again from the beginning knowing where it is going. Compare it with Dunstaple's Quam pulchra es, written around the same time on the other side of the Channel, for two solutions to the same problem."],"dufay-vergene-bella":["A setting of the opening stanza of Petrarch's great canzone to the Virgin — beautiful Virgin, clothed in the sun — for three voices, about six minutes. The top line is long-breathed and highly decorated, unfolding continuously rather than repeating; two slower voices move beneath. It is through-composed, which for a song of this date is unusual, and the effect is of a single enormous sentence that never quite comes to rest until the end.","Du Fay was in Italy in the 1420s, probably in the service of the Malatesta rulers of Rimini and Pesaro before joining the papal chapel. Petrarch had been dead for fifty years and his poetry was becoming the model for Italian literary art; setting him was a statement about ambition, since composers of the day mostly set anonymous courtly verse.","This is among the earliest polyphonic settings of Petrarch that survives — a full century before the madrigal made Petrarch the standard poet of Italian music. Du Fay sets only the first of the canzone's stanzas, so the piece is a fragment of a much longer prayer, and it ends where the poem is still beginning.","The dating rests on manuscript and stylistic evidence, so it is probable rather than known. The piece matters to historians as evidence that a northern composer working in Italy in the 1420s was already reaching for Italian literary prestige, and to listeners because it is the most emotionally direct thing in Du Fay's early output.","Listen to the top line as if it were a single unbroken sentence and try to hear where the breaths fall. Then read Petrarch's stanza and listen again. Landini fifty years earlier and Monteverdi two centuries later make the natural bookends."],"ockeghem-missa-prolationum-kyrie":["Only two voice-parts are written down, and each is read simultaneously by two singers at different speeds, so two lines become four, one pair steadily drawing away from the other. Every section of the Mass works this way, and across the whole cycle the distance between the canonic voices widens step by step, from unison to the octave. What you hear is not a puzzle but a dark, slow, continuously unfolding sound with almost no repetition and very few full stops.","Ockeghem was treasurer of the abbey of Saint-Martin at Tours and first chaplain to three successive kings of France — an establishment figure with a long career and, by all accounts, a famously deep bass voice. Canonic ingenuity was a courtly value, admired the way a table fountain or an astronomical clock was admired: as visible mastery over difficulty.","There is no borrowed chant and no borrowed song anywhere in the Mass. It is generated entirely from its own rules, which makes it the most self-contained large work of the fifteenth century. And the canonic interval expands by one step in each successive pair of movements — a plan invisible in any single section and only perceptible across the whole hour.","Admired since the sixteenth century and almost never sung until the twentieth. Editors still disagree over how the canons resolve at several points, which means recordings differ in detail. It is the piece invariably cited when someone claims that Renaissance polyphony anticipates twentieth-century serial construction, a comparison that flatters both and explains neither.","Do not try to follow the canon on a first hearing — it will only frustrate you. Listen instead for the low, shadowed sound and for how rarely the voices cadence together. Then read how it is made and listen again. Machaut's palindrome rondeau is the ancestor; any Bach canon is the descendant."],"ockeghem-intemerata-dei-mater":["Five voices, all pitched unusually low, in a prayer to the Virgin lasting about ten minutes across three sections. The lines are long and irregular, entering at unpredictable moments and rarely arriving anywhere together. There is no tune to hold onto and almost no repetition; the piece proceeds as a slow, dark, continuously shifting mass of sound, with the bass sinking further than almost anything else written in the century.","Ockeghem was mourned across Europe when he died — poets, composers and rival courts produced laments — and part of the reason was the sheer strangeness of music like this beside the clarity his contemporaries favoured. The text is a devotional poem rather than a liturgical one, which freed him from the usual structural obligations.","The voices are written so that they almost never cadence at the same moment: as one line closes, another has already begun its next phrase, sealing over the join. The result is ten minutes without a full stop. It is a technique for avoiding structure rather than building it, and it is why this music feels closer to weather than to architecture.","The low tessitura raises the question of pitch standard: sung at written pitch it demands basses of a range that may not have existed, so most modern groups transpose upward, which changes the character substantially. Ockeghem's reputation has swung between admiration for his craft and complaints about his obscurity ever since the sixteenth century.","Give up on following individual lines and listen to the sound as a whole, the way you would listen to a room. Notice how seldom the music stops. Then hear Josquin's Miserere, written a generation later, where the same darkness is organised around a repeating cry."],"ockeghem-missa-mi-mi-kyrie":["The bass opens alone with a falling leap of a fifth, and that gesture returns at the head of every movement of the Mass, so the whole cycle is stamped with one sound. Four voices, no borrowed chant, about four minutes for the Kyrie's three sections. The writing is dense and low, the lines overlapping continuously, but the recurring bass leap gives the ear something solid to hold, which the Missa prolationum deliberately withholds.","Ockeghem wrote for the French royal chapel across four decades and three kings, and his Masses circulated widely without ever becoming easy repertory. This is his most performed, largely because its identifying gesture is so immediate: a listener who has heard the first three seconds can recognise the piece anywhere in it.","The nickname is a pun from the way singers named notes. The two pitches of that opening leap were both called mi, in two different overlapping six-note scales that musicians used to navigate the gamut. So the Mass is named after a technicality of medieval solfege — an in-joke among professionals, preserved for five hundred years.","Sixteenth-century theorists praised Ockeghem's craft while later writers, from Burney onward, complained that his music was arid ingenuity. The modern rehabilitation began in the 1950s with complete editions, and recordings by groups such as the Clerks and Cappella Pratensis have made the case that the density is expressive rather than mechanical.","Memorise the opening leap in the bass, then listen for it at the start of each movement — it is the easiest structural device in fifteenth-century music to hear. After that, let the four voices blur and enjoy the darkness. Du Fay's Se la face ay pale makes the clearer, brighter comparison."],"ockeghem-requiem-introitus":["The opening of the earliest polyphonic Requiem that survives. It begins almost bare — plainchant, or two voices only — and then thickens, voices joining until four are sounding, so the movement grows out of chant rather than replacing it. Eternal rest grant them, the text asks. Roughly six minutes, and each successive section of the Mass uses a different number and combination of voices, which makes the work a catalogue of textures as much as a setting.","Du Fay left instructions for his own Requiem to be sung at his deathbed in 1474, but that music is lost, so Ockeghem's stands at the head of a line running through Victoria, Mozart, Verdi, Faure and Britten. Requiem chant varied from region to region before Trent standardised it, and Ockeghem uses French forms that later settings do not.","The borrowed chant is not buried in the tenor where a Mass would normally put it. It sits in the top voice, lightly decorated, so the plainchant and the polyphony are audible at the same time and the listener can hear exactly what is being elaborated. It is the most transparent use of a cantus firmus in the whole century.","The setting is incomplete: what survives breaks off before the Agnus Dei, and whether the rest was lost, never written, or never intended is unknown. Because the chant differs from the later standard version, editors have had to reconstruct the plainchant portions from French sources, so performances vary in what surrounds the polyphony.","Listen for the top voice and try to separate the chant from the decoration around it — here, unusually, you can. Notice how the texture thickens as the movement proceeds. Then jump to Victoria's Requiem of 1605 to hear where the tradition had travelled in a hundred and thirty years."],"josquin-ave-maria-virgo-serena":["It opens with the clearest demonstration of imitation ever written: one voice sings a phrase, a second enters below with the same phrase, then a third, then a fourth, each a fourth or fifth apart, so the texture assembles itself in front of you. What follows keeps changing shape — paired duets answering each other, a passage in swinging triple time, full four-voice writing — until the last line, where the polyphony stops dead and four voices ask together, in plain chords, that the Mother of God remember me.","Josquin spent his most productive years in Italy, in Milan, in the papal chapel in Rome, and later at Ferrara, and this piece belongs to that period. In 1502 the Venetian printer Ottaviano Petrucci opened Motetti A, the first printed book of motets, with it — the first piece of polyphony most European musicians would ever have bought rather than copied.","The middle of the text runs through five feasts of the Virgin in five short stanzas, and Josquin gives each one a different texture: a duet here, a trio there, a lilting dance for another. The form is a calendar. He also builds the opening from a plainchant sequence melody, so the imitation that sounds like pure invention is derived.","Contemporaries treated it as the model of the new style, and Luther's famous line that Josquin was master of the notes while others were mastered by them was written about a composer known through pieces like this. The great modern problem is attribution: dozens of works long credited to Josquin have been reassigned as the sources were re-examined. This one is secure.","Count the four entries in the opening thirty seconds — you can, easily, and that is the point. Then wait five minutes for the chords at the end, where the machinery stops and a single sentence is simply spoken. The Tallis Scholars record it with the clarity the opening needs."],"josquin-missa-pange-lingua-kyrie":["Instead of parking a borrowed melody in one voice, Josquin dissolves the hymn into all four. Each phrase of the tune becomes a shape that every voice takes up in turn, so the chant is everywhere and never quite audible as itself. The Kyrie's three sections work through the hymn's first phrases in order, so the ordinary text and the borrowed melody advance together. Five minutes of continuously imitative writing that sounds improvised and is anything but.","This is probably his last Mass, written in his final years as provost of the collegiate church at Conde-sur-l'Escaut, back in the north after decades in Italy. The hymn is Thomas Aquinas's Corpus Christi text, sung at the procession of the Blessed Sacrament. The Mass was not printed in his lifetime; it appeared in 1539, nearly twenty years after his death.","The technique has a name — paraphrase — and it solved a problem the cantus firmus Mass could not: how to make a borrowed melody structural and expressive at once. Listen to how each new section begins, and you will hear the next phrase of the hymn stated plainly in one voice before the others begin to disguise it.","Sixteenth-century theorists held it up as the summit of the style, and it has stayed there. Modern arguments are about performance: one voice per part or a choir, high pitch or low, and whether the Renaissance ideal of clarity that the Tallis Scholars represent is historically right or a twentieth-century English preference imposed on Flemish music.","Listen to the first ten seconds of each of the three sections and you will hear the hymn's phrases stated in turn, unmistakably. Then let the rest blur. Follow it with the Ave Maria to hear the same composer thirty years earlier, and with Palestrina to hear what came next."],"josquin-mille-regretz":["Two minutes, four voices, almost no counterpoint. A thousand regrets at leaving you, the text begins, and the music sets it in near-chords, syllable by syllable, the voices moving together. The melody sinks: the opening phrase descends, and the closing phrase — my days will soon be ended — descends further and is repeated, sagging each time. There is no display, no imitation to admire, nothing but the shape of the falling line and the weight of four voices agreeing.","The chanson was printed in Paris by Attaingnant in the 1530s and circulated everywhere. Tradition attaches it to the Emperor Charles V, who is said to have loved it, which earned it the nickname the Emperor's song. In 1538 the Spanish vihuelist Luis de Narvaez arranged it for solo plucked instrument as La cancion del Emperador, one of the first great transcriptions in Western music.","The attribution to Josquin rests on a single source, and modern scholarship has repeatedly questioned it: the piece may be by a contemporary and simply have acquired the famous name, as many works did. Cristobal de Morales built an entire Mass on it, so whoever wrote it, sixteenth-century composers treated it as canonical.","It has become the emblem of the late Josquin manner — plain, syllabic, stripped of everything that made the early motets dazzling — and simultaneously the standard test case in the attribution debates that have reshaped his catalogue since the 1990s. The two things pull against each other, which is why it turns up in almost every discussion of the composer.","Play it twice, then play the Narvaez vihuela version and hear the same music become an instrumental lament. Follow the top voice down. Then hear Dowland or Monteverdi's Lamento della ninfa for where the descending line of grief goes next."],"josquin-el-grillo":["The cricket is a good singer, the text declares, who holds a long note — and the four voices immediately do exactly that, first chattering in clipped repeated notes like an insect, then stopping dead to hold one chord for an absurdly long time. Under two minutes, chordal, fast, with a bouncing dance rhythm and a middle section that slows to mock-serious solemnity before the chattering returns. It is the funniest piece of the fifteenth century.","The frottola was the light Italian part-song of the courts of Mantua, Ferrara and Milan around 1500 — witty, in the vernacular, deliberately unlearned, and the fashionable alternative to Franco-Flemish seriousness. Petrucci published this one in his third book of frottole in Venice in 1505. Josquin, a northerner working in Italy, was writing in a local idiom for local patrons.","It reads as a joke about singers. The cricket who holds long notes and sings for love, unlike birds who sing only for money, is very likely a colleague — a musician named Grillo has been proposed at the Milanese chapel. If so, the piece is a professional in-joke that has outlived everyone who could have got it.","Like much of the corpus, its authorship has been questioned, since the frottola prints attached famous names freely. It is now Josquin's most performed piece among amateurs and school choirs, an outcome nobody would have predicted for a composer known in his day for enormous sacred works. Scholars use it to argue that his range was far wider than the solemn reputation suggests.","Listen for the moment the voices stop chattering and hold — the joke is entirely in the timing, and a good group makes it land. Then compare it with the Miserere written three years later by the same man. Lassus's Matona mia cara is the obvious next stop."],"josquin-nymphes-des-bois":["Five voices, five minutes, and two languages at once. Four parts sing a French poem calling on wood nymphs, fountain-goddesses and all skilled singers to change their clear voices to piercing cries, while the tenor holds the Latin chant of the Requiem introit beneath them. The final section abandons metaphor and names names: Josquin, Brumel, Pierre de la Rue, Compere — told to weep for the loss of their good father, and to put on mourning clothes.","Johannes Ockeghem died in February 1497, and the musical world of northern Europe responded with an outpouring of laments. Jean Molinet, the Burgundian court poet, wrote this one. Josquin set it and put himself in the text among the bereaved, which makes the piece a rare thing: a composer signing his own name inside the music, as a mourner.","Several manuscripts write the whole piece in black notation, at a period when the standard note-heads were hollow. The notes themselves are dressed in mourning. It is a visual pun the singers saw on the page and the listener cannot possibly hear — one of the clearest surviving cases of Renaissance music addressing the eye as well as the ear.","The piece is the closest thing we have to a group portrait of a professional generation, and historians mine it for exactly that: four leading composers named as pupils or heirs of a fifth. The form, a French poem set against a Latin chant, barely outlived Josquin. Its authorship, unusually for him, is undisputed.","Wait for the four names near the end — knowing they are coming turns the piece from a general lament into a specific one. Listen for the tenor's Latin underneath the French. Then hear Ockeghem's own Requiem, and Part's Cantus for Britten, another composer's memorial to a predecessor."],"josquin-miserere-mei-deus":["Psalm 51 set at enormous length — around eighteen minutes in three parts — for five voices, four of which declaim the psalm verse by verse in spare, mostly syllabic writing. The fifth voice sings nothing but the opening plea, have mercy on me, God, over and over: twenty-one times in all, stepping down the scale note by note through the first part, climbing back through the second, descending again in the third. Everything else in the piece happens around that cry.","Josquin spent 1503 and 1504 as maestro di cappella to Duke Ercole I of Ferrara, the highest-paid appointment in Europe and one he held for barely a year before plague drove him north. Ercole owned a copy of Savonarola's meditation on this psalm, Infelix ego, written in a Florentine prison cell before the friar was hanged and burned in 1498.","The repeating cry moves by exactly one step each time it returns, so across the work it walks down a whole octave and back up. You can follow it with a finger. What sounds like an expressive obsession is a precise structural scheme, and the two are the same thing — the plea's descent through the range is the piece's argument.","Contemporaries called it his greatest work, and it circulated across Europe in manuscript and print. The link to Savonarola was proposed by modern scholarship, above all by Patrick Macey, and is now widely accepted, though no document ties the commission to the meditation. Eighteen minutes of near-monotone declamation makes it a hard sell in concert, and recordings are fewer than the reputation suggests.","Track the repeated cry and nothing else on a first hearing: hear it come back lower, then lower again. Once you can follow the descent, the surrounding music makes sense as commentary on it. One voice per part keeps the ostinato audible; a large choir buries it."],"josquin-missa-l-homme-arme-sexti-toni":["The Agnus Dei falls in three parts, and the last is the reason people know the Mass. From two written voice-parts, sung simultaneously at different speeds, Josquin builds a six-voice texture in which the armed-man tune piles up on itself in slow motion, one entry overtaking another, the sound thickening without any new material arriving. It is a controlled accumulation lasting several minutes, and it ends the Mass by expansion rather than by cadence.","Petrucci printed this Mass in Misse Josquin (Venice, 1502) — the first printed book of Masses devoted to a single composer, a commercial decision that tells you what his name was worth. Josquin wrote two Masses on the L'homme armé tune, and setting the melody had become the professional proving ground for anyone with ambitions in sacred music.","The final Agnus carries a canon instruction at the head of the part rather than the extra voices themselves, so the printed page contains far fewer notes than the music sounds. Singers had to derive the missing parts from a written direction. The most overwhelming passage in the Mass exists, on paper, as an instruction.","The resolutions and the exact speed relationships between the canonic voices are still argued, and recordings differ by minutes as a result. The Mass has become the standard demonstration piece for the claim that Josquin combined technical display with expressive effect — the canon is a stunt and also the emotional climax, and no one has explained that away.","Go straight to the final Agnus and count the voices as they multiply. Then go back and hear the first two sections, which now sound like preparation. Compare with Du Fay's setting of the same tune forty years earlier, and with Ockeghem's canon Mass."],"obrecht-missa-maria-zart-kyrie":["Obrecht's longest mass — around seventy minutes complete — opens with a Kyrie for four unaccompanied voices that unfolds with unusual patience. The German devotional song 'Maria zart von edler Art' supplies the raw material, but Obrecht rarely lets it sound whole: fragments surface in one voice, then another, stretched or compressed, while the surrounding lines spin long garlands of melisma. The texture is dense, low-lying and continuous, with cadences that arrive late and softly.","Obrecht spent his career in restless motion — Bergen op Zoom, Cambrai, Bruges, Antwerp — resigning posts and being re-hired, forever chasing something better, and burying a father he memorialised in music. This mass probably dates from around 1503, near the end. Its song was a favourite of German-speaking devotion to the Virgin, so a patron east of the Low Countries seems likely. Within two years Obrecht had crossed the Alps to Ferrara for a plum court job and died there of plague.","The song is never simply quoted. Obrecht cuts 'Maria zart' into segments and deploys them by a private arithmetic — a phrase given out in long notes here, its second half only there, the whole tune withheld until late. Listeners in 1500 who knew the melody would have spent the mass waiting for it, catching pieces. Notice too that the lowest voice moves in a slower world than everything above it.","Nineteenth-century scholars, raised on Palestrina, found Obrecht arithmetical and cold, and the mass's sheer length told against it. Modern performers reversed the verdict, and recordings since the 1990s — by British and Flemish ensembles at low pitch, one or two voices per part — have made it a repertory piece. Debate continues over pitch and vocal scoring, and over whether the structural games were meant to be heard at all or offered to God alone.","Do not try to follow the tune; let the surface carry you. Listen instead for how long each phrase lasts before it turns a corner — much longer than in Josquin — and for the low, dark blend of the ensemble. Sample the Kyrie first, then jump to the closing Agnus Dei to hear where the argument lands. Low-pitch, few-voices-per-part recordings suit it best."],"obrecht-missa-sub-tuum-presidium-kyrie":["This Kyrie is scored for only three voices — the smallest sound in the mass — with the ancient Marian antiphon 'Sub tuum presidium' laid out in the top part in slow, plain notes while two lower voices weave beneath it. What makes it remarkable is what follows: each later movement adds a voice, until the Agnus Dei sings in seven parts, every added voice carrying a different Marian chant. The Kyrie is the seed of that expansion.","Obrecht wrote for a world saturated in Marian devotion: confraternities in Bruges and Antwerp paid handsomely for a daily Lady Mass, and a composer who could flatter the Virgin with ingenuity had steady employment. The antiphon he chose — 'beneath thy protection' — is among the oldest Marian prayers in Christian use, older than the Latin church that sang it. The mass probably dates from the 1490s, when Obrecht was moving between choirmaster posts in the Low Countries.","Count the voices at the start of each movement. Three in the Kyrie, four in the Gloria, five in the Credo, six in the Sanctus, seven in the Agnus Dei — and each new voice brings its own borrowed chant, so that by the end several different Marian melodies sound at once, at different speeds, without colliding. It is a musical rosary, and almost nobody notices the count on a first hearing.","The piece's fame rests on that scheme, which nineteenth-century writers cited as proof that Franco-Flemish composers cared more for puzzles than for prayer. Recent scholarship reads it the other way: the accumulating chants are an act of devotion, each one a further intercession. The attribution has never been seriously doubted, though the dating remains loose, resting on style and the manuscript's Low Countries origin rather than on documents.","Hear the Kyrie, then skip straight to the Agnus Dei and feel the difference in weight — the same music grown a body. Follow the highest voice throughout: it holds the antiphon in long notes and is your thread through the thickening texture. Try an ensemble that keeps the parts light and transparent rather than blending them into a wash."],"obrecht-parce-domine":["Two minutes for three low voices, and among the plainest things Obrecht wrote. The text is a single penitential cry from the prophet Joel — 'Spare, Lord, spare thy people' — set almost syllable to note, the voices moving close together in a narrow band of sound, mostly at a walking pace, with no melisma and no display. It ends where it began, subsiding rather than concluding. Most choirs sing it slowly and softly; the writing invites that.","Short penitential settings like this served Lent, processions, and times of plague, when a city needed music that could be learned quickly and sung by a handful of adult men. Obrecht knew such seasons at first hand: he died in a Ferrara epidemic in 1505, months after arriving. The piece circulated widely in manuscript and print across the sixteenth century, which is how a miniature by a composer known for hour-long masses stayed alive.","Notice how little the melody moves — most phrases turn around a single note, dropping a step and returning, as a spoken prayer would. The lowest voice is the one to watch: it repeatedly falls away beneath the others at 'populo tuo', darkening the sound at the exact word for 'thy people'. The attribution rests on sixteenth-century copies rather than an autograph, and a few scholars have queried it.","Because it is easy to sing and hard to sing well, 'Parce Domine' has been a choral-society staple for over a century, often in arrangements that add voices or an organ Obrecht never asked for. Specialists mostly prefer three solo men at low pitch. The piece contributes nothing to the technical arguments that dominate Obrecht scholarship, which is perhaps why it took so long to be taken seriously as music.","Play it twice in a row: once for the sound, once listening only to the bottom voice. Compare it with the vast Missa Maria zart by the same composer to hear how wide his range was. Then set it beside Byrd's 'Ave verum corpus' — another small, quiet, Catholic prayer written for hard times — and hear how similar restraint can be."],"taverner-missa-gloria-tibi-trinitas":["A mass for six voices — two trebles, alto, two tenors, bass — lasting some forty-five minutes and built throughout on the plainchant antiphon 'Gloria tibi Trinitas'. English style in the 1520s meant enormous vocal spans and endless melisma: single syllables stretched over dozens of notes, treble lines climbing far above anything continental composers asked for. There is no Kyrie, as English use required. Long trios for solo voices alternate with passages where the full six-part choir opens out.","Taverner became the first master of the choristers at Cardinal College, Oxford — Wolsey's grand new foundation, now Christ Church — in 1526, and this mass belongs to those years. Wolsey wanted the most lavish liturgy in England, and got it: a large choir of men and boys under a fan-vaulted roof, singing music designed to fill stone. Within a decade Wolsey had fallen, the break with Rome had begun, and the style became a liability.","In the Benedictus, at the words 'in nomine Domini', the six voices thin to four and the chant moves into the alto in even notes. That passage, lifted out and played by viols, became the 'In nomine' — a fixed tune on which English composers wrote hundreds of instrumental pieces for the next 150 years, through Byrd, Gibbons and Purcell. A cadence inside a mass turned into a national genre almost by accident.","Suppressed with the Latin rite, the mass survived in part-books and was not heard again until the twentieth-century revival of Tudor music. Scholars once repeated a story that Taverner repented of his 'popery' and burned his music; that reading of the documents has been largely abandoned. Performance debates now centre on pitch — many argue the music sounds better raised — and on whether boys or women should take the soaring top line.","Start with the Benedictus so you hear the 'In nomine' passage in its original home, then go back to the Gloria for the full six-part sound. Listen for how rarely all the voices sing together: Taverner keeps trading small groups, so the tutti moments land hard. A British cathedral-style choir at high pitch will show you what these acoustics were designed for."],"taverner-western-wynde-mass":["Four voices, about half an hour, and one tune repeated thirty-six times. The melody is 'Western Wynde', a secular English song about wind, rain and a lover's bed, and Taverner passes it around the choir — nine statements in each of four movements, mostly in the treble but sometimes moving down into the tenor or bass. Each repetition wears different clothing: new counterpoint above and below, changing numbers of voices, shifting speed in the surrounding parts.","English composers of the 1520s and 30s rarely built masses on secular songs — the continental habit of using a popular tune or a soldier's song for the liturgy never really took root here. Taverner's is one of only three English masses on the 'Western Wynde' melody; Christopher Tye and John Sheppard wrote the others, probably following him. It is a structural experiment, tighter and shorter than the vast votive style he otherwise practised.","The set-of-variations shape is the point, and it is unusual for a mass anywhere in Europe: rather than developing freely, Taverner runs the same frame thirty-six times, the way a later composer would write a chaconne. Count the statements if you like — the tune's rising opening marks each one. Only the words change the argument. The original song survives in a single manuscript, with just one verse of text.","The mass has been in print and in choirs since the Tudor revival began, partly because four voices are easier to muster than six. Arguments persist over whether the borrowed tune carried any secular meaning into church, or whether by 1530 it was simply good material. Some editors transpose the whole work upward, on the theory that Tudor pitch notation sat lower than modern reading suggests.","Listen for the tune and let yourself lose count — the pleasure is in noticing it re-enter after you had stopped expecting it. The Agnus Dei is the place to start: the writing thins, the tune slows, and the final statements feel like a farewell. Compare it with Byrd's four-voice Mass, written two generations later under very different pressure."],"taverner-dum-transisset-sabbatum":["An Easter responsory for five voices, roughly six minutes, alternating plainchant with polyphony. A solo voice intones the chant; then the full ensemble answers in long, floating lines that seem to have no downbeat at all. The text describes the three Marys buying spices to anoint Christ's body at first light. Taverner sets 'valde mane' — very early — with a slow rise through the voices, and closes with a long, unhurried 'alleluia'.","Matins on Easter morning, sung in the small hours, was the year's most elaborate service in an English monastic or collegiate church, and its responsories were the places where composers were allowed to expand. Taverner probably wrote this at Cardinal College or at Boston, Lincolnshire, where he spent his later years as a lay clerk and a guild official. Two settings of the text survive under his name; this is the more widely sung.","There is no clear pulse. Taverner deliberately staggers his entries so that no two voices agree on where a bar would begin, and the effect — floating, weightless — is what the Tudor revival prized most in him. Notice too that the chant is not decorated but embedded: it sits in an inner voice in long notes, so the plainsong you heard from the soloist is still sounding underneath everything else.","Rediscovered in the early twentieth century, it became one of the pieces that persuaded English listeners that Tudor church music was more than antiquarian. Editors disagree on where the chant sections stop and polyphony begins, and on whether the alleluia should be repeated. Choirs using women on the top line and choirs using boys produce markedly different results; period-minded groups also argue for a higher overall pitch.","Listen in a dark room; the piece was made for candlelight and a stone building. Follow the top voice for the first minute, then let go and hear the whole texture as weather. If it holds you, go to Sheppard's Easter responds next. Choose a recording with real reverberation — a dry studio sound strips away half the argument."],"taverner-christe-jesu-pastor-bone":["Three minutes for four voices, and by Taverner's standards almost curt. The music is a votive antiphon — a devotional piece sung before an image after the day's offices — with a clear, mostly chordal opening that breaks into gentle imitation, and a final petition for the king and the realm. Compared with his enormous six-voice masses the phrases are short and the words audible, which is exactly why the piece survived.","It began life as 'O Wilhelme, pastor bone', a prayer to St William of York that doubled as an advertisement for Cardinal Wolsey, whose Christian name it echoed and whose college employed Taverner. When Wolsey fell in 1529 and was dead a year later, the text became dangerous. Somebody — perhaps Taverner — replaced the saint's name with Christ's and the cardinal's petition with a prayer for Henry VIII, and the music sailed on.","Look at the words that survive from the original. The prayer to 'Wilhelmum' becomes an address to Jesus, but the setting's rhythm still fits the old syllables, and the closing plea for the king remains from the version that once named the cardinal alongside him. The piece is a small monument to how Tudor musicians survived regime change — not by writing new music, but by changing the names in the old.","Editions differ over which text to print, and choirs increasingly perform both versions to make the point. The rewriting was long taken as evidence of Taverner's Protestant conversion; scholars now read it as ordinary prudence, of a kind visible all over the period's music. The attribution is secure, but the date of the revision is not, and estimates range across the 1530s.","Three minutes of listening, then look up the original words and listen again — the piece changes character entirely. It also makes a good introduction to Taverner if his long masses feel forbidding. Pair it with Tallis's 'If Ye Love Me' to hear two English composers, a generation apart, writing plainly because the times required it."],"tallis-spem-alium":["Forty separate vocal parts, arranged as eight five-voice choirs, singing for about ten minutes. Tallis moves the sound physically: a single voice starts, the others enter one at a time so that the music travels across the choirs like a wave, then all forty converge on full chords before the wave sets off in the other direction. The text is a short prayer of dependence on God, taken from a responsory on the Book of Judith.","The most likely occasion is a contest of pride. In 1567 the Italian composer Alessandro Striggio visited England carrying a forty-part motet of his own, and an early account says an English duke asked whether any Englishman dared match it. Tallis, then in his sixties and a Gentleman of the Chapel Royal who had already served Henry VIII, Edward VI and Mary, evidently did. A performance in an octagonal room, singers ranged around the walls, is plausible.","The forty parts are not forty tunes. Tallis writes one flexible melodic shape and passes it through every voice, so the piece is closer to a single line seen forty times over than to forty-voice chaos. Listen for the moments when all forty sound at once — they are rare, they are the structure, and one of them lands on the words asking God to look upon our lowliness.","For centuries it was a curiosity known to antiquarians; the modern reputation dates from twentieth-century recordings and, more recently, from installations that give each voice its own loudspeaker so listeners can walk among them. Arguments continue about the occasion — a fortieth birthday for Elizabeth I or for Mary I are both proposed — and about layout, since the effect depends entirely on where the eight choirs stand.","Find a recording made in a large building, or better, an installation you can walk through. Listen first for direction: which side of the room the sound is coming from, and where it goes next. Then listen for the silences before the full-choir chords. If forty voices feel like too much at first, hear Tallis's 'If Ye Love Me' — four voices, two minutes — and come back."],"tallis-if-ye-love-me":["Two minutes, four voices, English words, and almost no ornament. Tallis sets Christ's promise from John's gospel in plain block chords for the first phrase, so every syllable lands together and is understood, then loosens into brief imitation at 'and he shall give you another comforter'. The second half is repeated exactly. Nothing is stretched, nothing is decorated, and the whole piece can be sung by four capable amateurs — which was the entire idea.","Under Edward VI the English church demanded music in English with, as Archbishop Cranmer put it, a syllable to a note. For a composer trained in the vast melismatic style of Taverner this was a demolition. Tallis complied, and did it well; the anthem survives in part-books copied around 1550 for parish use. He would live to see the Latin rite return under Mary and vanish again under Elizabeth, and adapted each time.","Listen to what happens under the clear surface. The lower voices are not merely accompanying — the tenor carries a shapely tune of its own, and at 'even the spirit of truth' the parts briefly disagree about the harmony, producing the sharp-against-flat clash English composers loved. Tallis smuggled his craft into a form designed to prevent it, and the exact repeat lets you catch it the second time.","The anthem became a fixture of Anglican worship and of school choirs, which flattened it into something merely pretty. Recent performances treat it as a Reformation document — deliberate plainness, made by a Catholic-leaning composer under Protestant instruction. Scholars now doubt the old picture of Tallis as a reluctant conformist; the evidence suggests a professional who wrote well in whatever idiom the government of the day required.","Sing along with the top line — you can, which is the point. Then play it again and follow the tenor instead. For contrast, put it next to 'Spem in alium' by the same man: the same craft applied at forty voices instead of four. Small chamber choirs, one or two per part, serve it better than large ones."],"tallis-o-nata-lux":["Under two minutes, five voices, and almost entirely in block chords — the parts move together, syllable by syllable, so that the Latin hymn for the Transfiguration is completely audible. Tallis repeats the second half. The piece works by harmony alone: a series of warm, slightly archaic chords broken by a deliberate clash where one voice sharpens a note another has just flattened, a sound English composers used like a catch in the throat.","It comes from Cantiones sacrae, the 1575 collection Tallis published jointly with his pupil William Byrd under a printing monopoly granted by Elizabeth I — thirty-four Latin motets, seventeen each, for the seventeenth year of her reign. The venture lost money badly. Latin motets had no liturgical home in the Elizabethan church, so the pieces were aimed at private devotion and at the cultivated household, Catholic or otherwise, that could read part-books.","The clash to listen for arrives at 'dignare clemens' — graciously deign — where the harmony sours for an instant before resolving. It is not a mistake and not an accident of notation: English music of the period cultivated these collisions, called false relations, and Tallis places this one at the hinge of the text, exactly where the hymn turns from praise to petition.","For a small piece it has had an outsized afterlife, becoming a favourite of chamber choirs and of composers: Morten Lauridsen built a setting of the same text in conscious homage. Some scholars hear traces of the plainchant hymn buried in the top voice. Performance debate is mostly about tempo — sung too slowly it becomes syrup, sung in time it keeps the hymn's tread.","Listen once for the words and once for the moment the harmony bends at 'dignare clemens'. Then compare it with Byrd's 'Ave verum corpus', published thirty years later by Tallis's pupil, to hear how the same restraint deepened. A small group of adult voices, one to a part, will show the clashes more clearly than a large cathedral choir."],"tallis-lamentations-jeremiah-i":["Around seven minutes for five low voices — no trebles, so the sound sits dark and close. Tallis sets the opening verses of Jeremiah's lament over the ruined city of Jerusalem, and includes the Hebrew letters that head each verse, ALEPH and BETH, spinning each one out in long wordless melisma before the text resumes. A formal announcement opens the piece and a refrain closes it: 'Jerusalem, turn to the Lord thy God.'","The Lamentations were sung in Holy Week at the night office of Tenebrae, candles extinguished one by one. By the time Tallis wrote — probably in the 1560s — that liturgy was illegal in England. The set survives only in manuscript part-books, never printed in his lifetime, and was almost certainly intended for private singing among people who still wanted the old rite, or simply for musicians who wanted the challenge.","Hear the piece politically and it changes. A ruined city, a people in exile, a temple emptied: for English Catholics after the Reformation the text read as autobiography, and contemporaries said as much about other Lamentation settings. Notice also that the Hebrew letters get the most elaborate music of all — the parts of the text that mean nothing in Latin or English, and therefore carry no doctrine anyone could prosecute.","The two sets — this and a companion — are usually sung together, though nothing proves Tallis conceived them as a pair. Nineteenth-century editors printed them at pitches that made them unsingable for the low ensembles they suit; modern groups transpose freely and argue about it. The music's reputation has risen steadily, and it now stands with 'Spem in alium' as the summit of his Latin work.","Start with the ALEPH: it is the piece's calling card, a slow unfolding on a single meaningless syllable. Then listen for the closing refrain, which returns in the second set and gives the two halves their frame. Look for a recording by a small consort of men at low pitch; the darkness of the scoring is not incidental but the whole atmosphere."],"tallis-salvator-mundi":["Two and a half minutes for five voices on a short prayer — 'Saviour of the world, save us' — built almost entirely from one rising melodic figure passed between the parts. The entries overlap so closely that the piece feels like a single line continually restarting before it has finished. Halfway through the texture opens and the voices climb together to the highest point in the piece, then subside quickly to a quiet close.","Another of Tallis's contributions to Cantiones sacrae, the 1575 Latin collection he and Byrd published on their royal printing patent. Two settings of this antiphon appear in the book; this is the first and more often sung. Elizabethan England had no liturgical use for such music, but there was a market of amateurs and collectors, and the Latin motet had become a demonstration piece — a way for English composers to show they could match the continent.","The rising figure is not decoration; it is the entire piece. Tallis inverts it, stretches it and stacks it, so that when the voices finally move together the ear recognises a shape it has been hearing all along without knowing it. Listen also for the way two of the inner parts shadow each other a beat apart for long stretches, a canon hidden inside a texture that never announces one.","Nineteenth-century English cathedrals adopted it with an added organ part and English words, and it survived that treatment. Modern consorts have restored the Latin and the a cappella scoring, and the piece has become a standard first encounter with Tallis for chamber choirs. Its date of composition is unknown — 1575 is only the year of printing, and the style suggests it may be considerably older.","Follow the opening figure through the first minute: once you can hear it entering, the piece organises itself. Notice the single loud climax and how briefly it lasts. Pair it with 'O nata lux' from the same book, which works by harmony where this works by line, to hear the two halves of Tallis's technique side by side."],"tallis-third-mode-melody":["A hymn tune, barely a minute and a half, harmonised in four parts with the melody in the tenor rather than on top — an old-fashioned arrangement even in 1567. It moves in even notes, phrase by phrase, with rests between, and it never settles comfortably: the mode it uses ends its phrases in places that sound unresolved to ears trained on major and minor. The words, 'Why fum'th in fight the Gentiles spite', paraphrase Psalm 2.","Archbishop Matthew Parker printed a private psalter around 1567 with nine tunes by Tallis at the back, one for each of the eight church modes plus a ninth — a scholarly gesture at a moment when English congregations were being handed simple metrical psalms. Very few copies were distributed. This is the third tune, and it would have vanished into hymnological footnotes had a young English composer not gone looking through Tudor sources three centuries later.","The melody's shape is the reason for its afterlife: it rises, falls back, and hangs on a note that refuses to become a home key. Ralph Vaughan Williams found it while editing The English Hymnal and in 1910 built his Fantasia on it — splitting his string players into groups placed apart in Gloucester Cathedral, which is very nearly what Tallis had done with the eight choirs of 'Spem in alium'.","As a hymn tune it entered general use under the name 'Third Mode Melody', usually stripped of its original psalm text. The Fantasia so overshadowed it that many listeners assume Vaughan Williams wrote the tune. Scholars have questioned how much of the four-part harmonisation is Tallis's own work and how much reflects printing-house convention, but the melody itself is not in doubt.","Hear the tune bare first — a minute, sung plainly — then play the Vaughan Williams Fantasia and listen for the moment the strings state it whole. Going in that order makes the later piece far more moving. For the original, seek a recording that keeps the melody in the tenor, where Tallis put it, rather than moving it to the top."],"palestrina-missa-papae-marcelli-kyrie":["Six voices — soprano, alto, two tenors, two basses — and about four minutes. The Kyrie opens with a rising figure in one voice, answered by the others in turn, and from there the parts never stop overlapping; each phrase enters before the last has finished, so the music has no seams. Nothing is loud, nothing is fast, and no voice leaps awkwardly. The middle section, 'Christe eleison', thins the texture before the full six return.","Pope Marcellus II reigned for twenty-two days in 1555 and, on Good Friday, told his singers to sing so that the words could be understood. The mass named for him was published in 1567, in a book dedicated to Philip II of Spain, at a moment when reformers were reviewing church music in the wake of the Council of Trent. Palestrina was then in his forties, working his way between the great Roman basilicas.","The Kyrie has only three words, so the famous claim that this mass saved polyphony by making text audible cannot be tested here — that argument belongs to the Gloria and Credo. What the Kyrie shows instead is the technical basis: every leap is followed by a step in the opposite direction, dissonances are approached and left smoothly, and no two voices ever pull apart. It is the sound of rules obeyed beautifully.","The story that this mass persuaded the Council of Trent not to ban polyphony was assembled long after the fact, given operatic form by Hans Pfitzner in 1917, and is not supported by the documents. Historians now see the Council's concerns as narrower and Palestrina's role as smaller. The music's status is untouched: from the eighteenth century onward it became the model from which counterpoint was taught, and still is.","Listen for what does not happen — no sharp accents, no sudden loud chords, no jagged leaps. Then play a Gesualdo madrigal from thirty years later and hear what Palestrina was refusing to do. A Roman-style choir with boys on top gives one sound; an English cathedral choir another; a one-voice-per-part consort makes the counterpoint clearest of all."],"palestrina-sicut-cervus":["Four voices, three minutes, and one of the most singable pieces in the repertory. The soprano opens alone with a long arching phrase on 'Sicut cervus' — as the deer longs — and the other three take up the same shape in turn, each entry lifting the music a little further. The lines are almost entirely stepwise, the rhythm gentle and irregular, and the piece ends without any grand cadence, simply coming to rest.","Psalm 42 was sung during the procession to the font at the Easter Vigil, where catechumens were baptised — water answering the psalm's thirsty deer. Palestrina published the setting in 1584, in a book of four-voice motets, deep into a career spent almost entirely inside the Roman church establishment: choirmaster at St Peter's, briefly a papal singer, and by then a wealthy man through his second wife's fur business.","The opening phrase never really stops. Palestrina overlaps every entry so that as one voice finishes the shape another has already begun it, and the same contour recurs, varied, right through the piece — the music has one idea and refuses to introduce a second. Notice that the highest note of the whole motet arrives late, on 'ad te Deus', and that he prepares it by keeping the sopranos low beforehand.","It is now the standard first piece of Renaissance polyphony for choirs everywhere, which has made it both universally loved and routinely over-sung, with swelling dynamics and a slow tempo Palestrina's notation does not ask for. Scholars point out that the second half, 'Sitivit anima mea', belongs to the same motet and is frequently dropped in performance, leaving the piece unfinished.","Follow one inner voice — the alto — from beginning to end instead of the melody; the piece is built to reward that. Sing the opening phrase yourself and you will find it fits the breath exactly. Seek out a performance that includes the second half, and try one at a brisk, speech-like tempo before settling for the hushed version most choirs prefer."],"palestrina-missa-papae-marcelli-gloria":["About five minutes, and the movement where this mass earns its legend. Palestrina abandons dense imitation for something closer to declamation: the six voices divide into small changing groups — three high, three low, four together — and hand the text back and forth in even chords, so that a long, wordy prayer is delivered clearly and quickly. Only at 'Cum Sancto Spiritu' does he open the full six-part counterpoint and let the movement widen.","Reformers complained that in polyphonic masses the words disappeared, and that secular tunes were smuggled into the liturgy. Palestrina answers both charges here: the Gloria borrows no pre-existing melody at all, and it is set so that a listener can follow the text phrase by phrase. Published in 1567 under a dedication to Philip II, the book was a public argument as much as a collection of music.","Count the voices at any moment and you will rarely find six. Palestrina keeps rotating subsets — the effect is of continuously fresh colour without any increase in volume — and reserves the full ensemble for the words that matter most. What later theorists described as a special declamatory manner is really a composer engineering audibility without giving up polyphony at all.","Whether the mass actually influenced the Council of Trent is now generally doubted; the Council had finished its work in 1563, and its surviving decrees say little specifically about music. What is not doubted is the afterlife: taught, imitated, and made the yardstick of Catholic church music for four centuries. Modern debate concerns forces — the papal chapel used a dozen or so male singers, not the large mixed choirs common today.","Listen for the moment the voices stop trading and finally sing together at 'Cum Sancto Spiritu' — it should feel like a door opening. Follow the words in translation the first time; the whole design is aimed at letting you. Then hear the Kyrie again and notice how completely different the two movements' methods are within one work."],"palestrina-super-flumina-babylonis":["Four voices, about four minutes, on the opening of Psalm 137 — the exiles by the rivers of Babylon, weeping at the memory of Zion. Palestrina sets it low and slowly, with the sopranos held down for long stretches so the texture stays shadowed. The word 'flevimus' — we wept — gets a falling figure that every voice takes up in turn, and the harps hung on the willows are painted by a sudden thinning of the sound.","Palestrina published motets steadily through the 1560s, 70s and 80s while running the music at St Peter's, and the four-voice books were aimed at a wide market: small chapels, confraternities, and the growing number of households that sang polyphony for pleasure. The Babylon psalm had obvious appeal in Counter-Reformation Rome, a city rebuilding itself as the new Jerusalem and preaching constantly about exile, captivity and return.","The piece is unusually restrained in range. Palestrina keeps almost the whole motet within a narrow band, so that the two or three moments where a voice finally reaches upward register as real events rather than ornament. Listen for the setting of 'Sion' — the name of the lost city gets the highest and longest note any voice sings, and then the music sinks back at once.","It is one of many Palestrina motets that live in the shadow of the masses, sung mainly by choirs looking for Lenten repertoire, and its exact place among his printed collections is not always agreed between cataloguers. Some scholars argue that its restraint reflects a deliberately older manner, closer to the mid-century Roman style Palestrina inherited than to the brighter writing of his late motet books.","Play it directly after Palestrina's 'Sicut cervus': same forces, same composer, opposite mood — longing that hopes against longing that has given up. Listen for the one high note on 'Sion'. A small choir singing quietly, without swelling, will let you hear the low tessitura for what it is rather than as gloom applied from outside."],"palestrina-tu-es-petrus":["Six voices and about four minutes of unclouded brightness. Christ's words to Peter — you are the rock, and on this rock I will build my church — are set high and forward, with the upper voices repeatedly launching a rising figure that the lower ones catch and push further. The music keeps arriving at bright, wide-spaced chords on the key words, and it ends with a long, ringing repetition of the promise to build.","The text is carved in letters two metres high around the inside of the dome of St Peter's, and it is the papacy's founding claim. Palestrina published this setting in 1572, in a book of motets for five, six and eight voices, while working at the heart of the Roman church. He later built a whole mass on it, recycling the motet's material — a normal practice, and a sign of how well the piece had been received.","Notice how often the music simply repeats a phrase at a higher pitch rather than developing it. That rising sequence is the engine, and Palestrina uses it to make six voices sound like far more. Listen also for the passages where the ensemble splits into two three-voice groups answering each other — the Venetian double-choir effect, imported into Roman writing decades before Gabrieli made it a speciality.","It became the papacy's ceremonial motet, sung at coronations and consecrations, which for a time made it hard to hear as music rather than as institution. Nineteenth-century Cecilian reformers held it up as the ideal; twentieth-century scholars, reacting, found it Palestrina at his most public and least searching. Recent performances at brisker tempos, with fewer singers, have restored some of its snap.","This is Palestrina at his most extroverted, so start here if the serene motets have left you cold. Listen for the answering three-voice groups, and for the final section's insistent repetitions. Then hear Gabrieli's 'In ecclesiis' to find out where that answering technique went once Venice got hold of it."],"palestrina-stabat-mater":["Eight voices in two four-part choirs, about nine minutes, on the medieval poem of Mary standing at the cross. Palestrina writes it almost entirely in chords rather than counterpoint: the two choirs alternate short phrases, sometimes overlapping, and join for the weightiest lines. The effect is a slow, controlled procession of harmony with very little melodic activity — a deliberate simplification from a composer who could have filled every bar with imitation.","Written probably in Palestrina's last years, for the papal chapel, where it belonged to the jealously guarded repertory sung during Holy Week and not made generally available. The Sistine singers kept such pieces in manuscript and performed them from memory, which is why the Vatican's Holy Week music acquired a legendary aura among visitors — the same aura that later surrounded Allegri's Miserere.","Watch how Palestrina uses the two choirs. Rather than throwing sound across space for its own sake, he uses the split to control weight: a single choir for the narrative lines, both together for the moments of grief. And listen to the very end — after eight minutes of chords the final petition finally breaks into flowing counterpoint, as if the piece had been holding its breath the whole time.","Richard Wagner, of all people, edited and conducted it in Dresden in the 1840s and left an admiring preface; his edition added dynamics and phrasing Palestrina never wrote, and shaped performance for a century. Modern scholars have stripped those back. The attribution is secure, but the date rests on style alone, and the piece's place among Palestrina's late works is still argued over.","Listen for the alternation first — which choir is singing, and when they combine. Then listen a second time only to the last minute, where the texture finally moves. Compare it with Victoria's Holy Week music, written in the same decades with far more heat. A recording that separates the two choirs in the stereo image helps enormously."],"lassus-lagrime-di-san-pietro":["Twenty-one madrigals for seven unaccompanied voices, followed by a Latin motet, running about seventy minutes. The subject is Peter after he has denied Christ three times: not the denial itself but the days of shame afterwards, circling the same wound. Lassus keeps the seven voices in constant, dense motion, rarely letting all of them rest, and the harmony darkens and brightens by small degrees. Nothing is dramatic; everything is interior.","Lassus finished the cycle on 24 May 1594 and dedicated it to Pope Clement VIII. He was sixty-two, had spent nearly forty years running the Bavarian court chapel in Munich, and was suffering from what contemporaries described as a melancholy so severe he could barely work. He died on 14 June, three weeks after signing the dedication, and the collection was published later that year. The poems are by Luigi Tansillo.","Seven is not an accidental number of voices: seven madrigals in each of three groups, seven parts, the arithmetic of penitence. Listen for how each madrigal ends — many close unresolved or in an unexpected place, so that the next begins mid-thought. The cycle never provides release. Even the closing Latin motet, which turns from Peter to address the listener directly, offers reproach rather than absolution.","Neglected for centuries as a difficult late work by a composer remembered for his chansons, it has become, since the 1990s, Lassus's most performed large piece. Peter Sellars staged it in 2016 with the Los Angeles Master Chorale singing from memory and moving, which divided critics and filled halls. Scholars still debate whether the grief is Peter's, Lassus's own, or a formal exercise in the fashionable spiritual madrigal.","Do not attempt all seventy minutes first. Take three or four consecutive madrigals and notice how the harmony refuses to settle between them. Then hear the closing motet, which changes language and address and lands like a verdict. Small professional consorts with one or two voices per part make the seven-part writing audible; large choirs turn it into fog."],"lassus-matona-mia-cara":["Two and a half minutes of comedy for four voices. A German mercenary serenades an Italian woman in broken Italian, mangling his grammar and mixing up his words, and the refrain is nonsense syllables — 'don don don, diri diri, don don don don'. The music is deliberately simple: block chords, a dance rhythm, repeats. Lassus makes the joke funnier by writing it perfectly, so that the elegance of the setting sits at odds with the fumbling text.","Lassus spent his career at the Munich court of Duke Albrecht V, writing for the chapel by day and for the duke's table by night, and this belongs to the table. It was published in 1581 among villanelle and moresche — light Italian genres in which a foreigner's accent was a standard gag. A Franco-Flemish composer working in Germany, writing an Italian song that mocks a German's Italian, is the joke doubled.","The soldier's Italian is not random. He addresses her as 'matona', a botched attempt at 'madonna', promises hunting trophies instead of poetry, and by the last verse has abandoned courtship for something considerably more direct — which is why concert programmes usually print a discreet translation. Listen for the 'don don' refrain returning identical each time: the man has exactly one idea, musical and otherwise.","It is by some distance Lassus's most performed piece, a fixture of amateur choirs, school programmes and early-music encores, and its popularity has slightly distorted his reputation — a composer of over two thousand works remembered for a novelty. Editors have quietly cleaned up the text for centuries. The moresca genre's use of ethnic caricature is now discussed openly in programme notes rather than passed over.","Read a translation before you listen; without it the piece is merely charming, and with it, it is funny. Then play the opening of 'Lagrime di San Pietro', finished by the same man in the last weeks of his life, and take in the distance between them. Choose a recording by singers willing to act rather than blend."],"lassus-timor-et-tremor":["Six voices, about five minutes, and one of the most anguished pieces of the sixteenth century. 'Fear and trembling came upon me, and darkness fell.' Lassus sets it with harmony that keeps sliding sideways into unexpected places, voices crowding into dissonance and holding it, and long stretches where nothing resolves. The second half turns to petition — hear my prayer — and the music grows more urgent rather than calmer, ending on a plea.","Lassus was the most published composer alive, writing for a Munich court that wanted everything: masses, chansons, drinking songs, penitential psalms. The 1560s were his most productive years there, and the sacred motets from that decade show him importing the harmonic daring of Italian madrigals into church music. The text stitches verses from the psalms into a single cry, of a kind Counter-Reformation devotion prized for its emotional directness.","Listen to what happens at the word 'tenebrae' — darkness. The voices drop, the harmony flattens, and the motion nearly stops, so that the word is illustrated rather than merely set. Lassus does this repeatedly: fear, trembling and confusion each get their own gesture. Madrigal technique, in other words, applied to a sacred Latin text years before that became routine anywhere in Europe.","The piece has become the standard demonstration that Lassus, not only Gesualdo, could make harmony ache. It is technically demanding — the six parts sit close together and small errors ruin the dissonances — so recordings vary widely in quality. Its exact place in Lassus's enormous output is uncertain, since motets were reprinted and reshuffled across decades and dating individual pieces within his career remains difficult.","Listen for 'tenebrae' and mark where it falls; the whole first half aims at it. Then hear Gesualdo's 'Tristis est anima mea' and ask which composer is more extreme, and which more disturbing. Small ensembles, one voice per part, keep the dissonances sharp; large choirs smooth them into general sadness."],"lassus-prophetiae-sibyllarum-prologue":["Ninety seconds for four voices, and a warning label in music. The text announces what follows: these are the chromatic songs you hear, in which the mysteries of the sibyls are sung. Lassus obliges by making the harmony lurch — chords arrive from unrelated places, sharps and flats collide, and in a few dozen bars the music passes through more harmonic territory than most Renaissance pieces visit in ten minutes. Then the cycle proper begins, and behaves far more normally.","The twelve sibyls were pagan prophetesses whom Renaissance Christians believed had foretold Christ — Michelangelo painted five of them on the Sistine ceiling. Lassus set their prophecies for four voices probably in the 1550s, when he was in his twenties, and the cycle was copied into a lavishly illuminated manuscript for Duke Albrecht V of Bavaria. It was not printed until 1600, six years after the composer's death.","The prologue is a demonstration piece, and the demonstration is theoretical: it works its way through chords built on almost every available note, which sixteenth-century writers called the chromatic genus and treated as an ancient Greek inheritance being recovered. The trick is that it still cadences correctly at every turn. Lassus is not breaking the rules so much as showing how far they stretch before they snap.","Nineteenth-century scholars, hunting for early tonality, seized on the prologue as a freak; twentieth-century ones connected it to the humanist experiments of Nicola Vicentino and Cipriano de Rore. Dating remains contested — some place the cycle around 1550, others later in the decade after Lassus reached Munich. Whether the young composer meant it as prophecy, provocation or a job application is anyone's guess.","Play the prologue twice; ninety seconds is not enough to take in what has happened. Then let the first two or three prophecies run and notice how quickly the music settles into normal behaviour — the shock belonged to the opening only. Compare it with Gesualdo forty years later, who made that shock his entire language."],"lassus-osculetur-me":["Eight voices in two choirs, about four minutes, on the opening of the Song of Songs: let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth, for thy love is better than wine. Lassus alternates the two groups in short, overlapping phrases, so the text passes back and forth like conversation, and brings all eight together at the richest words and again at the end. The writing is warm, fast-moving and unmistakably physical.","The Song of Songs was the most erotic text in the Bible and the most thoroughly explained away: preachers read it as Christ's love for the church, and composers set it constantly. Lassus published this among motets for many voices in the 1580s, when double-choir writing had spread north from Venice and Munich's chapel — one of Europe's largest, with singers, cornetts and trombones on the payroll — could easily field two separate groups.","The two choirs are not identical. Lassus scores them a little differently and sets them apart, so the antiphony sounds like two bodies rather than one group split down the middle. Listen at 'meliora sunt ubera tua vino' — better than wine — where the exchange speeds up until the groups overlap and the words tumble over each other. Whatever the allegorical reading, the music is not being allegorical.","Motets like this were once dismissed as Lassus at his most routine — he wrote hundreds — and only the recovery of the whole Munich repertory has shown how carefully he varied the type. Because the sources rarely specify, performances differ over whether instruments should double the voices, as court inventories suggest they often did. Modern recordings increasingly include cornetts and sackbuts.","Put the two choirs on either side of you if your recording allows, and listen purely for the geography of the exchange. It is short enough to hear three times in ten minutes. Then move to Gabrieli in Venice, where the same technique is used with brass and a much bigger building, and hear how the idea grew."],"byrd-mass-four-voices-agnus-dei":["The last movement of a mass lasting about half an hour, and roughly four minutes on its own. Byrd starts thin — two voices alone — adds a third for the second petition, and only at the final 'dona nobis pacem' brings in all four. The plea for peace is then repeated over slow suspensions, one voice hanging above the harmony and resolving downward again and again, before the music comes to rest very quietly indeed.","Byrd was a practising Catholic in a state that fined, imprisoned and executed them. In the early 1590s his printer issued three masses without title pages, publisher's name or date — books small enough to hide, for the illegal household services held at recusant houses like Ingatestone Hall in Essex, where Byrd's patron John Petre lived and where he probably heard this music sung by four singers in a private room.","Four voices meant four people, which meant a service that could be broken up and hidden in minutes. But listen to the 'dona nobis pacem': the word for peace carries the most painful dissonances in the whole mass, held and released and held again. For an English Catholic in 1593 peace was not a pious formula but a political request that could not be made out loud, and Byrd makes it over and over.","The masses were effectively invisible for two centuries and were not published openly until the nineteenth. Modern scholarship, particularly since the 1980s, has reconstructed the recusant world they served and made the political reading standard. Performance practice remains argued: cathedral choirs sing them beautifully but at a scale the music was designed to avoid, and one-voice-per-part recordings now compete strongly.","Listen to the whole Agnus Dei rather than an excerpt; the effect depends on the voices accumulating. Wait for the entry of the fourth voice and then for the suspensions on 'pacem'. Then hear Byrd's 'Ave verum corpus', from the same secret repertoire, which does something similar in four minutes flat. Try a small consort recording before a cathedral one."],"byrd-ave-verum-corpus":["Four minutes for four voices, and among the most quietly devastating short pieces in the repertory. It opens with a false relation in the very first bar — one voice sharpens a note another has just flattened — so the music is out of joint before the first word is over. The setting is mostly chordal, moving in slow deliberate steps, and the last section repeats 'miserere mei' over and over, the voices overlapping, until it becomes an insistence.","Printed in Gradualia, the two enormous books of 1605 and 1607 in which Byrd, now in his sixties and living quietly in Essex, set the propers for the whole Catholic church year. The timing was disastrous: the first book appeared in 1605, the year of the Gunpowder Plot, and copies were seized from people arrested in its aftermath. Byrd had spent decades protected by Elizabeth I's personal favour, and was publishing outlawed liturgy anyway.","The false relations are the argument. English composers used the clash of a sharp against a flat as an expressive device, and Byrd places them on the words about Christ's true body and his real death — the doctrine that divided England, whether the bread is truly the body. He sets the disputed word with the disputed harmony. Nothing about the placement is decorative.","For a long time the piece was sung in Anglican cathedrals as a beautiful Lenten anthem with no particular history attached. Byrd scholarship since the mid-twentieth century, and especially Joseph Kerman's work, restored the recusant context and changed how it is heard. Mozart's setting of the same text, written nearly two centuries later, is now often programmed beside it; the two could hardly be less alike.","Listen to the first ten seconds twice — the clash is right at the start, and once you have heard it you will hear it everywhere in Byrd. Then wait for the 'miserere mei' section and count how many times it comes back. Pair it with Mozart's 'Ave verum corpus' for the contrast, and choose a recording with one or two voices per part."],"byrd-bells":["Seven or eight minutes for virginals — a small plucked keyboard with a bright, quick decay — built over a ground of just two alternating notes, repeated without variation from beginning to end. Above that Byrd stacks section after section of increasing activity: slow chords first, then running figures, then hammering repeated notes and cross-rhythms, until the instrument sounds like a whole tower of bells swinging at once. Then it stops, abruptly.","Change-ringing — teams of ringers pulling tuned bells in shifting mathematical orders — was becoming an English obsession in Byrd's lifetime, and it is a national peculiarity: no other country developed it. 'The Bells' appears in My Ladye Nevells Booke, a beautifully copied manuscript of forty-two Byrd keyboard pieces completed in 1591 for a patroness whose identity is still argued about. The English virginal school effectively begins with this book.","The two-note ground never changes speed or pitch, so everything you hear as acceleration is happening above a completely static floor — the piece creates excitement out of nothing but density. Listen for the moment Byrd introduces notes that clash with the ground and simply leaves them there, as real bells do when their overtones collide. The ending stops mid-swing rather than cadencing, which is exactly how a peal ends.","Byrd's keyboard music was the most advanced in Europe in the 1590s and was known abroad; Sweelinck in Amsterdam knew English pieces of this kind. For centuries it was played on the modern piano, which blurs the repeated notes into mush. The harpsichord and virginals revival transformed its reputation, and the piece is now a standard demonstration of what the instrument does that a piano cannot.","Hear it on virginals or harpsichord, never piano — the whole effect depends on notes decaying fast. Listen only to the bottom two notes for the first minute so you register how unchanging they are, then let your ear go up. Follow it with Byrd's 'John come kiss me now' to hear the same variation instinct applied to a tune instead of a texture."],"byrd-vigilate":["Five voices, about four minutes, on Christ's parable of the householder who does not know when the master will come. Byrd sets it as a series of pictures: 'vigilate' — keep watch — is thrown between the voices in short urgent bursts; the four watches of the night, evening, midnight, cockcrow and dawn, each get their own music; and at 'gallicantu' the voices break into a fast repeated figure that is unmistakably several cocks crowing, out of step.","Published in Cantiones sacrae of 1589, one of two Latin motet books Byrd issued in his fifties under the printing monopoly he had inherited from Tallis. He was by then the most eminent musician in England, a Gentleman of the Chapel Royal, and repeatedly named in official lists as a recusant Catholic who did not attend church. Both facts were true at once, and Elizabeth I allowed it.","Read the text as a coded message and the piece sharpens. English Catholic writing of the 1580s used exactly this language — watch, be ready, you do not know the hour — about persecution, arrests at night, and priests hidden in country houses. Byrd's motet books are full of such texts: captivity, exile, waiting for deliverance. The cockcrow is a joke, and the joke is nervous.","Nineteenth-century editors treated Byrd's Latin motets as absolute music with unfortunate words. Twentieth-century scholarship, above all Joseph Kerman's, argued that the texts were chosen as political statements, and that reading is now standard, though a minority still hold that Byrd simply set what was available and moving. Either way, the motet's word-painting is among the most vivid in English music.","Listen for the cockcrow — you cannot miss it, and it is genuinely funny. Then go back and follow the four times of night as they pass. It works best with a small, agile group; large choirs cannot articulate the fast repeated notes. Follow it with 'Sing joyfully' to hear Byrd doing the same pictorial trick in English and in public."],"byrd-sing-joyfully":["A full anthem for six voices, about three and a half minutes, in English, on verses from Psalm 81. Byrd builds it entirely from overlapping imitation — a phrase given to one voice, then piled up in the others — so the piece keeps gathering weight rather than alternating loud and soft. The best-known moment comes at 'blow the trumpet in the new moon', where the voices imitate a fanfare, three notes rising, tossed from part to part.","The Elizabethan church wanted anthems in English, and Byrd, whose private music was Latin and Catholic, wrote some of the finest. This one circulated in manuscript rather than print and became one of the most copied English anthems of the period. A contemporary account records it sung at a royal christening in 1605, which if correct puts it in the Chapel Royal's most ceremonial repertoire while its composer was publishing illegal Catholic liturgy.","Listen for how Byrd handles a six-voice texture without ever letting it clog: he almost never uses all six at once, cycling instead through five-voice combinations so the sound keeps changing colour. And note the trumpet figure — it is not just word-painting but the seed of the last third of the anthem, returning in longer notes underneath the other voices at the close.","Sung continuously in English cathedrals since the seventeenth century, it is one of the few Tudor pieces that never needed rediscovery. That continuity brought accretions: organ accompaniments, Victorian dynamics, large forces. Scholars disagree about the date, with estimates ranging across the 1580s and 90s, and about whether the christening account refers to this setting or another.","Wait for the trumpets; then listen again from the start and notice how the piece has been preparing them. This is Byrd at his most public and confident, a useful counterweight if you have only met him through the secret Catholic music. A cathedral choir with boys suits it — for once, the big sound is what the piece was built for."],"byrd-john-come-kiss-me-now":["Sixteen variations on a popular tune, four or five minutes on the virginals. The melody is short — eight bars, over a harmonic pattern that simply repeats — and Byrd runs it round and round while the decoration above and below grows steadily busier: first plain, then running scales, then broken chords, then cross-rhythms that fight the beat, and finally a return to something close to the tune as it started. It ends without ceremony.","'John come kiss me now' was a bawdy ballad everyone in London knew, and Byrd's setting is one of the pieces that made keyboard variation a serious English form. It survives in the Fitzwilliam Virginal Book, a huge manuscript anthology copied in the early seventeenth century — possibly by a Catholic musician in prison — that preserves nearly three hundred pieces of English keyboard music which would otherwise be lost.","The tune is only eight bars long, so Byrd has to keep the ear interested by changing what surrounds it. Listen for the variations where the melody moves from the right hand into the left and hides underneath the figuration — still there, in the same rhythm, but you have to look for it. Notice too that he saves the hardest cross-rhythms for late, then deliberately lets the piece relax before ending.","English virginal variations were long treated as charming antiques, and Byrd's were played on pianos in Victorian editions. The period-instrument movement changed that completely: the writing turns out to be shaped precisely around what a plucked keyboard can do. Scholars now regard Byrd's variation sets as direct ancestors of the Baroque keyboard variation, reaching Sweelinck in Amsterdam and, through his pupils, Germany.","Count the variations as they go by — the eight-bar tune makes it easy — and notice where each one raises the stakes. Then listen to Byrd's 'The Bells' for the same technique applied to a texture instead of a melody. Any recording on virginals or harpsichord; if you have only heard this on piano, the difference will surprise you."],"victoria-o-magnum-mysterium":["Four voices, about four minutes, on the Christmas responsory that marvels that animals should see the newborn Lord lying in their manger. Victoria opens with a slow rising phrase in the soprano, imitated downward through the other parts, and keeps the whole first section hushed and unhurried. Then the metre changes and the piece breaks into a fast triple-time Alleluia — the single moment of release, and one of the most familiar in Renaissance music.","Victoria published it in Venice in 1572, in his first book of motets. He was in his early twenties, a Spaniard studying and working in Rome, where he had been sent to the German College and where Palestrina was the dominant figure — possibly his teacher, certainly his model. Rome in the 1570s was full of ambitious young clerics; Victoria was one who also happened to be the finest composer among them.","The famous Alleluia is not an afterthought. Its melodic shape is the opening phrase of the motet turned into a dance — the same rising figure, in fast triple time. Victoria has been preparing it for three minutes. Listen also to the setting of 'jacentem in praesepio', lying in a manger: the voices sink, and stay low, and the word for the manger is set lower than anything before it.","It is now one of the most performed pieces of the entire Renaissance, sung by choirs of every size every December, and Victoria later built a mass on it. That ubiquity has produced very different performance styles: Spanish and Latin American groups often take it faster and more rhythmically than northern European choirs, and some scholars argue for accompanying instruments, which Spanish chapels routinely used.","Listen for the moment the metre changes into the Alleluia — everything before it is a slow approach. Then hear Gabrieli's setting of the same text, written in Venice for eight voices and two choirs, and hear two utterly different temperaments on identical words. A small, warm-toned choir suits Victoria better than a very large one."],"victoria-requiem-1605-introitus":["Six voices, about six minutes, opening the Office of the Dead that Victoria published in 1605. The plainchant is intoned first by a solo voice; then the polyphony enters, with the chant itself running through the second-highest part in long, plain notes while five other voices move around it. Nothing is dramatic: no trumpets, no Day of Wrath, no display. The texture is thick, low and slow, and enormous weight moves very quietly.","Victoria had left Rome in 1587 to become chaplain to the Dowager Empress María — daughter of Charles V, sister of Philip II, widow of an emperor — at the Descalzas Reales, a convent of Poor Clares in Madrid where she had retired with her daughter. He served her for sixteen years. She died in February 1603, and this is the music for her obsequies, published two years later with a dedication to her daughter.","Listen to where the chant sits. Victoria puts it not in the top voice but just below, so it is half-buried in the texture and the ear keeps finding it and losing it again. Notice also how rarely all six voices sing at once: he keeps opening gaps in the sound. The book prints chant and polyphony in alternation, so a performance without the plainsong is only half the work.","The Officium Defunctorum was Victoria's last publication and is generally treated as the summit of Spanish Renaissance music and the end of an era — unaccompanied polyphony completed just as the Baroque was beginning elsewhere in Europe. Recordings differ sharply over pitch, over whether to include the plainchant sections, and over whether to add the bajón, the Spanish dulcian that Iberian chapels used to reinforce the bass.","Hear the whole Office if you can, not the Introit alone — an hour that never raises its voice. Start by listening only for the chant in the second voice from the top. Then compare it with Mozart's or Verdi's Requiem to feel how differently the same liturgy can be imagined. A Spanish or British consort at low pitch, plainsong included, is the way in."],"victoria-o-vos-omnes":["Four voices, three minutes, and one long descent. The text is Christ speaking from the cross, or the church speaking for him: all you who pass by, look and see if there is any sorrow like my sorrow. Victoria sets the opening as a bare call, drops the voices at 'attendite', and reserves his sharpest dissonance for 'dolor' — sorrow — which recurs and is intensified each time. The piece ends lower and quieter than it began.","A responsory for Holy Week, when the church contemplates Christ in the tomb. Victoria published a setting in his 1572 Roman motet book and returned to the text in the Officium Hebdomadae Sanctae of 1585, his complete Holy Week collection. Spanish devotion of the period — the Spain of Teresa of Ávila and John of the Cross, both his contemporaries — cultivated exactly this kind of concentrated meditation on the wounds of Christ.","The word 'dolor' is set more than once, each time with a suspension — a voice held over while the harmony changes beneath it, so the note becomes briefly wrong before it resolves — and Victoria makes each one last longer than the last. Listen also for what is missing: there is almost no imitation and no display of craft. He strips the technique away so nothing stands between the words and the ear.","It is Victoria's most performed motet after 'O magnum mysterium' and the piece most often cited to distinguish him from Palestrina — hotter, more Spanish, more willing to wound. Confusion persists between his two settings of the text, which are frequently mislabelled on recordings. The Renaissance's other famous version, by Gesualdo, arrives a generation later and pushes the same harmonic idea to the edge of coherence.","Listen only for the word 'dolor' and how it is treated each time it returns; that is the whole piece. Then hear Gesualdo's setting of the same words and decide which grief you believe. Choose a small ensemble singing quietly, at a slow but moving tempo — the suspensions need time, but the piece dies if it stops."],"victoria-tenebrae-factae-sunt":["Four voices, four minutes, on the Good Friday responsory describing the sky darkening at Christ's death. Victoria sets the narration low and flat, almost without motion, then lifts the voices suddenly and painfully for Christ's cry — my God, my God, why have you forsaken me — before sinking back. The final section, bowing his head he gave up the spirit, descends step by step in all four parts until the sound simply stops.","From the Officium Hebdomadae Sanctae, published in Rome in 1585: a complete musical provision for Holy Week — eighteen responsories, two Passions, lamentations and more — which no other single composer of the age attempted on that scale. Victoria was then a priest at San Girolamo della Carità, the church of Filippo Neri's circle, and about to leave Italy for good. He would spend the rest of his life in Madrid.","The cry is placed exactly where it will hurt most. Victoria keeps the top voice deliberately low for the preceding minute, so that when the soprano finally rises on 'Deus meus' it sounds like effort rather than beauty. And listen to the last phrase: the four parts descend in parallel, which sixteenth-century teaching discouraged, producing a deliberate slackness — the music going limp as a body does.","The Holy Week book was sung in Spanish and Latin American cathedrals more or less continuously for centuries, which is unusual for Renaissance polyphony, so it passed into the modern repertory without a real gap. Debate centres on pace and on the alternation with plainchant, which most recordings observe; some also add organ or bajón, as Spanish practice allowed. It is routinely paired with Gesualdo's Tenebrae settings.","Hear it in the dark, ideally in a Tenebrae service where candles are extinguished one by one — the music was written for exactly that. Listen for the moment the soprano rises on 'Deus meus'. Then compare Victoria's restraint with Gesualdo's harmonic violence on similar texts twenty-five years later."],"victoria-ave-maria-8":["Eight voices in two four-part choirs, about three minutes. The two groups trade the angel's greeting phrase by phrase — one choir announces, the other answers — and Victoria joins them for the weightiest petitions, 'Sancta Maria' and the final plea at the hour of our death. The writing is chordal and bright, the exchanges quick, and the piece ends with all eight voices in a slow, wide-spaced Amen.","Published in Venice in 1572 among Victoria's first motets, when double-choir writing was spreading from St Mark's across Catholic Europe. Roman churches had long placed singers in separate galleries for feast days, and the Spanish and German colleges where Victoria worked could field the numbers. The Ave Maria as a fixed prayer, ending with the petition at the hour of death, had only recently settled into the form we know.","The two choirs are not equal partners. Listen to which one gets the angel's words and which the church's added petition — Victoria splits the text so that the antiphony dramatises a dialogue rather than merely bouncing sound around a room. The exchanges also shorten as the piece goes on, until the two choirs are overlapping instead of alternating, and the music seems to accelerate without changing tempo.","A four-voice 'Ave Maria' circulated under Victoria's name for over a century, was recorded endlessly, and is now generally judged not to be his — probably a nineteenth-century editorial creation. This eight-voice setting, by contrast, is securely his, printed in his lifetime under his own supervision. The confusion has done real damage to his reputation among casual listeners, who often know only the spurious piece.","Find a recording that places the choirs apart in the stereo image; without that separation the piece loses half its point. Listen for the moment the alternation gives way to overlap. Then hear Gabrieli's 'In ecclesiis', where the same Venetian idea is scaled up with brass and organ, to see the technique at full stretch."],"victoria-versa-est-luctum":["Six voices, about four minutes, and the darkest thing Victoria wrote. My harp is turned to mourning, and my music into the voice of those who weep. He scores it low, with two bass parts, and keeps the whole motet within a narrow, shadowed range. The opening phrase falls; almost every subsequent phrase falls; and the piece ends by descending once more onto a chord that arrives without any sense of arrival.","It belongs to the 1605 Officium Defunctorum, the music for the Dowager Empress María's obsequies, where it stands slightly apart — not part of the Requiem liturgy proper but a motet added for the occasion. Victoria had served the Empress at the Descalzas Reales in Madrid for sixteen years, and this is the closest he comes in the whole publication to speaking in the first person. He outlived her by eight years.","The text is from the Book of Job, and it is about a musician: Job's instrument turned to lamentation. Victoria, writing for the funeral of the woman whose chapel he had directed for a third of his life, chose a text in which making music becomes weeping. Listen for the second bass part, added specifically here, which drags the whole texture down beneath its normal floor.","It has become the single most performed movement of the Officium Defunctorum, frequently excerpted for memorial services and for recordings of Renaissance funeral music. Some scholars argue that the added low part reflects Spanish practice of doubling the bass with a bajón; others hear it as purely vocal. Editors have also differed over where it belongs in performance, since it sits outside the Requiem text itself.","Play it once, then read the words and play it again — knowing that a musician is describing his own instrument changes everything. Listen for the lowest voice. It pairs naturally with the Introit from the same collection, and with Lassus's 'Lagrime di San Pietro', finished a decade earlier by another old man in much the same mood."],"gabrieli-sonata-pian-e-forte":["Eight instrumental parts in two groups of four, about five minutes. The first choir is led by a cornett — a curved wooden horn played with a small cup mouthpiece — over three trombones; the second by a violino, a large viola, over three more trombones. The groups alternate in long, grave phrases, marked soft when they play alone and loud when they combine, and the piece ends with all eight sounding together in a slow, wide cadence.","St Mark's in Venice has galleries on either side of the chancel, and by the 1580s its musicians were exploiting them systematically. Gabrieli, organist there and nephew of the previous organist Andrea, published this in Sacrae symphoniae of 1597, a collection that carried Venetian ceremonial music across Europe. Doges' processions, state feasts and the visits of foreign ambassadors all called for exactly this: sound as civic display, paid for by the republic.","The two words printed in the parts, piano and forte, are why the piece is famous — among the earliest instances of a composer writing dynamics into the score rather than leaving them to the players. Gabrieli also names the instruments, which was equally unusual: most music of the period could be played on whatever was to hand. Listen for the first moment both choirs sound together; that is where forte is marked, and it is a structural event.","Textbooks have repeated for a century that this was the first piece ever to specify dynamics; specialists now hedge, since Venetian practice and one or two earlier prints complicate the claim. What is not disputed is that the piece codified something. Modern brass ensembles play it constantly, usually on modern instruments in balanced quartets, which produces a far heavier sound than cornetts and Renaissance sackbuts.","Seek out a period-instrument recording first: cornetts and sackbuts are lighter and more vocal than modern brass, and the piece was written for their blend. Listen for which side of the ensemble is playing, and for the exact moment both join. Then hear 'In ecclesiis' to find out what Gabrieli did when he added voices and an organ to the same idea."],"gabrieli-canzon-septimi-toni-8":["Eight players in two four-part groups, about four minutes, and far livelier than the Sonata that sits beside it in the same collection. It opens with the rhythm that marks every canzona of the period — one long note and two short, repeated — and then the two choirs chase each other through it, snatching phrases, overlapping, dropping into a slower middle section in triple time before the opening figure returns. It ends brightly and quickly.","The canzona began as an instrumental arrangement of a French chanson and by Gabrieli's time had become a free instrumental form, the direct ancestor of the sonata. This one appeared in Sacrae symphoniae, 1597, the publication that carried Venetian music to Germany and drew students — Heinrich Schütz among them — to study with its composer. Venice was then at the height of its ceremonial self-presentation, and its music was an export.","The long-short-short opening is not arbitrary. It is the rhythm of a French chanson's first line, retained after the words were dropped, and every listener in 1597 recognised it as the signal that an instrumental piece was starting — a genre marker, like a fanfare. Listen for how Gabrieli disguises it later, stretching it out in the slow section and then snapping it back into place.","Gabrieli's canzonas are now standard repertoire for brass ensembles, which has given them a monumental sound they may never have had: sixteenth-century cornetts and sackbuts were lighter and quicker. The instrumentation is not specified in the print, which leaves performers free and scholars arguing. Some groups use viols or mixed consorts, and the difference in character is startling.","Listen for the opening rhythm and count how many times it returns; the piece is a game with one figure. Then hear a period-instrument version and a modern brass version back to back — few pieces show the difference more clearly. If you like it, Gabrieli wrote dozens of canzonas, and they are all short."],"gabrieli-ecclesiis":["Roughly seven minutes for four solo voices, a four-part chorus, six instruments — three cornetts, a viola, two trombones — and organ, the largest apparatus Gabrieli ever assembled. Solo voices sing first, each answered by the chorus with the same Alleluia refrain, which returns throughout like a pillar. A purely instrumental Sinfonia sits at the centre. At the end everything sounds at once, voices, brass and organ, in the loudest music Venice could make.","By the time this appeared, in the posthumous Symphoniae sacrae of 1615, Gabrieli was dead and the world had changed: Monteverdi was in Venice, opera existed, and the basso continuo — a bass line with an organist filling in harmony above it — had become standard. The piece uses it. St Mark's employed dozens of singers and instrumentalists for major feasts, paid for by a state that regarded its music as a diplomatic asset.","The Alleluia refrain is not just a repeated tag; it is the architecture, returning after each solo and each increase in forces, so you can hear the ensemble growing every time it comes back. Listen for the moment the continuo organ becomes audible on its own beneath a single voice. That thin sound is the future: not Renaissance polyphony, where every voice is equal, but a melody over a bass.","'In ecclesiis' is routinely called the culmination of the Venetian polychoral style and, equally routinely, the point at which that style becomes early Baroque. Nineteenth-century revivals used huge choirs and organs; the period-instrument movement restored cornetts and one-per-part singing and revealed a far more agile piece. Scholars still argue over how the spatial layout at St Mark's actually worked, since the gallery evidence is thinner than the legend.","Listen for the Alleluia every time it returns and note what has been added since the last one — the piece is a controlled crescendo of resources across seven minutes. Then play the Toccata that opens Monteverdi's 'Orfeo', written a few years earlier, and hear the same Venetian brass culture in the theatre. Period instruments, always."],"gabrieli-canzon-per-sonar-primi-toni":["Four minutes, eight instrumental parts in two groups, and the plainest of Gabrieli's published canzonas. It opens with the standard long-short-short canzona rhythm, works through a series of short imitative sections that each take up a new figure, and alternates the two choirs steadily rather than dramatically. There is no slow music and almost no display; the pleasure is in the constant handing-over, and in how neatly each section dovetails into the next.","Printed in Sacrae symphoniae of 1597 alongside the Sonata pian' e forte and a dozen other instrumental pieces. Gabrieli's job at St Mark's required a steady supply of such music: processions, the entries of the Doge, the great feasts of the Venetian civic calendar, all needed instrumental interludes of a few minutes. The 'primo tono' of the title simply names the mode, the way a later composer would name a key.","Notice how the piece is built from short blocks rather than long spans — each new imitative section is a fresh idea, tried out and dropped. That block construction is what the Baroque sonata inherited from the canzona, and Corelli's trio sonatas a century later still work this way. Listen for the joins: Gabrieli overlaps them so that one section's ending is already the next one's beginning.","Less famous than its companions and correspondingly less argued over, it is nonetheless one of the pieces that taught Europe what an instrumental ensemble could be. As with the other canzonas the instrumentation is unspecified, and performances range from brass quartets doubled, to mixed consorts of cornetts, sackbuts and strings, to modern brass choirs. Titles vary between sources, which has caused persistent confusion between similarly named pieces.","Use it as an introduction to the whole Venetian instrumental repertory: it is short, cheerful and makes no demands. Count the sections as they change. Then move to the Sonata pian' e forte, which does something much stranger with the same eight players, and after that to Corelli, who turned these blocks into a form."],"gabrieli-o-magnum-mysterium":["Eight voices in two four-part choirs, about four minutes, on the Christmas responsory. Gabrieli opens with slow, wide chords from one choir answered by the other, so the first minute is pure spatial effect rather than melody. The middle narrates the animals at the manger in quicker exchanges, and the closing Alleluia sets both choirs going in fast triple time, overlapping and reinforcing until eight voices sound like far more than eight.","From Sacrae symphoniae of 1597, the same book as the great instrumental pieces — the title means sacred symphonies in the old sense of sounding together. Christmas at St Mark's was one of the feasts at which the basilica's full musical establishment turned out, singers and instrumentalists in the galleries, and visitors' accounts of Venetian church music from these years read like reviews of a spectacle. Which, in a sense, they were.","Compare the opening chords with the way Victoria begins the same text and the difference in method is total: Victoria writes a melody and imitates it, Gabrieli writes a block of harmony and moves it around the building. Listen for the passages where the two choirs overlap by a beat rather than answering cleanly — the resulting blur was a deliberate effect, and it depends on the groups being physically far apart.","Gabrieli's polychoral motets were the model for German composers who studied in Venice, above all Schütz, and through them they shaped Lutheran music for a century. Modern performances usually add instruments doubling the voices, which contemporary accounts support though the print does not specify. The main argument is over distance: recordings that place the choirs close together lose the effect the music exists to produce.","Play Victoria's four-voice setting of the same words first, then this — five minutes that show two entire musical cultures on one text. Listen for the Alleluia, and for whether your recording really separates the choirs. Then follow the line onward to Schütz, who carried this sound back over the Alps into a Germany about to be destroyed by war."],"dowland-flow-my-tears":["A song for one voice and lute, about five minutes, built on the shape of a pavan: three musical strains, each played and sung twice with fresh words. It opens with four notes falling like drops, the figure that gave the piece its Latin nickname, Lachrimae. The lute does not merely accompany; it argues, adding lines that cut across the singer. Dowland also printed a version for four voices with lute, so a household could sing it around a table.","He published it in his Second Booke of Songs in 1600, printed in London by George Eastland while the composer himself was abroad, employed as lutenist to Christian IV of Denmark at a salary larger than most English courtiers earned. He had been refused a place in Elizabeth I's household, possibly because he had converted to Catholicism as a young man in Paris. Printed songbooks were a new commercial product, and Dowland was among the first musicians made famous by them.","The tune was a lute solo first: the Lachrimae pavan circulated in manuscript years before anyone put words to it. Once the song appeared, other composers could not leave it alone. Keyboard and consort settings of those four falling notes survive by the dozen across England and northern Europe, making the figure something close to shared property, quoted the way a later age would quote a hit record.","It was the song of its era, and its afterlife has never stopped. Modern scholarship is less romantic about the grief: Dowland was a shrewd operator who built a marketable persona around the motto semper dolens, always grieving, and lobbied hard for every post he wanted. Performers still argue whether the ayres want one singer with lute or four voices, and how much the lute part should be ornamented.","Start with the first four notes and keep listening for them; they head every strain. Try a version with one singer and one lute before any choral arrangement, then hear the wordless consort setting from Lachrimae to feel how much the words were carrying. Andreas Scholl, Emma Kirkby and Sting have all recorded it very differently."],"dowland-lachrimae-pavan":["Four minutes for one lute alone. A pavan is a slow processional dance in two beats, and this one has the standard three strains, each played twice: once plain, once broken into faster ornamental figures the player was expected to supply. Everything grows from the opening descent of four notes. The instrument is quiet, close-miked in any good recording, and the piece works as private music for one person in a room.","It circulated in handwritten lute books from the mid-1590s, before Dowland set words to it. The lute was the domestic instrument of educated Europe, and Dowland spent these years travelling it: Paris, the Kassel court of the Landgrave of Hesse, Venice, Florence, and eventually Denmark. He carried his pieces as calling cards, and copyists in half a dozen countries took them down, which is why the surviving sources disagree about details.","In 1604 he expanded the idea into a whole book, Lachrimae or Seaven Teares, for five viols and lute. Seven pavans all begin with the same four falling notes, then diverge: old tears, new tears, sighing, sad, forced, a lover's, true tears. It is a cycle of griefs graded like a taxonomy, and one of the first collections in music organised around a single obsessive idea.","Performance is the argument here. How much ornamentation belongs in the repeats, what tuning and stringing to use, whether the lute should sound bright or veiled. Nigel North and Paul O'Dette represent different answers. The 1604 consort book has had its own revival, with viol groups treating the seven pavans as an integral cycle rather than a set of arrangements.","Listen to a solo lute version first, at low volume, and follow the ornamented repeat of each strain against the plain one. Then hear the same music in the 1604 consort scoring, played by a viol group such as Fretwork or Hesperion XX, and notice how five instruments make the falling figure feel inescapable."],"dowland-come-again":["A strophic ayre: the same music repeated for each verse, voice and lute, four minutes at most. Its famous moment arrives early, when the singer climbs a five-step ladder on the words to see, to hear, to touch, to kiss, to die, each step higher and more urgent than the last, before the phrase drops back down. Everything else in the song is courtship poetry over a lute that keeps a light dance step underneath.","It comes from The First Booke of Songes or Ayres, London 1597, the book that invented an English publishing genre. Dowland had it printed in table-book layout: the parts face outward in different directions on the open page so four singers seated around a table could all read from one copy. It sold well enough to be reprinted four times in fifteen years, which for music in Shakespeare's London was extraordinary.","The last rung of that ladder is a joke. To die was Elizabethan slang for sexual climax, and the whole ascending sequence is built to arrive there. The rhetoric is borrowed from oratory, where a climax means literally a staircase, and Dowland treats a love song as an argument being pressed on a listener who has already said no.","The First Booke fixed the ayre as England's answer to the Italian madrigal: one voice with instrumental support rather than equal interweaving parts. Performers divide over whether these songs are solo pieces or partsongs, since the printing supports both, and over voice type; countertenors, tenors and sopranos all claim them. The bawdy reading of the text is now standard rather than daring.","Count the five rising steps on to see, to hear, to touch, to kiss, to die, then wait for the same shape in later verses. Hear it once sung by a solo voice with lute and once by four voices, and decide which you prefer. Then play In Darkness Let Me Dwell to hear how far the same composer could travel."],"dowland-darkness-let-me-dwell":["Four minutes for voice and lute, and unlike almost every other Dowland song it is through-composed: no repeating verses, the music simply follows the words wherever they go. The singer asks for a room walled in by grief. The lute grinds against the voice in dissonances that are not smoothed away. At the end the singer returns to the opening line, breaks off in mid-thought, and the lute stops without a closing cadence.","It appeared in A Musicall Banquet of 1610, an anthology assembled by his son Robert Dowland containing English ayres alongside Italian and Spanish songs, including pieces by Giulio Caccini. That mixture matters: English readers were being shown the new Italian solo song for the first time. Dowland was back in London by then, in his late forties, still without the court post he wanted; James I finally appointed him a royal lutenist in 1612.","Listen to the last fifteen seconds. The song does not end so much as stop, on an unfinished phrase and an unresolved chord, which almost nothing else in 1610 does. The trick is not decoration but structure: he refuses the listener the closure the form demands, which is exactly what the poem asks for.","Writers have reached for anachronism to describe it, calling it expressionist three centuries early, and the comparison with Schoenberg's Pierrot has been made often enough to become a cliche. Scholars caution that the effect depends on performance, and that a smooth, pretty reading kills it. Sting's Songs from the Labyrinth in 2006 put the song, unexpectedly, onto pop charts.","Play it once at the end of a long day and pay attention to the final seconds, when the music refuses to finish. Then go to Gesualdo's Moro, lasso and hear a contemporary reaching similar extremes by an entirely different route. A recording with a single singer and one lute, close and unglamorous, serves it best."],"dowland-can-she-excuse-my-wrongs":["Three minutes of complaint set, oddly, to a dance. The music is a galliard, a springing triple-time dance with a leap in it, and the words are a bitter question about a woman who will not say yes or no. The accents keep shifting so that the phrase seems to trip over its own feet, and the singer has to fight the dance rhythm to make the sentences land.","From The First Booke of Songes of 1597. The same music circulates in lute and consort sources under the title The Earl of Essex Galliard, which links it to Robert Devereux, Elizabeth I's favourite, who lost her favour and was executed for treason in 1601. Whether the song was written for him, about him, or simply attached to his name later cannot now be settled.","The rhythmic stumble is deliberate craft, not clumsiness: the tune repeatedly groups its beats in twos against the dance's threes, so the ear cannot settle. Dowland was working the same material in at least three formats at once, and the ayre, the solo lute galliard and the five-part consort version all survive, each with its own detail.","The Essex reading is attractive and unproven; the poem's author is unknown, and coded political complaint was common enough in Elizabethan verse to make attribution guesswork. What is not in doubt is the practice of recycling: Dowland treated a good tune as a resource to be reissued in every format the market would buy.","Tap the three-beat dance pulse and notice how often the melody refuses to line up with it. Then find the lute solo called The Earl of Essex Galliard and hear the same music without a word of text, which makes the dance obvious and the grievance vanish."],"dowland-frog-galliard":["A galliard for solo lute: quick triple time, three strains, each repeated with faster decoration. The galliard was an athletic dance of five steps and a jump, and you can hear where the jump goes. The same tune also exists as the ayre Now, O now I needs must part, so the melody has a second life carrying words about a lover's departure, which changes its character entirely.","Dance music was the ordinary business of an Elizabethan lutenist: pavans for procession, galliards for display, and a good tune could earn its keep for years. Dowland played at court functions and in private houses, and pieces like this one travelled across Europe in the notebooks of amateurs, appearing in Dutch, German and Danish sources within his lifetime.","The nickname probably points at Francois, Duke of Anjou, the French suitor Elizabeth I is said to have called her frog during the long marriage negotiations of the late 1570s. If so, a piece of court gossip has outlived the diplomacy that produced it, and the melancholy departure song attached to the same tune may be part of the joke.","Nobody knows which version came first, dance or song, and the sources do not settle it. The tune appears in virginal books, consort arrangements and continental lute manuscripts, sometimes anonymously, so a few settings credited to Dowland may be other men's work. This is normal for the period and a reminder that authorship was a looser idea then.","Hear the lute solo first and find the leap in each strain, then play the song version, Now, O now I needs must part, back to back with it. The same notes turn from cheerful to rueful, which tells you how much of a piece's meaning lives in its words."],"gesualdo-moro-lasso-al-mio-duolo":["Four minutes for five unaccompanied voices. The piece alternates two speeds and two worlds: slow, crawling chromatic chords for the lines about dying of grief, and quick, entirely conventional counterpoint for the lines about the woman who could give life and will not. The opening seconds are the famous part, a sequence of chords that slide sideways into places the ear cannot predict and the theory of the time did not describe.","Gesualdo published his fifth and sixth books of madrigals in 1611 from his castle in the hills of Basilicata, where he had lived in increasing isolation for years. He had murdered his first wife and her lover in 1590 and, as a nobleman, faced no trial. His second marriage, to Leonora d'Este, connected him to Ferrara, then Europe's laboratory for chromatic experiment, and to its composers and singers.","He paid to have the music printed in score, with all five parts stacked vertically, instead of the usual separate part-books. That is a scholar's format, not a singer's. He wanted the harmony seen as a vertical object, which suggests he knew exactly how strange it was. The fast sections matter too: they are textbook-correct, and their correctness is what makes the slow ones sound unhinged.","The music went unread for three centuries. Stravinsky's advocacy in the 1950s, culminating in his Monumentum pro Gesualdo, made him a modernist ancestor, and the murder story has done the rest, feeding novels, an Aldous Huxley essay and a Werner Herzog film. Scholars now push back, arguing the biography has eaten the music and that the Ferrara circle explains more than the crime does.","Play only the first thirty seconds, three times. The point is not to analyse the harmony but to notice that you cannot guess the next chord. Then hear Monteverdi's Cruda Amarilli, written in the same decade for the same forces, to compare two very different ways of breaking the rules. Seek an ensemble with one voice per part."],"gesualdo-tristis-est-anima-mea":["Six unaccompanied voices, four minutes, setting Christ's words in Gethsemane: my soul is sorrowful unto death. The shape is a responsory, so a long opening section is followed by a verse and then part of the opening returns, which means you hear the same harmonic shocks twice. On the words about the disciples taking flight, the voices scatter into fast running figures and abandon each other.","It belongs to the Responsoria of 1611, twenty-seven settings covering the Tenebrae services of the last three days of Holy Week, when candles are extinguished one by one until the church is dark. Gesualdo printed them in Naples in the same year as his sixth madrigal book. By then he was ill, guilt-ridden and, according to contemporaries, having himself beaten by servants as penance.","The chromatic slides are still here, but they are on a leash. Liturgical text imposes a shape and a pace that a madrigal does not, so the strangeness arrives inside a form the listener can follow, which arguably makes it land harder. Notice also how he splits the six voices into a high group and a low group so that phrases echo back from a different register.","For most of the twentieth century the madrigals were the whole story and the sacred music was treated as a curiosity. Recordings since the 1990s, from the Tallis Scholars and specialist Gesualdo ensembles, have argued the Responsoria are the stronger achievement. The usual performance question is pitch: whether the printed clefs indicate transposition upward, which changes the vocal colour completely.","Listen in a dark room, or better, in a resonant church. Follow the return of the opening section after the verse and notice that you now know where the harmony is going, which is its own kind of dread. Then play Victoria's Tenebrae responsories for a Spanish contemporary's very different answer to the same liturgy."],"gesualdo-belta-poi-che-t-assenti":["Three minutes, five unaccompanied voices, and among the most concentrated things he wrote. Beauty is leaving, so take my heart with you: the poem is four lines long and the music treats each phrase as a separate crisis. The word for harsh, aspro, gets harshness, a chord that rubs and will not settle, and the piece keeps returning to a home harmony that never quite feels like home.","From the Sixth Book of Madrigals, printed in 1611 by Giovanni Giacomo Carlino at the town of Gesualdo itself, on a press the prince effectively controlled. He was writing for a private ensemble of singers he employed, not for the open market, which removed the usual commercial pressure to be singable. The Ferrara connection supplied both the poetry and the taste for chromatic experiment.","The chromatic writing is not decoration laid over a normal piece; it is the structure. Voices move by half-steps in opposite directions until the chord underneath has drifted somewhere unrelated to where it began, then a single conventional cadence snaps everything back. Stravinsky chose this madrigal as one of the three he recomposed for orchestra in Monumentum pro Gesualdo in 1960.","Stravinsky's endorsement changed the reception history: after 1960 Gesualdo was discussed as a precursor rather than a freak. Analysts still disagree about whether his chromaticism follows a system he understood or is intuitive trial and error. The short madrigals like this one are the strongest evidence for the systematic reading, since the same procedures recur too precisely to be accidental.","It is three minutes long; play it twice in a row. On the second pass, ignore the individual voices and listen only to the chords as blocks, noticing how far the music has moved by the halfway point. Then hear Monteverdi's Cruda Amarilli, which shocks in an entirely different way."],"gesualdo-o-vos-omnes":["Three minutes for six unaccompanied voices, setting words from Lamentations that the liturgy gives to Christ on the cross: all you who pass by this way, look and see whether there is any sorrow like mine. Gesualdo keeps stalling the music on held chords, as if the question were being repeated to a street that will not stop walking. The texture divides into a high group and a low one, so the appeal comes back as an echo.","One of the Tenebrae Responsoria published in Naples in 1611 for the darkened services of Holy Week. The same text had been set by Victoria a generation earlier and was among the best-known laments in the Catholic repertory, which means Gesualdo was writing against a model his listeners already carried in their heads, and knew it.","The stalling is the device. Where a Renaissance composer normally keeps several voices moving so the music flows, Gesualdo repeatedly freezes all six on one chord and holds it a beat too long. Nothing is happening, and that is the effect. The chromatic writing then arrives into a silence he has manufactured.","Comparing this with Victoria's setting has become the standard way of teaching what changed between 1570 and 1611: the same words, one composer building a controlled arch, the other breaking the line into fragments. Recent scholarship treats the Responsoria as devotional music for private use as much as liturgical performance, given the small forces available at Gesualdo's castle.","Play Victoria's O vos omnes and this one back to back, in that order, and stop after each phrase. Victoria consoles; Gesualdo does not. A one-voice-per-part recording keeps the dissonances audible, where a big choir blurs them into general beauty."],"gesualdo-io-parto-e-non-piu-dissi":["Three minutes, five voices, no instruments. The first words are I depart, and said no more, and the music does exactly that: voices begin, then drop out, leaving gaps in the middle of the sentence. The texture thins to two singers and floods back to five. When the poem turns to the pain of parting, the harmony slides chromatically downward and the pulse effectively stops.","From the Sixth Book of 1611, printed at Gesualdo by Carlino. The prince kept a resident group of singers and a music-loving household; several of his madrigals were probably first heard by a handful of people in a room. The poetry, by an unnamed author of the Ferrarese circle, deals in the standard conceits of love as death, which Gesualdo takes at face value rather than as metaphor.","Count the silences. Rests interrupt words, not just phrases, which no earlier madrigalist would risk because it damages the text's intelligibility. Gesualdo trades clarity for enactment: the sentence is broken because the speaker cannot finish it. The trick is closer to twentieth-century theatre than to sixteenth-century rhetoric.","Composers rather than critics kept his reputation alive. Twentieth-century writers seized on pieces like this as evidence of a mind ahead of its time, while historians point out that fragmentation was already a madrigal technique and Gesualdo simply pushed it further than anyone dared. Both readings survive, and recordings now tend to let the silences run long rather than smoothing them over.","Listen for what is not there: the pauses inside phrases. Count them on a second hearing. Then play Josquin's Mille regretz, a parting song from ninety years earlier, and hear how much politer sixteenth-century farewell used to be."],"monteverdi-l-orfeo-toccata":["Barely ninety seconds of music, marked in the printed score to be played three times before the curtain rises. Five trumpet parts stack up over a single unchanging harmony, with drums beating underneath and strings doubling. Nothing modulates, nothing develops; it is pure noise and ceremony, the loudest possible way of telling a room to stop talking. Played on natural trumpets it is thrilling and slightly out of tune, which is correct.","L'Orfeo was staged in Mantua on 24 February 1607 for the Accademia degli Invaghiti, a private society of noblemen at the court of Duke Vincenzo Gonzaga, in a room rather than a theatre, for an audience of a few hundred men. Alessandro Striggio wrote the libretto. Monteverdi published the score in 1609 with a printed list of the instruments used, one of the earliest such lists we have.","This is not original music in the modern sense. A toccata was the fanfare Gonzaga trumpeters blew to announce the duke's arrival, and Monteverdi simply wrote down court ceremonial and put it at the head of an artwork. The audience would have heard their own daily life quoted back at them, then watched it turn into theatre.","L'Orfeo disappeared for nearly three centuries and returned through Vincent d'Indy's 1904 edition. Every modern performance is an argument: which instruments from the 1609 list play where, what pitch to tune to, how much percussion to add where none is notated. Harnoncourt's 1969 recording and later period stagings by Savall and Christie represent very different reconstructions of the same two minutes.","Play it loud, on a recording with real natural trumpets and a big drum. Listen for the fact that the harmony never changes, which is why it works as an announcement rather than a piece. Then go straight to Possente spirto later in the same opera to hear what Monteverdi could do when he wanted to persuade rather than command."],"monteverdi-vespers-1610-deus-adiutorium":["Two minutes that open the largest sacred work of its decade. A solo voice chants the opening versicle alone and unaccompanied, asking God to make haste to help. Then the full choir answers on one repeated note, all six parts moving in the same rhythm like a crowd speaking in unison, while cornetts, trombones and strings hurl fanfares around them. It ends on a fast, wheeling Alleluia.","Published in Venice in 1610 with a dedication to Pope Paul V. Monteverdi was still employed at Mantua, badly paid and openly unhappy, and the print reads as a job application aimed at Rome and Venice. It worked in the second direction: he was appointed director of music at St Mark's in 1613, the most prestigious church post in Italy, and held it for thirty years.","The instrumental music is the L'Orfeo toccata again, reused note for note three years after the opera. The same fanfare that announced a duke in a Mantuan drawing room now announces God at Vespers. Nobody at the time seems to have found this scandalous, which tells you how porous the wall between theatre and church music actually was.","The Vespers is a scholarly battlefield. Was it ever performed as a single service, or is it an anthology assembled to show range? Do the plainchant antiphons belong between the psalms? And the transposition question: some movements are notated in high clefs that may signal downward transposition, which changes the whole sound. Andrew Parrott and others have argued these points for decades.","Listen to the choir chanting on one repeated pitch while the brass spins overhead, and try to hold both in your ear at once. Then play the L'Orfeo toccata and hear that you already know this music. A recording with cornetts rather than modern trumpets gets the reedy, vocal quality Monteverdi wanted."],"monteverdi-lamento-della-ninfa":["Six minutes in three panels. Three men in ordinary declamation describe a girl walking out at dawn; then the lament itself, in which a soprano sings over four descending bass notes repeated more than thirty times without a break while the men interject pitying comments; then the men close the frame. The bass never stops falling, and the singer's phrases keep landing in the wrong places against it.","It appears in the eighth book of madrigals, Madrigali guerrieri et amorosi, published in Venice in 1638 and dedicated to Emperor Ferdinand III. Monteverdi was past seventy, ordained a priest since 1632, and running music at St Mark's. The book's preface sets out his theory of three styles, including the agitated manner of repeated notes he invented to depict warfare, and it reads as a summing-up of a long career.","The printed score instructs that the nymph should sing in the time of her feeling rather than in the beat of the hand, while the three men keep strict time. Two different clocks run at once, and every performance has to decide how far apart to let them drift. It is an extraordinarily modern instruction, written down in 1638.","The four-note falling bass became the standard European emblem of grief, turning up in Purcell's Dido, in Bach, and for centuries afterwards; musicologists have traced the pattern as a shared, recognisable sign rather than a borrowing from any single piece. Performance debates centre on how freely the soprano may push against the beat, and whether the male trio should sound compassionate or coldly observational.","Count the four bass notes, then keep counting while the soprano enters, and feel her phrases refusing to line up. Compare Dido's Lament, built on the same falling idea fifty years later. Any recording that lets the singer stretch the line rather than staying metronomic is the right one."],"monteverdi-zefiro-torna":["Six minutes for two tenors over a chaconne: a short bass pattern repeated more than fifty times without variation while the singers chase each other above it in increasingly athletic runs. The west wind returns, the meadows laugh, birds chatter, and the word for chattering gets a torrent of fast notes. Then, near the end, everything changes and the piece turns inward.","Published in Venice in 1632 in the collection Scherzi musicali, on a text by Ottavio Rinuccini. The chaconne pattern had reached Italy from Spain and, before that, from Spanish America, arriving as a slightly disreputable popular dance and being absorbed into art music within a generation. Continuo players would have strummed it on chitarrone or guitar, closer to a groove than to a chord chart.","Listen for the moment the bass pattern stops. Two-thirds of the way through, when the poet turns from the spring landscape to his own solitude, Monteverdi abandons the engine that has driven the whole piece. Free, unmeasured lament replaces it. Nothing announces the change; the ground simply disappears from under the singers.","There are two Zefiro torna settings, and they get confused: a Petrarch madrigal in the sixth book of 1614 and this chaconne on Rinuccini's poem. Performance practice for the chaconne is unusually free, and groups such as Concerto Italiano add guitar strumming and percussion for which there is no notation, on the reasonable argument that this is dance music.","Enjoy the vocal showing-off in the middle, then wait for the bass to vanish near the end and notice how bare the music suddenly sounds. A recording with two well-matched tenors and a plucked continuo, rather than organ, keeps the dance in it."],"monteverdi-si-dolce-e-l-tormento":["Four minutes, one singer, and a continuo instrument or two: theorbo, harpsichord, sometimes a bass viol. Four verses run over the same slowly descending bass with no instrumental interlude between them, so the song simply keeps circling. The tune is plain enough to be folk music, the harmony under it minor and unhurried, and the whole effect depends on how much the singer is willing to shade the repetitions.","It was published in Venice in 1624 inside a collection of arias assembled by Carlo Milanuzzi, a working anthology of the kind printers turned out for the domestic market. Monteverdi contributed a few pieces alongside other composers. This is not court or church music but something to be bought, taken home and sung by one person to a lute in a room, which was a genuinely new commercial category.","Nobody knows who wrote the poem. The song's fame is entirely modern: it sat unremarked in an anthology for three centuries and became a recital staple only in the twentieth, once early-music singers went looking for repertoire. Its popularity says as much about our taste for bare, slow, minor-key intimacy as about 1624.","Historically it is a minor piece; in performance it is now one of the most recorded things Monteverdi wrote, which creates an odd gap between importance and fame. Because the print gives only a vocal line and a bass, everything else is a modern choice: key, instruments, tempo, ornament. Countertenors, sopranos, tenors and crossover singers have all claimed it, with wildly different results.","Hear two recordings with different voice types, one high and one low, and notice how much the song's character changes when almost nothing on the page has. Listen for what each singer does differently on the repeated verses, since that variation is the whole performance."],"monteverdi-pur-ti-miro":["The last five minutes of L'incoronazione di Poppea. Two high voices, usually soprano and mezzo or soprano and countertenor, entwine over a slow rocking bass, singing I gaze at you, I possess you. Almost every phrase involves one voice holding a note that clashes with the other and then sliding into agreement, over and over, so the whole duet is a chain of small frictions resolving.","Venice, carnival season 1643, at the Teatro Santi Giovanni e Paolo: a public theatre where anyone with the price of a ticket could attend, not a court entertainment. Giovanni Francesco Busenello's libretto ends with Nero and Poppea crowned and triumphant, the philosopher dead and the wronged wife exiled. Monteverdi was seventy-six and died within months of the run.","It is probably not by Monteverdi. The same duet appears in a Venetian opera of 1641 with music credited to Benedetto Ferrari, and the two surviving scores of Poppea are Venetian and Neapolitan copies made in the 1650s, years after his death, differing from each other in significant ways. The most beloved music in his most famous opera may be someone else's.","Beyond the authorship problem there is a moral one: an opera that rewards adultery, murder and tyranny with the most tender music in the repertory, and appears to mean it. Nineteenth-century writers found this unforgivable; modern directors find it the point. The work vanished entirely until a 1904 edition and re-entered the stage only after the Second World War.","Listen only to the two voices and ignore the words: hear each dissonance form and dissolve, and count how many times it happens. Then read a synopsis of the plot and play it again, and decide whether the beauty is ironic. Any recording with two well-blended voices and a light continuo will do."],"monteverdi-cruda-amarilli":["Three minutes for five unaccompanied voices on lines from Guarini's pastoral play Il pastor fido: cruel Amaryllis, who teaches bitterness with your very name. Twice the top voice enters on a note that grates against the chord beneath it without any of the preparation the rules required, once on the opening cry and once on the sigh ahi lasso. Around those two moments the writing is elegant and conventional.","Published in the fifth book of madrigals in 1605, though the piece was circulating by the late 1590s. The Bolognese theorist Giovanni Maria Artusi had already attacked it in print in 1600, quoting the offending passages as specimens of the imperfections of modern music. Monteverdi replied in the 1605 preface and his brother Giulio Cesare expanded the reply in 1607, naming the doctrine: the seconda pratica, in which the words are the mistress of the harmony.","Artusi quoted the passages anonymously and without the text. That is the joke at the centre of the whole controversy: he printed the notes stripped of the words, then complained that the notes made no sense, when the entire defence rested on the words being the reason for them. The argument was decided before it began.","This small piece is the founding document of the split between ancient and modern that music has been re-fighting ever since. Historians argue about whether the second practice was a genuine theory or a slogan improvised under attack, and note that Monteverdi promised a treatise explaining it and never delivered one in more than thirty years.","Listen for two seconds specifically: the very first entry, and the sigh a little later. Then play some Palestrina and hear what the rules sounded like when kept. A one-voice-per-part recording makes the collisions audible; a large choir smooths them into pleasantness, which defeats the purpose."],"monteverdi-l-orfeo-possente-spirto":["Ten minutes at the centre of the opera, in which Orpheus tries to talk his way past the ferryman of the dead. Six verses run over the same bass, and each is answered by a different pair of instruments: two violins, then two cornetts, then a double harp, then strings. The vocal line grows more ornate with every verse, until the singer is spinning long decorated chains around a very simple tune.","Mantua, 1607, for the Gonzaga court, where a celebrated harpist was on the payroll and the instrument gets its own verse. The plot depends on this scene working: Orpheus's power is his singing, so the aria has to be genuinely persuasive or the drama collapses. Monteverdi solves the problem by making the persuasion audible as effort, verse by verse.","The 1609 printed score gives two versions of the vocal line on facing staves, plain and fully ornamented, so we can see exactly how a singer of 1607 decorated a melody. It is one of the most valuable documents in early performance practice. And the ornaments fail: Charon is not persuaded, he simply falls asleep. Orpheus wins by exhaustion, not artistry.","It is the standard test of Baroque tenor technique and the standard site of argument: sing Monteverdi's printed ornaments, improvise your own, or alternate. Nigel Rogers's recordings in the 1960s and 1970s reshaped expectations of what the ornamentation could sound like. Pitch, instrument choice and the size of the continuo group remain unsettled in every production.","Take it one verse at a time and name the instruments that answer each: violins, cornetts, harp. Then listen to how the singing changes when Orpheus stops decorating. Look for a recording that uses a real double harp, and compare Gluck's Orpheus 155 years later, where the same story is told with almost no ornament at all."],"schutz-musikalische-exequien":["Forty minutes in three parts. First a German burial service shaped like a Mass, with soloists and small groups trading short scriptural sentences over continuo. Then a double-choir motet on the words: Lord, if I have only you, I ask nothing of heaven or earth. Then the Nunc dimittis, sung by the main choir, while a separate small group placed somewhere else in the building answers with the words blessed are the dead.","Prince Heinrich Posthumus von Reuss died in December 1635 and was buried the following February at Gera. He had planned his own funeral in detail, choosing every biblical and hymn text himself and having them inscribed on the copper coffin he commissioned years in advance and kept where he could see it. The Thirty Years' War was in its eighteenth year and Saxony was being destroyed around them.","The third part is staged in space. The main choir sings the old man's farewell here on earth; two seraphim and a blessed soul, three voices placed apart and quieter, answer from somewhere else. The two groups finish together, and in a good performance you cannot quite tell where the distant voices are coming from.","It is regularly described as the ancestor of Brahms's German Requiem, which likewise abandons the Latin liturgy for chosen German scripture and shares one of its texts. Performance questions are mostly practical and unresolved: how many singers, whether to use instruments where the print is ambiguous, and how far away to place the second group without wrecking the ensemble.","Go straight to the final section and listen for the far-off trio; then start again from the beginning knowing where it is heading. Follow the German text, since Schutz sets it as speech rather than as decoration. Herreweghe and the Gabrieli Consort offer very different scales of performance."],"schutz-mit-tranen-saen":["Four minutes, five voices, no independent instruments. The text is from Psalm 126: they that sow in tears shall reap in joy. Schutz sets the two halves as opposites. The sowing is slow, the lines droop downward and overlap so the texture never quite clears; the reaping breaks into quick running figures that climb. The turn between them is the whole piece.","It comes from Geistliche Chormusik, published in 1648, the year the Peace of Westphalia finally ended the Thirty Years' War. In the preface Schutz complained that young German composers were rushing after the fashionable Italian concerted style with instruments and soloists, and neglecting the discipline of strict vocal counterpoint. He offered the collection as a corrective and a teaching book.","The man who had brought the Venetian style to Germany forty years earlier here writes music with no obligatory instruments at all, deliberately in the older manner. It is a polemic disguised as a psalm setting. Notice that the sowing and reaping music moves in literally opposite directions, down and then up, so the theology is built into the shape.","Geistliche Chormusik is usually treated as the summit of German motet writing before Bach, whose own motets descend from it directly. Critics read the preface as the defence of craft by an old man in a ruined country. Modern performances split over the optional continuo and over whether to use one voice per part or a full choir.","Follow the bass line through the slow opening, then listen for the exact moment the running notes begin. Play Brahms's German Requiem afterwards: its second movement sets these same words, two centuries later, to music that clearly remembers this tradition. Small ensembles suit it better than large choirs."],"schutz-saul-saul-was-verfolgst-du-mich":["Four minutes built on one sentence: Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me, the voice that stops Saul on the road to Damascus. It begins as a murmur from two basses, spreads upward through six soloists, and is then taken up by two additional choirs placed apart from the main group until the whole building seems to be saying the name. Then it recedes to a whisper and ends almost inaudibly.","Published in Symphoniae sacrae III, Dresden, 1650, two years after the war ended. The electoral chapel that had been reduced to a handful of underfed musicians was being rebuilt, and Schutz, in his mid-sixties, could finally write for large forces again. The layout of favoured soloists plus separated ripieno choirs is Venetian technique, learned from Giovanni Gabrieli four decades earlier and never forgotten.","The dynamics are written into the score, from soft to loud and back, which was still unusual in 1650. Schutz is notating a shape, not just notes: a sound that grows out of nothing, fills the space and withdraws. Count how many times the name Saul is repeated; the Bible says it twice, and the music says it far more, because the terror is in the repetition.","It is routinely named the finest German sacred concerto before Bach, and it depends absolutely on performance. Recordings that ignore the spatial separation, or that start too loud and leave nowhere to go, flatten it. Debate continues over how many singers the ripieno choirs need, since Schutz's own resources varied enormously between war and peace.","Use headphones or a recording with genuine spatial separation, and start it quiet enough that the opening is barely there. Listen for the moment the second and third choirs enter and the sound suddenly has depth. Then hear Gabrieli's In ecclesiis to find where the technique came from."],"schutz-selig-sind-toten":["Six minutes, six voices, from the Book of Revelation: blessed are the dead who die in the Lord, for they rest from their labours. The lines are long and unhurried, entering one after another so that the texture is never empty and never crowded. On the word for rest, one or two voices hold still while the others continue moving underneath, so stillness and motion happen at the same time.","From Geistliche Chormusik, 1648. Schutz was sixty-three. His wife had died in 1625 and both his daughters before him; the war had killed perhaps a third of the population of the German lands and repeatedly emptied the Dresden chapel he was employed to run. He had spent the previous decade writing on a small scale because there was nobody left to sing anything larger.","He had set these same words twelve years earlier, as the closing music of the Musikalische Exequien, sung by a distant trio of angel voices. This version is plainer, slower and closer to the ground, the same text re-heard after another decade of war. Hearing the two together is like reading the same sentence in two different handwritings.","Brahms owned and studied Schutz, and set the same passage as the final movement of his German Requiem, which has kept this motet in view. Modern editions restore the optional continuo that nineteenth-century performers dropped. The main interpretive question is tempo: taken too slowly it stalls, and the long lines need to feel like breath rather than ceremony.","Play the ending of the Musikalische Exequien and then this, in that order, and hear the same words twice from the same man. Listen for the held notes on the word Ruhe, rest, against the voices that keep moving. A small ensemble singing in a real church acoustic beats a big choir in a studio."],"lully-marche-pour-ceremonie-des-turcs":["Under three minutes of deliberately crude ceremony. Oboes and bassoons hammer a short repeated figure over drums while the strings keep the same stiff rhythm going without development or relief. It is designed not to go anywhere. In the play, this is the music for a fake Turkish initiation ceremony in which a gullible social climber is dressed up and made a nonsense nobleman by men speaking invented gibberish.","Le Bourgeois gentilhomme was staged at Chambord in October 1670 for Louis XIV, a comedy-ballet with a text by Moliere and music by Lully, who also acted in it. The Turkish material was topical: an Ottoman envoy had visited the French court the previous year and had conspicuously failed to be impressed by its magnificence, and the king enjoyed seeing the embarrassment turned into farce.","The exoticism is cheap on purpose. The scale, the drone, the thudding drums and the pretend Turkish language are all obviously fake, because the joke is not on Turkey but on Monsieur Jourdain, who cannot tell. Lully wrote deliberately bad music of a kind he was perfectly capable of writing well, which is a harder trick than it sounds.","The Lully and Moliere partnership ended in 1672, when Lully secured a royal monopoly over sung theatre in France and effectively legislated his collaborator out of business. The comedy-ballet as a form barely survived them. Modern revivals with dancers, spoken text and period band, notably by William Christie's Les Arts Florissants, have restored the piece to its theatrical context.","Watch or listen to it with the scene rather than alone, so the absurdity registers. Notice how little the music changes across three minutes. Then play Mozart's Rondo alla Turca and hear the same European fantasy of Turkish drums and clatter, a hundred and thirteen years later and no more accurate."],"lully-armide-passacaille":["Nine minutes of variations over a short repeating bass in swinging triple time, the longest continuous instrumental structure in French opera to that date. Small groups of solo strings and oboes alternate with the full band, and halfway through the chorus joins, so what began as a dance becomes a scene with voices. The pattern never stops turning; it simply gathers more people as it goes.","Armide opened at the Academie Royale de Musique in Paris in February 1686, with a libretto by Philippe Quinault after Tasso's crusader epic. Louis XIV, who had ordered the subject himself, never saw it: he had stopped attending the opera under the influence of Madame de Maintenon and a newly pious court. It was Lully's last completed tragedy in music, and he died the following year.","The passacaille is the sorceress's illusion made audible. A bass that repeats without end is exactly the right music for an enchantment that traps whoever enters it, and the increasing splendour of the variations is the trap closing. Notice that the entry of the chorus does not break the pattern; the voices are captured by it too.","Armide became the most admired French opera of the age and stayed a reference point for a century. Rameau analysed its recitative in print as a model of correct declamation; Rousseau attacked the very same monologue during the quarrel over French and Italian style. Both sides used the same scene as proof, which says something about how completely it dominated French taste.","Count the bass pattern going round, then stop counting and let it work. Listen for the moment the chorus enters and the texture thickens. Then play the Phaeton chaconne for the same technique on a smaller scale. Period-instrument recordings at low French pitch, from Christie or Herreweghe, get the darkness right."],"lully-te-deum":["Half an hour of state ceremony in sound. The music alternates a small group of soloists, the petit choeur, with the massed grand choeur and full band, so the texture swings between intimacy and blazing tutti every few minutes. Trumpets and drums punctuate throughout, and the dotted, stately rhythms are the same ones Lully used to open his operas, imported into church.","First performed in 1677 at the baptism of Lully's own son, with Louis XIV standing as godfather, which tells you how far a Florentine miller's son had climbed. It was revived in January 1687 to celebrate the king's recovery from surgery. At that performance Lully, beating time by pounding a long staff on the floor, struck his foot. The wound became gangrenous and killed him in March.","The staff is the reason anyone remembers this piece, and it is also a genuine piece of history: before the modern baton, French conductors kept a large ensemble together by audible thumping, so the beat was part of the sound the audience heard. He refused amputation, reportedly because he wanted to keep dancing, and died of the infection.","The anecdote has swallowed the music. Comparisons with Charpentier's Te Deum, written some fifteen years later, are inevitable and usually go against Lully, whose grandeur can seem more official than felt. Period-instrument recordings since the 1980s, by Christie, Niquet and others, have made a better case for it by restoring the sharp French articulation and low pitch.","Listen for the drums and the swing between small and large groups; that alternation is the piece's whole architecture. Then play Charpentier's Te Deum prelude and hear a contemporary do something lighter and more memorable with the same trumpets. Do not skip the quiet solo sections, which are where the music actually breathes."],"lully-phaeton-chaconne":["Six minutes of variations over a short repeating bass, danced on stage by the retinue of the Sun. Trios of oboes step forward and the full string band answers, so the music breathes in and out between three players and thirty. The five-part string writing gives it a distinctive thickness in the middle, quite unlike the brighter, thinner Italian sound of the same decade.","Premiered at Versailles in January 1683 with a libretto by Quinault, and nicknamed the people's opera for its popularity in Paris afterwards. The story is Ovid's: the boy who insists on driving the sun chariot, loses control and is destroyed to save the world. Performing that particular myth at the court of the Sun King was either extremely brave or a compliment nobody was allowed to examine.","The alternation of trios with full band is the signature of Louis XIV's orchestra. His twenty-four violins were divided into five parts, with the inner voices filled out by less senior players, producing a dense, dark middle register that no other European band had. When you hear that particular richness, you are hearing an institution, not just a scoring choice.","Lully's operas vanished from the stage after the Revolution and stayed gone for two centuries. Their return since the 1980s has been an argument about detail: French unequal rhythm, where written-equal notes are played long-short; ornament conventions; and a performing pitch roughly a whole tone below modern, which darkens everything.","Listen for the oboe trios stepping out of the band and back into it, and follow the bass pattern turning underneath. Then play the Armide passacaille to hear the same technique stretched further, and Pachelbel's Canon for a German taking the idea somewhere quite different."],"buxtehude-passacaglia-d-buxwv-161":["Six minutes for organ over a four-note bass in the pedals, repeated twenty-eight times. The variations come in four groups of seven, and between the groups the music pauses and shifts to a new key before the pattern starts again. Above the bass the hands build from single quiet lines to running figuration, then withdraw. The pedal ostinato is audible throughout, which makes the whole structure followable by ear.","Buxtehude ran music at the Marienkirche in Lubeck, a wealthy Baltic trading city, where he inherited the Abendmusiken: free evening concerts in Advent, paid for by merchant subscription, open to the public and famous across northern Europe. The church had large organs by the great north German builders, with independent pedal towers and bright reeds, and the organist was expected to improvise as much as he played.","Four groups of seven make twenty-eight, the days of a lunar month, and the key changes fall exactly where the moon's quarters would. Some scholars read the piece as a musical image of the moon, appropriate for evening music in Advent. The reading cannot be proved, but the numbers are not in dispute, and Buxtehude's generation took such structures seriously.","Bach's Passacaglia in C minor is the obvious descendant and usually overshadows this. Performers argue about registration, whether the pedal ostinato should be on a reed stop throughout or change colour with the sections, and about tempo, since the piece can be played as a stately procession or as something much more restless. Old unequal tunings sharpen the key changes considerably.","Count seven variations, notice the key change, then do it again three times; the shape becomes obvious quickly and the piece is far easier to follow than its length suggests. Then play Bach's Passacaglia and hear what a pupil did with the idea. Seek a recording on a historic north German instrument."],"buxtehude-toccata-f-buxwv-156":["Seven minutes for organ in five or six abrupt sections that do not blend into each other. Free running scales and broken chords alternate with strict fugues, and near the beginning the pedals get a solo passage of their own, the feet playing fast figures with the hands silent. The key is a bright F major, and the whole piece sounds like someone thinking out loud at speed.","This is the north German stylus phantasticus, the fantastic style, written for instruments built by Arp Schnitger, Friedrich Stellwagen and their rivals: bright, reedy, with genuinely independent pedal divisions that could carry a solo line. Buxtehude's duties at Lubeck included improvising before and after the sermon and, less romantically, managing the church's finances, at which he was reportedly very good.","The theorist Athanasius Kircher had described the fantastic style in 1650 as music bound by nothing: no text, no dance rhythm, no fixed metre, answerable only to the player's invention. Pieces like this are essentially improvisations that got written down, which is why the joins are abrupt. The rough edges are the evidence of how they were made.","Bach walked some 250 miles from Arnstadt to Lubeck in 1705 to hear Buxtehude, stayed four months instead of four weeks and was formally reprimanded on his return. Most of Buxtehude's organ music survives only in manuscript copies made by Bach's circle, which means the texts are secondhand and the authorship of a few pieces is genuinely uncertain.","Listen for the joins between sections and count them; the abruptness is the style, not a flaw. Pay attention to the pedal solo, which is the moment the feet take over. A recording on a historic instrument in unequal temperament, where some chords sound noticeably rougher than others, is much more interesting than a smooth modern organ."],"buxtehude-membra-jesu-nostri-ad-cor":["Ten minutes, the sixth of seven small cantatas addressed in turn to the feet, knees, hands, side, breast, heart and face of the crucified Christ. This one begins with an instrumental sonata, then a chorus, three arias and the chorus again. Its distinguishing feature is the scoring: five viols instead of violins, which replaces brightness with a soft, veiled, slightly grainy sound throughout.","Buxtehude assembled the cycle in 1680 and sent a copy to Gustav Duben, music director to the Swedish court, whose enormous personal collection is the reason it survives at all. The texts come from a medieval devotional poem long attributed to Bernard of Clairvaux, and the practice of meditating on the individual wounds was as common among Lutherans as among Catholics.","The instruments are the argument. Every other cantata in the cycle uses violins; the heart gets viols, the gentlest consort available, so the softest sound in the building is reserved for the softest organ of the body. The central aria then quotes the Song of Songs, you have wounded my heart, folding erotic love poetry into a meditation on the crucifixion without apology.","It is often called the first Lutheran oratorio cycle, though the label is loose. Its survival in the Duben collection at Uppsala, rather than in Germany, is a reminder of how much north German music we have lost. Performance questions concern forces: one voice per part or small choir, and whether the viol consort should be joined by organ or theorbo continuo.","Start here rather than at the beginning of the cycle, because the viol sound is the easiest way in, then go back and listen to all seven in order at a single sitting, roughly an hour. Notice how the instrumental sonata sets the mood before a word is sung. Recordings by Herreweghe and Suzuki take very different views of the tempo."],"buxtehude-ciacona-e":["Five minutes for organ over a short descending bass in the pedals, repeated some thirty times. The variations grow steadily: quiet single lines at first, then denser figuration, then full organ with the pedal line still audible underneath. Unlike the Passacaglia, this piece does not change key; it simply intensifies, which makes it feel shorter and more direct.","Written for the Lubeck organs and probably used both in services and at the Abendmusik concerts. Buxtehude produced three works of this kind, two ciaconas and a passacaglia, which is the only such group by any composer before Bach. In an age when organists mostly improvised, choosing to write down a strict variation set was itself a statement about permanence.","The distinction between ciacona and passacaglia was already blurred by 1700 and both words meant, in practice, variations over a short repeating bass. What separates these pieces is not terminology but treatment: the E minor Ciacona keeps one key and builds pressure, the D minor Passacaglia keeps moving house. Hearing them together shows a composer testing two solutions to the same problem.","Attribution is secure, which is not true of everything under his name. Organists debate registration, since the score gives none, and a few play the piece on pedal harpsichord, which is historically defensible for domestic practice and sounds startlingly different. It is a favourite recital opener because the structure is graspable on first hearing.","Follow the pedal line and count the repetitions until you lose track, which is the point at which the piece has taken over. Then play the D minor Passacaglia and notice how differently the same device behaves when the key is allowed to shift. Historic instruments in old tuning suit the E minor especially well."],"buxtehude-prelude-g-buxwv-149":["Seven minutes in five stretches: an opening flourish with pedal, a fugue, a free interlude, a second fugue, and a closing flourish. The two fugues are the interesting part, because their subjects are recognisably the same idea in different rhythms, so the piece argues with itself twice using the same material. Everything in between is deliberately loose, fast and unmeasured.","The north German praeludium was a whole genre in itself, not a short introduction to something else. An organist alone in a cold, dark church before or after the sermon was expected to fill time impressively, and these written-out examples preserve what that sounded like. Buxtehude's fame drew visitors from across Germany and Scandinavia specifically to hear it.","Compare the two fugue subjects: the second is the first re-metred, the same shape walking at a different gait. This is variation-fugue technique, and Bach later made a career of it, most obviously in works that spin an entire structure from one transformed idea. Buxtehude is doing it casually, inside what is essentially an improvisation.","Modern editions often split these praeludia into separate movements labelled prelude and fugue, which imposes a later shape on music that was conceived as one continuous flow. Bach did effectively make that separation in his own works, so the editorial habit has a genealogy, but it obscures how the north German pieces actually behave.","Listen for the second fugue subject and try to hear the first one inside it; once you catch the resemblance the whole piece clicks together. Then play a Bach prelude and fugue and notice how much more clearly the seams have been tidied. A bright historic organ with a strong pedal division is essential."],"charpentier-te-deum-prelude":["Under two minutes, and shaped as a rondeau: a bright trumpet-and-drum refrain that keeps returning, separated by shorter, quieter episodes for strings and oboes. The refrain is a marching tune in D major with a snap in its rhythm, scored so the trumpets sit on top of everything. It opens a half-hour setting of the Te Deum, the church's great hymn of public thanksgiving.","Probably written around 1692 for a Te Deum celebrating a French military victory, most likely Steenkerque. Charpentier had studied in Rome with Giacomo Carissimi and brought the Italian oratorio back to Paris, where he was music director at the Jesuit church of Saint-Louis. Barred from the court by Lully's monopoly, he built a career in churches and private households instead, which turned out to suit him.","Since 1954 this refrain has opened Eurovision broadcasts of the European Broadcasting Union, which is why hundreds of millions of people recognise it as the sound of a live transmission from Europe. The manuscript had only recently resurfaced. A piece written to thank God for killing Frenchmen's enemies now announces a song contest, which is not the worst fate for military music.","Charpentier was forgotten for two centuries, then recovered wholesale: his twenty-eight volumes of autograph manuscripts, the Melanges, entered the Bibliotheque nationale and were catalogued by H. Wiley Hitchcock in the 1980s, giving scholars an unusually complete picture of a Baroque composer's output. The revival has steadily moved him from Lully's shadow toward equal standing.","Do not stop after the famous two minutes; the rest of the Te Deum has the best music, especially the quieter solo sections. Listen for the refrain returning between episodes, which is what makes it a rondeau. Christie and Les Arts Florissants set the modern standard for it."],"charpentier-messe-minuit-pour-noel":["A complete Latin mass lasting about half an hour, in which nearly every section is built from a French Christmas carol. Ten different noels are woven in: the Kyrie alone uses three. Between the sung movements, flutes and strings play instrumental settings of further carols. The scoring is light, with flutes lending a pastoral colour, and the whole work moves at the pace of a village procession rather than a cathedral rite.","Written in Paris in the 1690s for one of the churches Charpentier served. Midnight mass at Christmas drew enormous crowds, including people who attended nothing else all year, and building the service out of tunes everyone already sang was both good theology and good crowd management. The noels themselves were street and church songs of long standing: Joseph est bien marie, Une jeune pucelle, Or nous dites Marie.","The congregation of 1694 would have recognised each tune as it arrived, the way a modern audience catches a quotation in a film score, so the mass is a sequence of small recognitions. The Agnus Dei returns to the same carol that opened the Kyrie, closing the circle. Nothing about this is explained in the score, because nothing needed explaining at the time.","It has become a Christmas concert staple, which flatters and distorts it: performed as a concert work it loses the alternation with spoken liturgy and organ playing it was designed for. Scholars disagree about where the instrumental noels belong and how much organ music should be interleaved. Charpentier's use of popular tunes in a Latin mass was unremarkable then and charming now.","Find a list of the ten carols and follow along, marking each as it appears; this converts a pleasant half-hour into a treasure hunt. Listen for the flutes, which carry the pastoral sound. Then play Josquin's Missa Pange lingua to hear the same borrowing technique two centuries earlier."],"charpentier-marche-triomphe":["Three minutes of processional music, usually paired in performance with a short trumpet air that follows it in the manuscript. Dotted rhythms, a drum tattoo underneath, trumpets carrying the tune and oboes doubling: it is designed to be walked to, not sat through, and its phrases are short enough to be started and stopped whenever a ceremony requires. Nothing develops; it simply proceeds.","Charpentier spent much of his career supplying music for Parisian church ceremony, first for the private household of Mademoiselle de Guise and later for the Jesuits at Saint-Louis and finally the Sainte-Chapelle. Feast days meant processions, and processions meant trumpets. Paris maintained corps of trumpeters for exactly this, and pieces like this one were working equipment rather than concert works.","The manuscript gives no occasion, no date and no fixed pairing, so performers order and combine the marches as they like. That looseness is authentic: this is functional music that could be extended or cut on the day. If you hear two recordings that seem to contain different pieces, both may be right.","It survives among the Melanges, the vast autograph collection Hitchcock catalogued, and owes its modern popularity partly to weddings and partly to its proximity to the famous Te Deum. Nobody claims it is major Charpentier. It is valuable as evidence of what French ceremonial sounded like when the composer was not trying to impress posterity.","Play it as it was meant to be used, as something people walk to, and notice how the phrases could be repeated indefinitely. Then hear Purcell's Trumpet Tune for the English equivalent, written for the same kind of occasion by a man of the same generation across the Channel."],"charpentier-litanies-vierge":["Twelve minutes of near-continuous repetition. The Litany of Loreto is a long chain of titles for the Virgin, each answered by the same three words, pray for us, dozens of times over. Charpentier scores it for six voices set unusually low, with two viols and continuo, so there is no bright soprano brilliance anywhere. The answer comes back the same in words and different in harmony, every single time.","Almost certainly written for the household of Mademoiselle de Guise, where Charpentier lived and worked for some seventeen years as composer to a private musical establishment of around fifteen singers and players. He sang in it himself, as a high tenor. Music of this kind was for a chapel with a few dozen listeners, not a public church, which explains the intimate scale and the dark scoring.","The low voicing is the interpretive key. By refusing high, radiant sonority for a Marian text, where radiance is the obvious choice, Charpentier turns public praise into something that sounds like private prayer said in the dark. Then listen to how the repeated response is reharmonised each time, so the ear never gets to settle into a pattern.","Because the Melanges preserve so much of the Guise repertoire, scholars have been able to reconstruct the ensemble almost singer by singer, an unusual level of detail for the period. The Litanies have been championed in recordings by Christie and Herreweghe, and are now among the pieces most often cited when arguing that Charpentier is the finer composer of sacred music of his generation.","Let the repetition do its work rather than waiting for events; twelve minutes in, it will have changed how the room feels. Listen in low light. Then play Gorecki's Totus Tuus, another piece that repeats a Marian text until repetition becomes the subject, three centuries later."],"corelli-christmas-concerto-op-6-no-8":["Fifteen minutes in six short movements rather than the usual three, several of them barely a minute long, with abrupt changes of tempo between them. A trio of soloists, two violins and a cello, steps in and out of the full string band throughout. The famous ending is the Pastorale: a swaying tune in lilting compound time over a held drone, marked in the score as optional.","Corelli spent his career in Rome, from the 1690s leading the orchestra of Cardinal Pietro Ottoboni at the Palazzo della Cancelleria, an ensemble that on big occasions ran to a hundred players or more. The score carries the heading that it was made for the night of Christmas. The twelve concertos of Op. 6 were published in Amsterdam in 1714, a year after his death.","The drone under the Pastorale imitates the zampogna, the bagpipe that shepherds from the Abruzzi hills brought into Rome every December, playing in the streets before Christmas. Corelli was copying a sound his listeners heard outside their windows each winter. The movement is marked ad libitum, which means the concerto can end without it, and some performances take him at his word.","Op. 6 became the model for the whole concerto grosso tradition, directly behind Handel's own Op. 6 and Geminiani's arrangements. The Christmas label has made this the one Corelli piece the general public knows. The liveliest current debate is about scale: Corelli's Roman orchestras were vastly larger than the eight or ten players most modern recordings use.","Go to the Pastorale first, feel the drone, then start the concerto from the beginning so the ending arrives properly. Notice how short and abrupt the middle movements are. Then play the Pifa from Handel's Messiah, which is the same shepherds' music borrowed by an admirer fifty years later."],"corelli-folia-variations":["Eleven minutes for one violin and continuo: twenty-three variations on a sixteen-bar harmonic pattern that everyone in Europe already knew. The theme is stated as slow, grave chords, then the violin works through every technique in its vocabulary in turn, running scales, leaping arpeggios, chains of double stops, and back to slow lament, before the final variations drive to a close.","Published in Rome on the first day of 1700 as the last item in the Op. 5 violin sonatas, dedicated to Sophie Charlotte, Electress of Brandenburg. The folia was an old Iberian dance formula with a fixed chord sequence, already 150 years old and already set by dozens of composers, so Corelli was contributing to a shared European tradition rather than inventing anything.","The same skeleton runs through settings by Marais, Vivaldi, Alessandro Scarlatti and, two centuries later, Rachmaninoff, who called his set Variations on a Theme of Corelli despite the theme being nobody's in particular. Corelli's own contribution is pedagogical: the twenty-three variations amount to a syllabus of everything he taught, arranged in ascending order of difficulty.","Op. 5 was reprinted more than forty times during the eighteenth century, an astonishing figure. A 1710 Amsterdam edition printed elaborate ornaments claiming to show how Corelli himself played the slow movements, and whether those graces are genuine or a publisher's invention is the central question in Baroque ornamentation studies. Nobody has settled it.","Listen to the theme twice so the pattern is in your ear, then track how far each variation departs before the harmony pulls it back. Compare a period violinist with gut strings against a modern one; the difference in attack is dramatic. Then find any other composer's folia and hear the same bones underneath."],"corelli-concerto-grosso-op-6-no-4":["Eleven minutes in four movements: a slow introduction that runs into a brisk Allegro, a short Adagio, a dancing Vivace and a final Allegro. The whole design rests on one contrast, three soloists against the full band, alternating constantly, sometimes every couple of bars. There is no thematic development in the later sense; the interest is entirely in texture, in bodies of sound stepping forward and back.","Corelli worked in Rome under two cardinal patrons, Pamphili and then Ottoboni, in palaces with long galleries and hard surfaces where the difference between three players and forty was physically audible. He published very little and revised endlessly. The Op. 6 concertos appeared posthumously in Amsterdam in 1714 from the press of Estienne Roger, the great music publisher of northern Europe.","Corelli marks the orchestral parts as optional: they may be doubled by many players or omitted altogether. The same music therefore works as a grand concerto or as an intimate trio sonata, depending on who turns up. It is scalable, which is one reason the collection spread so far so fast, and a useful corrective to the idea of a fixed authentic performance.","Op. 6 defined the concerto grosso for a generation; Handel's own Op. 6, written in a month in 1739, is a direct homage. Modern debate concerns Corelli's very slow rate of harmonic change, heard by some as serene and by others as static, and the balance question of how large the ripieno should be, given that the Roman originals were enormous.","Count how few different ideas he uses and how much he gets from them. Listen specifically for the moments the full band re-enters after the trio, which is where the effect lives. Then play a Brandenburg Concerto and hear how much busier the German version of the same idea became."],"corelli-concerto-grosso-op-6-no-1":["Thirteen minutes that refuse to divide into tidy movements. The piece runs through seven linked sections with abrupt changes of tempo, some lasting under a minute, so the listener is repeatedly stopped and restarted. Its finest stretch is a long slow passage in which the two solo violins hang suspended notes against each other and release them one at a time, a chain of small tensions unwinding.","The opening concerto of the collection published in Amsterdam in 1714. Corelli had died the previous January, wealthy, celebrated and buried in the Pantheon, an honour granted to almost no musician. He had spent decades in Rome as the most imitated violinist in Europe, running Ottoboni's Monday concerts and teaching the players who carried his style across the continent.","The sectional structure is old-fashioned even for 1714: it looks back to the seventeenth-century canzona, with its chain of contrasting blocks, rather than forward to the three-movement concerto Vivaldi was about to standardise. Corelli also mixes church and chamber manners inside one work, which the theorists of the day considered separate categories.","Charles Burney and the eighteenth century revered Corelli for exactly what a later age found dull: restraint, slow harmonic motion, no surprises. The story that he polished Op. 6 for decades before allowing publication is probably exaggerated but reflects a genuine reputation for fastidiousness. Period-instrument performance has restored the sharpness his music needs to avoid sounding merely pleasant.","Go to the slow section and follow just the two solo violins, listening for each suspended note rubbing before it resolves. Then notice how many separate sections the piece actually contains. A large-scale recording using twenty or more strings comes closer to Roman practice than the usual chamber-sized band."],"corelli-trio-sonata-op-3-no-2":["Nine minutes in four movements alternating slow and fast, with no dance titles because this is a church sonata rather than a chamber one. The two violins work a step apart almost throughout, one holding while the other moves, so the music proceeds as a continuous series of small frictions and releases. Underneath, cello and organ supply a bass that walks steadily and rarely surprises.","Published in Rome in 1689 and dedicated to Francesco II d'Este, Duke of Modena. The trio sonata was the standard chamber medium of Europe for well over a century, the equivalent of the string quartet later on: two treble instruments, a bass instrument and a keyboard, playable by any competent household or church ensemble anywhere on the continent.","Two things worth noticing. The violins almost never cross each other, so the first violin stays permanently above the second, a hierarchy Corelli maintains with unusual strictness. And a trio sonata requires four players, since the bass line is realised by both a melody instrument and a keyboard; the trio refers to the written parts, not the personnel.","Corelli's Op. 1 to Op. 4 did more than any theoretical treatise to teach Europe functional harmony. The chains of falling fifths that fill these sonatas became the textbook progressions of the next two centuries, absorbed by every composer who learned from the prints. Music theory in a real sense was reverse-engineered from what Corelli did by instinct.","Follow only the two violins and listen for the moment in each phrase when the held note rubs against the moving one, then releases. Once you hear it, you will hear it everywhere in Baroque music. Then play Bach's Concerto for Two Violins, where the same conversation is amplified to concerto scale."],"corelli-violin-sonata-op-5-no-1":["Twelve minutes for violin with cello and keyboard. It opens with a Grave that keeps starting, stopping and starting again, as though the player were testing the room. The second section is a fugue in which the violin plays two lines at once using double stops, arguing with itself while the continuo keeps out of the way. Faster movements follow, ending in a brisk running finale.","Published in Rome on 1 January 1700, the opening item of a set of twelve. The first six sonatas are in the serious church manner, the next five are suites of dances, and the twelfth is the folia variations. Corelli had trained in Bologna, then Europe's violin capital, and taught in Rome; his pupils, including Geminiani and Locatelli, exported the style everywhere.","The self-accompanying fugue is the technical heart of the piece: one instrument, two voices, sustained by bowing two strings at once. Corelli was famously restrained as a player, reportedly staying within the lower positions and prizing tone over acrobatics, so this display of contrapuntal control rather than speed is exactly the kind of virtuosity he approved of.","The 1710 Roger edition, which printed decorated versions of the slow movements as Corelli supposedly played them, is either the most valuable ornamentation document of the age or a marketing device; the argument runs on. The sonatas served as teaching material for two centuries, and Geminiani turned several into concertos, which kept them in circulation long after taste moved on.","Listen to the fugue and try to follow both voices at once; failing is normal and hearing the attempt is the pleasure. Then compare a recording that plays the slow movement plain against one using the 1710 ornaments, and decide which you believe. Bach's solo violin writing twenty years later grew from this soil."],"pachelbel-canon-d":["Five minutes for three violins and a bass. The bass plays eight notes, then plays them again, twenty-eight times in all, never changing. Above it the first violin begins a melody; two bars later the second violin starts the same melody, and two bars after that the third. Everything the second and third violins play has already been heard. The variations get busier toward the middle, then thin out.","Neither the date nor the occasion is known, and the only source is a nineteenth-century manuscript copy, so even the notes we play are at one remove. Pachelbel worked in Vienna, Eisenach, Erfurt, Stuttgart, Gotha and finally Nuremberg, where he was born. In Eisenach and Erfurt he was close to the Bach family: he taught Johann Christoph Bach, who in turn taught his younger brother Johann Sebastian.","It is a strict canon at the unison, meaning the harmony is generated entirely by delay: three identical lines, offset in time, produce every chord in the piece without anyone choosing them. That is a genuinely severe compositional discipline hiding inside the most relaxed music imaginable, and the fastest variation sits almost exactly at the midpoint.","Nobody had heard of it until Gustav Beckmann printed it in 1919, and it stayed obscure until Jean-Francois Paillard's 1968 recording, which added romantic phrasing and a slow tempo and made the piece inescapable in weddings, films and advertising. Early-music players object that the Gigue printed alongside it implies a brisker pace, and that most performances are far too slow and too sweet.","Listen once following only the bass, which is doing exactly one thing forever, and once following only the third violin, which is always two bars behind. Then let the recording keep playing into the Gigue, which almost nobody hears. A period-instrument version at a walking tempo is a genuine surprise."],"pachelbel-chaconne-f":["Eight minutes for organ: twenty-two variations over a slow four-bar bass in the pedals. It begins almost bare, a single line above the pattern, and thickens by degrees until the figuration is continuous, then withdraws again at the end. The key is the point. F minor on a Baroque organ is a dark, uncomfortable place, and the piece sounds like it is being played somewhere cold.","Pachelbel returned to Nuremberg in 1695 as organist of St Sebaldus, the church of his boyhood, a post he held until his death. He was a Lutheran organist in a Protestant merchant city, writing chorale preludes that became teaching material across central Germany. Roughly two hundred of them survive, and his pupils carried the style into the next generation, including into the Bach household.","Organs of the period were tuned in unequal temperaments that made some keys sweet and others harsh. In F minor several chords come out audibly rough, with intervals a modern ear registers as wrong. Choosing that key was therefore an expressive decision, not a neutral one, and the piece loses much of its bite on a modern equally tuned instrument.","It is usually named his finest keyboard work and was championed by the nineteenth-century organ revival, which valued its seriousness at a time when the rest of Pachelbel looked lightweight. The obvious comparison is Bach's Passacaglia in C minor. Recent performers have argued for restoring the historic tunings that make the harmonic discomfort audible again.","Find a recording on a historic organ in unequal temperament and listen for the chords that sound sour; then hear the same piece on a modern instrument and notice what has been smoothed away. Follow the pedal line throughout. Bach's Passacaglia afterwards will sound like the same idea with a much bigger engine."],"pachelbel-gigue-d":["Two minutes of fast dance in lilting compound time, for the same three violins and bass as the Canon it follows in the manuscript. Here the violins imitate each other freely rather than in strict canon, entering, overlapping and dropping out. It is brisk, cheerful and over before it can outstay itself, and it exists chiefly as the half of the pair that nobody plays.","The single surviving source gives the two pieces together under one heading, Canon and Gigue, which is the strongest evidence that Pachelbel conceived them as a pair for the same players on the same occasion, probably a domestic or courtly entertainment. Nothing else is known about when or why, and even the manuscript is a much later copy.","The pairing is a demonstration. The Canon builds everything from one melody chasing itself in strict, unbreakable delay; the Gigue builds from the same principle applied loosely, with the imitation free to bend. Heard together they are two answers to the same question of how much music you can generate from a single line, which is why leaving one out weakens both.","It is almost never programmed, and recordings that include it are a minority even among period-instrument groups. That neglect has shaped how the Canon is heard: taken alone, it becomes a slow standalone mood piece, whereas the presence of a fast dance after it implies the Canon was an introduction to something and should move accordingly.","The simplest experiment in this catalogue: play the Canon and do not stop the recording. Two minutes later you will have heard the piece Pachelbel wrote next, and the Canon will sound different in retrospect. Choose a period-instrument recording, since modern string groups tend to omit the Gigue altogether."],"pachelbel-aria-sebaldina":["Eight minutes: a plain, songlike theme in F minor followed by eight variations. The theme could be a hymn tune, slow and evenly paced, and Pachelbel varies it without ever burying it, so the melody stays audible under increasingly decorated surfaces. The variations grow denser toward the end and then the theme returns essentially as it began, which gives the whole set a quiet, circular shape.","It is the sixth and last piece in Hexachordum Apollinis, published in Nuremberg in 1699: six arias with variations, each in a different key. The name honours St Sebaldus, the church where Pachelbel was organist. He dedicated the collection jointly to Dieterich Buxtehude in Lubeck and Ferdinand Tobias Richter in Vienna, the leading organists of the Protestant north and the Catholic south.","That double dedication is a statement of position: Pachelbel is placing himself deliberately between the wild north German fantasists and the polished Viennese court style, claiming both inheritances. The title is a pun as well, since a hexachord is a six-note scale and the six arias run through six keys, so the collection is named after its own architecture.","Hexachordum Apollinis is the best preserved of his prints and the easiest place to meet him as something other than the Canon's author. Harpsichordists and organists both claim it, since the print does not specify, and the choice changes the piece completely: sustained and churchly on organ, articulate and intimate on harpsichord. Neither reading is wrong.","Hear it once on harpsichord and once on organ, and notice how differently the same variations behave when notes can or cannot be held. Keep the theme in mind and try to find it inside each variation. Then play the Aria of Bach's Goldberg Variations, written forty years later on the same principle."],"purcell-dido-s-lament":["The closing scene of the hour-long opera Dido and Aeneas. A short recitative, 'Thy hand, Belinda', hands the abandoned queen down a falling scale into the aria proper, 'When I am laid in earth'. Beneath her the cellos and bass repeat one slow sinking phrase, over and over, five bars long, eleven times without variation. She sings 'Remember me' four times, each higher and quieter. The strings then play her off in a short instrumental epilogue.","Purcell was organist of Westminster Abbey and of the Chapel Royal, writing coronation anthems one week and tavern catches the next. Dido was performed in 1689 at a boarding school for girls in Chelsea run by the dancing-master Josias Priest, with a libretto by Nahum Tate after Virgil. England had almost no tradition of continuously sung opera; the fashion was for spoken plays with musical scenes. This one hour of through-composed tragedy is an outlier in its own country.","The bass line does not simply repeat, it descends by half-steps, the old European sign for grief, and it never pauses. Purcell then makes the voice refuse to line up with it: Dido's phrases begin and end in the wrong places, so singer and bass are always slightly out of step. Only in the final 'Remember me' do they meet, and by then she has stopped asking for anything.","The opera dropped out of the theatre for almost two hundred years and was revived in 1895 for Purcell's bicentenary. No score in Purcell's hand survives; the earliest source dates from the 1770s and is missing the prologue's music, so every modern performance is partly a reconstruction. Scholars still argue whether the Chelsea school performance was the premiere or a revival of something written earlier for the court.","Ignore the voice for the first minute and follow only the bass, then listen again for the way the singer keeps arriving late. Compare Janet Baker's slow, weighted recording with a period-band version at brisker speed and lighter tone. If you want the shock of context, hear it after the cheerful sailors' chorus that comes just before it."],"purcell-music-funeral-queen-mary":["A processional sequence rather than a single piece: a solemn march for trumpets and muffled drums, a short instrumental canzona, and three funeral sentences for choir with organ, the last of them 'Thou knowest, Lord, the secrets of our hearts'. The march is built from four chords and almost no melody, which is the point; the choral writing is plain, chordal and slow, the words set so that every syllable of the English is audible in a large stone building.","Queen Mary II died of smallpox in December 1694, aged thirty-two. Her funeral took place at Westminster Abbey in March 1695, in bitter cold, with the streets hung in black. Purcell was the Abbey's organist and had written her birthday odes for years. He died himself in November of the same year, at thirty-six, and this music was performed again at his own funeral in the same building eight months later.","The trumpets are not ordinary trumpets. Purcell asked for 'flatt trumpets', instruments with a sliding tube that could play in a minor key, an English speciality that died out completely and had to be rebuilt from pictures and descriptions in the twentieth century. Their sound is duller and more choked than a normal trumpet, which is why the march feels like breathing rather than fanfare.","The music survives in scattered sources, and no document tells us the exact order in which it was performed, so conductors assemble their own sequence. Wendy Carlos's synthesizer version of the march opened Kubrick's A Clockwork Orange in 1971 and gave the piece a second, stranger life, familiar to millions who have never heard the choral sentences it was written to frame.","Start with the march and count how few notes it uses. Then hear 'Thou knowest, Lord' and notice how the choir moves entirely together, with no imitation and no display. A recording in a resonant church, with real slide trumpets, is worth seeking out; the reverberation is part of the writing."],"purcell-rondeau-abdelazer":["Ninety seconds of strings. A striding tune in the minor, harmonised almost entirely in block chords, returns three times between two short contrasting episodes, one of which brightens into the major before the minor tune shoulders back in. It comes from a set of nine short numbers Purcell wrote as entr'actes and dances for a revival of a revenge tragedy, and it was written to be played while an audience talked and settled.","The play was Abdelazer, or The Moor's Revenge by Aphra Behn, one of the first Englishwomen to earn a living by writing. London theatres of the 1690s expected music before the play, between the acts and during scene changes, supplied by a small band of strings. Purcell wrote dozens of such sets in his last years; this was among the last, from the final year of his life.","It is a rondeau in the French sense, a refrain circling back between couplets, a form Purcell took from Lully's theatre music across the Channel. Notice that the melody almost never leaves the top line: the tune is entirely in the first violins, and the lower strings simply supply harmony, which is why it survives arrangement onto any instrument at all.","In 1946 Britten took this tune as the subject of The Young Person's Guide to the Orchestra, running it through every section in turn, and in doing so made it the single most heard piece of Purcell in the English-speaking world. Purists occasionally grumble that a two-minute entr'acte now carries more fame than the anthems Purcell considered his serious work.","Hear it first on its own, then hear Britten's variations and listen for the moment each instrument family takes the tune. A small period string band gives it a rasp and bite that modern orchestras smooth away. Compare it with the Trumpet Tune to see how differently Purcell writes for a theatre and for a ceremony."],"purcell-hear-my-prayer-o-lord":["One sentence of the Psalms, set for eight voices in four minutes. The words are 'Hear my prayer, O Lord, and let my crying come unto thee', and that is all there is. Voices enter one at a time on the same rising phrase, pile up, overlap, and grind against each other until the full eight parts are sounding at once in a knot of clashing notes. Then it stops, without resolution, in the middle of the argument.","Purcell was in his early twenties and had just become organist of Westminster Abbey. The English church had reopened after the Puritan years, and Charles II preferred bright music with strings, so a severe unaccompanied anthem like this was already an old-fashioned gesture, written in the deliberately archaic 'full anthem' style that Tallis and Byrd had used a century before. It survives in a manuscript of Purcell's own anthems.","It is a fragment. The autograph breaks off at the bottom of the page after thirty-four bars, and nothing more was ever written or has ever been found. So the piece that seems to end on an unbearable dissonance was probably meant to continue, and what listeners now hear as a deliberate refusal to resolve is an accident of a torn or unfinished manuscript.","Nineteenth-century editors sometimes added endings; twentieth-century choirs generally stopped doing so, preferring the raw break. Cathedral choirs made it a Lent and Passiontide fixture, and it has become one of the pieces by which listeners test a choir's tuning, since the eight-part cluster near the end exposes any weakness instantly.","Follow one inner voice, an alto or tenor, rather than the top line, and hear how it collides with its neighbours. Then listen for the loudest bar, roughly three-quarters through, where all eight parts sound at once. Choirs of men and boys give it a colder, thinner sound; a mixed adult choir gives it more weight. Either way, hear it in a big acoustic."],"purcell-o-solitude":["A song for one voice and a bass instrument with keyboard, five minutes long, built over a short falling bass figure that repeats without a break from the first bar to the last, close to thirty times. The voice enters over the middle of the pattern rather than at its start, so the two are permanently out of phase. The words praise being alone, and the melody keeps rising and sinking back like slow breathing.","Purcell was twenty-five, newly appointed organist at Westminster Abbey, and writing songs for London's flourishing printed song trade as much as for the court. The poem is Katherine Philips's English version of a French text by Saint-Amant. Philips, known as 'the matchless Orinda', was one of the first Englishwomen whose verse circulated widely, and the fashion for solitude and melancholy retreat was a literary craze of the period.","The ground bass may not be Purcell's own. The same descending pattern turns up in French sources of the period, and he seems to have taken a ready-made bass and written a song on top of it, exactly as a jazz musician might take a standard's chord sequence. Listen for the join: the voice never once cadences at the same moment the bass does, until the final phrase.","Purcell's songs were long treated as apprentice work beside the theatre music. That changed with the early-music revival and with singers such as Alfred Deller, who put the countertenor voice back into English music and made this song part of the repertoire. Whether it suits a countertenor or a soprano, and how freely the ornaments should be added, are still matters of taste rather than settled fact.","Try to count the repetitions of the bass; you will lose count, which is the effect. Then listen for the one moment the voice reaches its highest note and holds it. Compare a countertenor with a soprano, and a lute continuo with a harpsichord one, since the accompaniment changes the song's temperature completely."],"purcell-sound-trumpet":["A three-minute duet for two high male voices over a short repeating bass. The two singers chase each other in close imitation, mostly a third apart, throwing the word 'sound' back and forth in long runs and springy dotted figures. There are no trumpets anywhere in the scoring. It comes third in a birthday ode of nine numbers for chorus, soloists and orchestra written for the reigning queen.","The ode is Come, Ye Sons of Art, Away, written for Queen Mary's birthday in April 1694, the last of six such odes Purcell supplied for her. Court odes were an annual obligation: a poet produced flattering verses, the Master of the Music set them, and the royal band performed them at Whitehall. Mary died of smallpox eight months later, and Purcell wrote her funeral music instead.","The trumpet fanfares are entirely vocal. Purcell writes the voices in the intervals a natural trumpet can play, so two singers imitate an instrument that is standing silent in the same room. Underneath, the bass is only a few bars long and cycles continuously, which is what gives the duet its wound-up, unstoppable momentum.","The ode was virtually unknown until the twentieth century revived Purcell's occasional music. The duet became a showpiece for countertenors, and Alfred Deller's recordings, later James Bowman's, established the sound most listeners now expect. Some argue the parts were written for high tenors rather than falsettists; both readings are performed and both work.","Listen for the moment the second voice enters, a beat behind the first, and follow the delay for the rest of the piece. Then go straight to Dido's Lament to hear the same composer working the same technique, a short bass repeated without pause, to opposite emotional ends. Any good period ensemble recording of the complete ode gives it its context."],"purcell-king-arthur-cold-song":["A bass voice is dragged out of the ice to sing 'What power art thou, who from below hast made me rise'. Every note is repeated and broken into shivering fragments, with silences cut into the middle of words, so the singer seems physically unable to hold a line. The strings shake in the same way underneath. Three minutes, almost no melody, and the whole thing sinks slowly through the harmony as if freezing solid.","King Arthur is a semi-opera, the English compromise form: a spoken play with sung and danced set-pieces for supernatural characters, so the star actors never had to sing. Dryden wrote the text, having already worked with Purcell, and the Frost Scene in Act Three is its most extravagant episode, with a whole chorus of shivering people summoned out of a frozen landscape by Cupid.","The trembling effect was not new. Lully had written a chorus of shivering figures in his opera Isis in 1677, and Purcell almost certainly knew of it. What he adds is the notation of the stutter itself, rests written inside the words so the shaking is composed rather than left to the performer, an early instance of a composer specifying a vocal effect that singers would otherwise improvise.","The full semi-opera is awkward to stage now, since the spoken play takes hours and the music is scattered through it, so this aria mostly travels alone. Klaus Nomi's 1981 new-wave recording, sung in a high strangled countertenor with synthesizers, introduced it to an audience that had never heard Purcell, and singers of every kind have claimed it since.","Listen to how the shivering is written into the rhythm rather than added by the singer, then compare a deep bass version with a countertenor one; the piece changes from earthbound to spectral. Follow it immediately with Vivaldi's Winter to hear another composer solving the same problem, how to make an orchestra sound cold, thirty years later."],"purcell-trumpet-tune-air":["A pair of short bright movements usually heard as a processional: a Trumpet Tune in march time, built on a rising fanfare figure answered by the same figure a step higher, followed by a gentler Air in the same key. Together they last about four minutes. The tune sits entirely on the notes a valveless trumpet can sound, and the harmony under it barely moves, which is exactly why it carries down a long aisle.","The music comes from Purcell's theatre work of the early 1690s, when London playhouses staged elaborate semi-operas with trumpets, drums and stage machinery. Purcell died in November 1695 leaving a mass of such short pieces scattered through play scores. Publishers gathered them into collections almost immediately, detached from their plays, and it is in that anthologised form that this pair has travelled.","The famous processional most people call 'Purcell's Trumpet Voluntary' is not by Purcell at all: it is the Prince of Denmark's March by his younger contemporary Jeremiah Clarke, misattributed for most of the twentieth century and still routinely credited wrongly on wedding programmes. This Trumpet Tune is the genuine Purcell article, and knowing the difference is half the pleasure.","Purists point out that these movements were theatre cues, not ceremonial music, and that playing them on a big church organ with a modern piccolo trumpet is a nineteenth-century idea. Others reply that Purcell's own century recycled theatre music into churches constantly. Either way, the arrangement heard at most weddings is a modern editor's, not the composer's.","Notice how few different notes the trumpet plays, then imagine a player with no valves and only one available harmonic series. Compare a period natural trumpet with a modern piccolo trumpet: the older instrument is rougher and more human. Then listen to Clarke's Prince of Denmark's March and settle for yourself which one you have been calling Purcell all these years."],"a-scarlatti-gia-il-sole-dal-gange":["Two minutes of pure forward motion. The singer describes the sun already blazing over the Ganges and setting the world alight, and the melody does the same thing: a rising figure that springs upward in wide leaps, in a lilting compound metre, over a bass that walks steadily beneath. There is no slow section and no lament, only two short stanzas of the same music, which is why singers use it to open recitals.","Scarlatti was twenty, working in Rome under the protection of Queen Christina of Sweden, who had abdicated her throne, converted to Catholicism and moved to Rome to run the city's most ambitious artistic circle. The aria comes from an early opera of his, staged when papal restrictions on public opera meant such works were given in private palaces to invited guests rather than in public theatres.","The accompaniment almost every singer uses is not Scarlatti's. The original gives only a bass line with figures, to be filled out at the keyboard on the spot. The flowing piano part, the dynamics and the phrasing marks come from Alessandro Parisotti's Arie antiche anthology of the 1880s, which turned these arias into student songs and quietly recomposed them into nineteenth-century Italian art song.","Generations of singers have met Scarlatti here first, in a volume of 'old Italian arias' that treats them as vocal exercises rather than opera. Period performers have pushed back, restoring the plain continuo and the theatrical speed, and arguing that the anthology's slow expressive manner mistakes a brisk exit aria for a meditation. Both traditions are alive on record.","Listen for the upward leap on the first syllable of 'Già', which is the whole piece in miniature. Then hear it twice: once with a modern piano accompaniment, once with harpsichord and cello alone, and notice how much lighter and faster the second becomes. It pairs well with Handel arias from thirty years later, which are built on the same Neapolitan grammar."],"a-scarlatti-stabat-mater":["The medieval poem on Mary standing at the cross, set in eighteen short movements for two voices only, with strings and continuo. There is no chorus at all. Soprano and alto alternate solos and duets, and the duets are the heart of it: the two lines lean on each other, one holding while the other moves, so that almost every bar contains a small ache that resolves a beat late. Around forty-five minutes.","Written near the end of Scarlatti's life for a lay brotherhood in Naples, the Confraternity of the Knights of San Luigi di Palazzo, who paid for a new setting to be sung each year in Holy Week. He was in his sixties, the acknowledged head of Neapolitan opera, and had trained most of the composers then filling Europe's theatres. Naples at the time supplied singers and scores to the entire continent.","Listen to the duet writing and you are hearing the direct model for Pergolesi. The same brotherhood commissioned Pergolesi's Stabat Mater about a decade later, for the same two voices and the same strings, to replace this one, and the younger man copied Scarlatti's chains of leaning suspensions almost gesture for gesture. The famous work grew straight out of the forgotten one.","Pergolesi's setting became the most printed piece of the eighteenth century and this one vanished with its composer. Recordings from the late twentieth century onward have made the case that Scarlatti's is the graver and stranger work, longer, less immediately charming, and harmonically more adventurous, while conceding that Pergolesi's is the better-shaped forty minutes.","Do not start at the beginning. Go to any of the duets, listen only to how one voice suspends while the other moves beneath, then hear the equivalent movement in Pergolesi and judge the family resemblance for yourself. A small period ensemble with one voice to a part keeps the texture clear; large-choir versions do not exist, because there is no choir."],"a-scarlatti-violette":["A tiny skipping aria in fast triple time, addressed to a bed of violets: dewy, fragrant, modest little flowers hiding among the leaves. The vocal line is all short clipped phrases separated by rests, so the singer seems to be darting from flower to flower, and the bass trots along beneath in an unvarying pattern. Two stanzas, the second sharper in tone than the first, and under two minutes in total.","From an opera staged in Naples in the mid-1690s, when Scarlatti was music director to the Spanish viceroy there and turning out several full operas a year. Neapolitan opera of the period ran on a production line of stock situations and stock aria types, of which this is one: the short, light, faintly moralising nature piece, dropped in to lighten a scene and let a singer charm the room.","The compliment turns. The first stanza admires the violets for their modesty; the second warns them that they are proud beneath their shyness and will be punished for it, which recasts the whole thing as a barbed remark about a person, not a plant. The music does not change at all between the two stanzas, so the sweetness of the tune has to carry the sting.","Like the rest of Scarlatti's short arias, it survives because Parisotti anthologised it in the 1880s for singing students, complete with an invented piano part. Its status is contested: teachers value it for breath control and diction, period specialists regard the standard edition as a Victorian rewrite, and the opera it came from is almost never staged.","Listen for the rests. The silences between phrases are as composed as the notes, and a singer who fills them ruins the piece. Then hear the second stanza's words with a translation to hand and notice how differently the same melody lands. It sits naturally next to Handel's lighter arias and Scarlatti's own Già il sole dal Gange."],"a-scarlatti-o-cessate-di-piagarmi":["Three minutes of slow complaint. The singer begs a pair of cruel eyes to stop wounding him, and the melody sags by half-steps on the word for wounding, sliding downward where a normal phrase would step cleanly. The bass moves at half the speed of the voice, so nothing hurries. Two short stanzas, the second more insistent than the first, and no relief at the end; the piece simply subsides.","From an opera on a Roman historical subject staged in Rome in 1683 at a private theatre in the Colonna palace, since public opera houses in the papal city were periodically closed by decree. Scarlatti, still in his early twenties, was writing for aristocratic patrons who could afford singers, machinery and an orchestra, and who used opera as a display of political standing.","The chromatic sagging on 'piagarmi' is the same device Purcell was using at exactly the same moment in London for Dido, and Monteverdi had used decades earlier: a melodic line that slips down by half-steps to signal pain. The convention was pan-European and instantly legible to listeners, a shared vocabulary of gesture that composers could deploy without explanation.","The aria is famous in isolation and almost unknown in context, since the opera has rarely been revived. It circulates in transpositions for every voice type, which means there is no single authoritative key, and the accompaniment most often heard is again a nineteenth-century keyboard realisation rather than Scarlatti's bare figured bass.","Follow the descending half-steps on the second syllable of the title word and you have the piece. Then hear Dido's Lament straight afterwards to see two composers in two countries reaching for the identical gesture in the same decade. A version with lute or theorbo continuo strips away the added Romanticism and lets the harmony bite."],"couperin-barricades-mysterieuses":["Two and a half minutes for harpsichord alone. A refrain returns three times between short contrasting couplets, and the refrain is one continuous rippling texture rather than a tune: notes are held down while others arrive on top of them, so chords smear into each other and the ear never gets a clean edge. The whole piece stays within a narrow range in the middle of the keyboard and never raises its voice.","Published in 1717 in the second of four books of harpsichord pieces. Couperin was organist at Saint-Gervais in Paris and harpsichordist to Louis XIV at Versailles, teaching the royal children and playing at the Sunday chamber concerts. He gave almost every piece a title, portraits of court figures, riddles, private jokes for a circle of listeners who would have recognised the references at once.","The rippling texture is called style luthé, the harpsichord imitating a lute, where the player holds keys down so that notes overlap the way a lute's strings keep ringing. The title has never been explained: proposals include ladies' hooped skirts, closed eyelids, the physical barricade of held-down fingers, and an in-joke among Couperin's circle. He left no clue, and probably intended not to.","Nineteenth-century Germany rediscovered Couperin partly through Brahms, who helped prepare a complete edition of his keyboard music. Ravel later wrote a whole suite in homage. The piece is now played on piano as often as harpsichord, and the argument is whether a modern piano's sustaining pedal reproduces the overlap Couperin wanted or replaces it with something blurrier.","Listen for what does not happen: no melody steps forward, no cadence lands squarely. Follow the bass instead, which moves in slow, deliberate steps under the shimmer. Compare a harpsichord recording with a piano one, and try Ravel's homage afterwards to hear what two hundred years did to the same sound-world."],"couperin-tic-toc-choc":["A fast, clicking machine of a piece. One hand keeps up a continuous chain of even notes while the other punches in short repeated figures that land between them, producing a tick-tock rattle that never lets up for three minutes. It is a rondeau, refrain and couplets, but the couplets change texture rather than mood: the mechanism simply reconfigures itself and resumes.","From the third of Couperin's four harpsichord books, published in Paris in 1722, when he was in his fifties and the acknowledged master of the French keyboard style. The subtitle refers to the Maillotins, possibly the Paris tax rebels of 1382 or a family of tumblers; the reference was probably transparent to his subscribers and is now lost.","Couperin marks it a pièce croisée, written for two keyboards with the hands crossing so that both play in the same register without colliding, one above the other in space but in the same pitch range. On a single-manual instrument or a piano the hands physically cannot do it as written, so almost every performance you hear involves a quiet act of rewriting.","Pianists took the piece up in the twentieth century and often redistribute the notes between the hands to make it playable, which changes the accents. Harpsichordists with two manuals argue that the crossing is the whole point, since the piece is designed around a physical trick that produces its particular rhythmic bounce. Both camps have made it a recital favourite.","Count the pulse in the steady hand and then try to place the interruptions against it; they land off the beat more often than on. Hear a two-manual harpsichord version first, then a piano one, and notice how much of the effect survives the translation. It sits very naturally beside Domenico Scarlatti's hand-crossing sonatas."],"couperin-rossignol-en-amour":["A nightingale in love, drawn as a slow, high, decorated single line over a quiet accompaniment. The melody is almost all ornament: turns, trills and little flurries that imitate birdsong without ever becoming literal. Couperin then supplies a written-out double, a repeat of the whole piece with the decoration multiplied, so the bird sings the same song twice, more elaborately the second time. Four minutes in all.","It opens an ordre of bird pieces in the third book of 1722, a set that includes a linnet, warblers and a famous imitation of hens. Bird-imitation was a French speciality going back to Renaissance chansons, and it suited an aristocratic audience that kept aviaries. Couperin, precise about everything, published a treatise on how his ornaments should be played, and complained that performers ignored it.","Couperin adds a footnote of his own admitting that the piece works better on the transverse flute than on his own instrument, when well played. A composer publishing a harpsichord collection openly telling you to hear it on something else is unusual in any century, and it explains the writing: a long singing line with almost no chords, conceived for breath rather than plucked strings.","Flautists have taken him at his word and claimed the piece; harpsichordists reply that the double's decoration only makes sense under fingers. The wider argument about Couperin's ornament signs, which he specified in obsessive detail, runs through all his music: whether to follow his table exactly or to treat the marks as suggestions, as most eighteenth-century players evidently did.","Play it twice, once on harpsichord and once on flute, and decide whether the composer was right about his own piece. Listen for the second half, where the same music returns thickly ornamented, and try to hear the plain line underneath. Then compare Rameau's hen, written a few years later, for the comic opposite of this tenderness."],"couperin-ombres-errantes":["Wandering shades, marked to be played languidly. Three voices move at once: a slow melody on top, a bass beneath, and in the middle a repeated two-note sighing figure that keeps falling and never settles where the ear expects. Notes are held across the bar-lines so the harmony is always slightly out of focus. It is a rondeau, and the refrain's return each time feels less like arrival than like circling. Around three minutes.","Published in 1730 in the fourth and last book of harpsichord pieces, three years before Couperin's death, when he was in poor health and had handed his Versailles duties to a relative. The pieces of this final book are mostly quiet, minor-key and inward, a long way from the court portraits and dance parodies of the earlier volumes.","The middle voice is the whole piece. It is a chain of suspensions, one note held while the harmony changes underneath so that it becomes a dissonance, then falling to resolve just as the next one is prepared. The chain never breaks from beginning to end, which is why the music seems unable to stop drifting, and why the closing bars sound like an interruption rather than a conclusion.","Couperin's fourth book was long treated as the work of a tired man. Twentieth-century pianists reversed that: it is now heard as his most personal writing, and this piece in particular is programmed alongside Satie and Debussy as an ancestor of French keyboard music that suggests rather than states. Whether the piano's sustain betrays or serves it remains disputed.","Listen only to the middle of the texture, not the top, and follow the falling sighs. Then hear it after Le Tic-Toc-Choc for the range of one composer's world, mechanism against mist. A harpsichord's quick decay makes the suspensions bite; a piano makes them glow. Satie's Gnossiennes are a good, disorienting follow-on."],"couperin-passacaille-b":["The largest single piece in Couperin's harpsichord books, around seven minutes. A grave refrain in slow triple time returns eight times, and between its returns come eight couplets that grow steadily more restless: running figures, then broken chords across both hands, then a couplet built entirely from clashing held notes. The refrain always comes back exactly as before, which is what makes the couplets sound like escalating attempts to escape it.","Published in the second book of 1717 at the close of an ordre otherwise made of light character pieces. The passacaille came into France through Italian and Spanish dance, and Lully had made the form a fixture of the Paris opera, where a long chain of variations over a repeating harmony would end an act with dancers on stage. Couperin brings that theatrical scale onto a domestic instrument.","It is not a true passacaglia. A strict one repeats a bass line continuously; Couperin instead writes a rondeau, a fixed refrain alternating with free couplets, and only the refrain carries the passacaille's characteristic tread. The seventh couplet is the crux: it abandons figuration entirely for a pile-up of suspensions, the harmonic temperature of the whole piece raised in a few bars.","Long overshadowed by the miniatures, it has been reclaimed as Couperin's most ambitious keyboard structure, and pianists including Angela Hewitt and harpsichordists including Christophe Rousset have made it a centrepiece. The interpretive question is pacing: whether the refrain should return at exactly the same tempo each time, or gather weight as the couplets intensify.","Count the returns of the refrain, all eight, and notice that it never varies while everything around it does. Go straight to the seventh couplet if you want the piece's emotional peak. Then hear Buxtehude's organ passacaglia and Britten's from Peter Grimes to trace what composers keep doing with one stubborn repeating idea."],"albinoni-oboe-concerto-op-9-no-2":["Three movements, about eleven minutes. A firm, striding opening for the strings, into which the oboe enters with the same material decorated; a slow middle movement where the oboe spins one long unbroken melody over quietly repeating string chords; then a quick finale in dancing triple time. The slow movement is the reason the piece is famous: the oboe simply sings, in phrases shaped for breath, with almost nothing happening beneath it.","Published in Amsterdam in 1722 as part of a set of twelve concertos, in the era when Dutch engraved editions were carrying Italian music across Europe faster than manuscripts ever had. Albinoni was a Venetian of independent means who described himself on his title pages as a dilettante, an amateur gentleman rather than a hired church or court musician, and who wrote around fifty operas alongside his instrumental music.","The oboe was still a recent arrival in Italy, a French invention of the previous century, and Albinoni was among the first Italians to write solo concertos for it. Because he spent his working life writing opera arias, he treats the instrument as a singer: the slow movement's line has breathing places built in, and never asks for the rapid finger-work a violinist would have been given.","For most of the twentieth century Albinoni's reputation rested on a piece he did not write. Recordings of the genuine Op. 9 concertos, particularly by period oboists, have gradually made the case for the real composer: less lush, more shapely, and considerably more interesting harmonically than the famous Adagio that bears his name.","Go to the slow movement first and follow the oboe's breathing, then listen to the outer movements to hear how tightly the string ritornello frames the solos. Then play the spurious Adagio immediately afterwards and hear how different, and how much later, its harmony sounds. Period oboes have a reedier, more vocal tone worth seeking out."],"albinoni-adagio-g-attrib":["Around nine minutes for string orchestra and organ. A slow repeating bass pattern begins alone; strings enter above it in long sustained chords; a single melody climbs in waves, each higher than the last, with a brief agitated section of running notes in the middle before the opening returns. The organ underpins everything with a steady sustained cushion, and the dynamic arc is one long swell and subsidence.","The piece was published in 1958 by Remo Giazotto, an Italian musicologist who had written the standard biography of Albinoni. He said he had reconstructed it from a fragment of an Albinoni slow movement recovered from the Saxon State Library in Dresden after the wartime bombing. No such fragment has ever been produced, the library has no record of one, and Giazotto held the copyright.","The harmony gives it away. The chord sequence, the long sustained organ, and the way the melody arches over a slowly changing bass belong to the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, not to Venice around 1700; no Venetian concerto of Albinoni's day used an organ as an atmospheric wash. Later in life Giazotto spoke of the piece more or less as his own composition.","Scholarship now lists it as Giazotto's work, sometimes as a hoax, sometimes more generously as a pastiche that outgrew its disguise. It has been used in Gallipoli, Rollerball and countless funerals, and remains among the most recorded 'Baroque' pieces in existence. The interesting argument is no longer about authorship but about why the twentieth century wanted this particular idea of the Baroque.","Listen to it once for what it is, a very effective twentieth-century slow movement, then play Albinoni's real Op. 9 oboe adagio and notice how much plainer, faster-moving and less upholstered the genuine article sounds. It sits far more naturally beside Barber's Adagio for Strings than beside anything written in 1700."],"albinoni-oboe-concerto-op-7-no-3":["Nine sunny minutes in three movements. The strings lay out a compact opening idea, the oboe answers with a decorated version of it, and the two trade the material back and forth in short alternating blocks. The central slow movement is brief and songlike rather than tragic, and the finale bounces along in triple time. Nothing is difficult for difficulty's sake; the writing keeps the oboe in its warm middle register throughout.","Published in Amsterdam in 1715, this set was among the first anywhere to include concertos for solo oboe, and it circulated fast through the northern European print trade. Albinoni lived comfortably in Venice on family money, outside the church and court employment that defined most Italian composers' lives, which let him publish what he liked at his own pace.","The twelve concertos of Op. 7 are laid out in a deliberate scheme, alternating groups with no soloist, with one oboe and with two, so the collection works as a survey of what the new instrument could do. Albinoni carefully avoids the oboe's weakest low notes and its most awkward key changes, evidence of a composer writing for specific players and their actual instruments.","These concertos were absorbed into the standard oboe repertoire during the twentieth-century Baroque revival, then re-examined by period players who restored faster tempi and lighter accompaniment. Compared with Vivaldi's oboe concertos they are less dramatic and more evenly proportioned, which earlier critics called dull and current performers tend to call civilised.","Listen for the alternation: full strings, then solo, then full strings again, a pattern you will hear in every Italian concerto of the period once you have caught it here. Follow it with Vivaldi's L'estro armonico to hear the same design at higher voltage. A period oboe with gut-strung accompaniment gives the piece its intended lightness."],"albinoni-sinfonia-g":["Eight minutes of strings in three movements, fast, slow, fast. The opening is all bustle and repeated chords, the kind of noise designed to quiet a room; the middle movement is short, in the minor, and thins the texture to a few lines; the finale is a quick dance. There is no soloist, and the first violins carry the melody almost throughout while the lower strings supply drive rather than counterpoint.","Albinoni published sets of sinfonias and concertos in Venice from 1700 onward, when the city had several opera houses running simultaneously and an insatiable appetite for instrumental music to open evenings and fill intervals. The Italian sinfonia at this point was essentially an opera overture that had wandered out of the theatre and set up on its own in the concert room.","The three-movement fast-slow-fast plan you are hearing is the ancestor of the symphony. Alessandro Scarlatti had standardised it as an opera curtain-raiser; Venetian composers detached it from the stage; and a generation later the same shape, expanded and given a minuet, becomes the classical symphony. Nothing here sounds revolutionary, which is precisely how influential forms usually travel.","Albinoni's sinfonias are catalogued incompletely and several circulate under uncertain numbering, so recordings do not always agree about what is what. They were dismissed as routine for a long time; period-instrument groups have argued that taken at the right speed, with a small band, they are exactly the crisp functional music Venice wanted, and that judging them as failed symphonies misses the point.","Listen to the shape rather than the tunes: three movements, fast, slow, fast, and hold that pattern in mind for the next hundred years of music. Then hear a J. C. Bach symphony from the 1760s and notice how little the outline has changed. A small period band of ten or twelve players is the right scale."],"vivaldi-four-seasons-winter":["Nine minutes in three movements. The first opens with the whole string band repeating one note in short jabs, quieter than seems possible, then louder, until the solo violin bursts in with stamping figures and teeth-chattering trills. The slow movement puts the soloist indoors, singing a calm melody, while the other violins pluck raindrops on the window. The finale is a walk on ice: careful steps, a slip, a fall, then the winds break loose.","Published in Amsterdam in 1725 in a set of twelve concertos dedicated to a Bohemian count who, Vivaldi says in the preface, already knew these four. He had been employed for decades at the Pietà, a Venetian orphanage for girls whose orchestra drew visitors from across Europe. Printed sonnets, one for each season and probably written by Vivaldi himself, accompany the music.","The sonnets are not a programme note; they are printed into the score itself, line by line, with letter cues pointing at the exact bars that illustrate them. 'The teeth chatter'. 'We run and stamp our feet'. It is the most detailed written correspondence between words and music before Berlioz, and it tells performers precisely what each figure is supposed to depict.","The Four Seasons disappeared with their composer and returned only in the twentieth century, through a 1939 Vivaldi week in Siena and then a 1950s recording that made them ubiquitous. They are now the most recorded music in the classical repertoire, which has produced a backlash and a counter-argument. Period bands take the outer movements at frightening speed; older recordings sound stately by comparison.","Start with the very first bars and listen to how little Vivaldi uses: one repeated note, growing. In the slow movement, ignore the tune and listen to the plucked raindrops behind it. Then hear Purcell's Cold Song, written thirty years earlier, for a different composer's answer to the same question of how to make music sound frozen."],"vivaldi-gloria-d-rv-589":["Half an hour, twelve short movements, on the single Latin text of the Gloria. It opens with the strings hammering out octave leaps and the choir shouting the word 'Gloria' in blocks. The second movement drops immediately into slow, sliding chromatic harmony for 'peace on earth'. After that the pieces alternate fast choruses, solo arias with obbligato instruments, and one duet, ending with a large fugue on 'Cum Sancto Spiritu'.","Written around 1715 for the chapel of the Pietà in Venice, where Vivaldi taught violin to orphaned and abandoned girls whose concerts, given from behind grilles, were a fixture of the European tourist itinerary. He was an ordained priest who had stopped saying Mass, citing a chest complaint, and who supplied the institution with a steady flow of sacred and instrumental music under contract.","The closing fugue is not Vivaldi's. He took it from a Gloria written a few years earlier by the Venetian composer Giovanni Maria Ruggieri and rescored it, a borrowing that was ordinary practice and openly done. Note too that at the Pietà all the parts, including the low ones, were sung by women and girls, so the original sound was quite unlike a mixed choir's.","The Gloria was unknown until Alfredo Casella programmed it in 1939 at the Siena Vivaldi week, and the edition that first spread it was heavily cut and rescored to twentieth-century taste. Scholarly editions followed, and the argument since has been about forces: large chorus and modern orchestra, or a dozen singers and period instruments at the pitch and scale Vivaldi actually had.","Listen to the first two movements back to back, the blazing opening and the desolate 'Et in terra pax', to feel how violently Vivaldi switches temperature. Then find the 'Domine Deus' aria, where an oboe or violin winds around the soprano over a gently rocking bass. An all-female period performance is worth hearing at least once."],"vivaldi-concerto-two-cellos-g":["It begins with the two cellos alone, no orchestra at all, growling and interrupting each other in the instrument's dark low register before the strings finally arrive to impose order. Three movements, about ten minutes: a taut first movement of alternating tutti and duet, a slow movement where the two soloists sing in long parallel lines over a bare bass, and a driving finale in which they chase each other in close imitation.","Written for the Pietà, where the cello was taught alongside violin and where several of the resident musicians were evidently accomplished on it. Vivaldi wrote around twenty-seven cello concertos, an extraordinary investment at a time when the instrument was regarded across most of Europe as a bass-line machine rather than a solo voice with its own repertoire.","This is the only concerto for two cellos to survive from the entire Baroque period, and Vivaldi opens it in the most exposed way possible: an unaccompanied duet, with the two soloists in the same register, so the ear has to separate two dark voices with no help from above. The orchestra's first entry is therefore a release, and he delays it as long as he dares.","Neglected until cellists went looking for pre-classical repertoire, it is now a favourite of duo partnerships and of period ensembles, and appears constantly in conservatoire programmes. Debate centres on whether the low duet writing should be played with gut strings and no endpin, which thickens and roughens the sound, or with modern setup, which clarifies it.","Listen to the first thirty seconds before anything else and try to hear the two separate lines. Then compare the finale in a period recording and a modern one, and decide whether you want the growl or the clarity. Bach's Concerto for Two Violins makes an obvious and rewarding comparison, the same argument conducted two octaves higher."],"vivaldi-four-seasons-spring":["Ten minutes in three movements. The first announces spring with a bright, symmetrical tune everyone knows, then breaks off repeatedly for episodes of birdsong, three violins trilling and twittering, a murmuring stream, a sudden thunderstorm with rushing scales, and the birds returning. The slow movement is a sleeping goatherd. The finale is a rustic dance over a drone, the violins imitating bagpipes at a village celebration.","The first of the twelve concertos published in Amsterdam in 1725 under a title meaning 'the contest between harmony and invention', the argument being whether music should obey abstract craft or depict the world. Vivaldi, running the Pietà's orchestra and writing operas at speed, answered the question by doing both at once and printing the poems to prove it.","In the slow movement a dog barks continuously. The violas play a repeated two-note figure marked in the score as the barking dog, and they never stop for the entire movement, underneath the sleeping goatherd's melody and the rustling leaves in the violins. Three separate things are depicted at once, on three layers of the texture, which is a genuinely modern piece of scoring.","Its ubiquity, in lifts, on hold, in advertising, has made it hard to hear. Recordings range from lush 1960s string orchestras to period bands playing with gut strings, gritty attacks and speeds that startle. The scholarly consensus is that Vivaldi expected a small group of soloistic players, not a large ensemble, and that the depiction should be enacted rather than smoothed.","Listen to the slow movement with the violas' barking dog in mind and you will never unhear it. Then hear the finale's drone and imagine a bagpipe. Try a period-instrument recording if you only know a modern one, then Max Richter's recomposition, which strips the piece down to its loops and shows what was underneath all along."],"vivaldi-four-seasons-summer-presto":["Three minutes of storm, the finale of the summer concerto, marked as a tempest of summertime. It opens with the entire string band playing one line together in a rushing torrent of scales, an unusually bare texture, then hurls in hard detached chords for hailstones flattening the corn. The soloist's music is not decorative here; it is the same violence, faster, and the movement ends without any softening at all.","The preceding movements have described exhaustion under a burning sun, a cuckoo, a turtle dove, and a shepherd terrified by approaching thunder. The printed sonnet's last lines describe hail cutting down the standing crops, an agricultural disaster rather than a picturesque effect, and Venice's mainland economy depended on exactly those harvests. Published in Amsterdam in the same 1725 set.","For long stretches the whole orchestra plays a single melodic line in unison and octaves, with no harmony at all. Vivaldi almost never writes this way, and the effect is of the music losing its footing, since there is nothing underneath to stand on. When the hail arrives, harmony returns as blunt repeated chords, which is why the contrast hits so hard.","This movement is the flashpoint of the period-performance argument. Groups such as Il Giardino Armonico and Fabio Biondi's Europa Galante take it at speeds that older listeners found violent and even distorting; conductors of the mid-twentieth century took it at a measured pace that now sounds oddly polite for a description of crop destruction. Both are defended in print.","Listen for the moments when the whole band is playing the same line and nothing else, then for the first hailstone chords. Play a 1950s recording and a modern period one back to back; few pieces show the change in performance style so plainly. Rossini's William Tell storm makes a good century-later companion."],"vivaldi-l-estro-armonico-op-3-no":["Eight minutes, three movements, and a model of clarity. The strings state a compact idea; the soloist takes it over and spins it into running figuration; the orchestra returns with the idea again, in a new key; and so on, alternating in blocks until the movement closes where it began. The slow movement is a single long violin melody over pulsing chords, and the finale returns to the alternating plan at speed.","Published in Amsterdam in 1711 as one of twelve concertos, the set that made Vivaldi's name across Europe within a couple of years. Engraved Dutch editions travelled where Venetian manuscripts never had, reaching German court libraries, English collectors and provincial organists, and turning a local Venetian teacher into the most imitated instrumental composer alive.","Bach copied several concertos from this collection out by hand and arranged them for keyboard and organ, teaching himself the ritornello plan you are hearing here. The alternation of a returning orchestral idea with freely developing solo episodes became the standard architecture of the concerto for the next two centuries, and it is laid out in this set with textbook plainness.","Op. 3 was praised in the eighteenth century, forgotten in the nineteenth, and rehabilitated in the twentieth partly because of the Bach connection, which for a long time was the only reason German scholarship took Vivaldi seriously at all. Modern performance argues over whether the set's small-scale scoring, a handful of players, should be preserved or expanded.","Listen for the returning orchestral idea and count how many times it comes back and in how many different keys; once you can hear that, every Baroque concerto opens up. Then hear Bach's A minor Violin Concerto and notice how much of the plan he took wholesale. Small period ensembles suit this music best."],"vivaldi-nulla-mundo-pax-sincera":["A Latin motet for one soprano in four sections: a long serene opening aria, a short declaimed recitative, a faster middle aria, and a closing Alleluia that bubbles away in unbroken running notes. The opening is the famous part, a wide, slow, floating melody over strings that pulse gently and never intrude, the voice rising by step to a held note and easing back down. Twelve minutes in all.","Solo motets like this were sacred music built entirely from operatic materials, written for a single virtuoso singer and a small string band, for use in church services where an aria was welcome and a staged opera was not. Vivaldi produced them throughout his career for Venetian institutions and for commissions elsewhere in Italy, using the same skills that filled his opera seasons.","The text says the opposite of what the music does. It insists there is no true peace in this world, that the world is full of deceit and the serpent's poison, while the music remains almost untroubled and radiant. Only in the middle section does Vivaldi allow the harmony to darken for the serpent, and then the calm returns as though nothing had been said.","The motet was little known outside specialist circles until the 1996 film Shine used the opening aria, after which it entered the standard soprano recital repertoire and the streaming charts alike. Emma Kirkby's recording with period instruments defined the light, vibrato-free approach; fuller-voiced sopranos have since made an effective case for a warmer reading.","Listen to the first aria for the shape of one long line and how rarely the singer breathes. Then skip to the Alleluia to hear the same voice let off the leash. Compare it with Mozart's Exsultate, jubilate, which is the same design, sacred Latin text as concert showpiece, half a century later and unmistakably descended from this."],"vivaldi-vedro-con-mio-diletto":["Five minutes of gently rocking triple time. The strings play a soft repeated figure, muted and low, and the voice enters with a phrase that falls, rises a little, and falls again, repeating the same shape with small variations rather than developing it. The middle section darkens, the voice tightens, and then the opening returns decorated, as the convention of the period required, before subsiding into the accompaniment.","From an opera on a Byzantine subject staged in Rome for the carnival season of 1724, when Vivaldi was working outside Venice and pursuing commissions in the papal city. Women were barred from the Roman stage by papal decree, so the role was written for a castrato, which is why the part now goes to a countertenor or a mezzo-soprano.","The aria is a recycled one. Vivaldi, producing several operas a year, moved successful numbers between works as a matter of course, and this melody appears in more than one of his scores. Eighteenth-century opera was assembled as much as composed, with singers carrying favourite arias between productions, and nobody involved considered the practice a compromise.","The opera itself waited more than two centuries for a modern staging, and the aria became famous alone, through countertenor recital discs of the 2000s and subsequent film and television use. It also circulates in every conceivable transposition and arrangement, which has made the piece more familiar as a mood than as a scene from a drama.","Listen for the way the melody keeps returning to the same falling phrase, and count how little new material there is. Then hear Handel's Lascia ch'io pianga for the same slow, plain, repeated-phrase construction on a bigger scale. Countertenor and mezzo recordings give the piece quite different weights; try both before deciding."],"vivaldi-stabat-mater-rv-621":["Twenty minutes for a single low female voice, strings and continuo, setting the first half of the medieval poem about Mary at the cross. There is no chorus and no bright colour anywhere: the alto stays in her darkest register, the strings play softly and often in their lower range, and the tempo rarely rises above a slow walk. Nine short movements, most of them quiet, ending with a plain Amen.","Composed in 1712 for a church in Brescia, on the Venetian mainland, where Vivaldi's father had family connections, and written for a Holy Week service rather than for the Pietà. He was thirty-four, ordained but no longer celebrating Mass, and had not yet published the concertos that would make him famous across Europe. It is among the earliest of his surviving sacred works.","The first three movements' music comes back note for note for the next three stanzas. A composer capable of writing at Vivaldi's speed chose deliberate repetition, so that the listener hears the same music applied to new words, the way a congregation hears a psalm tone. The effect is liturgical rather than dramatic, and quite unlike anything in his concertos.","It lay unknown among the manuscripts bought by the Turin national library in the 1920s, the discovery that returned most of Vivaldi's output to the world. Recordings by countertenors and by contraltos differ sharply in weight, and there is an ongoing argument about whether the piece wants an operatic voice or a liturgical one.","Listen to the first movement, then wait for its return four movements later and hear how differently the same music lands. Follow the lowest string line, which moves very slowly throughout. Then compare Alessandro Scarlatti's setting for two voices and Pergolesi's, and notice how much darker Vivaldi keeps his colours."],"vivaldi-mandolin-concerto-c":["Eight minutes, three movements, and a soloist that cannot sustain a note. The mandolin's sound dies almost as soon as it is plucked, so Vivaldi builds the solo writing from rapid repeated notes and short runs, which is why the piece glitters rather than sings. The outer movements are brisk and dancing; the slow movement is a quiet, halting melody that seems to be assembled out of separate droplets of sound.","The Pietà's inventories list mandolins among the instruments its girls played, alongside violins, cellos, oboes, recorders, theorbos and the rare viola d'amore. Vivaldi's contract obliged him to supply new concertos regularly, and he wrote for whatever was in the building, which is why his catalogue includes concertos for instruments almost nobody else composed for.","In the slow movement the strings stop bowing and pluck as well, so for several minutes nothing in the orchestra is being drawn with a bow at all. A wholly plucked ensemble is rare in any century, and the effect is of a music box: no line can be sustained, so the melody exists only as a chain of separate points that the ear joins up.","The concerto survived in the Turin manuscripts and re-entered circulation with the postwar Vivaldi revival, then reached a much wider public through film soundtracks that used the slow movement. Performers disagree about the instrument itself: a Baroque mandolin with gut strings is softer and more intimate, a modern one brighter and better able to compete with the strings.","Listen to the slow movement and try to notice the moment you realise nothing is being bowed. Then hear the finale for the sheer speed of the repeated notes. Compare a period gut-strung mandolin with a modern steel-strung one; the piece changes from a chamber curiosity to a small concerto and back again."],"vivaldi-nisi-dominus-cum-dederit":["The fourth movement of an eleven-part psalm setting, and the slowest music Vivaldi wrote. Muted violins repeat a soft rocking figure that never varies, the bass moves in slow detached steps, and the alto stretches the word for sleep across whole phrases, holding notes far longer than the accompaniment's pulse suggests. Six minutes in which almost nothing changes, which is exactly the point.","A setting of the psalm that begins 'Unless the Lord builds the house', written for the Pietà, where the eleven movements would have been distributed among the institution's soloists and instrumentalists. Elsewhere in the same work Vivaldi calls for the viola d'amore, an instrument with extra strings that vibrate untouched behind the played ones, giving a halo of resonance.","The text promises that God grants sleep to his beloved, and Vivaldi writes an accompaniment that refuses to settle: the muted string figure sighs slightly off the beat throughout, so the rocking never quite becomes rest. The voice, meanwhile, sits at the bottom of its range for almost the whole movement, which is why the effect is heavy rather than serene.","Nisi Dominus was virtually unknown before the postwar Vivaldi revival and this movement then became a countertenor showpiece, recorded by James Bowman, Andreas Scholl and Philippe Jaroussky among many others. Whether a countertenor or a female contralto better suits music written for a Venetian orphanage's women is a question that recordings continue to argue in both directions.","Listen to the muted violins alone for the first few bars and register how off-balance their rocking is. Then follow the voice's longest held note and time it. Afterwards, hear Bach's Erbarme dich for the same problem, grief slowed almost to a standstill, solved by a contemporary in a different language."],"telemann-viola-concerto-g":["Four movements in the old church-sonata order, slow, fast, slow, fast, about sixteen minutes. The opening Largo lets the viola sing a broad, unhurried melody over quiet strings. The Allegro is bustling and imitative, the third movement moves to the minor and is the emotional centre, and the finale is a brisk dance in triple time. The soloist is never asked to dazzle; the writing is lyrical from beginning to end.","Telemann taught himself around ten instruments, the viola among them, and wrote this early in a career that would run to more than three thousand works. The viola at the time was an inner-voice filler, played in orchestras by whoever was least needed elsewhere, with no solo repertoire and something close to a professional stigma attached to it.","The scoring solves a real problem. A viola is easily drowned, so Telemann keeps the accompanying violins low, often doubling the soloist an octave above rather than playing above it independently, and thins the texture whenever the solo line drops into its darkest register. It is the practical writing of a man who had sat in the viola section himself.","It is the earliest concerto for solo viola known to survive, and remains the instrument's calling card, a fixture of auditions, examinations and student concertos worldwide. Performance debate is mostly about ornament: whether the two slow movements should be played as written, as most conservatoires teach, or decorated freely, as eighteenth-century practice would have expected.","Listen for the sound itself first, the viola's slightly husky middle register, and notice how different it is from a violin at the same pitch. Then go to the third movement, the short minor-key one, which is the piece's heart. Walton's Viola Concerto two hundred years later makes an excellent, jolting sequel."],"telemann-tafelmusik-ouverture-d":["A French overture opening one of the three large instalments of Telemann's dinner-music collection. It begins in grand, jerky dotted rhythms, the whole band stepping in unison as if a door were being opened; a fast fugal middle section follows, the parts chasing one entry through the texture; then the stately opening returns. Nine minutes, after which a suite of shorter dances continues the meal.","Telemann published Musique de table in Hamburg in 1733 by subscription, printing the subscribers' names in the volume. He was the city's director of music, responsible for five churches, the opera and civic ceremonies, and he ran his own printing and distribution business, engraving plates himself. Each of the three instalments contains an overture-suite, a quartet, a concerto, a trio and a solo sonata: a whole evening in a box.","The subscriber list includes Handel, in London, and Handel subsequently borrowed a number of ideas from these volumes into works of his own. Borrowing on that scale was normal and openly practised, and Telemann appears to have been untroubled by it. The list is also a map of European musical trade, with names from Paris, Denmark, Switzerland and Spain.","Nineteenth-century critics, building the cult of Bach, used Telemann as the counter-example: too fluent, too many notes, too easily pleased. That verdict held until the period-instrument movement took him up, one leading ensemble even naming itself after this collection. He is now heard as a cosmopolitan who absorbed French, Italian and Polish idioms deliberately rather than carelessly.","Listen for the two speeds, the grand limping opening and the quick fugal middle, and for the return of the first music at the end; the shape is a French overture, and once you know it you will hear it everywhere from Lully to Handel. Then compare Bach's Third Orchestral Suite, written to the same recipe."],"telemann-trumpet-concerto-d":["Four short movements, around nine minutes, alternating slow and fast. The trumpet enters with rising fanfare figures and spends the quick movements in the very top of its range, trading phrases with the strings. The two slow movements are for strings alone or nearly so, with the soloist silent or barely present. The finale is a dance, and the piece ends without any grand peroration.","Court and town trumpeters were a closed guild with jealously guarded privileges, and a good one was among the best-paid musicians in a German court. Telemann worked in several such courts before settling in Hamburg, and wrote concertos for the specific players in front of him. The instrument was a plain coiled tube with no valves and no keys.","A natural trumpet can only sound the notes of one harmonic series, which is why the piece never leaves D major and why the slow movements give the soloist nothing to do: there are no usable notes down there. High in the range those available notes crowd together, so Baroque players worked in the extreme top register, on a shallow mouthpiece, where a melody becomes possible at all.","Most modern performances use a piccolo trumpet, invented in the nineteenth century, which makes the high writing safe but produces a bright, thin sound quite unlike the original. Period specialists play reconstructed natural trumpets, sometimes with small vent-holes that improve tuning and that purists regard as cheating. The argument has run for fifty years.","Notice how few different notes the trumpet plays, and how the piece is built around that limit. Then listen to a natural-trumpet recording and a piccolo-trumpet one and hear the difference in weight and grain. Haydn's Trumpet Concerto, written once a keyed instrument existed, shows exactly what those limits had been costing."],"telemann-wassermusik-ouverture":["The French overture that opens a suite of sea pictures: dotted, ceremonial opening music, a quick fugal middle where the oboes and strings pass one idea between them, then the opening returns. Eight minutes. The movements that follow give each sea deity a dance, sleeping and waking Thetis, an amorous Neptune, playing water-nymphs, a joking Triton and a storming god of the winds.","Written in 1723 for the hundredth anniversary of the Hamburg Admiralty, the body that governed the port's shipping, and performed at a banquet for the city's merchants. Telemann had been Hamburg's music director since 1721. The city's entire wealth came off the Elbe, and the piece flatters the river and the tides that paid for the evening.","The German title is not Water Music but Hamburg's Ebb and Flow, and one movement of that name simply rocks between two harmonies, in and out, without going anywhere, which is the tide itself. The familiar nickname invites confusion with Handel's Water Music of six years earlier, written for a royal barge on the Thames rather than for a merchants' dinner.","It is regularly programmed opposite Handel's suite, and the comparison is instructive: Telemann's is smaller, wittier and far more pictorial, Handel's grander and better at open-air splendour. Period ensembles favour it for its wind writing, and there is a mild argument about how literally to play the storm, which some directors take to the edge of caricature.","Listen for the overture's two speeds, then stay for the sleeping and waking Thetis movements, where the same idea is played slack and then taut. Compare the tide movement with Debussy's La Mer to see what two centuries do to the same subject. A period band with real Baroque oboes is worth the search."],"telemann-concerto-recorder-viola-da-gamba":["Two soloists two octaves apart: a treble recorder, bright and breathy at the top, and a viola da gamba, dark and reedy at the bottom, with nothing much in between. Four movements, around eighteen minutes: a rocking opening marked to be played sweetly, a fugal Allegro, a swaying Siciliana in which the two lines twine around each other, and a driving finale. The same phrase is constantly handed from one soloist to the other.","Hamburg in the 1730s, where Telemann wrote for whatever players the city could supply, including instruments already sliding out of fashion. The viola da gamba, a fretted bowed instrument held between the knees, was being displaced across Europe by the cello; the recorder was losing ground to the transverse flute. Telemann kept writing for both while there were still people to play them.","The gap between the two soloists is the compositional idea. Telemann keeps them so far apart in pitch that they can never blur, and he avoids the recorder's weakest lowest notes so it never sinks toward the gamba's territory. Then, in the Siciliana, he crosses them briefly, and the momentary confusion of who is who is the movement's best moment.","The piece became a staple of the recorder revival, particularly through Frans Brüggen's recordings, and it now anchors many period-ensemble programmes. The live questions are technical: what pitch standard to use, since a lower pitch changes both instruments' character, and whether the gamba should be strung and bowed as a soloist or kept in its older, more retiring manner.","Listen for the distance between the two soloists, then wait for the Siciliana where they briefly overlap. Compare it with Bach's Fifth Brandenburg for another composer's way of combining a flute-family instrument with strings. Any recording with a genuine viola da gamba rather than a cello substitution is the one to seek."],"rameau-indes-galantes-danse-des-sauvages":["Three minutes built from one short, hammering figure that repeats and repeats while the orchestra thickens around it. There is no melodic development at all: the tune simply gets louder, more heavily scored and more insistent, driven by a bass that pounds the same notes like a drum. The chorus then takes up the identical tune to sing of peaceful forests, so the audience hears it twice, orchestral and vocal.","Les Indes galantes is an opéra-ballet, a chain of separate short exotic love stories linked only by a theme, staged at the Paris Opéra in 1735 with a fourth episode added the following year. That episode is set in a North American forest. Rameau built its dance on a harpsichord piece he had written years earlier, after watching two visiting Native American men dance in Paris in 1725.","The same tune exists three times over: as a solo harpsichord piece of 1728, as this orchestral dance, and as the chorus that follows it. Listen to the bass, which sits on one note for long stretches like a drum, refusing the harmonic movement French music of the period normally demands. That refusal is what makes a dance from 1736 sound closer to a modern groove than to its neighbours.","The opera vanished after the eighteenth century and returned in the twentieth. In 2017 and again in a full 2019 Paris staging, Clément Cogitore set this dance on krump dancers from the Paris suburbs, which reopened every question the piece carries: a European fantasy of the 'noble savage', written for a colonial-era court, now danced by the descendants of the people that fantasy was built on.","Listen to the bass line rather than the tune and count how rarely it changes note. Then hear the chorus that follows and notice you already know the melody. If you can, watch the Cogitore staging; it makes the piece's politics audible in a way no concert performance does. Follow with the Rite of Spring for the long view."],"rameau-boreades-entree-polymnie":["A slow instrumental dance of about five minutes, with no singing at all, honouring the muse of sacred song and rhetoric. The strings move in overlapping lines that lean on one another: one instrument holds a note while the others change harmony beneath it, so the note becomes a dissonance and only resolves a beat later, after which another has already started. Over a walking bass, the effect is of continuous unfinished sighing.","Rameau was seventy-nine or eighty. The opera reached full rehearsal at the Paris Opéra in 1763 and was then abandoned, for reasons nobody has established: a theatre fire that year, the composer's age, official unease about a plot in which lovers defy a tyrant, have all been proposed. He died the following year with the score unheard, and it stayed unheard.","Almost every bar contains a suspension, a note held over from the previous harmony that rubs before it settles. Rameau wrote the treatise that named and systematised these relationships forty years earlier, so the old theorist is here demonstrating his own principles at their most expressive. The writing is also conspicuously vocal: long breathing phrases, for an orchestra with nobody to sing them.","The opera waited two hundred and nineteen years. John Eliot Gardiner conducted the first staged performances at Aix-en-Provence in 1982, and the reception reversed a settled judgement: Rameau's last opera turned out to be among his boldest, with orchestral writing more adventurous than anything in his famous earlier works. Stagings and recordings have multiplied since.","Follow one inner string line, not the top one, and hear how often it is holding a note that no longer fits. Then hear Gluck's Dance of the Blessed Spirits, written a year earlier in a very different spirit, for the contrast between French complexity and the new classical plainness arriving to replace it."],"rameau-tambourin":["Under two minutes and almost aggressively simple. The left hand thumps a single repeated bass note, over and over, like a drum being struck; the right hand plays a short, square, bright tune above it in clipped phrases. A middle section moves to the major and lightens, then the opening returns unchanged. There is no development, no counterpoint and no ornament to speak of, which is the whole point.","Published in Rameau's second harpsichord collection in 1724, when he was forty-one, known in Paris as a theorist and organist and not yet as a composer for the stage; his first opera was still nine years away. The tambourin is a dance from Provence, played by one performer holding a three-holed pipe in one hand while beating a long narrow drum with the other.","The drone bass is doing the work. Rameau keeps one note going underneath for whole sections, which is what the Provençal drum actually does, and it means the harmony above is far more restricted than a Paris audience expected. A composer who had just published the book that systematised chord relationships chose here to write music that barely changes chord at all.","It has been arranged for everything: piano, guitar, recorder, orchestra, and generations of examination syllabuses. Rameau himself reworked the material into his stage music. The performance argument is about weight, whether the drone should be hammered as a real drum or kept light, and about tempo, since a dance played too fast loses the physical tread the drum implies.","Listen only to the left hand for the first pass, then to the right hand, then to both. Try a harpsichord version and a piano version and decide whether the modern instrument's sustain helps or muffles the drum. Rameau's La Poule, from the same years, shows the same composer using a repeated figure for comedy instead of dance."],"rameau-poule":["A hen, rendered as one pecking figure: a note repeated quickly, then a sharp drop, then a pause, as if the bird has looked up. That figure runs through the entire four minutes, moving into every register, sometimes in the bass, sometimes high, sometimes in both hands at once. The piece keeps interrupting itself, stopping mid-phrase and starting again, and the harmony underneath grows steadily more agitated.","Published around 1728 in Rameau's last harpsichord collection, six years after the Treatise on Harmony that made his reputation and established the vocabulary of root chords, inversions and key relationships still taught today. He was in his mid-forties, respected as a theorist, regarded by some Parisians as a dry academic, and still five years from the opera career that would change his life.","Rameau wrote the clucking syllables into the score, spelling out the hen's noise beneath the opening notes so there could be no doubt what the figure represents. Beneath the joke the construction is rigorous: the pecking motif is transposed, inverted and answered between the hands exactly as a fugue subject would be. It is a comedy piece built by a theorist who could not stop being one.","Pianists from Marcelle Meyer onward have claimed it from the harpsichordists, and Respighi orchestrated it in his suite The Birds in 1928, which introduced it to orchestral audiences with the joke intact and the harpsichord's sharp attack lost. The main interpretive question is how far to push the comedy before the piece's real structural seriousness disappears under it.","Catch the pecking figure in the first bars, then track it as it moves between registers; it is almost never absent. Listen for the abrupt silences, which are the bird's alertness. Then hear Respighi's orchestral version and Couperin's nightingale, and decide which composer gets closer to an actual animal."],"rameau-gavotte-et-six-doubles":["A calm, plain gavotte, stated once, then dissolved through six variations that get faster and denser without ever changing the harmony underneath. The first doubles add running notes around the tune; the middle ones break into leaping figures and hand-crossings; by the sixth both hands are in continuous motion and the original dance has become a wall of sound. Eight minutes, and the escalation never lets up.","The closing item of Rameau's final harpsichord collection, published around 1728. Variation sets on a dance were a French tradition, but the steady ratcheting of difficulty is closer to what Handel and Bach were doing in the same decade with their own variation writing. Within five years Rameau had abandoned the harpsichord for the opera house and never returned to it on this scale.","The tune is never actually lost. In every variation the gavotte's melody is still there, usually on the strong beats, buried inside the figuration, and a listener who has memorised the theme can trace it through even the densest double. The sixth requires the hands to overlap on separate keyboards, the same physical trick Couperin used, so the piece ends as a feat of coordination as much as of speed.","It has become a favourite of pianists, Marcelle Meyer, Angela Hewitt and Alexandre Tharaud among them, who argue that the escalation works on a modern instrument. Harpsichordists reply that the last variations were written for two manuals and simply do not function without them. Both readings are recorded, and the difference in the final double is dramatic.","Memorise the gavotte on first hearing, it takes thirty seconds, then try to find it in each variation. Stop after the fourth if the piling-up gets overwhelming and come back to the last two later. Then hear the Goldberg Variations, written a decade or so afterwards, for the same principle handled at four times the length."],"rameau-castor-et-pollux-tristes-apprets":["Télaïre mourns her dead lover among funeral torches, and Rameau strips the orchestra almost bare to do it: dark low strings, and a bassoon winding a slow countermelody around the voice. The vocal line barely moves, returning again and again to the same held note before dropping away, in phrases separated by long instrumental silences. Six minutes, no display, and no rise to a climax at any point.","Castor et Pollux reached the Paris Opéra in October 1737, Rameau's third stage work, in the middle of a public quarrel between his supporters and the defenders of Lully, who had died fifty years before. His opponents called the harmony overloaded and un-French; his admirers called it the future. He revised the opera substantially in 1754, replacing the first act, and both versions are staged today.","The melody keeps returning to one repeated pitch, as though the singer cannot move away from it, while the harmony beneath her shifts underneath that fixed note and changes its meaning each time. It is a demonstration of Rameau's own theoretical claim that the bass governs the sense of a passage, staged as grief: she does not move, and the ground moves under her.","Berlioz singled the aria out for praise, and Debussy later invoked Rameau's clarity and proportion as the French answer to Wagner, making this piece a small piece of national argument. Scholars and directors still disagree about which version of the opera is stronger, since the 1737 first act supplies the mourning context that the 1754 revision cuts away.","Listen for the repeated note in the voice and count how many times she comes back to it. Then follow the bassoon, which is the only thing in the orchestra moving freely. Purcell's Dido's Lament makes the obvious pairing: two composers, two countries, both building grief out of refusing to let the music go anywhere."],"bach-brandenburg-concerto-no-3":["Nine string players, treated as nine equal voices rather than soloists against a crowd, with harpsichord and bass beneath. The outer movements run about five minutes each: a bustling opening built from one springy figure passed around the group, and a whirling finale in jig time. Between them sits Bach's famous joke, two chords lasting seconds where a slow movement should be. There are no wind instruments at all, and the sound is bright, busy and entirely string.","In March 1721 Bach, then court Kapellmeister at Köthen, sent a lavish presentation manuscript of six concertos to Christian Ludwig, Margrave of Brandenburg, with a florid French dedication recalling an audition some years earlier. The Margrave seems never to have had them played; the score sat in his library and was valued at a few groschen after his death. Köthen was a Calvinist court with no elaborate church music, so Bach spent those years writing instrumental pieces for a fine resident band.","The two-chord Adagio is not a missing page. It is a cadence marking a pause where a keyboard player was expected to improvise a short slow movement, and performers solve it differently every time: some insert a movement from another Bach work, some play a brief harpsichord flourish, some simply move on. Listen also to how the nine parts split into three choirs of three, answering each other like a conversation among equals.","Rediscovered in Berlin and published in 1850, the six concertos entered the repertoire slowly and became ubiquitous only in the twentieth century. The Third has been a battleground for period performance: how fast the finale should go, whether the cellos should be one to a part, and above all what to do with those two chords. Recordings range from a plush full string orchestra to nine players standing, and each argument is audible.","Count the entries at the start; the same short figure comes back nine times at nine pitches, and once you hear it you cannot stop tracking it. Then listen to the finale for the moment the whole group locks into one rhythm. Compare a one-player-per-part period recording with a big modern string orchestra: the difference is between a conversation and a chorus."],"bach-well-tempered-clavier-prelude-c":["Under two minutes, and almost nothing happens: one broken chord after another in a steady rippling figure that never changes shape. The right hand keeps the same rhythm throughout; only the harmony moves, sliding by small steps away from C major and back. Near the end the bass settles on one low note and the chords pile up over it before the last plain chord. It opens a collection of twenty-four preludes and fugues, one in every key.","Bach assembled Book I at Köthen in 1722 for the profit and use of musical youth desirous of learning, as his title page put it. The point was tuning: older keyboard temperaments made remote keys sound sour, and a well-tempered instrument let a player use all twenty-four. He was thirty-seven, employed by a music-loving prince, and teaching a growing household of sons and pupils. A second book followed some twenty years later in Leipzig.","An early manuscript in the hand of Bach's son Wilhelm Friedemann carries a shorter version, and one bar found in some sources was probably added by a later editor. Notice too that the whole prelude is a chord progression stripped of melody: Gounod could float an Ave Maria over it a century later precisely because the tune is missing and the harmony is complete.","The Well-Tempered Clavier circulated in manuscript for decades before its first printing in 1801, and Beethoven, Chopin and Schumann all learned from it. Its name has fuelled a long argument, since well-tempered probably does not mean today's equal temperament; scholars have proposed rival tunings, one of them read off the decorative squiggle on Bach's title page. Whether that reading holds is still disputed.","Play it once for the pattern and once for the bass, which walks downward almost the whole way; that descent is the piece. Then listen to the fugue that follows and hear how different the same key sounds when lines rather than chords carry it. Try a clavichord recording for intimacy, then Sviatoslav Richter's piano for weight."],"bach-mass-b-kyrie":["Four huge chords for chorus and orchestra on the word Kyrie, then silence. What follows is a long slow fugue in B minor, nearly nine minutes, in which five vocal parts wind a drooping chromatic subject around each other over a walking bass, doubled by flutes, oboes d'amore and strings. The middle Christe lightens into a duet for two sopranos in D major, and a second, brisker Kyrie in deliberately old church style closes the section.","In 1733 the Elector of Saxony died and a mourning period silenced Leipzig's church music. Bach used the pause to write a Kyrie and Gloria and sent the parts to the new Catholic court at Dresden with a petition for a court title, which he received in 1736. Only near the end of his life did he expand the work into a complete Latin Mass, far too long for any liturgy he ever served.","The chorus has five parts, with two soprano lines rather than one, and that gives the texture a top-heavy glow Bach kept for his grandest music. Listen for the sagging chromatic step near the start of the fugue subject: it returns transformed in the second Kyrie, where the orchestra doubles the voices note for note in the manner of Palestrina's century.","The complete Mass was not heard in full until the nineteenth century, and Berlin editions made it a concert monument rather than a service. Scholars still debate whether Bach conceived a unified work or assembled it late from older material, and whether he expected to hear it at all. Period performances since the 1980s have argued over choir size, some using a single voice per part.","Listen for the opening four chords, then the silence; everything after is a slow build. Track one line, the second sopranos or the bass, through the first fugue and notice how rarely anyone rests. Compare a large choral society with a one-voice-per-part period version: the first sounds like a cathedral, the second like five people arguing beautifully."],"bach-goldberg-variations-aria":["A slow sarabande in G major, thirty-two bars long, each half repeated, heavily decorated. The ornament is so thick that a tune is hard to hear at all; what matters is the bass underneath, thirty-two notes that will support the thirty variations to come. At the end of the hour-long cycle the same aria returns unchanged, and the effect of hearing it again after everything in between is the point of the entire design.","Published around 1741 as the fourth part of Bach's Clavier-Übung, for a two-manual harpsichord. His early biographer Forkel told of Count Keyserlingk, a Dresden diplomat with insomnia, and a young harpsichordist named Goldberg who played the variations through sleepless nights. The tale is doubtful, since the print carries no dedication and Goldberg was about fourteen, but the name stuck. The aria itself appears in a notebook Bach kept for his second wife, Anna Magdalena.","The bass, not the melody, is the theme. Every variation keeps the same harmonic skeleton, which is why the set can hold canons, a French overture and a closing quodlibet stitched from popular songs without ever losing its footing. Whether Bach wrote the aria himself has been questioned, since its manner is more French and galant than his usual hand; most scholars now accept it.","The variations were considered dry teaching material for much of the nineteenth century. Wanda Landowska's harpsichord recordings and then Glenn Gould's 1955 piano debut, fast and dry, turned them into a cornerstone, and his very different 1981 remake made the aria itself a subject of argument about tempo and repeats. Harpsichordists point out that the piece needs two keyboards; pianists cross hands and manage.","Listen to the aria, skip to the very end of a complete recording, and hear it again; that comparison is the fastest way to understand what variation form is for. Then follow the left hand rather than the right. Gould's 1955 and 1981 accounts differ by almost double in tempo, and hearing both in one sitting is an education."],"bach-st-matthew-passion-erbarme-dich":["An alto aria with solo violin, about seven minutes. The violin sings first, a long sighing line over a slow rocking accompaniment, and the voice then shadows it, the two circling each other for the whole aria. The words are Peter's, sung after he has denied Jesus three times and gone out to weep: have mercy, my God, for the sake of my tears. Nothing dramatic happens; the aria holds one moment of shame open.","Bach wrote the Passion for Good Friday vespers at St Thomas's, Leipzig, first heard probably in 1727 and revised into the version we know by 1736. He set the Gospel narrative in recitative, added chorales the congregation knew by heart, and interleaved reflective arias to texts by the local poet Picander. The forces are enormous for a church: two choirs, two orchestras and two organs, facing each other across the gallery.","The violin part is written as a second voice, and the two lines cross constantly, so it is often unclear which is leading. Bach reused the music in a later cantata with different words, a normal practice that shows how portable he considered such arias. Notice the repeated falling figure in the bass; it is a sigh, and it never resolves comfortably until the final bar.","The Passion vanished after Bach's death until Mendelssohn conducted a shortened version in Berlin in 1829, the event that launched the Bach revival and with it the whole idea of a classical repertoire. This aria has become the work's emblem, used in films and at funerals. Argument now centres on the size of the forces and on whether the alto should be a countertenor, a woman or a boy.","Listen to the violin alone for the first minute before the voice enters; that solo is the aria in miniature. Then hear it after the recitative that precedes it, the cock crow and the strings falling away, because the aria only lands if you know what has just happened. Kathleen Ferrier's old recording and a modern countertenor version make an instructive pair."],"bach-toccata-fugue-d":["It begins with the most recognisable gesture in organ music: a rapid turn high on the keyboard, plunging down two octaves, then a huge chord and a long silence. The toccata continues in that improvisatory manner, with flourishes, held chords and runs against pedal notes, for about three minutes. A fugue follows, its subject built from the same rocking figure, the feet joining late. The ending abandons the fugue entirely and returns to free, crashing rhetoric.","No autograph survives. The piece reaches us through a copy made by Johannes Ringk, probably decades after Bach's death, and its style points to a young organist steeped in the north German tradition of Buxtehude, whom Bach walked hundreds of miles to hear in 1705. A date around 1704 to 1710 is usually proposed. Organs of that time and place had bright, reedy voices and sharply defined pedals, quite unlike the thick nineteenth-century instruments it is often played on.","Several scholars have argued the work is not Bach's, or not originally for organ: it has odd parallel octaves, a very thin texture in places and a violinistic shape that has prompted reconstructions as a solo violin piece in A minor. Notice how much of it is single-line writing, long stretches where only one note sounds, unusual for Bach's organ music and entirely natural for a fiddle.","Mendelssohn played it publicly in 1840, and the nineteenth century adopted it as Bach at his most Romantic. Stokowski's orchestration for Disney's Fantasia in 1940 made it universal, after which horror films made it shorthand for menace. Doubt about authorship has grown since the 1980s without dislodging it from a single recital programme; audiences do not much care who wrote it.","Hear it twice: once on a big Romantic organ with a cathedral's reverberation, once on a small north German instrument where every line stays separate. Then listen to Buxtehude's free organ works and the family resemblance is obvious. Notice how often the music simply stops; those silences are as composed as the notes."],"bach-air-g-string":["The second movement of an orchestral suite: strings alone, no oboes or trumpets, about five minutes. The first violins carry a single unbroken melody that seems to rise and hang in the air, while the bass walks steadily beneath in even steps and the inner parts fill quietly between them. There is no contrasting middle section and no change of mood, only two halves, each repeated, in one of the simplest textures Bach ever wrote.","Written in D major for the Leipzig Collegium Musicum, the student and professional ensemble Bach directed from 1729 at Zimmermann's coffee house, where the city's music lovers paid nothing for admission and a great deal for coffee. The suite around it is a French-style entertainment: a grand overture, then dances. The nickname comes from the violinist August Wilhelmj, who in 1871 arranged the melody down a ninth so that it could be played entirely on the violin's lowest string.","Wilhelmj's arrangement changes the piece more than most listeners realise; the transposition and the single-string tone give it a nasal, throbbing quality Bach never wrote. In the original the tune sits in the violins' comfortable middle register and the effect is cooler and more level. Listen to the bass, which moves almost entirely in even steps: it is the tick against which the melody floats free.","The suites were published only in 1854 and were long treated as light music beside the Passions. The Air became a Victorian and then a wedding standard in arrangements for string orchestra, organ, brass and synthesiser. Period performances have restored the original scoring and a lighter, more dance-like pulse; the argument now is whether the movement should breathe slowly or move along as an air rather than a hymn.","Find a recording of the whole suite and hear the Air where it belongs, straight after a brilliant trumpet-and-drums overture; the sudden hush of strings alone is the effect Bach designed. Then compare the original D major version with Wilhelmj's arrangement and decide which one you have been carrying in your head."],"bach-cello-suite-no-1-prelude":["Two and a half minutes for one unaccompanied cello, almost all of it a single unbroken stream of broken chords in the same rhythm. The player never stops; there are no rests and no double stops until the very end. Harmony is implied rather than stated, the ear assembling chords out of a line moving too fast to hear as separate notes. Two thirds through, the music locks onto a low open string and grinds against it before releasing into the final flourish.","Probably written at Köthen around 1720, where Bach's employer kept excellent instrumentalists and no church choir to write for. The cello was then still a bass instrument climbing towards solo status, and nothing this demanding existed for it. No autograph survives; the earliest source is a copy in Anna Magdalena Bach's hand made after 1727, with slurs so ambiguous that every modern edition is partly guesswork.","Bach never writes a chord here. He gives one note at a time and lets your ear supply the rest, a technique called implied polyphony, and it is how a single line can sound like three. Listen for the long passage over that held bass note, where the harmony tightens without moving. The Anna Magdalena copy also omits almost all dynamics, so every swell you hear is the player's decision.","The suites were considered exercises until Pablo Casals found a copy in a Barcelona shop as a boy in the 1890s, studied them for twelve years and recorded them in the 1930s, the moment they became concert music. Since then the argument has run between his warm, rhetorical manner and leaner period playing on gut strings at lower pitch. A 2019 proposal that Anna Magdalena composed the suites has found no scholarly support.","Listen for the moment the bass note takes over and the music seems to stall; the release afterwards is the shape of the whole piece. Then hear the same prelude on a Baroque cello with gut strings and on a modern instrument: the first is rougher and speaks more clearly, the second sings. Casals, Rostropovich and Anner Bylsma make three different arguments."],"bach-jesu-joy-man-s-desiring":["A chorale movement about three and a half minutes long whose charm is a mismatch between two layers. The orchestra plays a flowing triplet melody that never pauses; over it the choir enters in long plain notes with a Lutheran hymn tune, phrase by phrase, waiting through the ripples between lines. Trumpet, oboes, strings and continuo support them. The same movement closes both halves of the cantata, with different words each time.","Bach wrote the cantata Herz und Mund und Tat und Leben for the Feast of the Visitation in Leipzig on 2 July 1723, expanding a shorter Weimar version from 1716. It was his first year in the job and he was producing a cantata almost every week for the city's main churches. The hymn tune is Werde munter, mein Gemüte by Johann Schop, familiar to every person in the pews.","The English title is not a translation. The German says roughly that Jesus remains my joy; the words everyone sings came from the poet Robert Bridges in 1926, fitted to Myra Hess's piano arrangement. Notice that the triplet melody is not accompaniment but a countermelody that begins before the choir and continues under the hymn without ever adjusting to it.","The movement escaped its cantata almost entirely. Hess's 1926 transcription, played at her wartime National Gallery concerts in London, made it a symbol of consolation, and it has since been arranged for every possible combination and installed at weddings worldwide. Purists point out that the original tempo is brisker and the choir smaller than massed performances suggest; heard in context it is a plainer, more dancing piece.","Listen for the entry of the voices several bars after the orchestra has begun, and notice they are singing something completely different from the tune you know. Then find the whole cantata and hear the movement arrive twice. A period recording with a small choir will sound faster and lighter than any arrangement you have met."],"bach-brandenburg-concerto-no-5":["Three soloists step out from a small string band for about twenty-one minutes. The first movement is bright and busy until the harpsichord takes over completely: everyone else falls silent for a cadenza of some sixty-five bars, a torrent of scales and broken chords that ends by hauling the orchestra back in. A quiet middle movement leaves only the three soloists alone together, and the finale is a lively fugue in dance rhythm.","Bach compiled the six concertos in 1721 for the Margrave of Brandenburg, but this one probably dates from around 1719, when he travelled to Berlin to buy a large new harpsichord for the Köthen court. He seems to have written the solo part to display the instrument he had chosen, and very likely played it himself. Nothing in earlier music gives a keyboard player this much unaccompanied space inside a concerto.","An earlier version survives with a much shorter cadenza, about eighteen bars, so Bach deliberately expanded it into the showpiece we know. Listen for the moment the strings drop out: the harpsichord's left hand keeps the bass going, so the accompaniment is not removed but absorbed. The flute part suits a transverse flute rather than a recorder, still a novelty in German courts.","The cadenza has been called the birth certificate of the keyboard concerto, and Mozart's and Beethoven's piano concertos descend from it. Nineteenth-century performances used a piano and a full orchestra; the early-music movement restored the harpsichord and one player per part, which changes the balance completely, since a harpsichord cannot dominate a large string body. Whether the string parts should be doubled at all is still argued.","Wait for the cadenza, which you will know by the orchestra vanishing, and listen to how long Bach dares to hold the stage. Then compare a harpsichord recording with one on modern piano; the piano makes it sound like early Mozart, the harpsichord like nothing else. The slow movement, for three players alone, is the best place to hear the soloists listening to each other."],"bach-concerto-two-violins":["Sixteen minutes in three movements, with two violins treated as exact equals against a small string group. The outer movements are tightly argued and fast. The famous one is the middle Largo, where the soloists exchange a long arching melody in overlapping phrases, each finishing the other's line over a gentle rocking accompaniment. Nothing separates them in difficulty or prominence, which is why the piece is so often used to describe friendship.","Usually dated to Bach's Leipzig years around 1730, when he directed the Collegium Musicum, though it may go back to Köthen a decade earlier. He also arranged it for two harpsichords, transposed down a tone, which suggests it was useful repertoire he kept in circulation. Vivaldi's concertos, which Bach had copied out and rewritten as a young man in Weimar, stand behind its structure; the counterpoint does not.","Listen to how the two soloists enter in the first movement: the second states the theme and the first answers a bar or two later at a different pitch, so the movement begins as a fugue rather than as a concerto. In the Largo the phrases are staggered so that neither player ever reaches the top of the line alone. Bach's own arrangement for two harpsichords survives complete.","The concerto has been a teaching and duet standard since the nineteenth-century Bach Gesellschaft edition. Famous recordings have often paired a great violinist with a pupil or colleague, David and Igor Oistrakh, Menuhin and Enescu, turning the piece into a document of transmission between generations. Period performers argue for faster outer movements and less vibrato in the Largo, and both readings are common today.","Follow one soloist only, all the way through the slow movement, and notice you can hear the other perfectly well anyway; that double attention is the pleasure of the piece. Then listen to the opening and count how many bars pass before the second soloist speaks. Menuhin and Enescu, pupil and teacher, remain the most affecting version."],"bach-violin-concerto":["Fourteen minutes in three movements for solo violin, strings and continuo. The first drives forward on a compact insistent figure that keeps returning between the soloist's excursions. The slow movement sets a long-breathed violin melody over a bass that repeats the same short descending pattern almost throughout, like a ground. The finale is a fast dance in three, with the soloist tearing through arpeggios and, near the end, playing over a held drone.","Probably assembled at Leipzig around 1730 for the Collegium Musicum, again possibly rooted in Köthen material. Bach also transcribed it for harpsichord in G minor, which is how many of his lost violin works survive at all. The Italian concerto model, fast then slow then fast with recurring orchestral refrains, reached him through Vivaldi, whose scores he had studied and rearranged in Weimar around 1713.","The slow movement's bass is a passacaglia in disguise, the same short figure over and over while the violin never repeats itself. Count it if you like, but the better trick is to notice the moments Bach breaks the pattern in order to change key. In the first movement, listen for where the soloist takes the orchestra's refrain and turns it inside out rather than answering it with new material.","Only two solo violin concertos by Bach survive complete, plus the Double, which has made them staples of every conservatory and competition. Joseph Joachim's performances established a big Romantic manner; the twentieth century swung towards leaner playing on gut strings, and the mainstream now sits between. There is a long argument over whether the harpsichord transcriptions preserve details lost from the violin versions.","Start with the slow movement and listen only to the bass for the first minute, then let the violin in and hear how it floats free of a floor that never moves. In the finale, notice the soloist's stubborn insistence on one rhythm. Hilary Hahn's modern account and Rachel Podger's period one are useful opposites."],"bach-orchestral-suite-no-3-ouverture":["Nine minutes in three clear parts: a grand slow opening in dotted rhythms with trumpets, drums and oboes blazing; a long fast fugal middle where the strings run and the trumpets punch through; then the opening music again, shortened. The scoring is Bach's most festive, and the first sound is a hammered chord that stops a room. The dances of the suite, including the celebrated Air, follow it.","Written in D major, the trumpet key, for the Leipzig Collegium Musicum around 1730 and using a form imported from Versailles. The French overture, invented by Lully for Louis XIV, was originally the music the king entered to, which is why it sounds like an announcement. Leipzig's Zimmermann coffee house had no king but did have a paying public and, for summer, a garden.","The dotted rhythms in the outer sections are written one way and, by convention, played another, sharper and more clipped than the notation looks; how far to over-dot them is a live performance question rather than a fixed rule. In the fast section, listen for the moment the trumpets stop competing with the violins and simply hold the harmony. Bach uses them sparingly to keep brilliance from becoming noise.","The four suites were long thought a unified set and are now understood as separate occasional pieces gathered later. Nineteenth-century performances used huge forces and slow tempos, and the trumpet parts, written high on a valveless instrument, became so difficult that they were reassigned or cut. Natural-trumpet players have restored them, and the debate has moved to pitch and to how many strings the balance needs.","Listen for the very first chord and then for the entry of the running strings; the switch from ceremony to motion is the whole idea of a French overture. Notice how the trumpets are used as colour rather than melody. Then hear a Lully overture beside it, and let the Air that follows complete the contrast."],"bach-italian-concerto":["A concerto for one player. Thirteen minutes in three movements, written for a two-manual harpsichord so that the keyboard can imitate an orchestra on one manual and a soloist on the other; Bach marks forte and piano to tell the player which. The outer movements bustle with the refrains and departures of an Italian concerto, and the middle Andante is a long ornate melody over a walking bass, among his most vocal keyboard inventions.","Published in 1735 as the first half of the second part of the Clavier-Übung, paired with a French Overture in B minor: Italian and French styles set side by side for comparison, a fashionable argument of the day. Bach was fifty, running Leipzig's church music and selling prints from his own house. He had absorbed Vivaldi's concerto style two decades earlier by copying and rewriting Italian scores for keyboard.","The dynamic markings are unusual for Bach and are not expression marks; they are registration instructions telling the player to change manuals, which is how a harpsichord fakes a crowd and a soloist. In the slow movement the left hand keeps a steady bass and a quiet inner voice at once while the right hand sings, three roles for two hands. On a piano all of this must be reinvented with touch.","The critic Johann Adolph Scheibe, who had attacked Bach's music as turgid and over-elaborate, praised the Italian Concerto as a model of its kind, a rare contemporary rave. Pianists from Busoni onward have claimed it, and the harpsichord-versus-piano argument is sharper here than in most Bach because the piece is built around a mechanical feature the piano does not have. Both traditions flourish.","Listen for the alternation of loud and soft in the first movement and hear an orchestra appear and disappear inside one instrument. Then give the Andante your full attention: it is an operatic aria with no singer. Compare a two-manual harpsichord with a pianist such as Perahia and notice what each must invent to replace the other's advantages."],"bach-passacaglia-fugue-c":["Fourteen minutes for organ, built on eight bars of bass line stated alone on the pedals at the very start. Remember it, because it will be there in one form or another for the next ten minutes. Twenty variations pile figuration above it, thinning and thickening, until the bass leaves the feet and becomes the subject of a fugue where it is combined with two other themes. The ending is a single enormous grinding chord before the close.","An early work, probably from Bach's twenties in Arnstadt or Weimar around 1708 to 1712, when he was an organist with a reputation for improvising too long during services and once for a street fight with a bassoonist. The first half of the ground bass is borrowed from a trio by the French organist André Raison. The north German tradition of Buxtehude and Pachelbel had made ostinato organ pieces a speciality.","The bass does not stay in the pedals. Around halfway through Bach moves it into the upper voices, which is why some variations feel as though the floor has been removed. The fugue then uses only the first half of the theme, combining it with a jaunty countersubject that had already appeared among the variations, so the fugue is a summary rather than an appendix.","Long treated as an apprentice work outshone by the mature Leipzig chorales, it is now among Bach's most admired structures, praised by Brahms and orchestrated by Stokowski, Respighi and others. Some scholars argue it was written for a pedal harpsichord rather than an organ, since Arnstadt's instrument had limited pedals. Organists disagree about registration: a steady build or terraced blocks.","Memorise the opening eight bars, hum them, then keep checking whether you can still find them. About two-thirds through you will lose them, which is exactly the point. Listen to a north German organ recording first, then a Stokowski-style orchestration to hear what a nineteenth-century ear found in it."],"bach-wachet-auf-ruft-uns-stimme":["A church cantata in seven movements, about half an hour. It opens with a slow processional in which the sopranos deliver the hymn tune in long notes over a striding orchestra in dotted rhythm while the lower voices weave excited commentary beneath. Two love duets for soprano and bass, the Soul and Christ, follow. Between them sits the movement Bach later published for organ, tenors singing the chorale against a suave string melody. A plain four-part harmonisation ends it.","First performed in Leipzig on 25 November 1731, the twenty-seventh Sunday after Trinity, a Sunday that exists only when Easter falls very early and which had not occurred during Bach's tenure before. He therefore had no earlier cantata to reuse and wrote a new one. The text builds on Philipp Nicolai's 1599 hymn about the wise virgins and the coming bridegroom, a parable Lutherans read as the end of time.","The duets are frank love songs, full of sighs, echoes and entwining lines, because Lutheran devotion borrowed the language of the Song of Songs without embarrassment. Listen for the opening movement's limping dotted rhythm: it is a procession approaching, and it never resolves into an even walk. Bach printed the fourth movement in 1748 among the Schübler Chorales, the only cantata movement he published in his lifetime.","This was among the first of the two hundred cantatas to enter the modern repertoire, helped by its tunefulness and by the organ arrangement's separate fame. Twentieth-century performance moved from large choral societies to small ensembles, and the one-voice-per-part case argued by Joshua Rifkin in the 1980s has changed how the opening chorus sounds, as chamber texture rather than a wall. The tenor movement is now heard both ways.","Go to the fourth movement first, the one with the tenors, and hear how the string melody has nothing to do with the hymn yet fits it perfectly. Then hear the same music on the organ. In the opening, follow the sopranos' long notes and treat everything beneath them as traffic moving around one slow figure walking through it."],"bach-sheep-may-safely-graze":["A soprano aria with two recorders and continuo, about five minutes. The recorders play in parallel almost throughout, a soft cool sound above a bass that walks in slow even steps; the voice enters late and moves in long calm phrases. Nothing is hurried and nothing is dramatic. It is pastoral music in the literal sense, a shepherd watching a flock, and the two flutes are the shepherd's pipes.","From the Hunt Cantata, written for the birthday of Duke Christian of Saxe-Weissenfels, probably in February 1713 and performed after a day's hunting. Bach was court organist at Weimar and taking freelance work where he could. The text flatters the duke: sheep graze safely where a good shepherd watches, and a country prospers under a wise ruler. The cantata is secular and mythological, with Diana and Pan among its characters.","The serenity is political. Its argument is that the ruler keeps the peace, and the recorders, already old-fashioned instruments in 1713, are chosen to signal an idealised countryside rather than a real one. Notice that the bass never varies its tread; the calm is manufactured by that regularity. Bach knew the effect and reused the cantata's material for later congratulatory pieces.","The aria travelled far from its origins. Piano transcriptions by Egon Petri and Mary Howe, orchestral arrangements by Stokowski and Walton, and choral versions have made it a devotional standard for listeners who assume it is sacred. Recent performances have returned it to the whole cantata, where its courtly flattery is audible again. Recorder players argue about pitch and whether one instrument per part carries in a hall.","Listen to the two recorders alone before the singer enters and hear how thoroughly Bach avoids letting them argue. Then find the aria inside a complete recording of the Hunt Cantata and notice how odd it sounds surrounded by horns and hunting choruses. Compare a period-recorder version with a modern flute arrangement; the older instruments sound smaller and stranger."],"handel-messiah-hallelujah":["Four minutes for chorus, trumpets, drums, strings and continuo, closing the second of the oratorio's three parts. The chorus hammers a single word in block chords, then breaks into a fugue on the promise that he shall reign for ever; a third idea, King of Kings, is sung on one repeated high note while the lower voices answer beneath. Handel keeps cutting between these three ways of setting text, and the trumpets, silent for long stretches, mark the arrivals.","Handel composed Messiah in twenty-four days in the late summer of 1741 to a libretto compiled entirely from scripture by Charles Jennens, and gave the first performance in Dublin on 13 April 1742 in aid of charities including a debtors' prison. London had lost its appetite for his Italian operas; English oratorio, sung in the vernacular in theatres rather than churches, was his solution and it worked. He conducted it annually for the Foundling Hospital until his death.","The chorus is not the end of the work. Two thirds of the way through, it marks the triumph of the risen Christ before an entire third part on resurrection. Listen for how rarely all the voices sing together; the impression of massed sound comes from alternation. The tradition of standing rests on a story that George II rose at a London performance, for which there is no contemporary evidence.","Messiah was received cautiously in London, and Jennens himself grumbled that Handel had not taken enough trouble. It grew steadily until nineteenth-century festivals used choirs of thousands, with Mozart's 1789 reorchestration in wide use. The twentieth century reversed course, since Handel's own performances used perhaps twenty singers and a small band. Both scales survive, and the argument is really whether the piece is a public monument or a theatre work.","Listen for the three separate ideas and count how often Handel cuts between them; the excitement is editing, not volume. Then hear the very end, where everything stops before the final chord. Compare a big amateur chorus at Christmas with a period band of thirty and you will hear two different pieces, both legitimate."],"handel-water-music-alla-hornpipe":["Three minutes of D major noise in the best sense: two natural horns and two trumpets answering each other across the ensemble, with oboes and strings between them, in a swinging dance rhythm. The two brass choirs never play the same thing at the same time; they trade phrases like rival bands on opposite banks. A quieter middle section for strings and oboes in the minor gives the ear a rest before the opening returns.","On 17 July 1717 King George I travelled by barge from Whitehall to Chelsea accompanied by a second barge carrying about fifty musicians, and a newspaper reported that the music pleased him so much he had it played three times. The suites we call the Water Music were assembled from that occasion. Outdoor performance dictated the scoring: horns and trumpets carry over water, harpsichords do not, and the D major suite is the loudest of the three.","Horns were a novelty in English orchestras in 1717, hunting instruments brought indoors, and this may be among the first English works to use them prominently. Natural horns and trumpets have no valves, so every note comes from the player's lips and the instrument's harmonic series, which is why the melodies leap in the shapes they do. The order and grouping of the movements is editorial; no autograph survives.","The story that Handel wrote the music to regain the King's favour after leaving Hanover is a nineteenth-century embellishment; the two men were on good terms years earlier. The suites were published piecemeal and their movement order remains unsettled. Modern performance splits between period bands using hand-stopped natural brass, with its slightly wild intonation, and modern orchestras that smooth everything; the older instruments sound more like an event.","Follow the horns, then listen again following the trumpets, and hear the piece as a conversation between two groups placed apart. Some recordings separate them in the stereo field, which is worth seeking out. Compare with the Royal Fireworks Music, written thirty-two years later for a far bigger wind band, to hear Handel scaling the same idea up."],"handel-sarabande-d":["A slow dance in three time, about four minutes with its two variations, whose power comes from bareness: a stern descending bass, plain chords and a melody that barely moves. Written for harpsichord, it consists of a sixteen-bar theme played twice and then decorated twice more, each variation adding faster notes without changing the harmony. Nothing is added at the end; the piece reaches its last chord and stops.","Published in London in 1733 in Handel's second collection of keyboard suites and probably written years earlier. His keyboard music was mostly a by-product of teaching and of his own improvising, printed partly to forestall pirated editions. The sarabande, originally a fast and possibly scandalous dance from the Spanish Americas, had become in France and England a slow and grave one, and its stately tread was a standard component of a suite.","The bass line, descending step by step, is one of the oldest laments in European music and appears in Purcell's Dido and in dozens of Baroque grounds. That inheritance is why the piece sounds tragic although Handel wrote no tragedy into it. The variations add motion but never harmony, so the ear hears increasing agitation over a floor that refuses to change.","The sarabande was a minor item until Stanley Kubrick used a heavily arranged orchestral version through Barry Lyndon in 1975, where its repetitions became the film's sense of fate. Since then it has been recorded on guitar, orchestra, synthesiser and choir, usually slower and grander than any Baroque performance would have been. Harpsichordists point out that on the original instrument it is drier and quicker, and closer to a dance than a dirge.","Hear it on a harpsichord first, where it behaves like a dance, then in the film version with its slow strings and drums. The gap between them is a lesson in how arrangement makes meaning. Then listen for the descending bass and go and find the same shape underneath Dido's Lament."],"handel-ombra-mai-fu":["Three and a half minutes, and it is a love song to a tree. The king Xerxes stands in a garden and praises the shade of a plane; the orchestra plays a short gently swaying introduction and the voice enters with a broad melody that rises and falls in long arcs over quiet strings. There is no fast section, no display and no contrasting middle, an unusually short and simple aria for an opera in 1738, which is part of why it became detached from one.","Serse, first staged at the King's Theatre, London, in April 1738, was among Handel's last operas and a commercial failure that ran five nights. Italian opera in London was collapsing under the cost of star singers and competition from English-language entertainment. The title role was written for the soprano castrato Caffarelli and is sung today by mezzo-sopranos and countertenors. The opera's mixture of comedy and seriousness confused audiences expecting one or the other.","The aria is a joke that stopped being funny. Serse opens with a king mooning over foliage, and the libretto, adapted from a much older Venetian text, is comic; Handel set it with complete sincerity, and the joke evaporated. The nineteenth century renamed it Handel's Largo, although the marking is larghetto, and fitted it with sacred words for church use.","Serse vanished for two centuries and returned only with the twentieth-century Handel revival, in which Germany led and Britain followed. The aria meanwhile survived alone in a thousand arrangements for violin, organ, brass band and funeral service. Its restoration to the opera reopened the question of voice type; countertenors now sing a role that transposing tenors and baritones once claimed, and in context the aria sounds strange again.","Listen once as the famous slow melody, then find a staged recording and hear it as the opening scene of a comedy about a man in a garden. Notice that the aria has no contrasting middle section: it says one thing and stops. Compare a countertenor with a mezzo-soprano, since the tone colour changes the character completely."],"handel-zadok-priest":["Five and a half minutes, and the first two are almost unbearable. Strings alone spin quiet arpeggios that rise, slowly, with the harmony refusing to settle, for twenty-three bars, and then the whole choir and orchestra crash in on one word. The rest is short: a stately middle section, then a hammering Amen and shouts of God save the King over drums and trumpets. Seven vocal parts, four trumpets, timpani, oboes, bassoons and strings.","Written for the coronation of George II at Westminster Abbey on 11 October 1727, one of four anthems Handel supplied; he had become a British subject earlier that year. The text comes from the anointing of Solomon in the Book of Kings and has been used at English coronations since 973. Handel had never written a coronation anthem before and reportedly ignored the clergy's suggestions about which words to set, choosing his own.","The power of the opening is a trick of withholding: the harmony keeps promising to resolve and the arpeggios keep climbing without the bass moving decisively, so the entry releases pressure built over two minutes. The first performance apparently went badly. The Abbey's forces were large and under-rehearsed, and a newspaper reported the anthems sung in the wrong order with one omitted entirely.","Zadok has been sung at every British coronation since 1727, an unbroken run that makes it the most durable occasional piece in the repertoire. It was rearranged for the Champions League anthem in 1992, which introduced its opening to audiences with no idea of its origins. Performance divides on tempo, a stately processional pace against Handel's likely brisker one, and on whether the anthem needs a cathedral's echo to work.","Do not skip the introduction; the whole piece is the wait. Listen to the strings climbing and try to guess when the choir will enter, then hear it live in a large building if you ever can. Compare a cathedral recording with a period-band one: reverberation smooths the harmony, while the drier version lets you hear how the tension is made."],"handel-music-royal-fireworks":["Eighteen minutes of wind band. A long French overture opens with dotted ceremonial rhythms and a fast fugal middle, then four shorter movements follow: a bourrée, a slow piece called La Paix, a bustling La Réjouissance and two minuets. Handel's original scoring is a wall of wind, with two dozen oboes, a dozen bassoons, nine trumpets, nine horns and three pairs of kettledrums, and no strings at all. He added strings later for indoor performance.","Commissioned for a public celebration in Green Park, London, on 27 April 1749 marking the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle, which ended the War of the Austrian Succession. A wooden pavilion four hundred feet long was built for the display. George II wanted only martial instruments and Handel wanted strings; the King won for the occasion. A rehearsal in Vauxhall Gardens six days earlier drew a crowd reported at twelve thousand and stopped traffic on London Bridge for hours.","The event itself was a fiasco. The pavilion caught fire during the display, part of it collapsed, and the designer reportedly drew his sword on the organiser. None of this is in the music, which is unfailingly cheerful. Listen to how Handel arranges the wind in blocks, oboes against horns against trumpets, so that the piece works outdoors, where counterpoint would vanish but alternating colours carry.","The huge original scoring was long thought impractical and rarely attempted; twentieth-century performances used ordinary orchestras until period bands began mounting the wind version, which sounds coarser, louder and far more like an event than a concert. Scholars have argued over Handel's own numbers, since his manuscript and the King's demands do not perfectly agree. Both versions, with and without strings, are now standard.","Find a recording with the full original wind scoring, dozens of oboes and bassoons, and play it loudly; the point is mass, not finesse. Then listen to La Paix, where the same forces play softly and turn into something like a hymn. Compare with the Water Music, the same composer solving a similar problem thirty-two years earlier."],"handel-lascia-ch-io-pianga":["A slow aria in F major, about five minutes, sung by a woman held captive by a sorceress: let me weep over my cruel fate, and let me long for freedom. The melody moves in short phrases separated by silences, as if the singer keeps having to stop, and the strings answer in the gaps. A middle section rises in agitation before the opening returns, decorated by the singer. The accompaniment is spare, strings and continuo and nothing more.","From Rinaldo, first performed at the Queen's Theatre, London, on 24 February 1711: the first Italian opera written for the London stage, produced with live sparrows, fireworks and elaborate machinery. Handel was twenty-five and newly arrived from Italy, and the opera made him famous in England, where he spent the rest of his life. The melody was not new, having served in a Hamburg opera and again as a dance in an oratorio in Rome.","The tune's history is the interesting part. It began as a dance in Almira in 1705, became an Eastern queen's aria in Il trionfo del tempo, and only in Rinaldo acquired the words everyone knows, which was normal working practice for a composer under deadline. Notice that the rhythm is still a sarabande, in three with a stress on the second beat: the aria is a dance slowed to the pace of grief.","Rinaldo was a sensation and a scandal, with Addison mocking its imported absurdities in The Spectator, and it held the stage for years before the whole genre collapsed. Revivals since the 1980s have restored it, and the aria has meanwhile had a second life in film, notably in Farinelli, where a castrato voice was synthesised from a soprano and a countertenor. Sopranos and countertenors both claim the role today.","Listen to the silences between phrases, which do as much work as the notes. Then hear the singer's decorated return of the opening section and compare how much they add, because that ornamentation is where a Baroque singer's personality lives. Try a period-instrument recording where the strings play without vibrato; the aria becomes cooler and sadder."],"handel-arrival-queen-sheba":["Three minutes of pure motion for two oboes and strings, opening the third act of an oratorio. There is no singing. The oboes chase each other in rapid alternating phrases while the strings drive underneath, and the whole piece is built from that one exchange, repeated at different pitches with brief string interludes between. It ends abruptly, because in the theatre a queen has just walked in and the chorus is about to greet her.","From Solomon, first performed at Covent Garden in March 1749. Handel had abandoned Italian opera for English oratorio and was writing for a middle-class audience that liked biblical subjects, big choruses and, in this case, a flattering parallel between Solomon's peaceful prosperous kingdom and Georgian Britain. Solomon contains a celebrated double chorus in eight parts; this sinfonia is the lightest thing in the score.","The name is a later addition. Handel's score simply marks a sinfonia at the head of the act, and the title we use comes from editors and from the stage action that follows. Listen for the two oboes: they are written as a duet rather than as solo and accompaniment, so the piece is essentially a trio sonata for two treble instruments and bass, expanded with strings.","Solomon was not among Handel's more popular oratorios and was neglected until Thomas Beecham's advocacy and the later Handel revivals. The sinfonia escaped and became a standard wedding processional and a broadcast signature, played at the London Olympics opening ceremony in 2012. Period performances take it faster and lighter than the wedding tradition, which tends to broaden it into a march.","Follow one oboe and try to keep track of when it is leading and when it is answering, because the two swap constantly. Then listen to the chorus that follows in a complete recording and the sinfonia turns from a standalone bonbon into an entrance. A period band with reedier oboes will sound sharper and less plush."],"handel-messiah-unto-us-child-is-born":["A four-minute chorus that begins almost secretly: the sopranos alone, quiet, with a phrase that runs quickly on one syllable. The other voices enter one by one and repeat the run, the texture thickening, and then the whole choir and orchestra land together on the words Wonderful, Counsellor with a shock of full sound. Handel does this three times, each time restarting from a hush, so the chorus is a series of controlled explosions.","Part of the first section of Messiah, which follows the prophecy of a coming Messiah before turning to the nativity. Handel wrote the whole oratorio between 22 August and 14 September 1741, borrowing heavily from himself throughout. This chorus began as an Italian secular duet composed a few weeks earlier on a text about blind love and Cupid's arrows; the vocal runs were originally two solo voices flirting.","The running passages give the game away. They are written for two agile soloists, not a choir, which is why they are so hard to sing cleanly in a large group, and knowing the original words changes how they sound: the chorus's brilliance is recycled courtship. Notice that the great chords on Wonderful are simply the point where Handel abandons the borrowed material and writes something new.","Handel's borrowing from himself and from others was noticed in his lifetime, became a nineteenth-century scandal about originality, and is now studied for what he did with the material. This chorus is a test of any choir, since too large a group turns the runs into a smear. The early-music movement's smaller forces have made the passagework audible again for the first time in two centuries.","Count the entries at the start, soprano then alto then tenor then bass, and notice each begins alone. Then wait for the crash on Wonderful and hear how quickly Handel resets to quiet afterwards. Compare a big festival chorus with a chamber choir of thirty: the runs will be a wash in one and a scatter of clear notes in the other."],"handel-harmonious-blacksmith":["The final movement of a harpsichord suite: a short square tune of sixteen bars played twice, then five variations that get progressively faster. The first variations decorate the melody in the right hand; then the left hand takes over the running notes while the right holds the tune; then both hands run at once in rapid triplets. About five minutes in all, and it never leaves the theme's harmony for a moment.","Published in London in 1720 in Handel's first collection of harpsichord suites, issued partly to head off unauthorised Dutch reprints. He was then running the Royal Academy of Music, London's Italian opera company, and keyboard publication was a sideline aimed at the growing market of amateur players. The theme is an air with variations of a kind common in English virginal music a century earlier.","The nickname is entirely invented and dates from about 1819, long after Handel's death, when a story attached itself about the composer sheltering from rain in a forge and hearing a smith sing over his anvil. The variations are technically a doubles set, each one halving the note values, which is a French tradition; Handel's Italian and German training is audible in the harmony but not in the form.","The piece was a staple of nineteenth-century piano teaching and appeared in every anthology, which is how the fictional anvil spread. Musicologists dismantled the legend by the late nineteenth century, and the title survives because it is better than Air and variations. Harpsichordists play it as part of a whole suite and pianists as an encore, with a long argument about whether the last variation should go as fast as possible.","Listen for the moment the running notes move from the right hand to the left; it is the piece's one structural surprise and it changes which hand you follow. Then hear the whole suite so that the variations arrive as a finale. A harpsichord recording is crisper and quicker, while a piano makes the tune sing and blurs the runs."],"d-scarlatti-sonata-k-141":["Four minutes of one idea taken to an extreme: a single note repeated so fast it becomes a buzz, over and over, while the left hand throws out sharp chords and leaping figures beneath. On a harpsichord the hammering produces a hard glittering rattle no other keyboard music makes. Between the barrages come brief passages of melody, quickly overrun. Two halves, each repeated, and no slow section anywhere.","Scarlatti spent his last twenty-five years in Madrid as music master to Maria Barbara of Portugal, who became Queen of Spain, writing sonatas apparently for her private use. Nearly five hundred and fifty survive, most in manuscript volumes copied in Venice in the 1750s rather than in print. He was in his sixties and had left Italy's opera world entirely; these pieces were not published, not performed publicly and not intended for anyone but a court.","The repeated notes almost certainly imitate a guitar technique, and behind that the mandolin and the Spanish street music Scarlatti heard daily. Iberian court harpsichords often had an action that made such repetition possible; on a piano the effect must be faked with alternating fingers. Listen also for the crushed dissonances, notes a semitone apart struck together, which sound like a flamenco strum landing hard.","The sonatas were treated as teaching pieces and curiosities until the twentieth century, when Ralph Kirkpatrick's 1953 catalogue and recordings reordered them and argued that many were conceived in pairs. Vladimir Horowitz made a handful famous on the piano; harpsichordists insist the writing is barely playable as written on anything else. K. 141 in particular has become a competition showpiece in which speed is the whole argument.","Hear it on harpsichord first, where the repeated notes rattle like a machine, then on piano, where they become a tremolo. Listen for the crushed, wrong-sounding chords and enjoy them, because they are deliberate. Then play a flamenco guitar recording afterwards and the family resemblance across two centuries will be obvious."],"d-scarlatti-sonata-k-466":["One of the slow ones, about six minutes, marked andante moderato: a long singing melody in the right hand over a quietly pulsing accompaniment, in F minor, with the tune constantly bending into notes that sound borrowed from outside the key. The mood is grave and slightly veiled. Like almost every sonata in the set it falls into two repeated halves, the second wandering further from home before finding its way back.","Written in Madrid in the 1750s for a queen who played every day, in a court where Scarlatti also worked alongside the castrato Farinelli, hired to sing a melancholy king to sleep. Nothing suggests these pieces were meant for an audience. The manuscripts were bound into thirteen volumes and left to Farinelli, which is how they survived at all; they reached Venice and only slowly reached print.","The strange colours come from the inflections of Spanish folk music, a flattened second degree that gives the melody its Moorish tinge, grafted onto Italian keyboard writing. Listen for the moments the bass stops moving entirely and the melody hangs unsupported. Kirkpatrick argued that many sonatas were composed as pairs sharing a key, and this one is usually played with the brisk sonata that follows it.","The slow sonatas were long overshadowed by the acrobatic ones, since the fast pieces made better encores. Pianists from Clara Haskil to Mikhail Pletnev have championed them, and the instrument question is sharpest here: a modern piano can sustain the melody in a way a harpsichord cannot, while the harpsichord's clarity exposes the harmony. Chronology remains unsettled, since almost none of the sonatas are dated.","Take it slowly and listen for the notes that sound wrong, the flattened ones, because that is where the Spanish accent lives. Then hear a fast sonata immediately afterwards and understand why Kirkpatrick thought they belonged in pairs. Compare Pletnev's piano with Scott Ross's harpsichord for two entirely different pieces."],"d-scarlatti-sonata-k-380":["Five minutes in E major, marked andante comodo, and it sounds like a ceremony seen from a distance: a stately walking rhythm, chords answering a simple call as if trumpets were sounding across a courtyard. Halfway through, the texture opens and a phrase of pure song appears before the procession resumes. The hand-crossing Scarlatti loves is here too, the left hand leaping over the right to place notes above the melody.","Composed in Madrid in the 1750s among the hundreds of sonatas written for Maria Barbara. Spanish court ceremony, with its processions, guards and distant military signals, was part of daily life at the Escorial and Aranjuez, and Scarlatti's sonatas absorb it alongside guitars and street song. He had come to Iberia in 1719 to serve the Portuguese court, never returned to Italy, married twice and died in Madrid in 1757.","The fanfare figures are written for a keyboard but shaped like brass calls, restricted to notes a natural trumpet could play, which is how Scarlatti conjures instruments that are not there. Notice the hand-crossings: they exist not for display but because the left hand must reach above the right to place a high note the right hand is too busy to take. This is writing for a player he knew intimately.","K. 380 became one of the most played sonatas after Horowitz recorded it in a broad singing manner that harpsichordists consider far too slow. It appears constantly as a recital opener and has been arranged for guitar, where the fanfares sound entirely natural. Scholars use it as an example of Scarlatti's Spanish manner, though how far that colour is real and how far a Romantic projection is debated.","Listen for the fanfare, the repeated chord figure, and hear a trumpet that is not there. Then find the moment where the music stops processing and simply sings; it lasts only a few bars. Horowitz on piano and a Spanish harpsichord recording make the strongest possible contrast, and a guitar transcription is worth hearing as a third view."],"d-scarlatti-sonata-k-27":["Four minutes of restless motion in B minor, fast throughout, with the hands constantly swapping roles: broken chords in one, a snatch of melody in the other, then the reverse. There are hand-crossings, quick descending runs and a habit of stopping suddenly on an unexpected chord before hurrying on. Like every sonata in the set it is in two repeated halves, and the second begins by turning the opening idea upside down.","Published in London and Venice around 1738 in the Essercizi per gravicembalo, the only substantial collection Scarlatti saw into print: thirty pieces dedicated to King John V of Portugal, whose daughter Maria Barbara he had taught. The preface asks the reader not to expect deep learning but an ingenious jesting with art. The book made his reputation across Europe and was reprinted in London within a year.","That preface is a warning against listening for counterpoint. Scarlatti is doing something else, and the something else is physical: what two hands can do on a keyboard when the composer is also the player. Watch for the crossing passages, where a hand must jump over the other and land accurately, since the sound of the leap is part of the music. The crushed-dissonance habit first becomes systematic here.","The Essercizi were admired immediately in England, where Thomas Roseingrave promoted them and Charles Burney later wrote about Scarlatti at length. Nineteenth-century editors tidied the harmony and softened the dissonances; twentieth-century scholarship restored them. Modern debate concerns tempo, since the pieces are often played faster than any harpsichord can articulate, and whether they are studies, as the title says, or concert works.","Listen for the hands crossing; on a good recording you can hear the physical risk. Then compare the two halves and notice how the second begins with the first idea in reverse. Try it on harpsichord, where the articulation is clean, then on piano, where the runs sound smoother and much less dangerous."],"d-scarlatti-sonata-k-87":["The quietest thing Scarlatti wrote. Five minutes in B minor, slow, with no display of any kind: four voices moving independently like a vocal ensemble, entering and overlapping, the harmony sliding into brief bitter clashes that resolve almost apologetically. There are no repeated notes, no hand-crossings and no guitar effects. It is closer to a Renaissance motet transferred to a keyboard than to anything else among the five hundred sonatas.","From the manuscript volumes copied in Venice in the 1750s and written in Madrid for the queen's private playing. Scarlatti had been trained in Naples and Rome by his father Alessandro in an older polyphonic tradition and had competed with Handel in Rome as a young man, reportedly winning at the harpsichord and losing at the organ. This sonata is the clearest trace in his late work of that early training.","Four independent voices on a keyboard is a strict-counterpoint discipline, and Scarlatti keeps it up almost without a lapse, which is why the piece feels weightless rather than laboured. Listen for the suspensions, notes held over into a new chord where they no longer fit and then stepping down. That device is centuries older than Scarlatti and is the whole emotional mechanism of the sonata.","K. 87 has become a favourite of pianists precisely because it contradicts the popular image of Scarlatti as an acrobat, and it appears on recital programmes as a moment of stillness. Harpsichordists note that its sustained lines fight an instrument that cannot hold a note, and that the piece rewards a clavichord or a fortepiano, both of which the Madrid court owned. Its date is unknown.","Follow the lowest voice throughout; it moves slowly enough to track and it tells you where the harmony is going. Then listen for the moments when two notes rub against each other and take a beat to sort themselves out. Compare a piano recording, where the lines sing, with a clavichord one, where they whisper and the intimacy is almost uncomfortable."],"d-scarlatti-sonata-k-455":["Four minutes of comedy in G major. The piece runs on repeated notes and abrupt stops, with a bustling figure that keeps tripping over itself and a habit of landing on a chord a beat too early or too late. The hands chase each other through crossings and leaps, and the texture is thin, mostly two lines, so every misstep is exposed. It is fast from beginning to end and finishes with an almost rude flourish.","One of the late Madrid sonatas, copied into the Venice manuscripts in the 1750s and written for Maria Barbara, by then Queen of Spain. Scarlatti was over sixty-five and producing these pieces at extraordinary speed with no apparent intention to publish. The court's keyboards included Florentine instruments by Cristofori, the inventor of the piano, so early pianos as well as harpsichords may have been available to him.","The humour is rhythmic. Scarlatti keeps writing phrases of uneven length so that the ear's expectation of four-bar units is repeatedly cheated; count in fours and you will fall over, which is the joke. The repeated-note figures again point at plucked strings, and the sudden stops imitate the way a street band cuts off. None of this appears in the score as an instruction: it is all in the phrasing.","K. 455 is one of the sonatas Horowitz revived on the piano and it has stayed in the repertoire as an encore, where its speed and wit read instantly to an audience. Harpsichordists argue it loses its edge on a modern piano because the crushed notes soften. Whether these late sonatas were conceived in pairs, as Kirkpatrick proposed, applies here as elsewhere and remains unresolved.","Try to tap along and notice how often the phrase lengths defeat you. Listen for the abrupt silences, which are the punchlines. Then hear it on harpsichord and piano back to back: the harpsichord is funnier, the piano faster. Any Scarlatti recital by Pierre Hantaï or Yevgeny Sudbin will place it among its neighbours."],"pergolesi-stabat-mater-duo":["Soprano and alto over strings and continuo, about five minutes, opening a setting of the medieval poem about Mary standing at the cross. The strings begin alone with a slow figure in which two lines rub against each other in dissonances that resolve only to clash again, a chain of sighs. The voices enter one at a time and repeat that rubbing in close harmony, so near to each other that they sound like one voice split in two.","Written in 1736 at the Franciscan monastery in Pozzuoli near Naples, where Pergolesi had gone for his health; he died of tuberculosis there at twenty-six, probably within weeks of finishing it. The commission came from a Neapolitan confraternity that had previously used a setting by Alessandro Scarlatti and wanted a replacement for Holy Week. The scoring is small, two voices, strings and organ, because the chapel was small.","The clashing pairs are suspensions used relentlessly: one voice holds while the other moves, so that almost every bar contains a moment of friction. Listen to how rarely the two singers separate; they move in parallel for long stretches, which is why the piece sounds like grief with a companion rather than a solo lament. Bach knew the work and arranged it as a German psalm setting.","The Stabat Mater became the most frequently printed piece of the eighteenth century, admired by Bach and criticised by Padre Martini as too theatrical for church, while Rousseau singled out this opening duet for praise. Nineteenth-century performances used choirs; modern practice has returned to two solo voices, usually a soprano with a countertenor or mezzo, a small string band and a much lighter sound.","Listen to the strings alone for the first thirty seconds and count the clashes before the singers even arrive. Then track the two voices and notice how rarely they part. Compare a boys' or countertenor version with two women, since the tone changes the piece from operatic to devotional. Bach's arrangement is worth hearing straight afterwards."],"pergolesi-serva-padrona-stizzoso-mio-stizzoso":["Three minutes of a maid ordering her employer around. The aria is quick, in A major, and its comedy is built from interruptions: short snapped phrases, sudden silences, repeated words and a passage where the singer commands him to be quiet and the orchestra obediently stops. The vocal writing is deliberately unheroic, patter rather than long lines, and the accompaniment is light strings that mimic and mock the voice.","From La serva padrona, a two-part comic intermezzo written to be played between the acts of Pergolesi's serious opera Il prigionier superbo at Naples in 1733. Intermezzi were interval entertainment using two or three singers and everyday characters. Serpina, the servant, schemes her way into marrying her master Uberto; the whole thing lasts under an hour and needs three performers, one of whom never sings a word.","The joke is that the orchestra takes the singer's orders. When Serpina tells Uberto to be quiet, the music itself falls silent, so the comedy is structural rather than verbal and you can enjoy it without a word of Italian. Notice too that her phrases are short enough to be spoken: Pergolesi is writing close to natural speech rhythm, which was the point of the new comic style.","A Paris production in 1752 detonated the Querelle des Bouffons, a two-year pamphlet war over whether Italian comic naturalness or French tragic grandeur was the future of opera, with Rousseau on the Italian side and Rameau's supporters against. The little intermezzo became a manifesto for a cause its dead composer never argued. It is now staged as a short standalone comedy, often with period instruments and a light, quick touch.","Listen for the moment the orchestra stops on command; it is the best joke in early comic opera. Then hear the whole intermezzo, which lasts about forty-five minutes and moves fast. A period-instrument recording keeps the textures thin enough for the words to land, and comparing it with Mozart's servants thirty years later shows where the line goes."],"pergolesi-salve-regina-c":["About fifteen minutes for solo soprano, strings and continuo, in four short movements setting the Marian antiphon. It opens slowly in C minor with the voice entering over a hushed accompaniment and rising through long phrases; faster middle sections bring some agitation, and the final plea returns to the opening's stillness. There is no chorus and no display writing, so the piece stays intimate throughout, closer to a private prayer than a liturgical showpiece.","Written in Naples in Pergolesi's last years, probably 1736, in the same period as the Stabat Mater and possibly at the same monastery in Pozzuoli. Naples had four conservatories that trained orphaned boys as professional musicians, and Pergolesi was a product of one of them; solo sacred works for a single voice with strings were a Neapolitan speciality. He had only a few years of adult working life in total.","Attribution is the interesting problem. Several Salve Regina settings circulate under Pergolesi's name and only some are securely his, because his fame after death turned his name into a selling label that publishers attached freely. The C minor setting is generally accepted. Listen for the way the phrases are shaped like spoken prayer, breaking where a speaker would breathe rather than where a symmetrical melody would.","By the 1760s so much music was being sold as Pergolesi's that modern scholars have had to strip most of it away; Stravinsky's Pulcinella, drawn in 1920 from supposed Pergolesi sources, is largely built on other composers' music. The genuine sacred works have been re-established since the 1980s through critical editions and period recordings, and this piece is now heard as the Stabat Mater's quieter companion rather than an afterthought.","Play it after the Stabat Mater and hear the same hand working alone rather than in pairs. Listen for the ends of phrases, where the voice sinks rather than lands. A soprano with a light straight tone and a small string group suits it better than an operatic voice, though countertenors have also claimed it."],"pergolesi-stabat-mater-quando-corpus-morietur":["The last movement, about five minutes, in two utterly different halves. First, soprano and alto sing very slowly and quietly over sustained strings, asking that when the body dies the soul be granted glory, with long held notes and almost no motion: the most static music in the work. Then, on the single word Amen, a brisk fugue erupts and the two voices chase each other through a busy contrapuntal race that ends the piece in a burst of energy.","The final section of the setting Pergolesi completed shortly before his death at Pozzuoli in 1736. The Amen fugue was a convention, since an eighteenth-century sacred work was expected to end with contrapuntal display proving the composer's learning, and Pergolesi, trained in Naples, supplies it in miniature for two voices rather than a choir. The confraternity that commissioned the work performed it on Fridays in Lent.","The contrast is doctrinal rather than merely musical: the slow half is death, the fugue is eternal life, and Pergolesi marks the change with an abrupt tempo shift and no transition at all. Listen to the held notes in the first half, written to be sung without any swell, which is very hard and audibly effortful. The fugue subject is deliberately old-fashioned, a nod to church style.","The abrupt Amen has divided listeners since the eighteenth century. Some hear an inspired jolt, others a convention tacked onto a personal work, and Martini's charge of theatricality was partly aimed at such effects. The romantic legend that Pergolesi wrote the last bars on his deathbed is unverifiable and probably false, but it shaped two centuries of reception. Modern performers vary the pause before the Amen considerably.","Listen for the exact moment the tempo changes; some performers pause, some cut straight in, and the choice reframes the whole work. Before that, count how long the singers hold their notes. Then go back to the opening duet and hear how the first and last minutes of the piece answer each other."],"gluck-dance-blessed-spirits":["Six minutes of ballet music from an opera's Elysian Fields scene. The outer sections are calm and pastoral, strings moving in gentle even phrases. At the centre a solo flute plays a long melody in the minor over quiet strings, unhurried and free of any harmonic complication, and then the opening music returns. Nothing dramatic happens: it is music for spirits who no longer feel anything, and it is scored to sound weightless.","Gluck first staged Orfeo ed Euridice in Vienna in 1762 with the librettist Ranieri de' Calzabigi, as a deliberate reform of an opera world they thought ruined by star singers. For Paris in 1774 he rewrote it in French as Orphée et Eurydice, expanded the ballets, since French audiences required dancing, and added this flute solo. The Paris Opéra was a state institution with its own conventions and a permanent corps de ballet.","The flute solo was written for the Paris version and does not exist in the Vienna score, so the music most people know as pure Gluck is a French addition. Listen for what is missing: no oboes, no brass, almost no motion in the bass, so the harmony sits still. The famous melody stays within a narrow range and never leaps, because Gluck's afterlife is a place where nothing strains.","The two versions of Orfeo have been a performance problem for two centuries: Vienna's alto castrato hero, Paris's high tenor, and Berlioz's 1859 hybrid for the mezzo Pauline Viardot, which held the stage for a hundred years. The Dance escaped all of it and became a flute recital standard and a funeral piece. Period recordings have restored the wooden flute's softer, breathier tone and slower Parisian tempos.","Listen to the flute's melody once for its shape and once for what accompanies it, which is almost nothing. Then hear the scene in a staged recording, where it follows the hero's arrival among the dead. Compare a modern silver flute with a period wooden one; the older instrument makes the line sound frailer and more human."],"gluck-che-faro-senza-euridice":["Four minutes in C major, and the strangeness is the key. Orpheus has just lost Eurydice a second time, and instead of a storm Gluck writes a clear, even, almost serene melody in the major, stated three times with short agitated passages between. The orchestra is plain, strings with light wind support, and the vocal line has no runs and no ornament. What makes it devastating is the mismatch between calm music and unbearable words.","From the Vienna Orfeo ed Euridice of 1762, written with Calzabigi as a deliberate manifesto. Gluck's preface to a later opera set out the programme: no vocal display for its own sake, no long da capo arias, no separation of recitative from aria, and an orchestra always serving the drama. The title role was written for the alto castrato Gaetano Guadagni, who had studied acting in London with David Garrick.","The aria's neutrality was the reform's most controversial gesture. Contemporaries complained that with different words the same music could express contentment, and Gluck's defenders answered that grief past a certain point sounds like nothing at all. Listen for the short unsettled interludes between the three statements of the tune: that is where the emotion is placed, and it is instrumental rather than vocal.","The objection that it could be sung happy was made in the eighteenth century and has never gone away; the writer Boyé used it as evidence that music cannot express anything definite. Orfeo is nonetheless the earliest opera after Monteverdi to have stayed continuously in the repertoire. Casting remains unresolved between countertenor, mezzo-soprano and tenor, each drawn from a different authentic score and each changing the aria's colour.","Try the thought experiment the eighteenth century proposed: imagine the melody with cheerful words, then listen again knowing the situation, and the interludes between the verses will start to sound different. Kathleen Ferrier's recording in English and a period countertenor version mark the two extremes of the tradition."],"gluck-iphigenie-en-aulide-overture":["About ten minutes. It begins with a slow heavy figure played in unison by the strings, a single line with no harmony beneath it, grave and exposed, before the fast section arrives with agitated string writing and stabbing wind chords. Three ideas alternate: the grim opening, a driving allegro and a plaintive wind melody. Gluck does not end it with a flourish, because the music runs directly into the first scene.","Gluck's first French opera, premiered at the Paris Opéra on 19 April 1774 under the protection of Marie Antoinette, his former singing pupil and by then Dauphine. The subject comes from Racine: Agamemnon must sacrifice his daughter to gain a wind for Troy. Paris was already primed for a fight over French versus Italian style, and Gluck's arrival triggered a war between his supporters and those of the Italian composer Piccinni.","The opening unison figure is not decoration. It returns in the opera as the sound of Agamemnon's dread, so the overture is already telling the story, which is exactly what Gluck said an overture should do. Wagner, who admired the work, made his own performing version in 1847 and wrote a concert ending for it, and that ending is the one most orchestras still play.","The premiere divided Paris and helped ignite the Gluckist and Piccinnist controversy, conducted in pamphlets by men who often had not heard the operas. The opera itself faded while the overture survived as a concert piece, largely in Wagner's arrangement, which alters the orchestration as well as the close. Recent period performances have restored Gluck's own text and its abrupt link into the first scene, which sounds far less symphonic.","Listen to the very first bars, everyone playing one line with no harmony, and notice how much weight a bare unison carries. Then compare Wagner's concert ending with a period recording that runs straight on into the opera. The difference tells you what the nineteenth century wanted from Gluck and what he actually wrote."],"gluck-o-del-mio-dolce-ardor":["Three and a half minutes, slow, for a single voice and modest orchestra: Paris, newly in love with Helen, addresses the object of his longing. The melody rises in long even steps and falls back, repeating its shape with small variations, while the accompaniment gives almost nothing beyond soft supporting chords and a quiet pulse. There is no ornament for its own sake and no fast section; the aria says one thing patiently and stops.","From Paride ed Elena, staged in Vienna in 1770, the third and least successful of the operas Gluck wrote with Calzabigi as reform manifestos. The court audience had accepted Orfeo and Alceste but found this one static, and it disappeared quickly; it is still rarely staged. The aria, extracted and reprinted in nineteenth-century anthologies of old Italian arias, became one of the first pieces most classical singers ever learn.","Its fame rests on an anthology. Alessandro Parisotti's late nineteenth-century collections of arie antiche transposed, rebarred and supplied piano accompaniments for a market of amateur singers, and the version taught in conservatories descends from that edition rather than from Gluck's score. In the opera the orchestration is thinner and the pacing less indulgent than the piano version suggests.","Paride ed Elena has never recovered, and even sympathetic critics find its want of action hard to stage. The aria's separate career has meanwhile made it a fixture of auditions and first recitals in every voice type, transposed at will. Revivals with period instruments have restored the original context and pitch, and heard in place the aria sounds cooler and more classical than its salon reputation.","Learn it first in the familiar recital version with piano, then find a complete recording of the opera and hear it with Gluck's orchestra; the second is plainer and better. Listen for how the melody keeps returning to the same rising shape without ever quite repeating. Any great lieder singer's account will show what patience does for it."],"cpe-bach-solfeggietto":["Under a minute and a half, and almost the whole piece is a single line of fast notes split between the hands, running up and down in C minor with no accompaniment underneath. The hands alternate so quickly that they sound like one, which is the trick: what seems a torrent of virtuosity is easy to play at speed and very hard to play cleanly. It ends with a sudden loud flourish after a moment of hesitation.","Published in 1766 in a Berlin collection, when Bach's second surviving son was in his early fifties and had spent nearly thirty years as harpsichordist to Frederick the Great, a demanding employer who played the flute, expected accompaniment on demand and did not much like his employee's more adventurous music. The keyboard of choice in that household was increasingly the clavichord, an instrument so quiet it can barely be heard in the next room.","The title is a diminutive, a little solfeggio, meaning a vocal exercise, applied to a keyboard study: a joke about a piece with no tune to sing. Notice the two brief pauses where the running stops dead. On a clavichord, whose keys stay in contact with the strings, those silences and the notes around them can be shaded in a way no harpsichord allows. The piece is a study in the illusion of speed.","Solfeggietto has been in every piano method book for a century and a half, which has given it a reputation as a children's piece rather than as C. P. E. Bach. That reputation obscures a composer whom Haydn and Mozart named as a master and whose keyboard works were more admired in 1780 than his father's. Editions vary, and most of the dynamics students learn are editorial rather than his.","Listen for the moment the running stops and hear how much a silence can do at that speed. Then find a clavichord recording, since the instrument is tiny, private and full of shading, and the piece stops being an exercise. Follow it with one of the same composer's fantasias to hear where the restlessness leads."],"cpe-bach-flute-concerto-d-finale":["The last movement of a three-movement concerto, about seven minutes, and it is aggressive. The strings hammer out a jagged theme and the flute answers with rapid, awkward, brilliant passagework. The music keeps stopping without warning and restarting somewhere unexpected, and the soloist is given cadenza-like flights that break the pulse entirely. Nothing about it is polite chamber music for a court evening.","Written around 1747 in Berlin, where Bach served Frederick the Great, an accomplished flautist who played a concerto most evenings. The work exists in versions for flute, harpsichord and cello, and Bach recycled his own concertos across instruments constantly. Berlin's court taste was conservative, and this piece pushes hard against it in the emotional manner Germans called Empfindsamkeit: sensitive, sudden and unstable.","The interruptions are the substance. Bach's treatise on keyboard playing of 1753 held that a musician must himself be moved in order to move others, and that meant abrupt changes of affect within a single movement rather than one mood per piece. Listen for the places where the orchestra drops out and the flute is left mid-phrase; those gaps are written into the score, not improvised, and they are the point.","The Berlin concertos were long treated as transitional curiosities between Baroque and Classical, and the son was measured against the father to his disadvantage. That verdict has reversed, and the contrasts once heard as incoherence are now heard as a deliberate project. Flautists and cellists both claim the piece, and period players use a one-keyed wooden flute whose uneven notes make the fast passages sound more strenuous.","Listen for the stops. Every time the music breaks off, notice where it goes next, which is almost never where you expect. Then compare the flute, harpsichord and cello versions of the same movement, since the instrument changes the character entirely. A period wooden flute sounds breathier and more effortful, which suits the music."],"cpe-bach-cello-concerto":["Twenty-two minutes in three movements for cello and strings. The first is restless and dark, the orchestra pressing forward while the soloist plays long climbing lines against it. The slow movement is the heart: the cello sings over a hushed accompaniment, with pauses that seem to lose the thread. The finale returns to nervous energy, the solo part full of leaps across the instrument's range and abrupt switches from loud to soft.","Composed in Berlin around 1750 and, like its companions, existing in versions for cello, flute and harpsichord. The cello was still establishing itself as a solo instrument outside Italy, and Bach treats it as a singer rather than a bass, high on its top string for long stretches. He would spend twenty-eight years in Frederick's service before escaping in 1768 to succeed his godfather Telemann as music director in Hamburg.","Listen for how often the orchestra and soloist disagree about the beat: Bach writes syncopations and off-balance entries that make the music feel pulled sideways. In the slow movement the accompaniment sometimes simply stops, leaving the cello exposed, an effect borrowed from the clavichord's way of holding a note and letting it die, transferred to an instrument that can genuinely sustain.","The three concertos in each version were seldom played until cellists looking beyond Haydn and Boccherini took them up in the later twentieth century. Editions differ, since the sources are copies rather than autographs, and the ornamentation is a matter of judgement. Whether the cello or the keyboard version is primary is a long argument whose answer probably differs concerto by concerto, and Bach may not have cared.","Go straight to the slow movement and listen for the silences, then to the first movement's opening to hear where all the tension comes from. Compare a modern cello with a period instrument at lower pitch and gut strings, which sounds more strained and more vocal. Anner Bylsma's recording is the standard period reference."],"cpe-bach-symphony-e-wq-178":["Thirteen minutes in three movements played without a break, for strings, two horns and continuo. The opening is startling: a violent figure in unison, a silence, then a shift to a key nobody expects, and the whole first movement proceeds by such swerves. The slow movement is short and shadowed; the finale dances but keeps tripping. Nothing lasts long enough to settle, and the joins between movements are deliberately abrupt rather than smoothed.","Written in Berlin in 1756, the year the Seven Years' War began, a conflict that would eventually shut down Frederick the Great's musical establishment and leave Bach underemployed. Symphonies at that court were curtain-raisers rather than the main event and were played by a small band. Bach's are far stranger than the Italian sinfonias they descend from, and he later wrote a set for a Viennese patron, saying he had composed without any restraint.","The three movements run together because the ends of the first two are written not to close: they stop on a chord that demands continuation. That structural refusal to conclude is the same instinct as the sudden silences in his keyboard music. Listen for the horns, which mostly hold long notes rather than play tunes, and for how often the strings play in bare unison instead of harmony.","Nineteenth-century historians filed C. P. E. Bach as a stepping stone to Haydn and his symphonies as awkward prototypes. Twentieth-century performance, especially by period orchestras from the 1970s onwards, found instead a composer whose unpredictability is deliberate and complete in itself. Haydn owned and studied his music, and Mozart's remark that he is the father and the rest are the children is quoted so often because it is accurate about influence.","Listen to the first thirty seconds twice: the gesture, the silence, the wrong key. Then try to predict any phrase's ending and enjoy being wrong. A period band with gut strings and hand horns makes the roughness audible, while a smooth modern reading tames exactly what is interesting. Follow it with an early Haydn symphony to hear the difference between surprise and wit."],"cpe-bach-fantasia-f":["About ten minutes for keyboard alone, written largely without bar lines: the music is not measured, and the player moves freely between recitative-like outbursts, wandering slow passages and sudden runs. Great chords are struck and left to fade, and long silences separate one thought from the next. A recurring slow sighing melody keeps returning between the storms. It is the most improvisatory-sounding piece any composer of the period wrote down.","Composed in Hamburg in 1787, the last year of Bach's life, when he had been the city's music director for nearly two decades. He later published a version with an added violin part subtitled C. P. E. Bach's Feelings, which makes the autobiographical claim explicit. Hamburg gave him a freedom Berlin never had, and his late keyboard collections for connoisseurs and amateurs contain his most experimental music.","The piece is written for the clavichord, whose keys stay in contact with the strings so that a player can shake a held note into a vibrato, an effect no harpsichord or modern piano can produce and one Bach's treatise describes at length. On any other instrument the long held notes simply die. Listen for the passages with no bar lines, where the notation itself refuses to fix the rhythm.","The free fantasia was his most admired achievement in his lifetime, praised by the poet Klopstock's circle and by English visitors such as Charles Burney, who described him playing until his shirt was soaked through. The nineteenth century, hearing only the Classical style he preceded, found the fantasias formless. Performers on clavichord and fortepiano have restored them as a direct ancestor of Beethoven's improvisatory late writing.","Listen on a clavichord if you possibly can, since the instrument is so quiet that recordings feel like eavesdropping and the wavering held notes are the whole point. Follow the returns of the sighing melody as landmarks in the chaos. Then hear Beethoven's late bagatelles or the Moonlight Sonata's first movement and the family line is unmistakable."],"haydn-symphony-no-94-surprise-andante":["The second movement of a four-movement symphony. A tune of nursery-rhyme plainness is played twice, the second time as quietly as the strings can manage — and then the whole band, timpani included, slams down a single chord. Four variations follow: the melody turned to the minor, decorated by solo oboe and flute, thundered out by the full orchestra, then dissolved into hushed wind chords. Six minutes, and the joke arrives in the first thirty seconds.","Haydn was sixty and, after thirty years in Esterházy livery, a free man in London, where the violinist-impresario Johann Peter Salomon paid him more for a season than a decade of court service had brought. The Hanover Square Rooms held some eight hundred paying listeners — a commercial public, not an aristocratic one — and concerts began late, after long dinners. The Surprise came in the 1792 series, one of twelve symphonies London bought from him.","The joke is only half the story: the loud chord never returns. Haydn plays it once and then spends the rest of the movement pretending nothing happened, which is funnier than any repeat would be. Listen also to the third variation, where the theme is buried in the bass while the violins scurry above it, and to the last bars, where the tune fades into wind chords and simply stops.","Haydn's biographer Griesinger reported him denying that he wrote it to wake dozing listeners; he said he wanted to surprise the public with something new. The story about sleeping ladies stuck anyway, and the German nickname — the drumstroke — is more accurate than the English one. Modern period-instrument bands, with gut strings and hard timpani sticks, restore a shock that plush modern orchestras tend to soften.","Do not brace for the bang; let it get you. Then follow the tune rather than the fireworks — it is four notes of a broken chord and a scale, and Haydn keeps finding new company for it. Compare a period band of Salomon's scale with a big modern orchestra, and notice how much of the effect belongs to the drum."],"haydn-symphony-no-104-london":["Haydn's last symphony: four movements, about half an hour. It opens not with a tune but with a bare fanfare for the whole orchestra in D minor, a question that the sunlit Allegro answers. A songlike Andante follows, then a heavy-footed minuet with a trio that keeps tripping over a rest, and finally a movement built over a droning bass note under a street-vendor melody. Clarinets, trumpets and drums make the sound fuller than any earlier Haydn symphony.","Written for Salomon's 1795 London season and first heard on 4 May at the King's Theatre, with Haydn at the keyboard directing. He was sixty-three, the most famous composer alive, and had earned more in England than the Esterházy court paid him in decades. London had a standing orchestra of around sixty players and an audience that bought tickets rather than inheriting seats; Haydn wrote for its weight and its appetite for the memorable.","The finale's tune sits over a bass note held so long it begins to sound like a bagpipe drone; Haydn probably picked the melody up in the street, though a Croatian folk origin has also been proposed. Notice too that the opening fanfare returns nowhere, yet its rhythm shadows the Allegro's cheerful first theme. The slow movement slips without warning into a stormy minor episode and back out again.","The London nickname is a British habit; on the continent this is simply the last of the twelve London symphonies, and Haydn gave it no title. Nineteenth-century performances doubled the winds and slowed the minuet to a crawl. Since the 1980s period bands have argued for single winds, brisk tempos and Haydn's own continuo keyboard, which changes the texture more than most listeners expect.","Start with the first thirty seconds — that stark fanfare — then hear how the movement fills the space it opened. In the finale, put your ear on the low strings and the held drone rather than on the tune. Compare a period-instrument recording with a mid-century full-orchestra one; the difference in the minuet's weight is a short lesson in performance history."],"haydn-emperor-quartet-poco-adagio":["The slow movement of a string quartet, and one of the simplest structures Haydn ever wrote: a hymn tune stated plainly, then four variations in which the melody is never altered at all. Instead it migrates — to the second violin while the first spins a countermelody above it, then to the cello, then to the viola, and finally home to the first violin. Seven minutes, ending in a hush rather than a flourish.","In January 1797 Haydn wrote Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser as an anthem for Emperor Francis II, partly in answer to Britain's God Save the King and to the revolutionary songs coming out of France while Napoleon's armies pressed toward Vienna. He liked the tune enough to build a quartet movement on it the same year, for the set of six published as Op. 76 and written for Count Erdődy.","Because the theme never changes, everything expressive happens around it: the countersubjects grow steadily more chromatic and the harmony beneath the tune darkens variation by variation, so identical notes mean something different each time. When the cello takes the melody, the writing above it briefly becomes a four-part invention. Haydn is said to have played the anthem at his keyboard in his last weeks.","The tune's afterlife has complicated the music. It became the Austrian imperial anthem, then, fitted to Hoffmann von Fallersleben's Deutschlandlied, the German national anthem — and the associations of the 1930s and 1940s cling to it for some listeners. Quartets programme the movement constantly, and the recurring argument is tempo: whether poco adagio means devotional stillness or a walking hymn.","Count the entries. Hear the tune four times and each time ask which instrument has it — that alone is the whole movement's drama. Then listen to the last variation, where Haydn thickens the harmony into something almost churchlike. Any good quartet recording works; period-instrument groups on gut strings give the hymn a plainer, more vocal sound."],"haydn-creation-himmel-erzahlen":["The chorus that ends Part One of The Creation, and the loudest thing in it. Over about four minutes the full choir declaims that the heavens are telling the glory of God in blazing C major; three soloists — the angels Gabriel, Uriel and Raphael — answer in quieter trio; the pattern repeats, and then a long fugal coda drives everything to a close with trumpets and drums going at full stretch.","Haydn had heard Handel's oratorios performed by massed forces in London and came home determined to write one. Baron van Swieten prepared a German text from an English libretto drawn from Genesis and Milton, and Haydn worked at it for over a year, saying he prayed before each session. Private performances for the aristocratic subscribers came first; the public premiere was at Vienna's Burgtheater on 19 March 1799.","The alternation is theology in sound: the crowd of the heavens shouts, the individual angels reflect, and only in the closing fugue do the two merge. Listen for the moment near the end when everything stops dead on the word Ehre — glory — before the final surge. Haydn also keeps the soloists largely out of the fugue, so the choir's arrival feels like a door opening.","The Creation was an immediate popular success across Europe and made Haydn money in an age before royalties; at a Vienna performance in 1808 the elderly composer was carried in and wept. Nineteenth-century tradition swelled it to Victorian choir-festival proportions. Recent performances have moved back toward Haydn's own numbers — large by chamber standards, but sung faster and more lightly than the Victorians managed.","Listen for the three-way architecture: choir, then the angels' trio, then choir again. Follow the trumpets, which Haydn saves for the biggest moments. If you have time for only one comparison, hear this next to the Representation of Chaos that opens the same work — the same orchestra, the exact opposite of light. A period-instrument recording keeps the counterpoint clear."],"haydn-trumpet-concerto-finale":["The last of three movements, a rondo of about four and a half minutes. A quiet, quick tune in the strings is snatched up by the trumpet, and from there the soloist runs scales, leaps octaves and — the point of the whole piece — plays smooth stepwise melodies in the low and middle register that no trumpet of the period could produce. The orchestra behind is strings, pairs of winds, horns and timpani.","Haydn wrote the concerto in Vienna in 1796, just back from London, for his friend Anton Weidinger, who had spent years developing a trumpet with finger-holes and keys along the tube, like a woodwind. Until then trumpets could sound only the notes of the natural harmonic series, which is why older trumpet parts fanfare rather than sing. Weidinger kept the instrument to himself and delayed the public premiere until 1800.","Haydn writes the new instrument's advantage into the drama. The soloist's first entry is deliberately old-fashioned — bugle calls anyone's trumpet could manage — and only later does the line start walking through notes that had previously been impossible, including a full chromatic scale. There is also a passage where the trumpet plays quietly in its low register against pizzicato strings, an effect that would have sounded like a different instrument entirely.","Weidinger's keyed trumpet never caught on; valves arrived a generation later and made it obsolete, and the concerto slept until the twentieth century. It is now the piece every orchestral trumpeter must play at auditions, usually on a modern E flat trumpet that makes Haydn's difficulties easy. A few players have revived keyed instruments, and the roughness they bring back is closer to what 1800 heard.","Listen for the moment the trumpet stops fanfaring and starts singing — usually within its first minute — because that phrase contains the whole historical argument. Then enjoy the sheer speed of the closing pages. Compare a modern virtuoso recording with a keyed-trumpet one: the modern is cleaner, the period version stranger and far more revealing."],"haydn-cello-concerto-no-1-first-movement":["A ten-minute opening movement marked Moderato. The strings lay out a courtly theme in full before the cello enters and takes it over, and thereafter the soloist rarely stops: long spun-out lines, rapid passagework climbing to the top of the instrument, double stops that ask for real accuracy. The accompaniment is small — strings with two oboes and two horns — so the cello is never buried and never has to force.","Haydn wrote it in the early 1760s, in his first years at the Esterházy court, almost certainly for Joseph Franz Weigl, the excellent principal cellist of the prince's orchestra. Court music then was chamber music: a few dozen players, an audience of the prince and his guests, and pieces built to show off the individual musicians Haydn worked beside daily. Concertos of this kind were written for an occasion, not for publication.","The solo writing sits astonishingly high for the 1760s, and what it demands suggests Haydn knew exactly what Weigl could do. Notice how the orchestra's opening theme returns in fragments, cut and re-spliced, rather than being restated whole — an early sign of the developmental habit that would define his symphonies. The oboes and horns mostly reinforce, dropping out the moment the cello turns quiet.","The concerto was known only from a catalogue entry in Haydn's own hand until 1961, when Oldřich Pulkert found a set of parts in the Prague National Museum; it was performed and recorded almost at once and entered the repertoire within a decade. Debate now centres on cadenzas — Haydn left none — and on whether to use a period cello with gut strings and a lighter bow.","Track the tune: hear it played straight by the orchestra, then hear what the cello does to it. The high passages near the end of the first section are where players earn their reputation. Compare a modern-instrument reading with a period one on gut strings; the older setup makes the high writing sound riskier, which is probably closer to the truth."],"haydn-symphony-no-45-farewell-finale":["The finale begins as a fast, brilliant Presto and appears to be heading for a normal ending — then stops, and an Adagio starts instead. One by one the players finish their parts, snuff the candle on their music stand and walk off: first oboe and second horn, then the bassoon, the second oboe, the double bass, the cello, and on until two muted violins are left alone playing a thin, high duet.","In 1772 Prince Nikolaus Esterházy had extended the summer season at his remote palace at Eszterháza, and the musicians — forbidden to bring their families — had been away from home for months. Haydn, as Kapellmeister, was the man who had to raise it. His early biographers say the prince took the point at once and ordered the court back to Eisenstadt the next day. The key was so rare the horn players needed specially made crooks.","The trick is not only theatrical but structural: the Adagio is an extra movement grafted onto the end, so the symphony has five, and the music dissolves rather than concludes. The exits are written into the score with instructions, and the departing lines are composed to leave holes — the harmony thins from the bottom upward, which is why the ending sounds not merely quiet but incomplete.","Haydn's contemporaries called the piece the Farewell and repeated the labour-protest anecdote, though the earliest sources are secondhand and the details vary. Nineteenth-century programmes treated it as a charming curiosity; recent scholarship reads it as an unusually pointed piece of workplace negotiation by a servant-musician. Performances now nearly always stage the exits, and conductors argue about whether to dim the hall lights.","Watch it rather than only hear it if you can — this is one of the few symphonies that is also stagecraft. Listen for the moment the fast music breaks off, then keep a mental tally of instruments as they vanish. Recordings that leave the departures audible, with players moving away from the microphones, come closest to the original point."],"haydn-symphony-no-101-clock-andante":["Six or seven minutes, and the reason for the symphony's nickname. Bassoons and pizzicato strings set up a steady two-note tick and a plain tune walks over it. Halfway through, the mechanism jams: the music lurches into the minor, the full orchestra breaks in loudly, and the ticking turns menacing before the tune returns decorated with solo flute and violin filigree. The pulse never stops until the final bar.","Written in Vienna over the winter of 1793–94 and first performed in London on 3 March 1794, in Salomon's second series. Haydn's London audiences liked a pictorial hook and reviewers seized on this one; the Morning Chronicle praised the movement at once. It was also the era of fashionable domestic clocks and musical automata — Haydn himself wrote pieces for the mechanical flute clocks built by Primitivus Niemecz, the Esterházy librarian.","The tick is not decoration but the theme's accompaniment from the first bar, which is why removing it would leave the melody floating in air. Listen for the minor-key eruption: the same ticking figure, played fortissimo by the whole orchestra, turns from cosy to threatening without one note of its rhythm changing. Near the end the flute and solo violin play high above the tune, the clock now running like jewellery.","The nickname is not Haydn's — it appears in Vienna some years later — and purists point out that half the movement has nothing mechanical about it. Critics have sometimes treated the Clock as the most amiable and least searching of the London symphonies, which underrates the violence of its middle. Period-instrument performances make the pizzicato drier and the loud interruption considerably harsher.","Set the tick going in your head in the first ten seconds and keep it there; everything else is measured against it. The moment to wait for is the sudden loud minor-key passage. Afterwards compare it with the Surprise Andante — two slow movements built on a joke, three years apart, with completely different nerves."],"haydn-piano-sonata-e-hob-xvi-52":["Haydn's last and largest piano sonata, about twenty minutes in three movements. The first is grand and almost orchestral, full of dotted rhythms and thick chords that need real weight behind them. The slow movement moves to the distant key of E major and unfolds in long ornamented lines. The finale is a quicksilver Presto whose main idea keeps being interrupted by silences and sudden shifts, as if the music forgets where it is and then remembers.","Written in London in 1794 for Therese Jansen Bartolozzi, a formidable pianist and pupil of Clementi who had settled there. English pianos of the 1790s — Broadwoods especially — were heavier, louder and wider in range than the Viennese instruments Haydn knew, and the sonata exploits all of it. He was in his sixties, at the height of his fame, and writing for a professional rather than an aristocratic amateur.","The jump from E flat to E major for the slow movement is a semitone that behaves like a doorway into another room, and Haydn does not prepare it at all. In the finale, listen for the bare octaves that open the movement and then keep returning in the wrong key; the joke depends entirely on your ear expecting home. Several passages ask for a sustained singing tone that only the English instruments could give.","For a century this sonata was overshadowed by Beethoven's, and Haydn's keyboard music was treated as apprentice work for the symphonies. Pianists from Schnabel onward argued otherwise, and it is now standard recital repertoire. The live question is the instrument: on a modern grand the first movement sounds Beethovenian, while on a period fortepiano its thickest chords separate into distinct voices.","Start with the slow movement if the opening feels forbidding — its key change alone is worth the entry price. In the finale, listen for the silences rather than the notes. A fortepiano recording will surprise anyone who knows only modern-piano Haydn, and comparing the two on the first movement's opening chords is the quickest way to hear what an instrument does to a piece."],"haydn-creation-representation-chaos":["The orchestral prelude to The Creation, some five or six minutes long, and unlike anything written before it. It begins with a bare unison from the whole orchestra, then wanders: phrases start and break off, the harmony refuses to settle, muted violins slide upward, bassoon and clarinet mutter in the dark, and cadences are deliberately dodged. Nothing resolves until, after the first sung words, the chorus explodes on the word Light.","Haydn composed The Creation between 1796 and 1798 in Vienna, at the end of his life, from a libretto Baron van Swieten adapted for him out of Genesis and Milton. Enlightenment cosmology was in the air — Herschel's great telescope, which Haydn had looked through in England, and arguments about how order arises from disorder. Depicting formlessness in an art built entirely on form was the deliberate challenge he set himself.","Haydn's method is withholding: no theme is allowed to complete itself, and almost every phrase ends on the wrong chord or on none at all. Listen for the muted first violins creeping upward against the basses, and for instruments emerging alone out of the dark — a flute, a solo clarinet. The C major blaze at Light works only because the preceding minutes have starved you of a resolution.","Early listeners found the piece bewildering; some nineteenth-century commentators heard proto-Wagnerian harmony in it, and twentieth-century writers have compared its suspended tonality to much later music. It has also been read, plausibly, as the model for the void-to-form openings of Beethoven's Ninth and Wagner's Rheingold. Conductors disagree about tempo: too slow and it sags, too fast and the disorientation evaporates.","Listen in the dark, and try to predict where each phrase will land — the point is that you cannot. Do not stop at the end of the prelude: stay for the recitative and the choral entry on Light, because the two belong together. Any recording that keeps the strings genuinely quiet will make the eventual outburst overwhelming."],"haydn-symphony-no-88-finale":["A four-minute Allegro con spirito that runs on a single hopping tune. Haydn brings it back again and again in rondo fashion, but every return is rerouted: through the minor, through a canon in which the strings chase one another a beat apart, through a passage where the music seems to get stuck on one chord and cannot escape. The orchestra is modest, with the drums and trumpets held back until they can do the most damage.","Composed in 1787 for Johann Peter Tost, a violinist in the Esterházy orchestra who left for Paris carrying this symphony and its companion to sell to publishers. Haydn was writing for the French market at the height of his continental fame, a few years after the Paris symphonies, and the model was the public concert rather than the prince's salon. Tost's dealings were not entirely honest and caused Haydn some trouble.","The canon is the trick most listeners hear without noticing. Two-thirds through, the violins and lower strings take the theme in strict imitation while the winds hold on, and the effect is of the tune tripping over its own feet. Listen also for the long stretch where the harmony simply refuses to move, a joke of stubbornness that Beethoven later borrowed in earnest.","The symphony sat in the shadow of the Paris and London sets until twentieth-century conductors adopted it; Furtwängler made the finale famous, and a much-quoted remark attributing Brahms's admiration to the slow movement is probably apocryphal. Its reputation now rests on exactly what once seemed slight — the density of invention packed into four minutes. Period bands take it faster than the older tradition did.","Learn the tune in the first fifteen seconds; the whole movement is a set of variations on your memory of it. Then wait for the moment the strings start chasing one another. Compare a brisk period-instrument reading with a grand mid-century recording — the piece survives both, but only one of them makes you laugh."],"jc-bach-symphony-op-18-no-2":["Three movements, about twelve minutes: a brisk opening Allegro, a short singing Andante and a bustling finale. The scoring is light and bright — strings with pairs of oboes and horns — and the manner is all clean melody over a simple pulsing accompaniment, with the tunes laid out in clearly contrasted pairs rather than developed at length. Everything is designed to be grasped on first hearing in a crowded room.","Published in London in 1781 as part of a set of six, several of them reworked from overtures to Bach's Italian operas — a common thrift of the period, since an overture and a symphony were then nearly the same thing. Bach had run the fashionable Bach–Abel subscription concerts in London since 1765, playing to a paying public in rooms built for the purpose, and was music master to Queen Charlotte.","Listen for what is missing: almost no counterpoint, no fugues, none of his father's machinery. The interest lies instead in how the melody is phrased — short, balanced, breathing like a singer — with the orchestra dropping to a murmur so the tune can be heard. Where a theatre overture would run straight into the first scene, a concert version needs a proper ending, and the joins sometimes show.","For most of the nineteenth century the London Bach was a footnote to his father and his symphonies were dismissed as merely pleasant. Their historical claim is real, though: this is the sound the young Mozart absorbed during the family's London visit and never entirely lost. Recent period-instrument recordings have made a case for the music on its own terms, played fast and lightly.","Listen with Mozart in your ears — a great deal of him comes from here, especially the shape of the phrases. Notice how much space the accompaniment leaves around the tune. A small period band is the right sound; a full modern string section turns the transparency into upholstery."],"jc-bach-keyboard-concerto-e-op-7-no":["One of six concertos published together for harpsichord or piano with a small string band. Three movements, about sixteen minutes: a spacious opening Allegro, a slow movement of unhurried song, and a light finale in dance rhythm. The keyboard writing is graceful rather than athletic — running scales, broken chords, decorated melody — and the orchestra mostly frames the soloist rather than arguing with them.","Bach published the Op. 7 set in London around 1770, at the moment the fortepiano was displacing the harpsichord in English drawing rooms; the title page offers either instrument, because publishers sold to whoever owned which. He had given what may have been the first public solo performance on a piano in London in 1768. Concertos like these were sold to well-off amateurs as much as played by professionals.","The clue to the piece is dynamic shading — long swells and sudden softenings that a harpsichord cannot make and a piano can. Bach writes them in anyway, hedging his market. Listen also for the moments where the strings simply stop and leave the keyboard alone: the earliest hints of the intimate unaccompanied passages Mozart would later build whole movements around.","These concertos were staples of the eighteenth-century amateur's shelf and then vanished for two hundred years. Their revival came with the fortepiano movement of the 1970s and after, which restored the sound the music assumes. Scholars still argue about which instrument was really intended in individual movements, and about how much freedom a soloist should take in decorating repeated passages.","Choose a fortepiano recording if you can find one; on a modern grand the balance tips and the strings disappear. Listen for the moment the orchestra falls silent and the keyboard is left exposed. Then hear a Mozart concerto slow movement immediately afterward and the debt becomes obvious — the same manners, with far more at stake."],"jc-bach-sinfonia-concertante":["A concerto for two soloists — violin and cello — against a small orchestra of strings, oboes and horns, lasting about twenty minutes. The pair are treated as a conversing couple: one begins a phrase, the other answers or completes it, and the finest passages are those where they run together in parallel thirds and sixths over a discreet accompaniment. Bright A major, with an easy dance step at the close and no display for its own sake.","The sinfonia concertante was a Parisian craze of the 1770s — a symphony with a group of soloists, made for the large public concerts of the Concert Spirituel and the Concert des Amateurs. Bach, based in London but working the continental market and travelling to Paris, wrote several. The form suited an age that wanted virtuosity without the single-soloist ego of the older concerto.","Listen to how the cello is used: not as a bass instrument grudgingly promoted, but as a tenor voice singing at the top of its range against the violin, which forces the orchestra's own cellos and basses to carry the foundation alone. The two soloists almost never compete; where a later concerto would stage a duel, Bach stages an agreement, and keeps the orchestra thin so the blend can be heard.","Bach's sinfonias concertantes were popular enough in his lifetime to be pirated, then forgotten along with the rest of his output. Their importance is now measured largely by what came next: the young Mozart met this fashion in Paris in 1778 and answered it the following year. Modern performances range from chamber-scale period readings to fuller modern-orchestra ones, which blunt the dialogue.","Follow the cello rather than the violin — it is the surprising voice here. Listen for the passages in parallel thirds, where the two instruments stop being soloists and become one wider instrument. Then put on Mozart's Sinfonia Concertante for violin and viola and hear the same idea taken somewhere considerably darker."],"boccherini-minuet-string-quintet-op-11-no":["Four minutes of pure poise: the third movement of a string quintet scored for two violins, viola and two cellos. Muted violins pick out a tiptoeing tune over a plucked, on-the-beat accompaniment, so light it sounds like a music box. The middle section relaxes into smoother lines before the opening returns exactly as it was. The quintet around it is in E major; the minuet itself sits in A.","Boccherini wrote it in 1771, three years after moving to Madrid, where he served the Infante Don Luis, the Spanish king's younger brother, at a provincial court. He was a cello virtuoso who effectively invented the two-cello quintet so that the interesting lower line could be his own. Chamber music at such a court meant a handful of players and a small aristocratic audience, several evenings a week.","The second cello, not the first, holds the piece together: it plays the plucked bass that gives the minuet its clockwork tread while Boccherini's own part sings above. Notice how deliberately veiled the whole texture is — the mutes are his instruction. And count the phrase lengths: the tune is nudged slightly off the regular eight-bar pattern, which is why it never quite sits still.","The minuet escaped its quintet almost immediately and has led a separate life ever since, in arrangements for everything from piano to salon orchestra, and in films whenever a scene needs eighteenth-century politeness. That fame has cost Boccherini: he is widely known for four minutes and unheard for the several hundred works around them. Period-instrument groups have been steadily rebuilding the case for the quintets.","Hear it inside its quintet rather than in the arrangement you already know — the surrounding movements change what the minuet means. Listen to the second cello throughout. Then compare a period-instrument version on gut strings with a plush string-orchestra arrangement; the first sounds like a room, the second like a soundtrack."],"boccherini-musica-notturna-delle-strade-di-madrid":["A string quintet in seven linked scenes, about thirteen minutes long, reproducing a night in the streets of Madrid. Church bells ring the Ave Maria; a soldier's drum passes; blind beggars play a lopsided minuet; a rosary is murmured; street singers strike up a passacalle; and at the end the night patrol, the Ritirata, marches by and recedes into silence. Two violins, viola and two cellos, playing effects more than melodies.","Composed in 1780 for Boccherini's Spanish patrons and later revised, it belongs to his Madrid years as chamber composer to the Infante Don Luis. He warned his Parisian publisher that the piece would be meaningless outside Spain and refused to let it be printed there. Street music was heard nightly in a city where curfew was enforced by an actual patrol, and his listeners would have recognised every sound in it.","The players are asked to become something other than a string quintet: they tap the wood of their instruments, strum them like guitars, imitate drums by bowing near the bridge, and slide their fingers to catch the wobble of untrained street voices. In the closing Ritirata the same march is repeated as variations that grow quieter and thinner — an early fade-out, because the patrol is not stopping, it is walking away.","Long treated as a novelty, the piece has been reassessed as one of the eighteenth century's most radical experiments in instrumental imitation, closer to a sound-portrait than to abstract chamber music. Its modern fame owes much to the film Master and Commander, which used the Ritirata. Performers argue over how far to push the noises: politely, as decoration, or roughly, as the street sounds Boccherini clearly intended.","Listen to the last movement first if you are impatient — the retreating patrol is the most striking three minutes. Then go back and follow the sequence as a walk through a city at night, noticing when each new sound arrives. Period-instrument groups are worth seeking out: gut strings make the imitations rougher and far more convincing."],"boccherini-cello-concerto-b":["Three movements, roughly twenty-two minutes, for cello with a small orchestra of strings, oboes and horns. The solo writing climbs constantly into the highest register, where the cello sounds almost like a viola or a soprano, and rattles off scales and arpeggios that lie awkwardly for anyone but a specialist. A singing central slow movement separates two brisk outer ones, and the mood stays sunlit rather than dramatic throughout.","Boccherini was one of the finest cellists in Europe and wrote around a dozen concertos for himself to play, this among them, in the years around 1770. He had toured to Paris with the violinist Filippo Manfredi before settling in Spain. At that date the cello was still climbing out of its job as the bass instrument of the orchestra, and virtuoso concertos were the argument for its promotion.","Most of the twentieth century heard this concerto in a version that is not Boccherini's. The cellist Friedrich Grützmacher published an edition in the 1890s that rewrote the solo part, added passages of his own, and replaced the slow movement with one taken from a different Boccherini concerto altogether. The original is leaner, higher and less romantic; the hybrid was a staple for decades and is still recorded.","Grützmacher's version made the piece famous and its authenticity a scandal, and the argument over which to play has become a small case study in nineteenth-century editorial habits. Casals and his successors played the arrangement without apology; period-instrument cellists now favour the original text. Boccherini's cello writing has also been reappraised as technically ahead of its time rather than merely charming.","Listen for the register: the solo line spends much of its time far above where you expect a cello to live. If you already know the piece, find out which version you have and then hear the other — a rare chance to compare two centuries' idea of the same music. Period cello recordings are the leaner option."],"boccherini-fandango-guitar-quintet-no-4":["The finale of a quintet for guitar and string quartet: seven minutes of fandango, the Andalusian dance in a fast triple time, built over a bass figure that circles round and round without ever resolving. The guitar strums, the strings answer with snapped chords and repeated notes, and castanets are usually added. The music builds by accumulation rather than by argument — the same handful of gestures, tighter and faster each time round.","In the 1790s, after his patron's death, Boccherini arranged a set of quintets for the Marqués de Benavente, an aristocratic guitar player who wanted music he could join in on. The fandango came from an earlier string quintet; Boccherini simply gave the guitar its natural home. He was by then living in reduced circumstances in Madrid, adapting his own back catalogue to survive.","The circling bass is a survival from much older Spanish practice — the same kind of repeating ground that runs under the folia and the passacalle — so a piece written in the 1790s stands on a seventeenth-century foundation. Listen for the strings imitating guitar technique: rapid repeated notes in place of strumming, chords struck all at once rather than rolled. The castanet part is left largely to the player.","Boccherini's guitar quintets survive partly in copies and their texts are uncertain, which has left performers considerable freedom and scholars considerable irritation. For much of the twentieth century they were regarded as lightweight arrangements; the guitar revival made them central repertoire. The current debate is how much Spanish rhythmic licence to take — the fandango played straight, or with the flexible push and drag of flamenco.","Turn it up. This is music that rewards volume and a good percussionist. Listen for the bass pattern and count how many times it comes round; the excitement lies entirely in what gets stacked on top. Then compare a strict classical reading with one by players who bring flamenco phrasing — the same notes, two different centuries of dance."],"boccherini-symphony-casa-del-diavolo":["A symphony of about twenty minutes whose subtitle — The House of the Devil — refers to its finale. A slow, ominous introduction leads to a driving Allegro; a gentler middle movement follows; and then the last movement returns to the opening music before launching a chaconne depicting hell, with hammering repeated chords, jagged leaps and a headlong rush to the end. The orchestra is strings with oboes and horns, and it is worked hard.","Boccherini composed the Op. 12 set in Madrid in 1771 and openly modelled the finale on the Dance of the Furies from Gluck's Orfeo ed Euridice, saying so in the score. This was the Sturm und Drang moment across Europe — minor keys, syncopation, theatrical violence carried into instrumental music — and Boccherini, usually the most polished of composers, joined in with unusual ferocity.","The finale is a chaconne, meaning the same short harmonic pattern repeats underneath while the surface grows wilder — a Baroque device pressed into service as a picture of damnation. Listen for the return of the introduction's material at the start of the last movement, which frames the whole symphony as one gesture rather than three separate ones. Gluck's borrowed music is transformed rather than quoted verbatim.","Boccherini's symphonies were long dismissed as chamber music with extra players, an impression the elegant quintets encouraged; this piece is the standing rebuttal. It was revived on record in the later twentieth century and remains his most performed orchestral work. Scholars have used it to argue that the Spanish court was far less isolated from continental fashion than the old story of Boccherini's exile suggests.","Go straight to the finale, then double back for the rest — the hell music is the reason the piece exists. Listen for the repeating chord pattern underneath the fury. Hearing Gluck's Orfeo first makes the debt audible, and a period band, with biting gut strings and raw horns, makes the devil considerably more frightening."],"salieri-sinfonia-veneziana":["A short, sunny symphony of about twelve minutes in three movements: a bustling Allegro, a brief singing slow movement, and a quick finale in dance rhythm. The scoring is theatrical — strings with oboes, horns and trumpets — and the writing favours crisp repeated notes, rushing scales and long crescendos over any sustained argument. Everything is built to seize an audience's attention quickly and then let it go again.","Salieri assembled it around 1780 from music for his comic opera La scuola de' gelosi, first given in Venice, which is where the nickname comes from. Recycling an overture and a couple of numbers into a concert symphony was routine: opera houses and concert rooms shared an orchestra and an audience, and a successful theatre tune had commercial value beyond its show. Salieri was then in his late twenties and rising fast in Vienna.","You can hear the seams if you listen for them. The opening movement behaves like a curtain-raiser — it whips up excitement and then stops, rather than developing its ideas — and the finale's tunes are cut to the length of a stage number. The crescendos, built by piling instruments onto a repeated figure, come straight from the Italian opera pit, where they were used to bring a house to the boil.","Salieri's instrumental music vanished for nearly two centuries behind the poisoning legend, and what revival there has been followed the film Amadeus making him notorious again. Musicologists have used works like this to argue that he was a fluent, theatrically intelligent composer rather than a mediocrity. The symphony remains a curiosity rather than a repertory piece, and editions differ on which movements belong.","Listen for the crescendos — Salieri was among the composers who taught Vienna the trick Rossini would later industrialise. Keep your expectations theatrical rather than symphonic and the piece delivers. A brisk chamber-orchestra recording suits it far better than a heavy one; compare it with a Haydn symphony of the same years to hear the difference between theatre and argument."],"salieri-piano-concerto-c":["Three movements, about twenty-two minutes, for keyboard with a modest orchestra of strings, oboes and horns. The opening Allegro is broad and formal, the slow movement a quiet aria without words, the finale lighter and dancing. The keyboard part is elegant rather than muscular — scales, broken chords and decorated melody rather than power — and the orchestra keeps a respectful distance, framing the soloist more than challenging them.","Salieri wrote two keyboard concertos in 1773, in his early twenties, shortly after Gassmann and Gluck had established him in Vienna. This was the moment the fortepiano was overtaking the harpsichord in Viennese households, and the concerto was the fashionable vehicle for a composer-performer to display himself. Mozart would arrive in the city a decade later and turn the same genre into something entirely different.","The finale is the surprise: rather than driving to a brilliant close, it winds down into a gentle dancing rhythm and ends in charm rather than triumph. Listen also for the woodwind, given the occasional independent phrase — small hints of the wind writing that later made Salieri's operas admired. The solo part is notated plainly and assumes the player will decorate it, so no two performances should sound quite alike.","Salieri wrote no more keyboard concertos, and these two sat unplayed until the late twentieth century, when interest in everything around Mozart brought them out. Opinion divides between those who hear a competent conservative and those who hear a genuine if unadventurous melodic gift. Nobody claims they are Mozart's equal; the useful question is what Viennese concert music sounded like before he changed it.","Hear it as evidence rather than as competition: this is the ordinary excellent standard of 1770s Vienna, the level Mozart's concertos left behind. Listen for the slow movement's long vocal lines — Salieri was an opera composer first and last. A fortepiano recording, with its lighter tone and quicker decay, keeps the balance the music assumes."],"salieri-danaides-overture":["The orchestral prelude to a five-act tragic opera, running about six minutes. It begins darkly and slowly, with heavy chords and restless string figures, then accelerates into a driving fast section full of hammered rhythms and sudden silences. No cheerful second tune arrives to relieve it; the music stays taut and runs straight into the first scene, where fifty brides are being married off to men they have been ordered to murder that night.","Les Danaïdes opened at the Paris Opéra on 26 April 1784. Gluck, too old and ill to write it, had secured the commission and allowed his name to stand alongside Salieri's — then, once the piece succeeded, announced publicly that his pupil had written all of it. Paris was still arguing about the reform of opera into serious drama, and Salieri arrived from Vienna to win the argument in Gluck's name.","The overture refuses the era's standard bargain, which was to entertain for five minutes and then let the drama begin. It has no self-contained ending: the music runs into the opening scene, so the audience is inside the story before they realise the curtain has gone up. Listen for the low strings and bassoons, which carry a menace the melody never states, and for the abrupt cut-offs that leave silence hanging.","The premiere was a triumph and the opera held the Paris stage for four decades; Berlioz, who heard it as a student, wrote with astonishment about its final scene in hell. Then the whole of Salieri disappeared and only the poisoning legend survived. Revivals since the 1980s have argued that Les Danaïdes is a genuinely powerful post-Gluck tragedy, and the overture is the shortest way in.","Listen for what does not happen: no tune arrives to comfort you, and the music never relaxes. If the ending seems to stop rather than conclude, that is correct — it was written to run into the opera. Compare it with Mozart's Don Giovanni overture, three years later, for two composers' answers to the same question of how to make an audience afraid."],"salieri-requiem-c-introitus":["The opening movement of a Requiem Mass: about five minutes for four solo voices, chorus and orchestra. It begins almost without sound — low strings, and a hushed choir intoning Requiem aeternam on repeated notes — and grows only gradually, with the soloists emerging out of the choral texture rather than standing apart from it. Trombones double the voices in the old church manner, and the movement stays dark and restrained throughout.","Salieri composed it in 1804, at fifty-four, and explicitly for his own funeral; he kept it unperformed in a drawer for twenty-one years, and it was first sung at his memorial service in 1825. By then he had given up writing opera, taught Beethoven, Schubert and Liszt without charging, and outlived the world in which he had been Vienna's most powerful musician. Church music was what remained to him.","The scoring tells you what he was thinking about: trombones shadowing the voices, a deliberately archaic device reaching back past Mozart into the older Viennese church tradition. There are almost no passages of solo display — remarkable from a composer who spent his life writing arias — because the piece was designed to be heard once, over his own coffin. The choir's opening is very nearly monotone.","The Requiem was published soon after Salieri's death and then followed the rest of his output into obscurity. Its rediscovery has been slow and partly voyeuristic, driven by curiosity about the man rather than the music. Critics who take it seriously note its restraint and its refusal of theatrical effect; others find it merely correct. Recordings are few, and no performing tradition has settled.","Come to it directly after Mozart's Requiem and hear how differently two Viennese composers imagined their own deaths — one unfinished and blazing, this one quiet, finished and shelved. Listen for the trombones under the voices. A modest choir, closer to the size Salieri expected, suits the music far better than a large festival chorus."],"clementi-sonatina-op-36-no-1":["Five minutes of piano music in three tiny movements: a bright Allegro, a short Andante, and a skipping Vivace. The whole thing is arranged so that a young player's hands never stretch beyond an octave or leave the middle of the keyboard, and yet the shapes are real music — a proper first theme, a contrasting second one, a development, a return home. Nothing in it is padding, and nothing is condescending.","Clementi published the six progressive sonatinas of Op. 36 in London in 1797, at the height of his second career. He had arrived from Rome as a teenager under the patronage of an English gentleman, become a celebrated performer, and then built a business: a publishing house, a piano factory, and a stream of teaching material for the growing middle-class market that owned instruments and wanted lessons for its children.","The first movement is a complete sonata form in about ninety seconds — exposition, development, recapitulation — which is why generations of students have absorbed the shape of Classical music from it without ever being told. Listen for the left hand's rippling broken chords, the Alberti bass: it is the single most characteristic sound of the era, and here it is being taught. Clementi later revised the set, and editions differ in small details.","No music has been more played and less listened to. Because the sonatinas are pedagogy, concert pianists have mostly left them alone, and their reputation is bound up with childhood practice rather than artistic merit. Horowitz's advocacy of Clementi's full-scale sonatas prompted a partial reassessment; the sonatinas themselves are now sometimes defended as models of how much can be done with very little.","Listen to it played by a real pianist rather than remembered from a lesson — the difference is startling. Follow the first movement's two tunes and notice when the first comes back; you have just heard sonata form. On a period fortepiano the textures are drier and the whole thing sounds less like a lesson and more like a piece."],"clementi-gradus-ad-parnassum-excerpt":["One study drawn from a hundred, published in three volumes as the summit of piano technique. Each piece isolates a problem — running scales in thirds, crossed hands, repeated notes, a melody sung by one finger while the others accompany — and turns it into a two- or three-minute composition, some of them fugues, some genuinely beautiful. The best are concert pieces disguised as exercises; the rest are honest drill.","Clementi issued Gradus ad Parnassum between 1817 and 1826, in his sixties and seventies, as the culmination of a life spent teaching, publishing and building pianos. The instrument had changed beyond recognition since his youth: heavier action, wider range, far more sound. The title, Steps to Parnassus, was borrowed from the eighteenth-century counterpoint manual every trained musician knew, claiming the same authority for the piano.","Later editors did Clementi no favours. Carl Tausig's popular selection kept only the exercises and discarded the fugues and character pieces, so the work's reputation as pure drudgery is largely an editorial accident. Listen for the studies that are really preludes in disguise, with proper harmonic journeys. Debussy knew the collection well enough to parody its plodding earnestness in the first piece of Children's Corner.","For a century Gradus was the technical bible of the conservatoire, and its name became a byword for tedium — Debussy's joke landed because everyone recognised the target. Modern pianists mostly use it selectively, while scholars have argued that the complete collection is a serious late work in the tradition of Bach's keyboard cycles. Complete recordings exist but remain rare.","Do not try to hear all hundred. Find a recording that includes the fugues and the slower character studies, and start there. Then listen to Debussy's Doctor Gradus ad Parnassum for the joke — a child's fingers grinding through exercises and then escaping. On a period instrument the studies sound noticeably less mechanical."],"clementi-sonatina-op-36-no-3":["The third of the six progressive sonatinas, and a clear step harder than the first: about six minutes in three movements. It opens Spiritoso with a fanfare-like figure answered by quick runs, settles into a short songlike slow movement, and ends with a rondo whose tune keeps coming back after brief excursions elsewhere. The hands now have to move around the keyboard, and the left hand is given real work rather than accompaniment.","Part of the same 1797 London set, and the same commercial project: Clementi's firm sold the pianos, published the music and supplied the teachers. The English middle class had money, drawing rooms and daughters expected to play, and a graded series carrying a beginner from first steps to something presentable was a product as much as a pedagogical idea. It has never been out of print since.","The set is genuinely progressive — difficulties are introduced in order, one new problem per piece — so playing all six in sequence is a curriculum rather than an anthology. In this one the new demand is independence: the left hand stops being a simple accompaniment and starts carrying phrases of its own. Listen for the moments when the melody moves down into the bass, which is where young players usually stumble.","As with the rest of Op. 36, professional performance is rare and critical attention rarer; the sonatinas exist in the world as objects of practice, not of listening. When they are recorded it is usually as a complete set, by a pianist making a case for Clementi. The strongest argument in their favour is a negative one: nothing in them is filler, and almost no other teaching music can claim that.","Play it if you can, even badly — this music was written to be under the fingers rather than in the ear. Otherwise listen to the left hand and notice how much more it does than in the first sonatina. Hearing all six in order takes half an hour and shows a teacher's mind at work, step by careful step."],"clementi-piano-sonata-op-25-no-5":["A full-scale sonata of some sixteen minutes in three movements, and nothing like the sonatinas. The first movement is restless and dark, driven by an angular theme and by sudden dynamic swerves; the slow movement is broad and vocal; the finale returns to the minor and refuses to brighten. The keyboard writing is thick — octaves, wide leaps, and long passages where both hands are fully occupied at once.","Published in 1790, when Clementi was at his peak as a performer and had already fought his famous keyboard contest with Mozart before Emperor Joseph II, declared a draw by imperial diplomacy. The key was unusual and uncomfortable, and the sonata assumes an English piano with heavier touch and more sustaining power than the Viennese instruments Mozart preferred — which partly explains the two men's mutual incomprehension.","Beethoven owned Clementi's sonatas and taught from them, and this is the sort of piece that shows why: the drama comes from rhythm and register rather than from melody, and the development pushes ideas around instead of decorating them. Listen for the passages where both hands play the same line an octave or two apart, a bare sound Clementi used for menace and Beethoven then took over wholesale.","Mozart's private verdict — that Clementi was a mechanic with no taste and not a farthing's worth of feeling — followed him for two centuries and was quoted far more often than his music was played. Horowitz recorded the sonatas in the 1950s and began the rehabilitation; scholars now treat Clementi as a genuine forerunner of Beethoven's keyboard style rather than a rival to be scored against Mozart.","Come to it having heard the sonatinas and prepare to revise your opinion of the composer. Listen for the bare octaves, and for how rarely the music offers a comfortable tune. Then put on an early Beethoven sonata and the family resemblance is unmistakable. A period fortepiano with an English action makes the heaviness sound intended rather than clumsy."],"mozart-symphony-no-40-g":["Four movements, about half an hour, and one of only two symphonies Mozart wrote in a minor key. It starts without preamble: the violas murmur for half a bar and the violins are already sighing out the main theme, a three-note figure that will not leave the movement alone. Then a slow movement of uneasy calm, a minuet that stamps rather than dances, and a finale that opens with a rocketing scale and ends without consolation.","Completed on 25 July 1788 in Vienna, in the same summer as the E flat and Jupiter symphonies — three symphonies in roughly six weeks. Mozart was thirty-two, in financial trouble, writing begging letters to his fellow Mason Michael Puchberg, and had just moved to cheaper lodgings outside the city walls. No record of a performance in his lifetime survives, though he later revised the score to add clarinets, which suggests one was planned.","Almost everything grows from that opening three-note sigh: it turns up inverted, stretched, buried in the bass, hammered out by the full orchestra. The strangest passage is the finale's development, which begins with a jagged phrase of bare octaves running through nearly all twelve notes of the chromatic scale in a few bars — music that unsettled listeners for a century. The minuet's phrases are cut to three bars, not four, so it limps.","The nineteenth century heard this symphony as Greek grace and lightness; Schumann called it Grecian, and the description survived decades of performances that smoothed it over. The twentieth century heard tragedy instead, and period-instrument bands since the 1980s have made it faster, harder and more violent again. Which version of the wind parts to use — Mozart wrote the piece before adding clarinets — remains a live editorial choice.","Do not wait for an introduction; the tune starts immediately, and its first three notes are the key to everything. Listen for that figure returning where you least expect it. Then compare a period-instrument recording with a large modern-orchestra one: the first sounds like an argument, the second like a lament, and both are defensible readings of the same notes."],"mozart-piano-concerto-no-23-adagio":["The middle movement of a piano concerto, about seven minutes long, and the only music Mozart wrote in F sharp minor. The piano begins alone with a drooping, uneven melody in the swaying rhythm of a siciliano — a slow dance in compound time — before the orchestra answers. There are no trumpets, no drums and no oboes: only clarinets, bassoons, flute, horns and strings, a deliberately soft-edged sound with the brightness taken out.","Mozart entered the concerto in his catalogue on 2 March 1786 and played it that spring in Vienna, in the same weeks he was finishing The Marriage of Figaro. He was writing concertos at speed for his own subscription concerts — the freelance system he had invented for himself after leaving Salzburg: sell the tickets, book the hall, hire the players, perform your own new work, keep the takings.","The piano's opening melody is left almost bare, a single line over the thinnest accompaniment, and its off-balance phrasing keeps landing a beat later than the ear expects. Listen for the woodwind entry in the middle section, where flute, clarinet and bassoon take over the melody and the piano becomes the accompanist. Mozart wrote no cadenza here; the movement's one flourish is short and written out in full.","The A major concerto has long been the one recommended to newcomers, and this movement is its emotional centre; it is also the piece most often cited when people argue that Mozart's minor-key writing is more desolate than his major-key drama. Performance debate concerns ornamentation — whether the soloist should decorate the returns of the theme, as eighteenth-century practice assumed, or play the bare text.","Start here rather than with the concerto's cheerful outer movements; this is one of the few pieces where the middle is the point. Listen for the moment the winds take the melody away from the piano. Fortepiano recordings make the accompaniment audible in a way modern instruments cannot; hear one alongside a famous modern-piano version."],"mozart-marriage-figaro-overture":["Four minutes flat out. A whispered scurrying figure low in the strings and bassoons rushes upward into a full-orchestra shout, and the piece never stops running: tunes tumble over one another, the winds interrupt, the whole band pulls up short for a beat of silence and then plunges on. There is no slow introduction and no development section — Mozart lays out his material twice and finishes, in a blaze of D major.","Le nozze di Figaro opened at Vienna's Burgtheater on 1 May 1786. Da Ponte's libretto came from a Beaumarchais play that Joseph II had banned from the Viennese stage as an incitement against the nobility; the librettist talked the Emperor round by cutting the most inflammatory speeches and keeping the plot, in which servants outwit their master. Mozart wrote the overture last, close to the first night.","The overture quotes nothing from the opera — unusual for Mozart, who often previewed a tune — and instead conveys the plot's mechanism: everyone talking at once, nobody listening, events outrunning intention. He originally sketched a slower middle section in the minor and crossed it out, which is why the piece is so short. Listen for the bassoons, which carry the opening figure and set the comedy's nervous energy.","The Vienna premiere was a success but not an overwhelming one; Prague made the opera a sensation later that year, and Mozart wrote that people there talked of nothing but Figaro. The overture has been a concert item ever since, and the recurring argument about it is tempo: fast enough to sparkle, or so fast that the wind detail vanishes. Period orchestras have generally chosen speed.","Count the seconds before the first loud chord — the quiet opening lasts only about fifteen. Then listen for the wind instruments, which is where the jokes live and where a too-fast performance loses them. Compare an opera-house recording with a concert one: theatre versions tend to be lighter, because the conductor knows exactly what is about to happen on stage."],"mozart-requiem-lacrimosa":["Three minutes near the middle of the Requiem's Sequence, and the last music Mozart wrote. Strings rock in a sighing two-note figure, the choir enters quietly and climbs in a long crescendo through Lacrimosa dies illa — that day of tears — to a single high cry, then falls back. Basset horns and trombones darken the sound, and the movement closes on a plain Amen cadence rather than a flourish.","In the summer of 1791 an anonymous messenger commissioned a Requiem; the patron was Count Franz von Walsegg, who intended to pass it off as his own memorial for his young wife. Mozart, simultaneously finishing The Magic Flute and La clemenza di Tito, worked at it through the autumn while his health collapsed. He died on 5 December, and his widow Constanze needed the score finished in order to keep the fee.","Mozart wrote out only the first eight bars of the Lacrimosa — the rocking strings and the voices' entry — before stopping. Everything after that is by another hand, most likely his pupil Franz Xaver Süssmayr, working from sketches and memory. The join is audible once you know: the music after the climb is simpler and more four-square. A separate sketch for an Amen fugue surfaced in the 1960s, suggesting Mozart meant to end the movement quite differently.","Nineteenth-century Europe turned the Requiem into a death-mask, complete with a stranger in grey and Salieri's poison, legends later dismantled by scholarship. The real argument is textual: Süssmayr's completion is the one everybody knows and is manifestly weaker than Mozart, and at least half a dozen modern completions have tried to do better. None has displaced it in the concert hall.","Listen to the first eight bars twice — once for the music, once knowing that the pen stopped there. Follow the rising line in the sopranos; the whole movement is one long climb and collapse. Then compare Süssmayr's ending with a modern completion such as Levin's or Maunder's, and decide whether the familiar version has earned its familiarity."],"mozart-clarinet-concerto-adagio":["Seven minutes, and about as plain as Mozart ever allowed himself. The clarinet sings a long unhurried melody almost without ornament, the orchestra answers, and the tune returns after a short middle section — that is the entire design. The soloist's line moves constantly between registers, so the same melody is warm in the middle, silvery at the top and woody at the bottom. Strings, flutes, bassoons and horns; no oboes, trumpets or drums.","Written in October 1791, in the last weeks of Mozart's life, for Anton Stadler, a fellow Freemason and the finest clarinettist in Vienna. Stadler played a modified instrument, the basset clarinet, with extra keys extending its range downward by several notes, and Mozart wrote for that range specifically. He was working simultaneously on The Magic Flute and the Requiem and had less than two months to live.","The version everybody plays is a compromise. Mozart's autograph is lost, and the concerto survives only in a posthumous printed edition that transposed Stadler's low notes upward to fit an ordinary clarinet — so the solo line as usually heard leaps up where it should descend. Reconstructions for basset clarinet restore the plunges, and the melody's shape changes noticeably. Listen for the places where the line jumps for no apparent reason.","Long the most loved of clarinet concertos and, since the film Out of Africa, one of the most recognised pieces of classical music anywhere. Basset-clarinet reconstructions of the last half-century have changed professional practice without displacing the standard version, since few players own the instrument. Interpretive argument centres on whether the Adagio should be played straight or with the improvised decoration a period soloist would have added.","Listen for register rather than for melody — the tune is simple, but its colour changes constantly as the line moves up and down. Then hear a basset-clarinet recording and follow the low notes ordinary performances cannot reach. It rewards a quiet room and no distractions; nothing here grabs you, and everything can be missed."],"mozart-eine-kleine-nachtmusik":["A serenade in four movements, about seventeen minutes, for a small string group — usually string quartet with a double bass, sometimes a full string orchestra. It opens with a rising fanfare in bare octaves that everyone recognises, follows with a Romanze of almost nursery simplicity, then a brisk minuet, and a rondo finale that trots to the finish. The textures are transparent throughout: melody on top, accompaniment beneath, almost no counterpoint.","Mozart entered it in his personal catalogue on 10 August 1787, while at work on Don Giovanni. Serenades were functional music — played outdoors or at parties, in the evening, to accompany conversation and eating — and Vienna consumed them by the cartload. Nobody knows who commissioned this one or whether it was performed in Mozart's lifetime; it went unpublished until 1827, decades after his death.","The autograph shows five movements, not four: a minuet and trio between the first and second movements has been cut out of the manuscript, the page physically removed, and it has never been found. So the piece as we know it is missing a limb. Notice too that the famous opening is not a tune but a signal — a rising arpeggio and a falling answer, the eighteenth-century way of calling a room to attention.","Ubiquity has done the piece damage: it plays in lifts and adverts, and serious listeners often assume there is nothing in it. Musicians disagree. The live argument among performers is size — one player per part, as the title's kleine suggests, or a full string orchestra, which is how most recordings do it. The chamber version restores the wit and loses the gloss.","Hear it with one player per part if you can find such a recording; the difference is like cleaning a varnished painting. Listen past the first movement, which everyone knows, to the Romanze and its unexpectedly stormy middle. And keep the missing minuet in mind — the piece was built with a different sense of proportion from the one we have."],"mozart-rondo-alla-turca":["The finale of a three-movement piano sonata, about three and a half minutes, and unusual in that the sonata around it contains no movement in ordinary sonata form at all. The rondo alternates a clipped minor-key march tune with a swaggering major-key refrain full of hammered chords and rolled left-hand figures. The effect is percussive rather than melodic: the piano is imitating a band of drums, cymbals and shrill pipes.","Mozart composed the sonata around 1783 and published it the following year. Habsburg Vienna had a long and anxious relationship with the Ottoman Empire, whose armies had twice besieged the city; by the 1780s that fear had curdled into fashion, and Turkish music — the janissary band's noise of bass drum, cymbals and triangle — was the exotic sound of the moment. Some Viennese pianos even had a pedal that added a real drum and bells.","The imitation is precise: the repeated left-hand chords stand in for cymbals, the grace notes for the shrill pipe, the crashing octaves for the bass drum. On an instrument with a janissary pedal the effect would have been literal rather than suggested. A page of an earlier manuscript surfaced in Budapest in 2014 with readings that differ from the standard text in small but audible details, including some ornaments.","Everybody's first Mozart and, for that reason, everybody's least examined. Nineteenth-century pianists made it a warhorse and added octaves; twentieth-century purists stripped them off again. The piece also sits at the centre of an uncomfortable conversation about musical exoticism — whether it is affectionate borrowing, caricature, or simply what a Viennese audience heard as noise from elsewhere.","Listen for the drum: the left hand is a percussion section, not an accompaniment. Then find a recording on a fortepiano with a janissary stop, where the bells and drum actually sound — it turns a familiar piece into something strange and much funnier. Compare it with the sonata's opening variations to hear how far the work travels."],"mozart-magic-flute-queen-night-aria":["Three minutes of controlled fury: the Queen of the Night's second aria, in which she puts a knife in her daughter's hand and orders her to murder Sarastro. The voice takes off from a declamatory opening into runs that vault to top F — the highest note in standard operatic repertoire — four times, staccato, like laughter or gunfire. Fast, in D minor, with the orchestra hammering out an accompaniment that leaves the singer almost nowhere to breathe.","Die Zauberflöte opened on 30 September 1791 at Emanuel Schikaneder's suburban Theater auf der Wieden — a popular house, not a court theatre, playing to a mixed paying audience in German rather than Italian. Mozart wrote the part for his sister-in-law Josefa Hofer, whose extreme upper range he knew exactly. He conducted the first performances and was dead nine weeks later.","The coloratura is not decoration but characterisation. The Queen's runs are shaped like an instrumental flourish, so she sounds less like a person than like a mechanism — precisely the point in an opera about reason defeating night. Listen too for the orchestra's stabbing chords between phrases: they give the singer no time, so the breathlessness you hear is composed in. Her first-act aria begins gently and only then unleashes the same range.","The opera has been read as Masonic allegory, as pantomime, as Enlightenment parable, and as a work with an ugly streak in its treatment of the Queen and of Monostatos, and all these arguments remain live. The aria itself is the standard test of a coloratura soprano; casting debate concerns whether to prize accuracy in the staccato high notes or venom in the words, since few singers deliver both.","Listen for the four staccato high notes and count them. Then go back and listen to the words rather than the acrobatics — this is a mother threatening to disown her child. Compare a bright, precise recording with a heavier, more dramatic one; the piece can be a firework or a threat, and the best performances manage both at once."],"mozart-don-giovanni-overture":["Six minutes in two halves. It opens in D minor with slow, weighted chords and a shuddering rising-and-falling figure in the strings — music of stone and dread — then breaks into a fast D major Allegro of nervous energy and quick contrasts, ending without a proper cadence so that it runs straight into the first scene. The drums and trumpets are prominent in the slow opening and the strings never settle in the fast one.","Don Giovanni was written for Prague, which had adored Figaro, and opened at the Estates Theatre on 29 October 1787. Mozart was thirty-one; his father had died that May. The story that he wrote the overture the night before the premiere, kept awake by Constanze telling him stories, comes from early biographies and cannot be verified — though the ink was certainly barely dry and the orchestra sight-read it.","The slow introduction is not an introduction at all: it is the music of the Commendatore's statue arriving to drag Giovanni to hell in the last scene, heard two and a half hours early. Nobody in the audience can know that yet, which makes the overture a warning that only becomes legible in retrospect. Listen for the two-note falling figure in the strings; it returns at the supper table.","Prague was ecstatic; Vienna, which heard a revised version the following year, was cooler, and Joseph II is reported to have said the food was not to the teeth of his Viennese. The nineteenth century treated the opera as the greatest ever written — Kierkegaard and E.T.A. Hoffmann wrote at length on it — while modern productions argue over whether the moralising final ensemble should be cut, as it often was.","Listen to the first ninety seconds, then skip to the opera's penultimate scene and hear the same music return with a voice on top; the overture makes far more sense backwards. In concert performances a composed concert ending is tacked on, so seek out a recording from a complete opera set, where it runs straight into the action as intended."],"mozart-symphony-no-41-jupiter-finale":["Ten minutes that end the last symphony Mozart wrote. Five separate melodic ideas — the first of them only four long notes — are introduced, developed, and then, in the closing pages, played simultaneously in every possible combination, five voices interlocking without a wasted note. The movement is fast, in C major, scored with trumpets and drums, and its coda is among the loudest and most complex passages in eighteenth-century music.","Completed on 10 August 1788, the last of three symphonies written that summer. Mozart was in debt, his six-month-old daughter had died in June, and the concert life that had made him rich in 1784 had dried up. So far as anyone knows he never heard the piece performed. The nickname Jupiter is not his — it probably came from the London impresario Johann Peter Salomon, the man who later brought Haydn to England.","The four-note opening figure was not new: Mozart had used it in earlier works, and it appears in chant and in music by his contemporaries, functioning almost as common property. What is new is the counterpoint. In the final combination each of the five themes keeps its own character while fitting the others, and the passage can be turned upside down and still work — invertible counterpoint, learned from studying Bach and Handel at Baron van Swieten's.","For the nineteenth century this was the summit of the Classical symphony and the proof that Mozart was Bach's heir as a contrapuntist; Brahms and others studied the coda closely. Twentieth-century writers occasionally complained that the display is academic. Period-instrument performances have made the case for speed, taking the coda faster than seemed possible and letting the lines blur into brilliance rather than separating them.","Learn the four-note opening first — long, long, long, long — because you will need to hear it inside the traffic later. Then wait for the last two minutes and try to track two of the five themes at once; nobody catches all five. Compare a period band with a large modern orchestra: the smaller group keeps the lines audible, the larger delivers the weight."],"mozart-piano-concerto-no-21-andante":["Seven minutes, and one of the strangest textures Mozart devised. Muted violins float a long arching melody over a triplet pulse in the lower strings and a plucked bass, so the accompaniment sounds like a heartbeat under a dream. The piano takes the tune, embroiders it, and wanders through remote keys before finding its way back. The orchestra is a full one, with trumpets and drums that stay silent for the whole movement.","Entered in Mozart's catalogue on 9 March 1785 and played by him the following night at Vienna's Burgtheater, in the most successful season of his life; his father Leopold was visiting and wrote home in astonishment about the money and the applause. Mozart was producing concertos as fast as he could sell subscriptions, three or four a season, performing each himself from a part often still incomplete on the page.","The bass line refuses to sit still: it keeps stepping downward through chromatic notes that pull the harmony sideways, which is why the movement feels unsettled despite its calm surface. Listen for the moments when the melody hangs on a note that clashes with the chord beneath before resolving — the effect Romantic composers spent the next century chasing. The mutes on the violins are Mozart's instruction, not a conductor's idea.","The movement became world-famous in 1967 as the theme of the Swedish film Elvira Madigan, and for a generation the concerto was sold under that title, to the irritation of musicians. That fame has made the Andante a shorthand for sweetness, which underrates its harmonic restlessness. Performance debate concerns ornamentation, and whether the soloist should improvise a lead-in to the return of the theme.","Listen to the bass, not the tune — the low strings are where the movement's unease lives. Then notice how rarely the piano plays the melody straight. A fortepiano recording keeps the muted strings audible under the soloist; on a modern grand the balance tilts and the accompaniment recedes into wallpaper."],"mozart-piano-concerto-no-20-d":["Half an hour in three movements, and the first of Mozart's two minor-key piano concertos. The orchestra begins alone with a syncopated muttering in the low strings that never quite settles onto the beat, building to an outburst before the piano enters — not with that theme but with a new one of its own, as if refusing the argument. A serene central Romanze is torn open by a stormy middle section, and the finale drives hard before a sudden turn to the major.","Mozart finished it on 10 February 1785 and played the premiere the next evening at the Mehlgrube in Vienna, with the copyist reportedly still working on the parts. His father Leopold, newly arrived from Salzburg, sat in the audience and wrote home about the success in tones close to disbelief. Mozart was twenty-nine and at the peak of his earnings, running his own subscription series in a city hungry for new concertos.","The soloist and orchestra never really agree. The piano enters with material the orchestra has not heard and largely keeps its own themes throughout the first movement, turning the usual concerto conversation into an argument. Mozart left no cadenzas for this concerto; the ones most often played are Beethoven's, written some fifteen years later, and they pull the music forward into another century. Listen for the hidden downbeat in the opening bars.","This was the Mozart concerto the nineteenth century could love: Beethoven played it, Brahms wrote cadenzas for it, and it was almost the only one in regular circulation while the rest were forgotten. That Romantic adoption shaped how it is played — heavier, slower, more tragic than the 1785 orchestra could have sounded. Period performances have restored its sharpness without much reducing its darkness.","Listen to the opening minute before the piano enters, and try to find the downbeat; the unease is rhythmic before it is harmonic. Then wait for the finale's last two minutes, where the storm simply evaporates into D major. Hear it once with Beethoven's cadenzas and once with a period soloist improvising their own — two different pieces."],"mozart-ave-verum-corpus":["Forty-six bars, under three minutes, for four-part choir with strings and organ. There is no soloist, no fugue and almost no counterpoint: the choir moves in blocks, phrase by phrase, through a short Latin text on the body of Christ, while the strings mostly double or gently support the voices. Quiet throughout, with one moment of chromatic shadow at the words about the cross before the music settles again.","Mozart wrote it on 17 June 1791 for Anton Stoll, a schoolteacher and church choirmaster at Baden, the spa town outside Vienna where Constanze was taking the waters late in her pregnancy. Stoll's forces were small and amateur, so the piece is written to be singable by a village choir. Mozart had months to live and was about to begin work on The Magic Flute and the Requiem.","The restraint is the craft. Mozart writes almost nothing a good amateur choir cannot manage, and gets his whole effect from where the harmony moves — one unexpected chord, a single suspended dissonance, a phrase that ends higher than the ear expects. Listen for the strings dropping out and returning; they are used as light rather than as accompaniment. The motet was probably written in a single sitting.","Long treated as an occasional trifle, it is now among the most performed short choral pieces in the world and a fixture at funerals and Easter services. Its reception has been coloured by hindsight — it is hard not to hear a dying composer in it, though nothing in the circumstances supports that reading. The performance argument concerns size: a huge cathedral choir, or the dozen or so voices Stoll actually had.","Follow the harmony rather than the tune; the piece's events are chord changes, and there are only a handful of them. Listen for the one moment the music darkens. Then hear it sung by a small choir, one or two to a part, and compare with a large one — this is music that grows quieter and stranger the fewer people sing it."],"mozart-exsultate-jubilate-alleluia":["The finale of a three-movement sacred motet for solo voice and orchestra: two and a half minutes on a single word. The voice bounces through a fast dancing melody, repeating Alleluia in runs, leaps and long held notes, with the orchestra — strings, oboes and horns — providing a light springy accompaniment. It functions less like church music than like an operatic display aria that happens to be in Latin and sung in a church.","Mozart was sixteen, in Milan for the premiere of his opera Lucio Silla, and wrote the motet in January 1773 for its leading man: Venanzio Rauzzini, a castrato of exceptional agility. It was first sung on 17 January at the Theatine church. Italian sacred music of the period borrowed opera's manners cheerfully, and a virtuoso soloist in a church was a normal spectacle rather than a scandal.","The piece was written for a male soprano, and its brilliance is bound up with a voice type that no longer exists; sopranos and countertenors now divide the repertoire between them. Listen for the way the melody is built from a single rising figure repeated at higher and higher pitches — a device from the opera house designed to raise the temperature of a room. Mozart later revised the motet for a different occasion.","The Alleluia has long escaped its motet and turns up as a standalone showpiece, an audition aria and a wedding item, which flattens its context. Scholarly interest has focused on Rauzzini and on what castrato technique actually sounded like; recordings by countertenors, boy sopranos and women make an unusually direct case study in how much a voice type changes a piece. Period accompaniments have grown faster and lighter.","Hear it sung by two different voice types back to back — a bright soprano and a countertenor — and the piece changes character completely. Listen for the rising sequence that keeps ratcheting the excitement upward. And keep the composer's age in mind: sixteen, in a foreign country, writing for the star of his own opera."],"mozart-horn-concerto-no-4-rondo":["Four minutes in the swinging six-eight rhythm of a hunting field. The horn announces a tune built from the notes of a bugle call, the orchestra answers, and the melody comes round again and again with brief detours in between — including one into the minor that briefly sours the mood. The orchestra is small, strings with two oboes and two horns, kept light so the soloist carries the line without strain.","Written in Vienna in 1786 for Joseph Leutgeb, a Salzburg horn player who had followed the Mozart family for decades and by then also ran a cheesemonger's shop in the city. Mozart teased him mercilessly in the manuscripts, and this concerto's autograph is written in four colours of ink, apparently at Leutgeb's expense. The horn of the day had no valves: notes outside the natural harmonic series were produced by shading the bell with the right hand.","The hand-stopped notes are the secret of period horn playing — they sound muffled and slightly off-colour beside the open ones, so an eighteenth-century performance has a patchwork of bright and veiled tones built into the melody. Modern valve horns even it all out and lose the effect entirely. Listen for the moments where the tune leaves the bugle-call notes; on a natural horn you can hear the player working.","The rondo entered British popular culture through Flanders and Swann's comic song Ill Wind, which fitted words about a stolen horn to Mozart's tune. Musicologically, the four horn concertos have been reshuffled: the numbering does not match the order of composition, and the autographs are incomplete, so what is played as a set was assembled by editors. Natural-horn recordings have grown steadily more common since the 1980s.","Find a natural-horn recording and listen for the notes that sound covered or slightly strangled — those are the ones the player is stopping by hand, and they are supposed to be audible. Then hear a modern valve-horn version for the sheer smoothness. The tune is a hunting call, so listen for open air rather than concert hall."],"mozart-sinfonia-concertante-andante":["The central movement of a double concerto for violin and viola, about eleven minutes long and the darkest music Mozart had written by 1779. The orchestra sets out a grieving theme; the two soloists enter one after the other, then begin trading phrases, each finishing the other's sentence. Long stretches have them singing in close harmony over a walking bass. Oboes and horns only — no flutes, no trumpets, no drums to lighten it.","Written in Salzburg in 1779, soon after Mozart returned from a disastrous eighteen-month job hunt through Mannheim and Paris during which his mother died. He was twenty-three, back in a provincial post he despised, working for an archbishop he would break with two years later. The sinfonia concertante was the Parisian fashion he had just encountered at first hand, and this is his answer to it.","The viola part is written a semitone lower than it sounds, with an instruction to tune all four strings up — scordatura — so the strings are under greater tension and the instrument speaks brighter and louder against the violin. Most modern violists ignore this and play in ordinary tuning. Listen for the moment the two soloists first sound together: Mozart arranged for them to be exactly matched in weight, and the retuning was how he guaranteed it.","The piece is Mozart's finest concerto for strings and has never lacked advocates, though the viola's traditional status as the orchestra's least glamorous instrument slowed its rise. Scordatura is the live argument: purists want the retuning restored, players answer that it makes the part harder to read and puts the instrument under strain. Recordings exist both ways, and the difference in balance is clearly audible.","Listen for the dialogue and try to hear which instrument has the lead at any moment — the pleasure is in how often it changes hands. The passage where they move in parallel over a slow bass is the heart of it. Compare a recording using the original scordatura tuning with a standard one; the viola's presence changes markedly."],"mozart-cosi-fan-tutte-soave-sia-il":["Three minutes for two sopranos and a bass, near the start of Act One. Two sisters are seeing their fiancés off to war — a deception staged by the cynic Don Alfonso, who joins them in wishing the ship a gentle wind. Muted strings ripple continuously underneath, the three voices weave in close harmony instead of taking turns, and the music never rises above a murmur. A woodwind halo hangs above the strings throughout.","Così fan tutte opened at the Burgtheater in Vienna on 26 January 1790, the third and last collaboration with Lorenzo Da Ponte. Its premise — that two men can win each other's fiancées in disguise within a single day — was calculated to amuse a court audience that liked its cynicism elegant. Joseph II died a month later, the theatres closed for mourning, and the run was cut short.","The music is completely sincere and the situation is a lie: two of the three singers know the departure is staged, and Alfonso is enjoying himself. Mozart writes nothing to signal the deceit, which leaves the audience holding both truths at once. Listen to the strings, which do not stop moving for the whole number — an image of water that also keeps the voices from settling into a cadence until the very end.","The nineteenth century found the opera immoral and the libretto beneath Mozart's music; Beethoven and Wagner both disapproved, and it was performed in rewritten versions or not at all. Rehabilitation came in the twentieth century, and this trio was often the exhibit for the defence — proof that the composer took the feelings seriously even when the plot did not. Modern productions argue about whether the ending can be played straight.","Listen with the situation in mind: beauty deployed over a con trick. Follow the middle voice, which is where the harmony's ache lives. Then compare an opera-house recording with a concert one — staged versions tend to be quicker and more anxious, because the singers know what is coming. Any performance that lingers too lovingly misses the joke."],"cherubini-requiem-c-dies-irae":["The Dies irae of Cherubini's C minor Requiem is scored for mixed chorus without any vocal soloists, a choice that keeps the terror collective rather than personal. It erupts with a single stroke on a tam-tam, a gong borrowed from the theatre, before the choir hurls out the Day of Wrath in blunt unison. Brass and low woodwind grind beneath; the music swings between shouted panic and hushed pleading for roughly nine minutes without offering a moment of consolation.","Cherubini wrote the Requiem in 1816 for the restored Bourbon monarchy, to mark the anniversary of the execution of Louis XVI, and it was first heard at the royal basilica of Saint-Denis the following January. He was in his mid-fifties, a Florentine long settled in Paris and soon to run the Conservatoire. France was raw from twenty-five years of revolution, terror and Napoleonic war, and this was an act of state mourning as much as a church service.","Listen for the tam-tam. That single unpitched crash was close to unprecedented in sacred music, and Berlioz, who thought this Requiem surpassed Mozart's, complained that conductors let it ring on too long. Notice too that no soprano or tenor ever steps forward: every voice stays inside the choir. And the movement returns to the same hushed choral murmur it interrupted, so the gong sounds like a door slamming inside a service already underway.","Beethoven, who called Cherubini the greatest of his living contemporaries, is said to have preferred this setting to every other, and it was sung at his own memorial service in Vienna in 1827. Church authorities later objected to women singing in the liturgy, which pushed Cherubini into writing a second, all-male Requiem in D minor. The C minor work then sat on the shelf for most of the twentieth century, revived by conductors arguing it links Mozart to Verdi.","Come to it straight after the Mozart Requiem and hear how much starker it is: no operatic soloists, no comfort. Start at the gong stroke and follow the chorus rather than the orchestra, since the words are delivered almost as speech. Then try Verdi's Dies irae to see where this theatrical violence ends up. Recordings that place the chorus well forward suit it best."],"cherubini-medee-overture":["A concert overture in all but name, and the gateway to Cherubini's tragedy about the sorceress who murders her own children. It begins with hard, stabbing chords for the full orchestra, then settles into a driving minor-key allegro whose main idea keeps trying to climb and being knocked back. Clarinets and bassoons carry much of the melody while the strings supply nervous repeated figures underneath. It runs about eight minutes and never relaxes into a comfortable tune.","Médée opened at the Théâtre Feydeau in Paris in March 1797, three years after the Terror, in an opéra-comique format with spoken dialogue between the numbers. Cherubini was a Florentine who had made Paris his home and had absorbed Gluck's principle that opera should be drama before it is song. Audiences of the Directory wanted rescue plots and last-minute reprieves; he gave them infanticide. The work did modest business in France and made its real career on German stages.","Listen closer: the overture withholds a proper second theme. Where a Mozart overture offers contrast, Cherubini keeps recycling the same restless material, so the ear is never let off the hook, exactly the technique Beethoven would use in his Coriolan and Egmont overtures a decade later. Notice also how often the music simply stops, leaving a bare chord hanging in the air, as the opera's heroine stops mid-thought.","The opera was long known through a nineteenth-century Italian translation with sung recitatives added by Franz Lachner, the version Maria Callas made famous from 1953 onwards; her performances sent the overture back into concert programmes. Scholars now favour the original French with spoken dialogue, which changes the pacing completely. Brahms kept a score of Médée on his shelves, and Beethoven ranked its composer above every living rival.","Play it next to Beethoven's Egmont Overture and the family resemblance is obvious: the same clipped, unsentimental fury. Follow the woodwind rather than the violins, because the oboe and clarinet lines carry the character. Then hear the opera's final act, where related music returns with the voices on top. A lean period-instrument band brings out the bite better than a plush modern orchestra."],"cherubini-anacreon-overture":["The curtain-raiser to a two-act opera-ballet about the ageing Greek poet Anacreon and his loves. A slow, ceremonious introduction gives way to a bounding allegro in which the strings chase a single skipping figure while horns and trumpets punch out fanfares. Roughly nine minutes long, it is built like a compact symphony first movement, with a real development where the themes are pulled apart, and it ends by piling on speed and volume in a way Rossini would soon patent.","Anacréon, ou L'amour fugitif reached the Paris Opéra in October 1803 and failed quickly: the libretto was thin and Napoleonic Paris had little appetite for mythological ballet. Cherubini was in his early forties and out of favour with the First Consul, who preferred Italian tunefulness and said so to his face. The composer salvaged the overture, and within a few years German orchestras had adopted it as a concert piece, long after the opera itself had vanished.","Weber and Berlioz both admired this overture, and the English composer William Shield singled out its long build as the most effective orchestral crescendo he had ever heard. That is the thing to listen for: Cherubini freezes the harmony and simply adds instruments and repetitions, the engine Rossini would run on for the rest of his career. The fanfares are written for horns that could not yet play every note, which is why they circle the same few pitches.","For a century the Anacréon overture was standard fare, recorded by Toscanini and programmed as a curtain-raiser across Europe. It then slipped away with almost all of Cherubini's stage music, kept alive by conductors making the case for him as the missing figure between Haydn and Beethoven. The opera closed after seven performances in 1803 and is still almost never staged, so the overture stands in for a piece nobody hears.","Time the last three minutes: the music accelerates in steps rather than smoothly, and it is fun to catch each new gear. Try it beside Cherubini's own Médée overture from six years earlier, which is far darker, and then beside Beethoven's Egmont. Any brisk mid-sized orchestra suits it; older recordings tend to be grander and slower than the music actually wants."],"beethoven-symphony-no-5":["Four movements, roughly thirty-three minutes, built almost entirely from four notes: three short, one long. The opening hammers that cell out in bare unison and then never lets it go. It drives the C minor first movement, hides inside the flowing second-movement variations, and returns as a groping figure for the horns in the scherzo. That scherzo dissolves into a long held whisper on strings and timpani, out of which the music breaks into a blazing C major finale with piccolo, contrabassoon and three trombones added.","Beethoven worked at the symphony on and off from 1804 to 1808, alongside the Pastoral. Both were unveiled at a four-hour benefit concert at the Theater an der Wien on 22 December 1808, in an unheated hall, with an under-rehearsed orchestra that broke down and had to restart part of the programme. He was in his late thirties, his deafness advanced and irreversible, and was negotiating for a salary that would keep him in Vienna while Napoleon's armies moved across Europe.","Listen closer: the trombones that make the finale so bright had almost never been used in a symphony before. They came from opera and church, so their arrival is a change of institution as much as of colour. Halfway through that finale the ghostly scherzo returns, quiet and in the wrong place, as if the victory had to be won twice. The line about fate knocking at the door comes from Beethoven's unreliable secretary Schindler, not from Beethoven.","E. T. A. Hoffmann's review of 1810, which described the symphony as opening onto the immense and the immeasurable, effectively invented Romantic music criticism. The premiere itself was a shambles and the work made its name slowly, then completely. Modern arguments concentrate on Beethoven's fast metronome marks, on whether the scherzo repeat should be observed, and on how much weight to give the opening pause, which can sound like a threat or like a stumble.","Count the opening figure, short-short-short-long, then hunt for it everywhere: it is in the cellos under the slow movement and in the horn call of the scherzo. The best way in is the transition into the finale, the long dark hush and the crescendo out of it. Compare a period band, quick and rough-edged, with a big modern orchestra; Carlos Kleiber's recording splits the difference."],"beethoven-symphony-no-9-ode-joy-finale":["The last of four movements and nearly half the symphony: about twenty-four minutes for orchestra, four solo singers and chorus. It opens with a shriek from the winds, after which cellos and basses argue in wordless recitative, rejecting fragments of the three earlier movements one by one until they find a plain, stepwise tune. That melody is repeated and reharmonised until a baritone stands up and sings the first words. A Turkish march with bass drum and cymbals, a double fugue and a headlong coda follow.","Beethoven finished the Ninth early in 1824 and it was first heard at the Kärntnertortheater in Vienna on 7 May, with the composer on stage beating time beside the conductor although he could hear nothing; a singer turned him round to see the applause he had missed. He had wanted to set Schiller's An die Freude since the 1790s, when its language of universal brotherhood carried a political charge that Metternich's police state had since made dangerous to voice.","Listen closer: the joy tune spans barely more than an octave and moves almost entirely by step. It is written to be singable by anyone, which is the whole point. Beethoven had already used a close relative of it in the Choral Fantasy of 1808. And the Turkish percussion is a joke with teeth, since the march sets words about a hero running his course to the sound of a Janissary band, the music of the empire that had twice besieged Vienna.","For a century critics treated the choral finale as an embarrassment: Verdi admired the first three movements and thought the last badly written, and Debussy mocked it. Wagner made it his founding text instead. It has since served the Third Reich, the fall of the Berlin Wall and the European Union, which is part of the argument, because the tune is open enough for anyone to claim. Scholars still debate the exposed, near-unsingable choral writing.","Start where the low strings first sing the tune alone, unaccompanied and almost shy, and follow how each repetition adds instruments. Then jump to the double fugue and hear the joy theme and the hymn of embrace stacked on top of one another. A large chorus recorded with clear diction helps; a period-instrument version is worth hearing too, since the voices are less swamped by the orchestra."],"beethoven-moonlight-sonata":["Three movements, about fifteen minutes, in a deliberately upside-down order: the slow music comes first. The opening Adagio sostenuto is a single unbroken stream of quiet triplets, with a bare melody of repeated notes above and a slow bass beneath, a texture that hardly changes for six minutes. A short, courtly Allegretto follows. Then the Presto agitato erupts, using the same rising broken chords as the opening but hurled upwards at speed, with hammered accents and two crashing cadenzas.","Beethoven published the sonata in 1802 as one of a pair described as quasi una fantasia, sonatas that behave like improvisations. He was thirty-one, at the height of his fame as a Viennese pianist, and had just written the Heiligenstadt Testament admitting that his hearing was going. The dedicatee was his teenaged pupil Countess Giulietta Guicciardi. Pianos of the time had a lighter, faster-fading sound, and the instrument itself was being redesigned around music like this.","Beethoven's instruction over the first movement is to play it without dampers throughout. On his piano the raised pedal blurred the harmonies into a haze rather than the mud a modern grand produces, which is why many pianists half-pedal here. The nickname arrived only in 1832, from the poet Rellstab, five years after the composer's death. Notice too that the finale is the first movement's accompaniment figure, identical in shape and violent in speed.","Its popularity has always embarrassed serious opinion, and Beethoven is reported to have grumbled that everyone talked about the C sharp minor sonata when he had written better things. Nineteenth-century listeners heard moonlight on a lake; twentieth-century writers heard a funeral procession. Performance argument now centres on tempo, since the first movement is marked in a broad two rather than a slow four, and on how much pedal blur a modern instrument can take.","Try the finale first. If you only know the famous opening, the fury of the last movement rearranges the whole piece, and the slow movement then sounds less like moonlight and more like something being held down. Listen for the moment several minutes in when the melody finally moves. A recording on a period fortepiano shows exactly what that pedal marking was for."],"beethoven-symphony-no-3-eroica-first-movement":["Two blunt chords, and then a cello tune that immediately goes wrong, sliding onto a note outside the key. What follows lasts about sixteen minutes, longer than many complete symphonies of the previous generation, and its centre is a huge development in which the themes are broken up, stacked against one another and driven into grinding dissonance before an entirely new melody appears in a remote minor key. The recapitulation then arrives early, a horn creeping in before the orchestra has resolved.","Beethoven composed the Eroica in 1803 and 1804 and had written Bonaparte on the title page. When news came that Napoleon had declared himself Emperor he scratched the name out so hard, according to his pupil Ries, that he tore the paper; the published score commemorates the memory of a great man instead. He was thirty-three, had accepted that his deafness was permanent, and had told a friend he was taking a new path. Private performances at Prince Lobkowitz's palace came first.","That early horn entry is the most argued-over bar in the symphony. The horn plays the theme while the strings still hold the previous harmony, and the clash sounds like a mistake; Ries said so out loud at rehearsal and got his ears boxed. Listen too for the way the rhythm lurches into groups of two against the three-beat bar for long stretches, so the music seems to pull against its own metre.","Early listeners found it exhausting. One Viennese review suggested it would gain by being cut, and a voice from the gallery at an early performance offered a coin for it to stop. Within a generation it was the model for what a symphony could be. Modern debate turns on tempo, since Beethoven's metronome mark is brisk, and on whether to take the exposition repeat, which adds several minutes but restores the movement's proportions.","Listen for the moment the music seems to break down completely, roughly two thirds of the way through, and then for the quiet new tune that emerges from the wreckage. Compare a period performance, where the natural horns bray and the timpani are hard-sticked, with a large modern orchestra. Then play the opening of the Fifth and hear the same composer solving a different problem."],"beethoven-string-quartet-op-131":["Seven movements played without a break, roughly forty minutes, for two violins, viola and cello. It opens with a slow fugue of extraordinary quiet, each voice entering with the same rising phrase. Then comes a sudden, sunlit dance; a short recitative-like passage; a long set of variations at the centre; a scherzo that keeps stopping dead and starting again; a brief lament; and a hard-driven finale that turns the opening fugue subject into a march.","Written in 1826, the last full year of Beethoven's life, in the sequence of quartets begun for the Russian prince Nikolai Galitzin. He was completely deaf, in poor health, and dealing with the collapse of his relationship with his nephew Karl, who attempted suicide that summer. There was no commission for this one and no obvious market: quartets were domestic music for amateurs, and this asks for players able to hold forty minutes of unbroken concentration.","Each movement begins in a key a third away from where the last one ended, so the whole work drifts through unrelated harmonic country and still lands home. The finale's opening is the first movement's fugue subject hammered flat and turned aggressive, the same handful of notes at the start and the end of the piece. Scholars have long noticed that a related four-note cell recurs across all the late quartets, disguised differently each time.","Schubert asked to hear it a few days before his death in 1828 and by one account fell into a fever of excitement. Nineteenth-century audiences found the late quartets incomprehensible and publishers thought them unsaleable; Wagner's essay of 1870 helped turn them into scripture. Beethoven is said to have named this his own favourite. Modern quartets argue over how seamless the seven movements can really be, since the players cannot retune along the way.","Give it the whole forty minutes in one sitting, because the point is that nothing stops. If that is too much at first, start with the variations at the centre, where the theme is passed around and reinvented as a march, a dance and finally something almost weightless. Then compare the opening fugue with a Bach fugue and hear how much more slowly Beethoven's counterpoint breathes."],"beethoven-fur-elise":["A three-minute bagatelle for solo piano. The famous opening alternates two notes a semitone apart in the right hand before falling into a curling little tune over a rocking left hand. That refrain returns twice, framing two contrasting episodes: a warm, songlike passage in the major with a repeated-note bass, and a darker one that builds over a rumbling low trill before a chromatic scale tumbles the music back to the beginning.","Beethoven wrote it in 1810 and never published it. He was forty, deaf, and in the middle of an unsuccessful search for a wife. The piece surfaced in 1867, when the scholar Ludwig Nohl printed it from a manuscript he said was inscribed for Elise as a memento, a manuscript that has since disappeared. Small character pieces like this were social currency in Vienna, written into friends' albums rather than sold to publishers.","Nobody knows who Elise was. The leading candidates are Therese Malfatti, whom Beethoven proposed to that year and among whose papers the manuscript was found, since Nohl may simply have misread the handwriting, and the singer Elisabeth Röckel. A different and more elaborate version of the piece survives in Beethoven's own later hand, so the tidy text everyone learns may not be his final thought at all.","It is probably the most played piece of classical music in the world and has been dismissed for exactly that reason, a trinket beside the late sonatas. Scholars point out that the composer kept returning to it and that its refrain-and-episode structure is handled with real care. Because the source manuscript is lost, even the title rests on one nineteenth-century editor's word, which is an uncomfortable footing for so famous a piece.","Skip past the opening you already know and wait for the second episode, where the left hand growls low and the music turns genuinely stormy for twenty seconds. Compare a fast, light performance with a slow, dreamy one and notice how different a piece it becomes. On a period fortepiano that low rumble sounds far more sinister than on a modern grand."],"beethoven-pathetique-sonata-second-movement":["The slow movement of the C minor sonata, about five minutes long and in a warm A flat major. A single long melody sits in the middle of the piano's range, under the player's thumbs with harmony above and below it, and simply unfolds, calm and unhurried. It returns twice as a rondo refrain, separated by two episodes: one in a darker minor, the other with the accompaniment broken into triplets which then carry over and set the last return rippling.","Published in 1799 as the Grande Sonate pathétique, with the title on the first edition and almost certainly with the composer's blessing; the word then meant moving or full of feeling rather than pitiful. Beethoven was twenty-eight, newly famous in Vienna as a pianist, and dedicated the score to his patron Prince Lichnowsky. He had just begun to notice the ringing in his ears. Pianos were gaining weight and range every year, and he wrote for that new power.","The melody never travels far. It circles a few notes, which is why it sounds like breathing rather than singing. Listen for the moment the triplets arrive in the second episode and then refuse to leave: the tune comes back identical but the ground beneath it has started to move, a trick Chopin built a career on. The register Beethoven chooses is exactly where a cello would sit, and pianists usually voice it that way.","This was Beethoven's first popular hit and it has never been out of print. The nineteenth century read the sonata as autobiography, the defiant deaf young man, which modern scholars resist since the deafness was barely beginning. Billy Joel borrowed the tune wholesale in 1983. Performers argue mainly about the first movement's slow introduction; here the questions are simpler, mostly about how much freedom the long line can carry before it sags.","Follow the left hand rather than the tune. Its steady quiet pulse is what makes the melody float, and when it splits into triplets you will hear the whole texture change temperature. Play it next to the slow movement of a Mozart piano concerto to hear what Beethoven added: weight, and a much longer breath. Kempff and Brendel are good places to start."],"beethoven-symphony-no-6-pastoral-finale":["The fifth and last movement of the Pastoral Symphony, about ten minutes, headed Shepherd's Song: happy and thankful feelings after the storm. It grows straight out of the preceding thunder without a break. The rain thins, and a clarinet, then a horn, sounds a simple rising call over held strings. That call becomes a broad hymn passed around the orchestra and repeated with growing warmth, until the strings settle and a muted horn sings the melody once more as the music fades.","Premiered alongside the Fifth Symphony at the marathon Vienna concert of 22 December 1808. Beethoven, whose hearing was failing, walked daily in the countryside outside the city and filled sketchbooks there. He wrote at the head of the score that the work was more an expression of feeling than tone-painting, aware that programme symphonies were considered a cheap trick. The shepherd's call he uses is a ranz des vaches, the herding tune of Alpine cowherds.","Listen closer: the thanksgiving hymn is a folk-style tune that never quite finishes, since every phrase runs into the next, so the movement has no visible seams. The muted horn at the very end is a rare marking for Beethoven and puts the shepherd at a distance, as if the listener were walking away. This is also his only symphony in five movements, with the last three joined without a pause.","Critics have always treated the Pastoral as the easy sibling of the Fifth, and Beethoven's own caution about tone-painting has been used in its defence. Berlioz wrote an ecstatic analysis of it; twentieth-century modernists found it sentimental. Recent writing takes it seriously as an early piece of environmental imagination, a city composer's argument that nature is a place of feeling rather than scenery. Faster, lighter period performances have done it particular good.","Start the recording in the middle of the storm so that you hear the transition; the moment the rain lifts and the clarinet calls is the finest thing in the movement. Then listen for the horn at the very end, quiet and covered. Compare it with Vivaldi's Spring, which paints each bird by name, to hear what Beethoven meant by feeling rather than painting."],"beethoven-symphony-no-7-allegretto":["Not really a slow movement, since the marking is allegretto, a walking pace, and it lasts about nine minutes. A bare wind chord opens and closes it, hanging unresolved. Violas, cellos and basses then set out a rhythm of one long and two short notes and repeat it, almost without melody. Layer by layer the strings add a counter-tune above, the volume grows, and the whole orchestra is marching. Twice the music turns to the major and softens; a fugue-like passage near the end breaks it apart.","Composed in 1811 and 1812, partly while Beethoven was taking the waters at Teplitz, the summer he met Goethe and wrote the letter to the Immortal Beloved. The premiere came on 8 December 1813 at a charity concert in Vienna for soldiers wounded at the battle of Hanau, sharing a bill with his battle piece Wellington's Victory. The orchestra was packed with famous musicians, and Europe was in the last convulsions of the Napoleonic wars.","The audience demanded the Allegretto again on the spot, and it was encored at the second performance too. Publishers soon issued it separately, and for decades conductors inserted it into performances of the Second and Eighth Symphonies to give them a better slow movement. The chord that opens and closes it is the same unstable harmony, so the movement seems to begin and end in mid-air rather than ever settle.","Wagner called the whole symphony the apotheosis of the dance, and this movement has since carried more funerals and film scenes than any other Beethoven slow music. Critics who dislike it call it a one-idea piece, which is precisely its method. Arguments now concern speed: many conductors take it as a dirge, though the marking and the dance-like tread suggest something quicker and lighter, which period bands have restored.","Listen for the layers arriving. The rhythm never changes, so the interest lies entirely in what is added on top and how loud it becomes; try to catch the second tune entering above the first. Then find a period-instrument recording at the faster tempo and decide whether the movement still sounds like mourning to you, or like an implacable procession."],"beethoven-violin-concerto-first-movement":["Five quiet strokes on the timpani open the movement, and that gentle knocking is heard behind almost everything that follows. It runs roughly twenty-four minutes, longer than many complete Mozart symphonies. The soloist does not enter for several minutes, and when it does it arrives from above, in slow octaves, before rising into long garlands of decoration high on the top string. There is little display: mostly the violin sings and comments while the orchestra argues beneath it.","Written at speed in 1806 for Franz Clement, leader of the orchestra at the Theater an der Wien, and first played there on 23 December that year. Clement was working from a barely finished score and, by contemporary report, entertained the audience between movements with a party trick played on one string with the violin held upside down. Beethoven was in the most productive stretch of his life, having just completed the Fourth Symphony and the Razumovsky quartets.","Those opening drum beats are the movement's genetic material: the same four repeated notes turn up in the violins, in the woodwind, and at one point as a bare, ominous drumbeat interrupting the flow entirely. Beethoven wrote no cadenza for the violin, but he later arranged the work for piano and did write one there, including a part for the timpani, which some violinists now adapt back into the original.","The premiere passed with little impact and the concerto disappeared for decades, criticised as disjointed and lacking brilliance. Its rescue came in 1844, when the twelve-year-old Joseph Joachim played it in London with Mendelssohn conducting, and it has been the centre of the violin repertoire ever since. The main debates now are over which cadenza to use and how far the movement can be slowed before its walking pulse collapses.","Count the five drum taps at the start, then listen for them coming back; that is the thread to hold. Wait for the violin's first entry, one of the most patient in the repertoire. Compare Beethoven's own timpani-and-piano cadenza, used by Schneiderhan and Kremer, with the standard Joachim or Kreisler ones, then hear Paganini's First Concerto for the opposite idea of what a soloist is for."],"beethoven-piano-concerto-no-5-emperor-adagio":["The middle movement of the E flat concerto, about eight minutes long and in the remote key of B major. Muted strings play a slow hymn on their own; the piano then enters not with that tune but with something else entirely, a hesitant decorated line high above the orchestra, before finally taking the hymn for itself. Near the end the music sinks, a bassoon and horn drop a single semitone, and the piano murmurs a new idea under its breath: the finale's theme, arriving before this movement has stopped.","Composed in 1809, the year Napoleon's army bombarded Vienna; Beethoven spent part of it in his brother's cellar with cushions over his head to protect what hearing remained. The court, including his patron Archduke Rudolph, had fled the city. It is his last piano concerto, and by the premiere in Leipzig in 1811, followed by Vienna in 1812 with Carl Czerny, he was too deaf to play the solo part himself for the first time.","The join between slow movement and finale is the concerto's real invention. Instead of a break, the harmony slides down a semitone and the piano tries out the rondo tune quietly, as though thinking aloud, then bursts into it. Listen also for the three separate treatments of the hymn, strings alone, piano decorating, piano singing it plainly: a set of variations concealed inside what sounds like one continuous stretch of song.","The Emperor nickname is not Beethoven's and probably came from an English publisher, which has irritated scholars ever since, given that the work was written under French occupation and dedicated to an Austrian archduke. The Vienna premiere was coolly received. Performance argument now centres on the size of the piano, since a modern concert grand can swamp the muted string writing that fortepiano recordings restore to balance.","Listen for the moment the piano first takes over the hymn, several minutes in; until then it has only been circling around it. Then keep listening past the end of the movement, because the transition into the finale is the point of the whole thing and is ruined by a track break. Compare a fortepiano version with a big modern one to hear how much the balance changes."],"beethoven-appassionata-sonata":["Three movements, about twenty-three minutes. The first begins almost inaudibly, two hands two octaves apart creeping down and up, then twice detonates without warning: a movement of extremes with almost nothing in between. The slow movement is a set of variations on a chorale-like theme that grow steadily busier and higher until a single dissonant chord shatters the calm. The finale then runs without stopping, all whirling sixteenth notes, and ends faster still, in defeat rather than triumph.","Written between 1804 and 1806 and published in 1807 with a dedication to Count Franz von Brunsvik, brother of two women Beethoven was close to. He was in his mid-thirties, in the middle of the heroic decade that produced the Eroica and Fidelio, and increasingly isolated by deafness. His pupil Ries described a walk near Vienna in a downpour during which the composer hummed and roared to himself, then went home and played the finale for an hour.","The finale breaks the rules of its own form: instead of repeating the exposition, as convention demands, Beethoven marks the development and recapitulation to be played twice, so the drama rather than the material comes round again. Listen too for the four-note rhythm in the first movement, short-short-short-long, the same cell that would run the Fifth Symphony he was drafting at the time. The nickname came from a publisher three decades later.","Nineteenth-century pianists made it the ultimate test piece, and Lenin told Gorky it was astonishing, superhuman music that made him want to stroke people's heads. Early listeners were unnerved by an ending that offers no victory at all. Today's debates concern the finale's tempo, often taken faster than the pianos of 1806 could articulate, and whether to observe that unusual second-half repeat, which most recordings quietly drop.","Begin with the slow movement and let the variations climb; the shock is the chord that ends them and launches the finale without a pause. Then go back to the opening and listen for how quietly it starts, since most of this piece is soft, which is what makes the loud parts frightening. A fortepiano recording makes the violence sound less comfortable and more dangerous."],"beethoven-waldstein-sonata":["About twenty-five minutes in three linked spans. The first movement opens with pulsing repeated chords, low and quiet, behaving more like an engine than a tune, and its second theme, a chorale, arrives in an unexpectedly bright key. In place of a full slow movement there is a short, dark introduction. Then comes the finale: a long rondo whose theme floats over held pedal harmonies, gathering octave runs and trills before a famous passage of glissando octaves and a headlong prestissimo.","Composed in 1803 and 1804 and dedicated to Count Ferdinand von Waldstein, who a decade earlier had helped send the young Beethoven from Bonn to Vienna with a note hoping he would receive Mozart's spirit from Haydn's hands. The immediate spur was an instrument: the Paris firm of Érard had sent him a piano with a wider range and a different action, and the sonata explores the extra notes and the sustaining pedal like a first tour of a new house.","There was originally a long, songful slow movement, which Beethoven cut on a friend's advice because it unbalanced the sonata; he published it separately as the Andante favori, and it is still played on its own. The glissando octaves in the finale are far easier on a light-actioned 1803 piano than on a modern grand, where many pianists quietly redistribute them between the hands.","Pianists have treated the Waldstein as the moment the modern instrument's full range arrives, and it marks the start of Beethoven's middle period as clearly as the Eroica does in the orchestra. Critics of the time found the finale's pedal effects blurry and strange. Recordings now split between those who take the pedal markings literally, producing a deliberate haze, and those who clean everything up for modern ears.","Listen to the very opening at low volume: the repeated chords should feel like a motor turning over, not a melody. Then hold on for the finale's last two minutes, where the tune returns at double speed. Hearing it on a period instrument first is worth doing, since the pedalled blur that sounds like a mistake on a Steinway is exactly the effect Beethoven asked for."],"beethoven-egmont-overture":["About nine minutes for orchestra, opening with a heavy, dragging chord and a slow tread in the strings answered by soft woodwind: a courtly dance rhythm turned into a threat. The main allegro is terse and driven, more argument than melody, and it does not resolve. Instead the music is cut off dead, and a fanfare topped by piccolo launches a blazing victory march in the major that has nothing to do with anything heard before it.","Written in 1809 and 1810 as the first number of incidental music for a Vienna revival of Goethe's Egmont, staged at the Burgtheater that June. Goethe's play concerns a Flemish count executed in 1568 for resisting Spanish rule of the Netherlands. Beethoven, who revered Goethe and met him two years later, took no fee. Vienna had just endured its second French occupation, so a drama about a nation under foreign soldiers had an obvious local charge.","The victory music at the end is not a conclusion but a quotation from the future: the same Siegessymphonie returns at the very end of the play, after Egmont has been beheaded, so the triumph is posthumous. The overture's opening rhythm is a sarabande, a stately Spanish court dance, which means the oppressor's own music is used to open the piece.","Egmont has been the least contested of Beethoven's overtures, adopted as a concert opener across the political spectrum and played at moments of national crisis in a dozen countries. The rest of the incidental music, including two songs for Clärchen and a spoken melodrama, is rarely heard, which strands the ending: without the play, the victory march can sound like a cheap conclusion rather than an act of defiance.","Listen for the silence just before the end, where the music stops completely and what follows breaks in like a different piece. Then find a recording of the complete incidental music to hear that same march close the drama over Egmont's corpse. Compare it with Cherubini's Médée overture from thirteen years earlier, whose tight, unsentimental fury Beethoven clearly admired."],"beethoven-kreutzer-sonata-first-movement":["Beethoven marked the sonata as written in a very concert-like style, almost as a concerto, and the first movement makes good on it: about fourteen minutes of confrontation between violin and piano. It opens with the violin alone, unaccompanied, in a slow major-key chord, the only calm in the movement. The piano answers in the minor, and the presto that follows is all abrupt attacks, snatched silences and long stretches where the two instruments seem to interrupt rather than accompany one another.","Composed in a rush in 1803 for George Bridgetower, a violinist of African and Polish descent then touring Europe, who premiered it with Beethoven at an eight in the morning concert in Vienna's Augarten, reportedly sight-reading from the composer's manuscript. The two men then quarrelled over a remark Bridgetower made about a woman, and the printed dedication went instead to the Paris virtuoso Rodolphe Kreutzer, who never played the piece and called it outrageously unintelligible.","At the premiere Bridgetower improvised an imitation of one of the piano's flourishes, and Beethoven jumped up to embrace him in mid-performance; the story comes from Bridgetower himself, written into his copy years later. Listen for how rarely the two instruments actually agree. They trade the same material rather than share it, and the piano part is far harder than any accompaniment had a right to be in 1803.","Tolstoy's novella of 1889 took its title from this sonata and argued that music like it drives listeners to adultery and murder; the story was banned in Russia and made the piece notorious. Janáček then wrote a quartet on Tolstoy's story. Modern scholarship has restored Bridgetower to the picture, and performers still argue about whether a violinist should attempt the improvised flourishes he apparently added.","Start with the violin's unaccompanied opening bars and notice how quickly the mood curdles. Then follow the piano, because the point is that it is an equal combatant, not support. Compare a big-toned modern duo with a period version using gut strings and a fortepiano, where the balance tips and you can actually hear the violin fighting back."],"beethoven-missa-solemnis-kyrie":["The opening movement of a mass that runs some eighty minutes, this Kyrie alone lasts about ten. Three great chords, marked to be played with feeling, then the chorus enters on a single word, slow and enormous, with the orchestra thickening beneath. The middle Christe section drops to the four soloists, weaving over a quicker pulse in the minor, before the opening plea returns. The choral writing is punishingly high and long-breathed, sopranos held near the top of their range for bars at a time.","Begun in 1819 for the installation of Beethoven's pupil and patron Archduke Rudolph as Archbishop of Olmütz, and finished three years after the ceremony had passed. He was totally deaf, in poor health, and by the accounts of visitors worked himself into a frenzy over it; Schindler described finding him roaring and stamping at the Credo. He then hawked manuscript copies to European courts by subscription, treating a mass like a luxury commodity.","Beethoven wrote at the head of the score that it came from the heart and should go to the heart, an inscription about intention rather than doctrine, from a man whose relationship with the Church was unorthodox at best. Listen for how he sets the single word Kyrie: not as a phrase but as separate cries, each one a slab of sound, with the soloists emerging out of the choir rather than standing apart from it.","Only three movements were performed at the Vienna concert of May 1824 that also premiered the Ninth, billed as hymns to evade a ban on liturgical texts in the theatre; the complete premiere had taken place in St Petersburg a month earlier. The work has always been more admired than loved, too long and difficult for church use and too devotional for concerts. Choirs still complain, accurately, that the writing lies impossibly high.","Take the Kyrie on its own before attempting the whole mass. Listen for the shift when the soloists take over at the Christe and the music suddenly moves faster and more intimately. Then compare a large romantic-style chorus with a smaller period-instrument performance, where the words stay audible; Gardiner makes the case for lean forces, Klemperer for sheer weight."],"hummel-trumpet-concerto-e-finale":["The last of three movements, about four minutes, and a headlong rondo. The trumpet's tune keeps bouncing back between excursions, and around it Hummel gives the soloist rapid runs, wide leaps and chains of notes that a natural trumpet simply cannot produce, including full chromatic scales and melodies down in the middle of the instrument's range. The orchestral accompaniment stays light and Mozartian throughout, so nothing ever covers the soloist.","Written at the end of 1803 for Anton Weidinger, who had invented a keyed trumpet, an instrument with holes and covers like a woodwind, and who had already prompted Haydn's concerto seven years earlier. Hummel, a former pupil of Mozart, produced it as his calling card on taking up Haydn's old post with the Esterházy family, and it was first played on New Year's Day 1804. The keyed trumpet was a dead end: valves arrived within twenty years and swept it away.","The rondo breaks its own rules. Near the end the expected return of the main tune never comes; in its place Hummel quotes a soldiers' march from the second act of Cherubini's rescue opera Les deux journées, a Paris hit that Vienna had taken up two years earlier, so the borrowing would have been recognised in the room. The concerto was also written in E major, a bright key for the keyed instrument, though almost every modern trumpeter plays it a semitone lower in E flat.","The manuscript went unpublished until the 1950s, so for a century Haydn's concerto had the field to itself; since then Hummel's has become a standard audition and competition piece, arguably the harder of the two. Purists point out that on Weidinger's keyed trumpet the notes had markedly different colours from one another, while modern valves make everything smooth and even, losing the strangeness the music was written to show off.","Listen for the low, singing passages as much as the high fireworks, since those middle-register melodies were the real novelty. Play it directly after the finale of Haydn's concerto, written for the same player, and hear how much further Hummel pushes. A recording on a reconstructed keyed trumpet is a revelation, uneven and reedy in a way no modern instrument can be."],"hummel-piano-concerto-no-3-b":["Three movements, about thirty-five minutes, and the most turbulent of Hummel's concertos. The orchestra sets out a brooding minor-key theme before the piano enters with cascades of light, fast figuration, filigree in both hands, scales in thirds, leaps across the keyboard, which never quite settles into pure display because the minor key keeps pulling it back. A songful slow movement in the major follows, and the finale is a quick rondo with a swaggering tune and a bright ending.","Composed around 1819 and published soon after, when Hummel was among the most celebrated pianists alive: Mozart's former lodger and pupil, Haydn's successor at Esterházy, Beethoven's friend and rival, and shortly to be Kapellmeister at Weimar in Goethe's town. Concertos then were vehicles a virtuoso carried from city to city, printed only once they had stopped being secret weapons. Pianos were growing louder every year, and this writing assumes an instrument that can be heard over an orchestra.","The teenage Chopin played and taught Hummel's concertos, and the fingerprints are unmistakable: the way a melody dissolves into rippling decoration, the light left hand, the reluctance to let the orchestra do anything dramatic. Compare the opening of Chopin's E minor concerto with this one and the debt is obvious. Hummel's cadenza-like passages are also woven into the movement rather than parked at the end of it.","Hummel was ranked with Beethoven in his lifetime and forgotten within a generation of his death, a victim of the taste for symphonic weight over elegance. Revivals since the 1970s, led by pianists such as Stephen Hough and Howard Shelley, have made the case that his concertos are the missing link between Mozart and Chopin. The counter-argument is that the orchestral writing is thin, which is fair and largely beside the point.","Listen to the piano's first entry and hear how completely it ignores the orchestra's mood, brilliance laid over storm. Then play Chopin's First Concerto immediately afterwards; the family likeness settles the question of where his style came from. Hough's recording is the standard, quick and unsentimental, which is what this music needs if it is not to sound merely decorative."],"hummel-rondo-e-op-11":["About seven minutes for solo piano, a rondo whose refrain is a light, spinning tune that returns four times, always slightly redecorated. Between the returns come contrasting episodes, one songful, one busier and shadowed by the minor, with running passages that keep the hands moving in parallel. Nothing is dramatic. The pleasure is entirely in the surface: evenness, sparkle, and the way the tune reappears wearing new clothes each time.","Published early in the nineteenth century and known ever since as the Rondo favori, a piece Hummel played himself and one of those on which his reputation as the most elegant pianist in Europe rested. This was the music of the Viennese salon rather than the concert hall, printed for the growing market of amateur pianists in bourgeois drawing rooms, where a household with a piano was advertising its status as much as its taste.","The refrain is a study in one thing: the light, detached touch Hummel taught in his enormous piano method, where the fingers do all the work and the arm stays still. That technique died with the heavier pianos of the 1830s. Listen for how sparingly the sustaining pedal is needed, since the clarity is written into the notes; on a modern instrument too much pedal turns the whole piece to soup.","Pieces like this made Hummel's fortune and then sank his posthumous reputation, because nineteenth-century critics who wanted music to be profound heard only jewellery. Schumann, who admired him, still complained that he was too fond of pretty passagework. The revival of interest in early pianos has helped, since on a light Viennese instrument the writing sounds crisp and characterful rather than merely decorative.","Take it as a palate cleanser between heavier things. Listen for the differences between the four returns of the tune, because that is the whole game. Then hear Chopin's waltzes, which play the same trick with more harmony and more shadow. A recording on a period fortepiano is worth seeking out; on a modern grand the piece can sound thinner than it is."],"hummel-septet-d":["Roughly thirty-five minutes for a strange, top-and-bottom-heavy group: piano, flute, oboe, horn, viola, cello and double bass, with no violin at all. The result is a dark, woody sound with a virtuoso piano part threaded through it. There are four movements, a stormy first, a scherzo, a slow movement and a finale that recovers into brilliance. The piano dominates, but the wind instruments get real solos and the bass gives the texture an unusually deep floor.","Written around 1816, when Hummel was between posts and building a career as a touring virtuoso. Chamber music for piano and mixed winds was a Viennese speciality, Beethoven's Septet having been a runaway hit fifteen years earlier, and such pieces were played in private houses by mixed groups of professionals and amateurs. Leaving out the violin was practical as much as artistic: it puts the piano in charge and lets it be heard without effort.","This scoring had a direct consequence. In 1819 an amateur cellist in Steyr, Sylvester Paumgartner, commissioned a quintet from Schubert and asked for the same unusual line-up of piano with viola, cello and double bass, which is why the Trout Quintet has no second violin. Listen for the double bass here: it is not doubling the cello but holding the whole texture down an octave beneath it.","Contemporaries rated the Septet among Hummel's best works and Schumann praised it, after which it vanished with the rest of his output. Modern chamber groups programme it occasionally as a curiosity, and the usual complaint is that the piano part is so busy that the winds are reduced to decoration. Played with a fortepiano rather than a concert grand the balance is far more equal, and the argument mostly evaporates.","Listen for the horn, which gets some of the best lines and colours the whole ensemble. Start with the first movement for the storm, then go straight to Schubert's Trout Quintet to hear the same family of instruments used for sunlight instead. Recordings with period instruments are the ones to seek out, since modern pianos simply overwhelm the group."],"paganini-caprice-no-24":["Five minutes for unaccompanied violin: a bald little sixteen-bar theme, then eleven variations and a finale, each a different problem. One is all rapid string crossings; one is in octaves; one is in thirds; one has the left hand plucking the strings while the bow keeps playing; one throws the bow at the string to make it bounce. There is no accompaniment and no rest, and the theme itself is deliberately plain, a skeleton on which to hang techniques.","The twenty-four Caprices were published in 1820, dedicated to the artists rather than to a patron, though Paganini had been drafting them since about 1802. He rarely let anyone see his music, cultivating the story that his playing could not be written down at all. In the 1820s he finally toured beyond Italy and became the first international instrumental celebrity, playing to audiences who half believed the rumour that his skill came from a pact with the devil.","Almost every technique here can be traced to earlier violin treatises, in particular Locatelli's labyrinth caprices of 1733, which Paganini owned. His innovation was to combine them at speed and to build a whole piece from the accumulation. Listen for the variation in which the melody is plucked by the left hand while the bow keeps sounding: two players' worth of music out of one pair of hands.","For decades the Caprices were considered unplayable except by their author, and violinists published simplified versions while Schumann and Liszt added piano accompaniments or made transcriptions instead. This theme has since been varied by Liszt, Brahms, Rachmaninoff, Lutosławski, Blacher and Andrew Lloyd Webber, making it the most re-used tune in the repertoire. Critics who dismiss it as a stunt tend to concede that the stunts are organised with real formal sense.","Count the variations and notice that each takes one idea and exhausts it. Then hear Rachmaninoff's Rhapsody, which takes the same sixteen bars and turns them inside out, since its famous slow tune is this theme upside down. Compare a modern recording with a gut-string performance, where the sound is thinner, rougher and much closer to what audiences in 1820 actually heard."],"paganini-violin-concerto-no-2-campanella":["The finale of the B minor concerto, roughly ten minutes, and known as the rondo with the little bell. A handbell is placed in the percussion and struck on a single note; the soloist answers with the same pitch played as a harmonic, high and glassy, so that violin and bell chime together. Between returns of the tune the solo part runs through the whole catalogue, ricochet bowing, tenths, and passages played entirely in harmonics, over a light, almost operatic accompaniment.","Paganini wrote the concerto in the mid-1820s for his own tours and, as with all of them, kept the solo part out of print during his lifetime, sometimes handing out orchestral parts only on the day of the concert. He was by then the most talked-about performer in Europe, gaunt and dressed in black, the subject of pamphlets claiming he had murdered a mistress and strung her intestines on his violin. He carried a letter from his mother to prove he had a human birth.","The bell effect works because the violin's harmonic and the struck bell are the same note in different colours, so the ear hears a single sound splitting in two. Listen for how deliberately thin the orchestral writing is: Paganini scored to be heard rather than accompanied. In other concertos he tuned his violin up a semitone so his open strings would ring brighter than anything the orchestra could produce.","Liszt heard Paganini in Paris in 1832 and set out to do the same thing at the piano; his own Campanella, based on this rondo, is now more played than the original. Nineteenth-century critics dismissed the concertos as circus music with an orchestra attached, an accusation that has stuck to the accompaniments if not to the solo writing. Recent recordings using gut strings and period instruments make the whole thing sound less garish.","Listen for the bell first, because once you have heard it the violin's answering harmonics are unmistakable. Then play Liszt's piano Campanella and hear the same tune with the ringing built into the keyboard's top octave. Try an older recording by a violinist unafraid of sliding between notes: this is opera without words, and the portamento is part of the language."],"paganini-violin-concerto-no-1-first-movement":["About twenty minutes, opening with a long orchestral introduction full of drum rolls and cymbals, theatre-pit music, as though a curtain were about to rise. The violin then enters high above it and stays there, spinning long operatic melodies broken by display of every kind: double stops, leaping arpeggios, whole passages in harmonics that whistle above the orchestra, and rapid staccato runs thrown off in a single bow stroke. The mood alternates between swaggering march and aria.","Composed around 1817 in Italy, where Paganini had spent years as a court and theatre musician in Lucca before going independent. The manner comes straight from the opera house: Rossini was the reigning composer of the day and the solo violin behaves like a soprano, cadenzas included. Paganini toured the piece for a decade before Europe north of the Alps heard him at all, since he did not leave Italy until 1828.","The concerto was written in E flat major with the solo violin tuned up a semitone, so the player reads and fingers in D while sounding in E flat: the open strings ring brighter and cut through an orchestra playing in a flat key. Almost everyone now performs it in D with the orchestra transposed down, which loses the effect. Listen for the passages high in harmonics, which only sit comfortably under the original fingering.","Only two of Paganini's six concertos were published in his lifetime, and this one appeared after his death with the orchestration edited, so scholars still argue about what he actually played. Critics from Berlioz onwards split between admiration for the sheer sound and contempt for the material. Twentieth-century violinists often cut the movement heavily, and complete performances using the original tuning remain rare.","Wait for the first entry of the violin and notice that it does not play a theme so much as make an entrance. Follow the harmonics, the thin flute-like notes near the end, and remember that they are produced by barely touching the string. Then hear Beethoven's Violin Concerto from a decade earlier for the exact opposite idea of what a soloist is for."],"paganini-cantabile":["Four minutes, a single unbroken song for violin with guitar, though it is usually heard today with piano. There are no tricks. The violin plays one long melody, mostly on the top string, over a simple strummed accompaniment, and the only decoration is a light turn at the ends of phrases. The tune rises and falls like a bel canto aria, pausing where a singer would breathe, and it ends by fading rather than finishing.","Paganini played the guitar as well as the violin and wrote a great deal for it, well over a hundred pieces, most of them for domestic music-making rather than the stage. This belongs to that private side of him and was published after his death. In Italy in the 1820s the guitar was the household instrument of choice, cheap and portable, and violin-and-guitar duos were a normal evening's entertainment in a family drawing room.","Listen for what is missing: no double stops, no harmonics, no left-hand pizzicato. The composer who built his fame on impossibility wrote a piece with almost no difficulties in it, which is why teachers give it to students. Its one demand is a bow arm that can sustain a single line for four minutes without a bump, which is arguably harder than the fireworks.","The intimate Paganini has taken a long time to register. Nineteenth-century critics knew only the showman, and the guitar works stayed unpublished or unplayed until modern editions appeared. Performers now disagree about the accompaniment: piano gives a fuller sound, guitar the original balance, and with a guitar the violinist can play far more quietly than usual, which changes the character completely.","Find a version with guitar rather than piano and hear how much lighter and more Italian it becomes. Listen for the phrase endings, where the violinist should breathe like a singer. Play it alongside a Bellini aria, and then alongside the Caprice No. 24, to hear the two halves of the same musician in the space of ten minutes."],"paganini-moto-perpetuo":["Four minutes of unbroken sixteenth notes for solo violin and orchestra, something over two thousand of them, played without a single rest from the first bar to the last. The bow scrubs continuously while the left hand runs up and down the fingerboard; the tune, such as it is, hides inside the stream as the top note of each pattern. The orchestra merely marks time underneath, and the piece ends by arriving at the finish line rather than by concluding.","Published after Paganini's death and typical of the encore repertoire he built around himself: short, immediately impressive, and easy for an audience to describe to friends afterwards. He toured Europe from 1828, earned enormous sums and gave much of the money away, and needed pieces that could close a concert to an ovation. The nineteenth century loved a stunt with a number attached, and the count of notes was part of the sell.","The difficulty is not speed but stamina and bow distribution: the violinist has to plan four minutes so as not to run out of bow, and the passagework is designed to keep the left hand in shapes it can repeat. Listen for the melodic outline hidden in the top notes of the pattern, which is the only thing that stops the piece from being a pure exercise.","Trumpeters, flautists, xylophonists and brass bands have all stolen it, and recordings exist on almost every instrument capable of moving fast. Critics have used it as evidence for the prosecution against Paganini ever since, which is fair enough, since it is a stunt. It survives because it is a well-made one, and because audiences have never stopped wanting to watch somebody do something impossible.","Watch it rather than only hearing it; a video makes the point in a way that audio cannot. Then compare it with Rimsky-Korsakov's Flight of the Bumblebee, a similar trick with better tunes. For a corrective, follow it immediately with something that barely moves, such as Satie's first Gymnopédie, and notice how differently four minutes can pass."],"rossini-barber-seville-overture":["Seven minutes and a machine for raising the temperature. A slow, mock-serious introduction gives way to a scampering allegro with a chattering woodwind tune, and then Rossini's signature device takes over: a short figure repeated over and over, adding instruments and volume each time until the whole orchestra is roaring, followed by an abrupt drop back to a whisper so that it can all be done again. Nothing develops; everything accumulates, and it ends in a scramble.","Il barbiere di Siviglia was written in a few weeks and staged at the Teatro Argentina in Rome in February 1816, when Rossini was twenty-three. The first night was a catastrophe: a cat wandered on stage, a singer fell, and supporters of the older composer Paisiello, who had set the same play, heckled throughout. By the second performance the audience had changed its mind, and he had a hit that has never left the repertoire since.","The overture has nothing to do with the opera. Rossini had already used it for a tragedy about the Roman emperor Aurelian in 1813 and again for a drama about Elizabeth I of England in 1815, since recycling was standard practice for a composer writing several operas a year. Listen to how the tune is scored: the oboe and clarinet lines are written like vocal duets, which is what they had originally accompanied.","That the same music once served a Persian queen and an English one has embarrassed admirers ever since, and Rossini's reputation as a lazy magpie took two centuries to shake off. He is now credited with something more serious, an orchestral comic style built on rhythm and repetition rather than melody and development. Period-instrument performances have restored the bite of the wind writing and much faster tempos than the tradition allowed.","Count the crescendos: there are two big ones, and you can hear exactly where each begins because everything suddenly drops to a whisper first. Then find a recording of the opera's first act and hear the same trick used to fill a stage with confused characters. A lean orchestra with hard timpani sticks suits it far better than a lush one."],"rossini-william-tell-overture-finale":["Three minutes of pure adrenaline, the last of four linked sections in a twelve-minute overture. After a dawn scene for five solo cellos, a storm, and a shepherd's call on the cor anglais, a trumpet fanfare cuts in and the strings launch a galloping march. The tune is short and repeats endlessly, gathering brass and percussion, dropping back and building again. There is no development whatever; the music simply accelerates towards its own ending.","Guillaume Tell reached the Paris Opéra in August 1829, a four-hour grand opera about Swiss peasants throwing off Austrian rule, written for the largest stage in Europe. Rossini was thirty-seven, the most successful composer alive, and it was his last opera: he lived another thirty-nine years and wrote almost nothing more for the theatre. The revolution of July 1830 swept away the government that had contracted him, which cannot have helped.","The galloping rhythm is a cavalry charge, Swiss troops riding to the rescue in the opera's final act, and Rossini scores the horns and trumpets so that they seem to approach from a distance. Listen for the point where the theme, having built to full volume, suddenly drops to almost nothing so that the crescendo can begin again: the same engine as the Barber overture, running at twice the speed.","The overture became so famous that the four-hour opera behind it is rarely staged, and Rossini himself is said to have joked about whether anyone would really sit through the whole thing. The Lone Ranger radio and television series attached the finale permanently to horses and heroism in the English-speaking world, and Kubrick used it, sped up, for comedy. Purists insist the overture be heard whole.","Listen to all twelve minutes rather than the last three: the dawn music for five cellos alone is some of Rossini's finest writing, and the gallop means more after the storm. Then compare Verdi's Triumphal March from Aida for the same trick in grander clothes. Any orchestra that can play it fast and dry beats a heavy, ceremonial reading."],"rossini-largo-al-factotum":["Figaro's entrance aria in the first act of The Barber of Seville, about five minutes for baritone and orchestra. He announces himself as the town's factotum, barber, surgeon, matchmaker and fixer, over a bouncing accompaniment, and the music grows faster and more crowded as he lists his clients until he is shouting his own name over and over. The famous passage near the end fires off syllables at a rate that leaves the singer no room to breathe.","Written in 1816 for Luigi Zamboni, the first Figaro, in an opera drafted in roughly three weeks. Beaumarchais's play had been a pre-revolutionary scandal, its servant hero cleverer than his masters; by 1816 that edge had softened into comedy, but the aria still puts a working man centre stage boasting about how indispensable he is. Italian comic opera then depended on a buffo baritone who could deliver text at speed and stay funny doing it.","The patter is not merely fast, it is metrically tricky, because Rossini keeps shifting where the stresses fall, so a singer who simply goes quickly will trip. Listen for the Figaro qua, Figaro là passage, where the name is tossed between registers as though several people were calling for him at once: a one-man crowd scene. The aria also sits unusually low at the start and unusually high at the end.","It is the most famous baritone aria in existence, which has made it a cartoon staple and a burden: every Figaro is measured against a century of recordings, and the temptation to clown is enormous. Critics disagree about ornamentation, since Rossini expected singers to add their own but the aria is so familiar that additions sound like mistakes. Tempo is the other battleground, because faster is not automatically funnier.","Follow the words, or a translation, because the joke is in the list. Listen for the moment the orchestra drops away and the voice carries on alone. Then compare two or three recordings, an old broad Italian buffo against a modern fleet one, and decide whether you want the character or the athletics. Recordings taken from a staged performance carry the comedy best."],"rossini-gazza-ladra-overture":["Ten minutes, and it opens with something no overture had done before: two snare drums, alone, rolling and cracking like a firing party. A grand march follows, then the body of the piece, a bright skipping allegro with a wistful minor-key second tune, driven forward by two long crescendos of the kind Rossini built his career on. The drums return to punctuate the argument, and the ending is a stampede.","La gazza ladra opened at La Scala in Milan in May 1817 and tells a near-tragedy: a servant girl is condemned to death for a theft actually committed by a magpie and is saved at the last moment. Rossini was twenty-five and writing several operas a year under contract. The story came from a French melodrama based on a real judicial killing, and the drums in the overture are the execution the plot keeps approaching.","That is the point of the snare drums, and audiences in 1817 understood it at once, because military percussion meant execution. Listen for how Rossini keeps interrupting his own cheerfulness with them, so that the comedy is shadowed throughout. The wistful second theme belongs to the accused girl in the opera itself, which makes this overture a genuine summary rather than the recycled curtain-raiser he so often supplied.","Contemporary critics found the drums a vulgar stunt, and Rossini reportedly enjoyed the outrage. The overture long outlived the opera, which was rarely staged until the late twentieth-century Rossini revivals restored it. Kubrick's use of the music in A Clockwork Orange in 1971 attached it permanently to stylised violence, an association that troubles some listeners and would probably have amused the composer.","Listen to the first thirty seconds with the volume up: the drums should sound like a threat, not a fanfare. Then follow the minor-key tune, the only glimpse of the opera's darkness. Compare it with the Barber overture, which uses the same machinery for pure fun. A recording with genuinely military-sounding drums, well separated in the stereo picture, makes all the difference."],"rossini-una-voce-poco-fa":["Rosina's aria in the first act of The Barber of Seville, about six minutes. It begins slow and confiding, since she has heard a voice and her heart is set, with a melody that keeps curling around itself in decorated turns. Then the tempo doubles for the second half, in which she explains cheerfully that she is docile and obedient until crossed, at which point she becomes a viper. The vocal writing turns into runs, leaps and trills that grow more elaborate the more emphatic she gets.","Written in 1816 for a contralto, Geltrude Righetti-Giorgi, and sopranos took it over during the nineteenth century, transposing it up and adding higher ornaments. The two-part shape, a slow cavatina followed by a fast cabaletta, was the standard architecture of Italian aria for fifty years, and Rossini did as much as anyone to fix it. The character is a teenager kept under guard in her guardian's house, plotting her own escape.","The joke is in the music, not the words. Rosina claims to be sweet-natured while the vocal line becomes steadily less controllable, so the ornaments themselves give her away: the more docile she declares herself, the wilder she sings. Rossini also wrote out the decorations rather than leaving them to the singer, unusually for the period, because the comedy depends on exactly which notes come where.","Nineteenth-century sopranos treated the aria as a hook on which to hang their own fireworks, and by 1900 the interpolations were often longer than what Rossini wrote. The Rossini revival from the 1950s onwards, driven by Callas, Horne and Bartoli, pushed back towards the original range and the composer's own ornaments. Which version counts as authentic is still contested, since Rossini did expect embellishment, just not necessarily this much.","Listen for the change of gear halfway through, where confiding turns into warning. Then hear two singers, a light soprano in the higher key and a mezzo in the original, and notice how much darker and funnier the lower version is. Bartoli's recording makes the case for the mezzo; older soprano versions show what the tradition did with it."],"rossini-stabat-mater-cujus-animam":["The tenor aria from Rossini's setting of the medieval poem about the grieving mother at the cross, roughly five minutes. It is a bright, striding march with a jaunty accompaniment that would not be out of place in an opera, which is the very last thing the words suggest. The tenor's line climbs steadily through the verses, and the aria ends with a famously exposed leap to a note near the top of the voice, held while the orchestra hammers underneath.","Begun in 1831 for a Spanish state councillor who commissioned it while Rossini was travelling in Spain. Illness stopped him halfway, so he handed the remaining movements to a colleague, Giovanni Tadolini, and the hybrid was sung in Madrid in 1833. When a publisher tried to print that version years later, Rossini rewrote every borrowed number himself, and the complete work was heard at the Théâtre-Italien in Paris on 7 January 1842. He had given up opera in 1829 and was composing in retirement.","The text being set describes a soul pierced by a sword, and Rossini writes a march, which is either a failure of decorum or a deliberate reading of the words as a procession to Calvary. Listen for the accompaniment, which uses the same drumbeat rhythm found under his opera choruses. The famous high note is not written as an outburst of feeling but as the end point of a long line of ascent.","Heine mocked the piece, and generations of German critics attacked it as an opera in church clothes; Wagner wrote about it with a mixture of contempt and admiration. Others, including Donizetti, who conducted an early Bologna performance, thought it the finest thing Rossini had written since retiring. The question of whether sacred music may be theatrical has never closed, and this aria is its main exhibit.","Wait for the last thirty seconds and the leap upward, on which tenors have built careers. Then listen to Pergolesi's setting of the same poem, written a century earlier, and decide for yourself whether Rossini's cheerfulness is a scandal or simply a different kind of faith. A big-voiced Italian tenor of the old school gives it the swagger it wants."],"schubert-erlkonig":["Four minutes for one singer and piano, and the singer plays four parts: a narrator, a frightened child, a reassuring father, and the Erlking who wants the boy. Each has its own register and harmony, the father low and steady, the child high and rising, the spirit sweet and lilting in the major. Underneath, the pianist hammers repeated triplet octaves without pause, the horse galloping through the night, until the last line, when everything stops and the narrator says plainly that the child was dead.","Schubert set Goethe's ballad of 1782 in 1815, when he was eighteen and working as an assistant in his father's schoolroom. A friend described him reading the poem aloud, pacing, and writing the song at a single sitting. It circulated in manuscript for six years before a group of friends published it privately as his opus 1. A copy was sent to Goethe, who returned it without comment; the poet preferred simple strophic settings and never acknowledged him.","The boy cries out three times, and each cry is a step higher and more dissonant than the last, so the terror is built into the pitch rather than the words. Notice also that the Erlking is the only character who sings in the major key and the only one accompanied gently: seduction is scored as relief from the pounding. The triplets are punishing to play, and Schubert, no virtuoso, is said to have simplified them for himself.","It made his name and remains the most famous song in the German language, but the piano writing has always divided performers, some taking it fast enough to be genuinely frightening, others slow enough to be playable. Liszt's transcription turned it into a piano showpiece. Modern scholarship has revised the romantic story of instantaneous composition, since sketches survive showing revisions and four different versions of the song exist.","Follow a translation on first hearing, because the whole song is dialogue and the shocks are in the words. Then listen again for the piano alone and hear how it never stops until the final bar. Compare a dark bass-baritone with a lighter tenor, and try Fischer-Dieskau against a soprano; the four characters land very differently depending on the voice."],"schubert-unfinished-symphony":["Two movements, about twenty-five minutes, and it does not sound incomplete. The first opens with cellos and basses alone, low and quiet, muttering a theme that never returns in full; over shivering strings the oboe and clarinet sing together, and then the cellos give out the famous swinging second tune above a syncopated accompaniment. The second movement is calm, with a wandering clarinet solo over a plucked bass, and then it simply ends, because nothing more was ever orchestrated.","Schubert began the B minor symphony in 1822, the year he contracted the illness that would kill him six years later, and stopped after two movements and a sketched scherzo. He gave the manuscript to a friend for the music society in Graz and never asked for it back. It stayed in a drawer belonging to Anselm Hüttenbrenner for more than forty years, until the conductor Johann von Herbeck extracted it and premiered the work in Vienna in December 1865.","Nobody knows why he stopped, and the usual explanations, illness, distraction, dissatisfaction, are guesses. What survives of the third movement is a handful of orchestrated bars and a piano sketch, which is why completions exist and convince nobody. Listen for the trombones: Schubert uses them not for volume, as everyone else did, but softly, as a colour inside the harmony, decades ahead of general practice.","Its posthumous discovery made it a Romantic legend, the fragment as a finished artwork, and nineteenth-century critics heard the incompleteness as poetic rather than accidental. Scholars have since shown that the two movements are unusually alike in key scheme and metre, which may be exactly why he could not find a third. Completions using the sketches, sometimes with the Rosamunde entr'acte as a finale, surface periodically and are always contested.","Start at the very beginning with the volume low, because the opening theme is meant to be barely audible, a sound emerging from the dark. Then wait for the cello tune, which arrives about two minutes in and is one of the great melodies. Compare a period-instrument recording, lighter in the trombones and gut strings, with a broad romantic reading."],"schubert-string-quintet-c-adagio":["The slow movement of the C major quintet, about fifteen minutes, and the reason many musicians name this the greatest piece of chamber music written. Schubert adds a second cello rather than a second viola, so the sound has a dark floor. In the outer sections the three inner instruments hold a slow hymn almost without moving, the first violin decorates above and the second cello plucks below. Then the middle section breaks in, violent and in the minor, before the hymn returns transformed.","Written in September 1828, two months before Schubert died at thirty-one, in the same burst of work that produced the last three piano sonatas. He had lived with the effects of syphilis for five years and knew what was coming. He offered the quintet to a publisher, who was not interested; it was not performed until 1850 and not printed until 1853. Two cellos rather than two violas was Boccherini's scoring, not the Viennese norm.","Listen to what the second cello does in the outer sections: a quiet plucked note that is the only thing moving, so time is measured out in drops. In the middle section that same instrument switches to a furious bowed line. And when the hymn returns, the first violin's decoration has become an anxious triplet figure that will not settle, so the calm is audibly damaged by what came between.","Nobody heard it for twenty-two years, which shaped its reputation as a private message. Artur Rubinstein asked for the Adagio to be played at his funeral, and it has since become standard mourning music; some critics find that use sentimental and argue the movement is about stillness rather than grief. Performers disagree sharply about tempo, since too slow and the music stops breathing, too fast and it loses its strangeness.","Listen for the plucked notes in the lowest instrument and follow them rather than the tune, because they are what makes the movement feel suspended. When the middle section erupts, note how completely the texture changes. Then compare the calm of the return with the calm of the opening. Recordings with Pablo Casals as second cellist remain a benchmark."],"schubert-ave-maria":["Five minutes for voice and piano, built on the simplest possible plan: a rocking accompaniment of broken chords that never stops, and a melody that rises to the same yearning phrase in each of three verses. The vocal line stays in a narrow, comfortable range and the harmony hardly strays from home. Nearly everyone hears it in Latin, but the piano's steady figuration and the shape of the phrases were designed around German words.","Composed in 1825 during a walking summer in Upper Austria, as one of seven songs on Walter Scott's poem The Lady of the Lake in a German translation. This is the third song of the heroine Ellen, sung as she shelters in a cave, and Schubert was delighted that publishers might sell it in both German and English. He was twenty-eight with three years to live, and Scott was the most popular author in Europe.","It is not a setting of the Latin prayer. Ellen's German text merely begins with the words Ave Maria, and the familiar Latin was fitted to Schubert's melody by other hands after his death, which is why some phrases sit awkwardly on the Latin syllables. Listen for the piano's inner voices, which move by tiny steps beneath the rocking figure and carry almost all of the song's harmonic feeling.","Its ubiquity at weddings and funerals has cost it critical respect, and singers who deliver it with maximum vibrato do not help. Scholars point out that it belongs with the other six Scott settings and makes far more sense heard in that sequence. Performance practice is divided between the operatic tradition and a plainer approach with a light voice and a quick, quiet accompaniment, closer to what Schubert's own circle would have heard.","Find a recording of the German original and hear how differently the words sit on the tune. Then listen to the accompaniment on its own terms, since the rocking figure is the water and the cave of Scott's poem. Compare it with Gounod's Ave Maria, floated over a Bach prelude, for two completely different ideas of the same three words."],"schubert-symphony-no-9-great-first-movement":["About fifteen minutes, and it begins with two horns alone playing a slow, open call that could be a hunting signal or a hymn. The introduction gathers the whole orchestra before the main allegro arrives with a springing, repeated-note rhythm that runs almost without pause for the rest of the movement. Trombones, usually kept for weight, are given melodies. The scale is enormous, and the momentum comes from rhythm rather than from drama or contrast.","Written in 1825 and 1826, though for a century it was dated to Schubert's last months. The Vienna music society accepted the score, found it too long and difficult, and it was never played in his lifetime. In 1839 Schumann visited Schubert's brother Ferdinand in Vienna, found the manuscript in a pile of papers, and sent it to Mendelssohn, who conducted the premiere at the Leipzig Gewandhaus that March. Orchestras in London and Paris then defeated themselves on it for years.","The horn call at the opening is not an introduction that gets discarded. It returns at the climax of the movement, blazing out in the full orchestra, so the whole span turns out to be an expansion of the first eight bars. Listen too for the repeated triplet rhythm: once you notice it you cannot stop hearing it, and it is exactly how Schubert keeps a very long movement moving forward.","Nineteenth-century orchestras rebelled, and London players laughed at the repeated string figures and refused to continue. Schumann's phrase about heavenly length has been debated ever since, with conductors from Toscanini onward taking cuts and modern performers restoring every repeat. The redating to 1825 and 1826 changed how the work is heard, moving it from a last testament to a confident mid-career piece written in open competition with Beethoven.","Listen for the horn call at the start and then wait for it to return in full cry near the end; that arc is the movement. Do not fight the length, because the pleasure is in the momentum. Compare a fast, lean period-instrument performance with a broad traditional one and note how much difference the repeated-note rhythm makes when it is played crisply."],"schubert-death-maiden-quartet-second-movement":["About fourteen minutes: a theme and five variations for string quartet on music Schubert had written seven years earlier. The theme is not the song's melody but its piano accompaniment, a slow bare chordal tread, the sound of Death speaking calmly in the original. Each variation keeps that pattern and changes what happens above it: an anxious cello line, a violin racing overhead, a violent full-quartet outburst, a hushed turn to the major, and a coda that fades into the light.","Composed in 1824, the year after Schubert's syphilis diagnosis and the treatment that cost him his hair and his health. That March he wrote to a friend that he was the most unhappy and wretched creature in the world, whose health would never come right again, and in the same letter described working towards a grand symphony. The song he raided sets a poem in which a terrified girl begs Death to pass her by, and Death answers gently.","Death's music, not the girl's, is what the whole movement is built on: Schubert takes the consoling half of the dialogue and turns it into a structure nobody escapes. Listen for the fifth variation, where the key turns major and the music seems about to console, and then for the final bars, which do not resolve so much as evaporate. The quartet's other three movements share the same rhythmic tread.","The quartet was played privately in 1826 and published only after Schubert's death, and the nineteenth century found it too bleak. Mahler arranged it for string orchestra, a version still performed and still argued about, since the intimacy of four players is arguably the point. Modern debate concerns tempo and vibrato, with period-style groups using less of both and producing a harsher, more frightening sound.","Listen first to the song itself, which lasts barely two minutes, so that you know where the theme comes from. Then take the variations and count them, noticing that the underlying tread never changes. Compare a modern quartet with a gut-string group, since the older sound makes the theme less like a hymn and more like a warning."],"schubert-trout-quintet-fourth-movement":["Eight minutes of theme and variations on Schubert's own song about a trout, for the unusual line-up of piano, violin, viola, cello and double bass. The strings state the tune plainly; then the piano decorates it, the viola and bass take it over, it turns stormy in the minor, and it is passed round the group like a story being retold. Only in the last variation does the piano's rippling figure from the song, the fish darting in clear water, finally return.","Written in 1819, when Schubert was twenty-two and spending a happy summer in Steyr in Upper Austria with the singer Johann Michael Vogl. A local mine manager and amateur cellist, Sylvester Paumgartner, commissioned it, asked for variations on the Trout song, and specified the scoring of a popular arrangement of Hummel's septet, which is why there is a double bass and no second violin. The quintet was not published until after Schubert's death.","The rippling piano figure everyone associates with the song is deliberately withheld for most of the movement: Schubert varies the melody first and saves the accompaniment for last, so the water only appears at the end. Listen also to the double bass, which frees the cello to play tunes in its singing register, the reason the quintet sounds brighter and more open than a piano quartet does.","The Trout is the most popular chamber work Schubert wrote and has been criticised for exactly that, as too sunny and too easy, an entertainment. Defenders point to the harmonic turns in the variations, several of which are anything but carefree. Because Paumgartner was an amateur, the string parts are grateful and the piano part is hard, which still shapes how the piece is balanced in performance.","Listen to the two-minute song first, then the variations, and try to spot the moment the piano's water music returns. Notice how much the double bass changes the sound of everything above it. Then hear Hummel's Septet, the model for the line-up, for the darker version of the same idea. Recordings with a fortepiano bring the keyboard back into balance with the strings."],"schubert-impromptu-op-90-no-3":["Six minutes for solo piano, and one of the calmest pieces in the repertoire. A long melody sings in the middle of the texture while the right hand keeps a continuous rippling accompaniment around it and the left hand supplies a slow bass, three things at once from two hands. The tune spins out for pages without ever repeating itself exactly, and the only real event is a darkening turn towards the minor in the middle, after which the opening returns.","Written in 1827, the last full year of Schubert's life, as one of a set of four. The publisher took the first two pieces and shelved the rest; this one appeared thirty years later, and the first edition transposed it into G major and halved the note values, because six flats was thought too frightening for amateur pianists. Impromptus were a new commercial genre, short character pieces aimed squarely at the domestic market.","The key matters. In G flat the pianist's hands sit mostly on the black keys, which changes the touch and gives the piece its floating quality; the G major version of 1857 sounds subtly different and is now discredited. Listen for the bass line, which descends in slow steps beneath the singing, and for the moment in the middle when the ripple stops being decoration and turns anxious.","The impromptus were long treated as salon miniatures beside the sonatas, and only in the twentieth century were they taken as seriously as they deserve. This one is now standard repertoire and a standard trap, since the melody must sing over an accompaniment played by the same hand, and pianists disagree entirely about how audible the ripple should be. Schnabel, Lupu and Brendel offer three quite different answers.","Listen for the three layers and try to separate them: melody in the middle, ripple around it, bass beneath. Then find where the music turns dark, roughly halfway through, and notice how little Schubert does to achieve it. Compare a modern grand with a period instrument, where the middle register is clearer and the melody comes through without any effort."],"schubert-standchen-serenade":["Four minutes for voice and piano, and one of the most familiar tunes Schubert wrote. It is a serenade under a window: the piano imitates a plucked guitar with light detached chords, and the singer's melody rises in the minor and slips into the major at the end of each verse. The song's best idea is the echo, since the piano answers each vocal phrase with the same notes, quietly, as though the night itself were repeating the words back.","From Schwanengesang, the collection of fourteen songs Schubert wrote in 1828 and a publisher assembled after his death under a title he never chose. The poems for the first seven are by Ludwig Rellstab, who had sent them to Beethoven hoping for settings; after Beethoven's death in 1827 the manuscripts passed to Schubert. He was living his last year, ill, and producing music at an extraordinary rate right to the end.","The piano echoes are not accompaniment but a second character, and in Schubert's later version of the song for alto and women's chorus the echo is given to real voices, which makes the device explicit. Listen for the shift from minor to major at the end of each verse: the same words, the same tune, and only the light changes. Liszt's piano transcription is how many people first meet it.","Familiarity has done it damage, since it has been arranged for every instrument imaginable and turned into background music. Sung as written, quietly and at a flowing pace, it is a much sharper piece than the crooning tradition suggests. Scholars have questioned whether the Schwanengesang songs should be performed as a cycle at all, since the two poets and the shifting moods do not obviously belong together.","Listen for the echo in the piano and follow that rather than the singer. Then find Liszt's solo transcription and hear the same music turned into a piano piece. A light tenor at a flowing tempo makes it a serenade again, while a slow, heavy performance turns it into something else entirely. Try it beside Schubert's Ave Maria for two kinds of night music."],"schubert-gretchen-am-spinnrade":["Four minutes for soprano and piano. The right hand turns a spinning wheel in continuous sixteenth notes from the first bar while the left marks the treadle. Over it Gretchen, from Goethe's Faust, tries to describe the man who has ruined her, and the music climbs higher and tighter until, at the memory of his kiss, the wheel stops dead. There is a silence, then the wheel starts again, hesitantly and in fits, and the whole song rebuilds towards the same edge.","Schubert wrote it on 19 October 1814, aged seventeen, while working as an assistant teacher in his father's school. He had been reading Goethe's Faust, published complete two years earlier and the sensation of German literature. Gretchen sits at her spinning wheel after her seduction, alone with a task that will not stop. The song circulated among Schubert's friends and was published only in 1821, as his opus 2.","The stopped wheel is the moment the whole song exists for, and it is achieved with nothing but a rest: the accompaniment simply ceases in the middle of a bar. Listen for the restart, deliberately uneven, as though the hands were shaking. Notice too that the vocal line's highest note falls on a different word each time, so the obsession keeps finding a new object to fasten on.","Historians of the Lied routinely call this the beginning of the modern German art song, on the grounds that the piano stops accompanying and becomes a character. That is a tidy story about a seventeen-year-old's song, and some scholars point out that Zumsteeg and Reichardt had already gone part of the way. What is not disputed is that nothing before it makes a piano figure carry a psychological state so completely.","Listen once for the wheel alone: start it, then wait for the moment it stops, roughly two thirds of the way through. That silence is the point of the song. Then hear Erlkönig, written a year later, for the same trick at higher voltage. A soprano who can sing quietly at the top of her range serves it better than a big dramatic voice."],"schubert-piano-trio-no-2-andante":["Ten minutes for violin, cello and piano, the slow movement of a large four-movement trio. The piano begins alone with a trudging march figure, and the cello enters with a bleak, folk-like tune that is repeated rather than developed. The violin takes it up, the music briefly grows grand, and then it collapses back onto the same tread. That march is the movement's entire substance: it opens, returns three times, and is still walking when the movement closes.","Composed in November 1827, eight months after Beethoven's death, at whose funeral Schubert had been a torchbearer. He performed the trio at the first and only public concert devoted to his own music, in Vienna in March 1828, on the anniversary of that death, and he died eight months later. It was one of very few major works published in his lifetime, and he fussed over the details with his Leipzig publisher.","The cello's tune is not Schubert's. In 1827 he heard the Swedish tenor Isak Albert Berg sing a song about the sun going down, and the melody stayed with him; the resemblance was identified only in the twentieth century. Listen for the piano's opening march rhythm, which returns in the trio's finale hundreds of bars later, so the whole work turns back to look at this movement before it ends.","Schumann wrote that the trio passed across the musical world like an angry comet and that one glance at it made the miseries of human life disappear. It has had a second life on screen, since Kubrick used the Andante throughout Barry Lyndon in 1975, and its slow tread has scored a great deal of candlelight ever since. Performers argue about the large cuts Schubert sanctioned in the finale, which most modern groups reject.","Follow the cello on its first entry and then notice how little the tune ever changes, since the effect is cumulative rather than developmental. Listen for the piano's march to return in the last movement, at which point the whole trio suddenly makes sense as one piece. Try a period-instrument group, where the lighter piano lets the cello's low register speak without being covered."],"donizetti-una-furtiva-lagrima":["Near the end of L'elisir d'amore the village simpleton Nemorino notices that Adina has been crying, and decides she loves him. A solo bassoon spins out the tune first, over plucked strings and harp; the tenor then sings two matching verses in a slow rocking six-beat pulse. The minor key opens into the major on the words \"M'ama, sì, m'ama\", and the aria closes with a small unaccompanied flourish rather than a grand high note.","Donizetti wrote the whole opera at speed in the spring of 1832 for Milan's Teatro della Canobbiana, where it opened on 12 May. Felice Romani's libretto was adapted from a French text by Scribe, and the company was a scratch one filling a gap in the season. Italian audiences in 1832 expected comedies to end in noise and laughter; a comic opera's biggest number was not supposed to be a lament for solo tenor with a bassoon.","Romani reportedly argued against the aria, insisting a sad tune had no place in a comedy. Listen for the joke buried underneath: Nemorino has just drunk cheap Bordeaux believing it a love potion, so the most sincere music in the opera is sung by a man who is slightly drunk and completely wrong about why he is happy. The two verses are identical on paper, which leaves the singer to vary the second.","L'elisir became the most performed opera in Italy for the next decade, largely on the strength of this number. Caruso's early recordings fixed a performing tradition that many later tenors treat as scripture, including interpolated notes and a lingering final cadence Donizetti did not write. Critics who once dismissed bel canto as vocal wallpaper now read the aria as a small study in self-deception.","Follow the bassoon before the voice enters — the singer is really answering it. Then listen for the moment the harmony brightens and the tenor stops arguing with himself. Compare a light, conversational reading with a full-throated one; Caruso and Pavarotti sit at opposite ends of that argument, and the aria survives both."],"donizetti-lucia-di-lammermoor-mad-scene":["Lucia, forced into a marriage she did not want, comes down the stairs in a bloodied nightdress having killed her husband. The scene runs about a quarter of an hour in three stages: broken recitative, a slow aria (\"Ardon gl'incensi\") in which she hallucinates her wedding to the man she loves, and a fast closing cabaletta, \"Spargi d'amaro pianto\". A single high instrument shadows her the whole way, echoing her phrases like a second self.","Naples, Teatro San Carlo, 26 September 1835, with Fanny Tacchinardi-Persiani as Lucia. Salvadore Cammarano's libretto came from Walter Scott's The Bride of Lammermoor, part of a Europe-wide craze for Scott's misty, feuding Scotland. Donizetti was writing three or four operas a year under contract; Bellini, his one real rival, died in Paris within days of the premiere, leaving Donizetti alone at the top of Italian opera.","Donizetti first scored the obbligato for glass harmonica — the instrument survives in his autograph — and replaced it with flute, probably after trouble with the player. Some modern productions restore the glassy original. More telling still: at the height of her delusion Lucia sings back the melody of the love duet from Act I. The tune the audience heard when she was happy returns as evidence that her mind has stopped moving forward.","For decades sopranos treated the scene as a canary contest, and the famous flute cadenza is a later addition rather than Donizetti's text. Maria Callas in the 1950s played it as a woman destroyed rather than a display piece, and the reading stuck. Critical editions have since restored original keys, cut passages and the scene's place in the drama, and glass-harmonica performances have become a genuine alternative.","Do not listen for high notes; listen for the duet between voice and obbligato instrument, and ask which one is leading. Then find the earlier love duet and hear the same tune sung sanely. Callas, Joan Sutherland and Diana Damrau offer three quite different women; a period-instrument version with glass harmonica makes the scene stranger and colder."],"donizetti-fille-du-regiment-ah-mes-amis":["Tonio, a Tyrolean peasant, has just been allowed to enlist and so to marry Marie, and the regiment congratulates him. The number begins as a genial exchange with the men's chorus, then launches into the fast second half, \"Pour mon âme\", which climbs to a high C nine separate times in about ninety seconds. The orchestra keeps a bright march step underneath; the effect is athletic rather than romantic, joy expressed as sheer altitude.","Donizetti had moved to Paris in 1838 and was writing for French houses in French, with spoken dialogue in the Opéra-Comique tradition. The premiere, on 11 February 1840, came in a season when he had works running in several Paris theatres at once. Berlioz, struggling to get his own music performed in the same city, complained bitterly in print that Donizetti was colonising the Paris stages one theatre at a time.","The nine high Cs were less shocking in 1840 than now: the note could still be taken in a lighter, mixed voice rather than the full-chested trumpet blast that later became obligatory. What is easy to miss is how the tune shrugs — each C is thrown off inside a phrase that keeps moving, so the singer never gets to stop and pose. The regiment's marching tune returns later as \"Salut à la France\".","The premiere was received politely rather than rapturously, and Berlioz's review was openly hostile. The aria's modern reputation dates from twentieth-century revivals: Joan Sutherland and Luciano Pavarotti made it a headline event in the 1960s, and in 2007 Juan Diego Flórez broke a decades-old ban on encores at La Scala by repeating it. Purists still debate whether the Cs should be chest voice at all.","Count the Cs — everyone does, including the tenor. Then listen a second time for the chorus, which behaves like a crowd of friends slapping a back. Compare a big Italianate reading with a lighter French-style one; the aria is a comedy number, and a singer who sounds anxious has already lost."],"donizetti-l-elisir-d-amore-quanto-e":["The opera's first solo. Harvesters rest in the shade while Adina reads them the story of Tristan's love potion; Nemorino, watching from a distance, sings that she is beautiful, that he is a fool, and that he has no idea how to make her notice him. The tune is short-breathed and slightly halting, punctuated by the chorus carrying on with its own business behind him, so the aria plays as private muttering overheard.","Written for the Teatro della Canobbiana in Milan in spring 1832, in a genre — Italian comic opera — that had been Rossini's property for twenty years. Romani's libretto keeps the old stock types, the quack doctor and the swaggering sergeant, but gives the tenor an inner life. Village comedies were expected to open with a bustling chorus; giving the first real melody to the least important character on stage was a small structural risk.","The potion story Adina is reading aloud is Tristan and Isolde, seventy-three years before Wagner's opera reached the same legend by a different road. Nemorino's aria is also the seed of the whole plot: he hears about a magic drink and immediately believes in it. Notice how the vocal line keeps stopping mid-thought, as though he cannot finish a sentence about her.","For much of the nineteenth century the piece was treated as a warm-up for the famous Act II romanza and often cut or curtailed. Modern productions restore it, partly because the character now interests directors more than the plot machinery around him. Scholars point to it as evidence that Donizetti's comic writing carries genuine psychological detail, not just efficient craft.","Listen to what the chorus is doing while the tenor sings — the aria is designed to be slightly ignored on stage. Then hear the Act II romanza straight afterwards and notice how much steadier the same voice has become. A recording of the complete opera serves it better than an aria compilation, which strips away the crowd it needs."],"bellini-casta-diva":["The Druid priestess Norma, secretly in love with the Roman occupier, cuts the sacred mistletoe and asks the moon for peace. A flute traces the melody first; the voice then unwinds one enormously long line over rippling arpeggios, phrases stretching further than seems physically reasonable, with the chorus answering quietly. A faster, brighter cabaletta follows in which she privately begs her lover to return. Almost the whole aria sits at a hush.","La Scala, Milan, 26 December 1831, with Giuditta Pasta as Norma and a libretto by Felice Romani after a recent French play. Bellini was thirty, the most admired melodist in Italy, and working at a fraction of Donizetti's speed — he reportedly rewrote this melody eight times before it satisfied him. Pasta initially thought the aria unsuited to her and had to be persuaded to sing it.","The aria is not in the key Bellini first wrote: he moved it down from G to F to sit comfortably in Pasta's voice, and it has stayed there ever since, though a few sopranos restore the original. Listen to how the phrases grow rather than repeat, each one reaching a little higher before sinking. The prayer is also a lie — the woman asking the goddess for peace has broken her vows and had two children.","The premiere was a failure that Bellini himself described as a fiasco, and the opera recovered only over the following nights. Wagner later conducted Norma with enthusiasm and even wrote an extra aria for it. In the twentieth century the piece became inseparable from Maria Callas, and the bel canto revival of the 1950s was argued out largely over how much dramatic weight this one melody can carry.","Try to breathe with the singer and you will discover the point: the line is longer than a lung. Listen for the flute at the start, then for the moment the chorus enters underneath without disturbing her. Callas, Rosa Ponselle and Montserrat Caballé give three different answers about whether this is a prayer, a seduction or a confession."],"bellini-i-puritani-te-o-cara":["Arturo arrives for his wedding during the English Civil War and greets his bride in front of the assembled castle. The tune floats in above a soft accompaniment, unhurried, and the other soloists and chorus gradually join underneath so that the aria turns into an ensemble without ever losing the tenor's line on top. The tessitura is extraordinarily high and stays there, the voice hovering rather than climbing to isolated peaks.","Bellini's last opera, written in Paris for the Théâtre-Italien and premiered on 24 January 1835 with the so-called Puritani quartet: Giulia Grisi, Giovanni Battista Rubini, Antonio Tamburini and Luigi Lablache. Count Carlo Pepoli, a political exile rather than a professional librettist, supplied the text. Rossini, living in Paris and effectively retired, advised on the score. Bellini died eight months later at Puteaux, aged thirty-three.","The part was tailored to Rubini, a tenor who sang extremely high notes in a light head voice as a matter of course, and the aria touches F above high C. Nobody now sings it as written; most transpose down or redistribute the top. Bellini also prepared a second version of the opera for Naples with the role reworked for Maria Malibran, which went unperformed for a century and a half.","Paris was ecstatic — Bellini wrote home that the French had gone mad for it — and the opera confirmed that Italian melody could conquer the French capital without French forms. Later critics attacked Pepoli's incoherent plot, and for decades I puritani survived on its tunes alone. Recordings by Rubini's heirs, real and self-appointed, keep the argument alive about whether the original tessitura is singable at all.","Notice that the tenor never seems to push; the line should sound as if it costs nothing. Listen for the point where the other voices slip in below and the solo becomes a canopy over an ensemble. Compare a transposed modern reading with a light-voiced attempt at the original heights, and hear how differently the wedding feels."],"bellini-ah-non-credea-mirarti":["The last scene of La sonnambula. Amina, sleepwalking in front of the whole village, crosses a rotten mill bridge and mourns the flowers she is holding, which have faded as fast as her lover's faith. The melody descends in long sighing steps over a bare accompaniment, with a wind instrument doubling her at a distance. When she wakes to find herself forgiven, the mood breaks into the brilliant cabaletta \"Ah! non giunge\".","Teatro Carcano, Milan, 6 March 1831, with Giuditta Pasta and Rubini — the same pair Bellini would write for again. Romani's libretto came from a French ballet-pantomime about somnambulism, then a fashionable medical curiosity discussed in salons alongside mesmerism. The whole opera is set among Swiss villagers rather than gods or kings, and its crisis is a misunderstanding rather than a crime.","The first line of this aria is carved on Bellini's tomb in Catania cathedral. Musically, the trick is that the sleepwalking scene uses almost no harmonic drama at all: the pathos comes from the shape of the descending line and the silence around it. Verdi, no friend of vagueness, singled out these \"long, long, long melodies\" as something nobody had written before.","La sonnambula was an immediate success and travelled everywhere; Liszt made a virtuoso piano fantasy on it and Chopin absorbed its manner wholesale. Later opinion turned against the opera's harmlessness, and the sleepwalking device came to seem a contrivance. Recent staging has taken it more seriously, treating Amina's condition as real rather than picturesque, which restores the aria's chill.","Listen for how little is happening underneath — almost nothing but held chords, so the voice carries everything. Then hear the switch into the fast cabaletta and notice how the same singer must sound like a different person. Callas and Sutherland recorded it within a few years of each other with entirely opposed ideas of what sleepwalking sounds like."],"bellini-oh-quante-volte":["Giulietta, alone in her room in a wedding dress she does not want, asks how many times she has begged for the man she loves to appear. A harp sets the scene, a horn answers, and the voice enters with a slow decorated melody that keeps circling back to the same pleading turn of phrase. The aria is a cavatina in the old shape: a preparatory recitative, then a single long-breathed song with no fast conclusion to release the tension.","La Fenice, Venice, 11 March 1830. Bellini took the commission at short notice when another composer defaulted, and had roughly six weeks. Romani recycled his own earlier libretto, which draws on Italian versions of the Romeo story rather than Shakespeare; Romeo is sung by a mezzo-soprano in male dress, a convention already going out of fashion. Venice expected a new opera every season and did not much care where the plot came from.","The melody is not new either: Bellini lifted it from his opera Zaira, which had failed in Parma the previous year. Reusing a tune from a flop was ordinary practice, and this is the most famous survivor of the habit. Listen for the harp, which functions as a stage property — the sound of a girl alone in a room — rather than as part of the orchestral blend.","Berlioz saw the opera in Florence in 1831 and was appalled, not by the music but by the Italians' willingness to abandon Shakespeare's play for a tidier libretto. For most of the century Bellini's final scene was replaced in performance by one from Vaccai's rival setting, at singers' insistence. Modern revivals restore Bellini's ending, and the trouser-role Romeo is now valued rather than apologised for.","Start with the harp and horn before the voice arrives; they establish the loneliness the singer then fills. Listen for the repeated falling figure on \"quante volte\" and how its decoration changes each time. Then find the duets for the two women's voices later in the opera, which are the real reason the piece survives."],"berlioz-symphonie-fantastique-march-scaffold":["The fourth movement of a five-movement symphony with a printed plot: the artist dreams he has murdered his beloved and is marched to the guillotine. Two ideas alternate — a descending scale growling in cellos and basses, and a brassy, coarse military march. Timpani and low brass build a crowd. At the end a solo clarinet begins the beloved's theme, is cut off by a single crashing chord, and the strings drop a bouncing pizzicato figure: the head hitting the basket.","Berlioz was twenty-six, obsessed with the Irish actress Harriet Smithson after seeing her Shakespeare performances in Paris in 1827, and had distributed a programme note describing an opium dream. The premiere took place at the Paris Conservatoire on 5 December 1830, months after the July Revolution had put barricades in the streets, and days before he left for Rome as a Prix de Rome laureate. He had never held an orchestral post.","The march was not written for this symphony: Berlioz salvaged it from his abandoned opera Les francs-juges and claimed to have adapted it in a single night. The beloved's theme, which saturates the other movements, appears here for only a few bars before the blade falls — the one place in the work where it is silenced rather than transformed. The ophicleides, a now-extinct keyed brass, give the march its swaggering ugliness.","Liszt made a piano transcription in 1833, and Schumann's long 1835 essay on that transcription established the symphony as a serious object rather than a scandal. Later critics fought over whether the printed programme is essential or an embarrassment; Berlioz himself revised it and once suggested the movements could stand alone. Period-instrument recordings with real ophicleides and gut strings have changed the sound decisively.","Listen for the two bassoon-heavy statements of the march, one grim and one gleeful, and notice that the crowd seems to be enjoying itself. Then wait for the clarinet at the very end and count how long it survives. John Eliot Gardiner's period recording restores the snarl; a big modern orchestra gives you the weight instead."],"berlioz-symphonie-fantastique-witches-sabbath":["The symphony's finale. The dead artist finds himself at his own funeral among a crowd of monsters. Muttering strings and shrieking woodwind gather; the beloved's theme returns played by a squealing high clarinet, turned into a vulgar dance. Two tolling bells announce the plainchant Dies irae, intoned by low brass and answered by mocking echoes. A whirling fugue, the Witches' Round, then combines with the chant and drives to a brutal close.","Written in the spring and summer of 1830 alongside the rest of the symphony, at a time when Berlioz was living on lessons and journalism and following the July Revolution through the streets. Paris audiences knew supernatural spectacle from the stage — Weber's Wolf's Glen scene had been a sensation — but not inside a symphony, a form still understood as Beethoven's abstract inheritance.","The beloved's melody, noble in the first movement, is here handed to a small E flat clarinet and given a trill and a grace-note snigger — the same tune made grotesque without changing its outline. In the Witches' Round the strings are told to strike the strings with the wood of the bow, a rasping effect Berlioz did more than anyone to popularise. The Dies irae is real liturgical chant, quoted straight.","Contemporaries found the movement either visionary or repellent, and conservative critics used it for decades as proof that Berlioz could not write. Its influence proved larger than its reputation: the diabolical orchestral scherzo, the Dies irae as shorthand for death, and the sound of a supernatural crowd all descend from here, through Liszt and Saint-Saëns to modern film scores.","Wait for the small clarinet and recognise the tune you have heard four times already, now with a leer. Listen for the two bells and the chant that follows, then for the moment the fugue and the chant run at once. A recording with genuine ophicleides and hard sticks on the timpani makes the noise far more interesting than a smooth modern blend."],"berlioz-requiem-dies-irae":["The Day of Wrath movement of the Grande Messe des morts. It begins quietly, the choir chanting an almost plain melody over restless strings, and builds through four rising entries. Then the Tuba mirum arrives: four brass bands stationed at the corners of the building fire a fanfare at each other across the room while sixteen timpani, tuned to make chords, roll underneath. After the shattering climax the music subsides to a single line of frightened voices.","Commissioned by the French government in 1837 to honour the dead of the July Revolution, then repurposed as a memorial for General Damrémont and soldiers killed at Constantine in Algeria. The premiere took place at Les Invalides in Paris on 5 December 1837, with Habeneck conducting. Berlioz had to fight for payment and feared the commission would be cancelled outright; state funerals in the July Monarchy were political theatre with music attached.","The four brass groups are not there for volume but for direction: Berlioz specified their placement so that the sound arrives from different points of the compass, an idea he took from the acoustics of large buildings rather than from any orchestral tradition. The timpani are tuned to sound harmony, not rhythm. Berlioz also refuses a fugue here, keeping the choir in near-unison so the words stay audible under the brass.","Hostile critics used the movement as evidence that Berlioz confused size with substance, a charge that stuck for a century. Berlioz said that if all his works but one were to be destroyed he would ask mercy for the Requiem. He also told, and probably improved, a story about Habeneck taking snuff at the crucial entry, forcing the composer to step in and beat the bar himself. Performances still depend on finding a building large enough.","Hear it in a big resonant space if you possibly can, or in a recording made in one — a dry studio destroys the point. Listen first for how long the quiet opening lasts, so the brass entry lands as an interruption. Then follow one brass group and try to hear the answers coming back from the other three."],"berlioz-symphonie-fantastique-un-bal":["The second movement: the artist glimpses his beloved across a crowded ballroom. Two harps set the room shimmering before the waltz proper begins, elegant and continuous. Halfway through, the beloved's theme slides in on woodwind, dressed as a waltz tune itself and slightly out of step with the dancing around it. The room reasserts itself, the waltz accelerates, and the movement ends in a rush without ever quite settling the interruption.","In 1830 the waltz was still faintly disreputable in Paris — a close-hold couple dance associated with Vienna and with modern city pleasure — and it had no place inside a symphony, where a minuet or scherzo was expected. Berlioz was writing for a public that went to the opera and the ballroom on the same evening, and his programme note frames the movement as a social gathering the dreamer cannot enjoy.","Two harps were an expensive luxury and their glitter is doing scene-painting, not accompaniment. Berlioz later added an optional cornet obbligato that decorates the main waltz tune; it survives in a copyist's score, is absent from most editions, and only some conductors play it, so two recordings of the same movement can contain visibly different music.","Early listeners were less scandalised by the ballroom than by the idea of dance music inside a serious symphony at all. The movement's real influence was slow and enormous: the symphonic waltz as a portrait of society runs from here through Tchaikovsky's ballets to Mahler's ironic dances and Ravel's disintegrating La Valse. Whether to include the cornet part remains a live editorial argument.","Listen for the harps at the very start, then wait for the beloved's theme to arrive on the woodwind and notice how it does not quite fit the dance. Compare a recording that includes the cornet obbligato with one that does not — the difference is startling. Then hear the first movement again to recognise the tune in its original form."],"berlioz-roman-carnival-overture":["A concert overture in two halves. A brief fast flourish opens, then the cor anglais sings a long love melody, passed around the strings and woodwind and quietly built to a full-orchestra statement. The second half is a saltarello — a fast Roman street dance in a bounding triple lilt — with tambourines, cymbals and cross-rhythms that keep tripping the beat. The love tune returns inside the dance, and the two collide in the final pages.","Berlioz's opera Benvenuto Cellini had failed badly at the Paris Opéra in 1838, and he rescued its best material into a stand-alone overture, first played on 3 February 1844 at the Salle Herz. The subject is the Roman carnival Berlioz had watched during his Prix de Rome years, and the piece was aimed squarely at the concert public that had rejected his opera. It was an immediate and rare public success.","The cor anglais melody is a recycled love duet from the opera, \"O Teresa, vous que j'aime\", so the overture's most tender passage is a stolen scene from a failure. In the dance, Berlioz keeps setting a two-beat pattern against the three-beat pulse, which is why the music feels crowded and jostling rather than merely fast — the sound of a street rather than a stage.","Berlioz recalled that at the first rehearsal the wind players failed to appear, that he told the strings the tempo, and that the piece went perfectly regardless — a story he enjoyed telling at the expense of conductors. It was encored at the premiere. Later it became a standard concert opener and, unfairly, the only Berlioz many orchestras played, which delayed serious attention to Benvenuto Cellini itself.","Listen for the cor anglais entry and remember that it is a duet with one singer missing. In the fast half, try to tap the beat and notice how often the orchestra pulls it out from under you. Compare a French orchestra's lighter, sharper sound with a big international one; the tambourines should sound like a crowd, not a percussion section."],"berlioz-damnation-faust-hungarian-march":["Faust, wandering on a Hungarian plain, watches an army march past. Berlioz starts almost inaudibly — a bare woodwind statement of the tune over plucked strings, as if the column were still a mile off — and then builds a five-minute crescendo of approach, adding brass and drums by stages until the whole orchestra is thundering past at close range. A quiet contrasting episode acts as a breathing space before the final blaze.","Berlioz spent 1845 and 1846 touring central Europe as a conductor. Advised that a Hungarian audience would respond to the Rákóczi tune, a national melody associated with an eighteenth-century prince and periodically frowned on by the Habsburg authorities, he orchestrated it overnight and performed it in Pest in February 1846 to an ovation. He then wrote it into Faust by simply relocating Goethe's hero to Hungary.","Berlioz cheerfully admitted the geography was an excuse, remarking in his preface that he had felt free to take Faust anywhere he wished. The march's real invention is dynamic rather than melodic: the tune barely changes, but the orchestration expands from near-silence to full force, which is why it works as a picture of distance closing. Liszt arranged the same melody for piano.","The Damnation of Faust failed at the Opéra-Comique in December 1846, leaving Berlioz deeply in debt, and he never recovered financially from the venture. The march survived on its own as a concert item and became a Hungarian patriotic emblem. Modern staged versions of Faust have restored the work's reputation, but the march is still usually met outside its context, as an encore.","Start it quiet and leave the volume alone; the piece is a crescendo and adjusting the dial ruins it. Listen for the piccolo cutting through as the band gets closer, and for the drums arriving last. Compare Berlioz's version with Liszt's piano arrangement of the same tune to hear what an orchestra adds."],"berlioz-nuits-d-ete-villanelle":["The opening song of a set of six on poems by Théophile Gautier, and the lightest thing Berlioz wrote. Two lovers plan to go into the woods when the new season comes. Three verses run to almost the same music at a walking pace, the voice skipping in short phrases while the strings trot underneath. In the last verse a cello slips a countermelody in beneath the singer, and the song stops without a climax.","Berlioz set Gautier's poems in 1841 with piano accompaniment, at a time when his marriage to Harriet Smithson was collapsing and he was surviving mainly on music criticism. He orchestrated one song in 1843 and the remaining five in 1856, for a German publisher. French song in the 1840s meant the domestic romance; the idea of a cycle with orchestra had essentially no precedent.","Berlioz orchestrated the six songs for different voice types, so the cycle as he left it cannot be sung complete by one person — the version everybody performs, with a single singer throughout, is a later convenience. In this song, listen for the descending cello line in the third verse: it is the only real change to an otherwise strictly repeated strophe, and it arrives exactly where the poem turns physical.","The set went almost unnoticed in the nineteenth century, overshadowed by Berlioz's enormous public works and awkward for a recital culture built on German lieder. Its rehabilitation came in the twentieth century, with Régine Crespin's 1963 recording the decisive event. Scholars now treat it as the first orchestral song cycle, a form later claimed by Wagner, Mahler and Strauss.","Listen for the walking string figure that never stops, then wait for the cello to join the singer near the end. Hear the piano version too — it is a different, plainer piece. Crespin's recording remains the reference; a lighter tenor voice makes the song sound younger and more careless, which suits the poem."],"glinka-ruslan-lyudmila-overture":["Five minutes at close to top speed. The strings tear off in scurrying figures under a bright brass call, a broad second tune arrives in the woodwind and lower strings, and the whole thing is repeated and driven to a finish. Near the end the bass instruments walk down a scale in which every step is the same size — an even, floorless sequence that belongs to no key and represents the evil dwarf Chernomor. Then the D major rush returns and ends the piece.","Pushkin's mock-heroic fairy tale was the obvious subject for Russia's first ambitious opera composer, but Pushkin was killed in a duel in 1837 before he could write a libretto, and five different hands patched one together. The premiere at St Petersburg's Bolshoi Kamenny Theatre on 9 December 1842 went badly: the imperial family left before the end, and the court took its cue accordingly.","That descending whole-step scale is one of the earliest systematic uses of the whole-tone scale in European music, half a century before Debussy made it a signature of dream and vagueness. Glinka uses it as a monster: because no note in it sounds like a home, the sorcerer has no key and no gravity. The overture also lifts its main material from the opera's final chorus, so it is a summary played in advance.","Glinka was wounded by the reception and left Russia soon afterwards. Liszt, touring Russia in 1843, played and praised music from the opera, which helped its standing at home. In the Soviet era the overture became an orchestral calling card and a speed contest, with Mravinsky and Svetlanov setting tempos that most Western orchestras decline. Its harmonic experiments matter more to historians than its plot.","Play it loud and once; then play it again listening only to the basses at the end for that even, sinking scale. Compare a Russian orchestra's headlong tempo with a Western one's — the piece is partly about risk. Then hear Debussy's Faun and notice the same scale doing the opposite job."],"glinka-kamarinskaya":["A seven-minute orchestral fantasy on two Russian folk tunes: a slow wedding song and a fast dance. Neither melody is developed in the German manner. Instead each is repeated over and over, essentially unchanged, while everything around it is rebuilt — new counter-lines, new instruments, harmonies that shift under a tune that will not move. The dance accelerates through dozens of repetitions, and the piece ends with a joke: two abrupt chords after a sudden silence.","Glinka wrote it in Warsaw in 1848, in his restless later years spent mostly outside Russia. He was a landowner and amateur by training who had studied briefly in Berlin, and he had no model for building a Russian orchestral piece out of folk material: the Western symphonic method assumes tunes can be broken into fragments, and Russian folk melodies resist being broken.","Glinka noticed that the wedding song and the dance tune share a phrase, which is why the two can sit in one piece at all — the whole fantasy grows from a coincidence he spotted. Listen also for the horns, which hold on to a note while the harmony moves away underneath, producing clashes Glinka described as a discovery rather than a mistake. This variation-by-background became the standard Russian technique.","Tchaikovsky wrote that the whole Russian symphonic school is contained in Kamarinskaya as the oak is in the acorn, and no later assessment has improved on that. Mussorgsky, Borodin and Rimsky-Korsakov all worked from its method; Stravinsky, who disliked much Russian nationalism, praised Glinka's ear. The piece's brevity and lightness have caused it to be underrated as a mere curtain-raiser.","Count the repetitions of the dance tune and watch what changes each time — the tune is the constant, the orchestra is the variable. Listen for the horns holding their note as the harmony slides. Then hear Rimsky-Korsakov's Capriccio Espagnol or Stravinsky's Petrushka and recognise the same engine running at higher speed."],"glinka-life-tsar-overture":["A slow introduction of quiet, weighty chords opens out into a brisk sonata movement that previews the opera's two worlds: square, sturdy Russian material and the swirling triple-time Polish dances of the invaders. Themes from the opera proper are quoted rather than invented afresh. The writing is more conventional than anything in Glinka's later scores, but the orchestration is clean and the closing pages have a swagger that made the piece a favourite curtain-raiser.","The story is of a peasant, Ivan Susanin, who leads a Polish detachment to their deaths in the forest to save the new tsar. Glinka, a wealthy amateur back from study abroad, offered it to the imperial court; Nicholas I accepted the dedication and the opera was renamed in his honour. Its premiere on 9 December 1836 in St Petersburg made it the season-opening ritual of the Russian imperial theatres for eighty years.","The opera assigns national character to musical style rather than to costume: the Poles get mazurka and polonaise rhythms, the Russians get folk-derived, modally coloured melody, and the two never mix. That decision, audible already in the overture's contrasting themes, is the seed of everything later Russian composers did with the idea of national sound.","Some St Petersburg aristocrats sneered at the score as \"coachman's music\", and Glinka took the insult as a compliment, since coachmen were exactly whose music he wanted. After 1917 the tsarist plot was intolerable; the work was restaged in 1939 under its old title Ivan Susanin with a rewritten libretto that removed the monarchy. Post-Soviet productions have restored the original text, and the argument about it continues.","Listen for the change of gear from the solemn introduction to the fast section, then for the two contrasting kinds of melody — one square-footed, one whirling. If you go on to the opera, follow the Polish act and hear the same rhythms doing dramatic work. Russian orchestras play it with more edge and less polish, which suits it."],"glinka-valse-fantaisie":["A chain of short waltz episodes strung around one wistful minor-key refrain that keeps returning. The scoring is deliberately light — strings and woodwind carry most of it, with the brass held in reserve — so the piece hovers rather than sweeps. Its oddity is rhythmic: the melodies fall into three-bar groups instead of the four-bar squares a ballroom waltz needs, which gives the dance a persistent slight limp, as if it were remembered rather than danced.","Glinka wrote it for piano in 1839, in the middle of an unhappy marriage and an infatuation with Ekaterina Kern, daughter of the woman Pushkin addressed in one of his best-known lyrics. He orchestrated it in 1845 and again, definitively, in 1856, the year before he died in Berlin. The waltz by then was the standard currency of urban Russian social life and of the salon piano.","Those three-bar phrases are the whole trick: the ear expects a fourth bar that never comes, so the dance keeps arriving early. Notice too how sparingly the full orchestra is used — Glinka reserves weight instead of spending it, an approach closer to Berlioz's thinking about colour than to the Viennese waltz factories.","Long treated as a charming trifle beside the two operas, the piece has been reassessed as the direct ancestor of the Russian symphonic waltz that Tchaikovsky perfected in the ballets. George Balanchine choreographed it, which returned it to the dance world it never quite belonged to. Its three orchestrations complicate matters: the 1856 version is standard, the earlier one lost.","Try to count the phrases in fours and notice that you cannot. Listen for the returning minor refrain and how the episodes between it grow brighter and then fade. Then hear Tchaikovsky's Serenade waltz for the same material handled with a full orchestral hand, and Glinka's restraint will stand out."],"fanny-mendelssohn-jahr-september":["The ninth panel of a twelve-month piano cycle, subtitled \"At the River\". The right hand runs a continuous rippling figure from the first bar to the last while a melody surfaces inside it, sometimes in the thumb, sometimes below. The water never stops; the tune has to find room within it. About four minutes long, it stays quiet almost throughout and ends by thinning the current down to a few drops rather than by arriving anywhere.","Fanny Hensel wrote Das Jahr in 1841, the year after the Hensel family's long Italian journey, which she described as the happiest of her life and which broke a long compositional silence. In Berlin she ran the Sunday Musicales at the family house on Leipziger Strasse, directing a chorus and orchestra for an invited public — a serious concert series in a private drawing room, since public performance was closed to her.","The presentation copy is an art object as much as a score: written on coloured paper, headed with poetic mottoes, and illustrated with vignettes by her husband, the painter Wilhelm Hensel. Each month carries an epigraph from Goethe, Tieck or Eichendorff, so the cycle is a collaboration between three arts. The complete cycle waited until 1989 for a full published edition.","For a century and a half the piece did not exist as far as concert life was concerned. Its recovery, part of the broader reassessment of Hensel's 450-odd works, has led scholars to place Das Jahr alongside Schumann's cycles as one of the ambitious piano projects of the 1840s — longer and more structurally deliberate than the character pieces it superficially resembles.","Listen for the melody hiding inside the running figuration; try to follow it and notice how often it changes register. Then hear December from the same cycle for the extreme contrast. Recordings by Lauma Skride and Els Biesemans differ sharply on tempo — the faster the water, the harder the tune is to find."],"fanny-mendelssohn-notturno-g":["A single-movement nocturne of about five minutes. A rocking left hand supports a singing melody that sits low, in the middle of the keyboard rather than in the bright top register, so the piece has a darker grain than most salon nocturnes. The middle section pushes into more restless harmony and a thicker texture before the opening returns, decorated and quieter. It ends by fading rather than by resolving emphatically.","Berlin, 1838. Hensel was thirty-two, at the centre of the city's most serious private musical life through the Sunday Musicales, and composing steadily with no expectation of publication: her father had told her that music could only ever be an ornament for a woman, and her brother, whose judgment she trusted, agreed for most of his life. The nocturne was written for the instrument in her own house.","Placing the melody in the tenor register, with accompaniment both above and below it, is a Hensel fingerprint and demands real independence between the fingers of one hand. Her harmony wanders further and faster than Felix's in comparable pieces — listen to how the middle section keeps sidestepping the key you expect, arriving somewhere warmer or colder than the phrase promised.","The wider question of attribution shadows all her music: several of her songs appeared under Felix's name in his Opp. 8 and 9, and the one Queen Victoria sang to him as her favourite turned out to be Fanny's. Since the 1980s musicologists have reconstructed her catalogue from the Berlin manuscripts, and pianists have begun programming the nocturnes beside Chopin's rather than as curiosities.","Follow the melody's register: notice that it is not on top. Compare it directly with a Chopin nocturne of the same years and listen for the difference in colour — hers is denser and less decorated. John Field's nocturnes make a useful third point of comparison for hearing what each composer took from the genre."],"fanny-mendelssohn-jahr-dezember":["The last month of the twelve-part cycle. It opens with a restless, snow-blown figuration in constant motion, then slows and clears; out of the texture rises the Lutheran Christmas chorale \"Vom Himmel hoch, da komm ich her\", stated plainly in full chords like an organ heard through a wall. The running music returns beneath and around it, and the movement closes in calm, with the chorale still audible in the bass.","Das Jahr was completed in 1841 in Berlin. The Mendelssohn family had converted from Judaism to Lutheranism, and Bach's church music was the household's shared inheritance — Fanny had sung in the Singakademie chorus for her brother's celebrated 1829 revival of the St Matthew Passion. Quoting a Luther chorale in a private piano cycle places a public, communal music inside a domestic frame.","The cycle does not end here: a Postlude follows, built on the New Year chorale \"Das alte Jahr vergangen ist\", so the year closes twice, once with Christmas and once with a farewell. The December chorale is the same tune Bach harmonised in his Christmas Oratorio, and Hensel presents it almost without decoration — the plainest music in an otherwise elaborate cycle.","The manuscript's coloured paper, mottoes and drawings by Wilhelm Hensel led early cataloguers to treat Das Jahr as an amateur keepsake rather than a work. Modern scholarship reads the visual apparatus as part of the design and the cycle as a coherent large-scale structure, with December and the Postlude as its planned goal. Complete recordings have only become common since the 1990s.","Wait for the chorale to emerge from the flurry — the moment is unmistakable, and it is the point of the whole cycle. Then play the Postlude that follows, and September for contrast. If the chorale is unfamiliar, hear a Bach setting of it first; recognising the tune changes what the piece feels like."],"fanny-mendelssohn-piano-trio-d-first-movement":["Nine minutes of sustained agitation. The piano opens with sweeping broken chords across the whole keyboard and barely stops; the violin and cello sing above and through the wash rather than trading motifs with it. A warmer second theme arrives without slowing the current. The development pushes the piano hard — this is virtuoso writing, not an accompaniment — and the movement drives to a close that does not relent into the major.","Written in 1846 as a birthday present for her sister Rebecka, and played at the Sunday Musicales with the composer at the piano. That same year Hensel finally published, issuing her Op. 1 songs at forty against her family's long discouragement. On 14 May 1847 she collapsed and died of a stroke while rehearsing for one of her own concerts; Felix, shattered, died six months later.","The piano dominates because she wrote the part for herself and never had to defer to a hired soloist. Notice that the strings rarely get the running figures — they are given the long lines, the piano the weather. The trio's later movements include a song-without-words slow movement and a finale that begins with a free, recitative-like passage before the theme arrives.","Breitkopf published the trio posthumously in 1850 as Op. 11, and it then vanished for well over a century. Its return has been the single strongest argument for taking Hensel seriously as an instrumental composer rather than a songwriter: the piece is technically demanding, formally confident, and harmonically bolder than her brother's trios of the same decade.","Listen to the piano's left hand — the sheer amount of keyboard covered is the movement's signature. Then follow the cello, which carries the most singing material. Compare it directly with Felix's D minor trio of 1839 or Clara Schumann's G minor trio; the three make one of the most revealing programmes in chamber music."],"fanny-mendelssohn-schwanenlied":["Three minutes on a short Heine poem: a star falls, blossoms drop from the apple tree, a swan sings and dies. The piano carries a gently falling figure that runs almost continuously, so the whole song descends. The vocal line is plain and mostly syllabic, rising only once, near the end, before sinking again. The postlude drifts downward after the voice has stopped and simply ceases, without a closing gesture.","This is the first song of her first published opus, issued in Berlin in 1846 when she was forty. Friends, including the diplomat and amateur musician Robert von Keudell and her husband, pressed her to publish; Felix withheld his approval at first, on the grounds that public authorship was not right for her, and gave his blessing only afterwards. She had by then written some 250 songs that nobody outside the house had heard.","Heine's poem is barely eight lines, and Hensel resists every opportunity to dramatise it — no storm, no outburst, one small climb and then descent. The song's weight sits in the piano's postlude, which continues the falling motion after the singer's last word and leaves the phrase unfinished, as though the music has run out rather than concluded.","Op. 1 was reviewed warmly in the Berlin press, and Hensel wrote with visible delight about being read as a composer in public. She died the following year, before the reception could develop into a career. Later editors mostly ignored her songs; since the 1990s recital programmes have paired them with Felix's and with Clara Schumann's, and the comparison is not to her disadvantage.","Listen to the piano alone for the first bars and notice that everything is falling. Then follow the one place where the voice rises. Hear it beside Schumann's Heine settings from Dichterliebe, written six years earlier on the same poet, for two entirely different temperaments reading the same kind of poem."],"mendelssohn-violin-concerto-e":["Three movements run without a break for about twenty-seven minutes. The soloist enters in the second bar with the main theme, before the orchestra has said anything — no long preamble. The first movement's cadenza comes before the return of the theme rather than at the end, and the soloist keeps playing arpeggios underneath when the orchestra takes the tune back. A single held bassoon note bridges into the slow movement; the finale is a fast, light-footed E major romp with fanfares.","Mendelssohn told the violinist Ferdinand David in 1838 that a concerto in E minor was running in his head and giving him no peace, then took six years over it, consulting David on every technical detail. He was running the Leipzig Gewandhaus orchestra and founding its conservatory. The premiere on 13 March 1845 had David as soloist and Niels Gade conducting, because Mendelssohn was too ill to appear.","The cadenza's placement is the structural invention: by putting it before the recapitulation and letting the soloist continue playing through the orchestra's return, Mendelssohn turns the traditional showpiece into a hinge. Listen also for the bassoon that refuses to stop at the end of the first movement, holding one note across the gap where applause would normally go — an early and very deliberate way of sealing a work shut.","Joseph Joachim called it the heart's jewel among the great German violin concertos, and it has never left the repertoire; the Nazi regime banned it as the work of a Jewish-born composer and commissioned substitutes that nobody plays. Debate now runs on style rather than value: gut strings and lighter bows, David's own fingerings, and how far the finale should be taken as fairy music rather than display.","Notice how quickly the solo arrives — count to two. Then listen for the bassoon holding on at the end of the first movement, the moment the concerto refuses to let you clap. Compare Heifetz's speed with Hilary Hahn's poise, and a period-instrument recording for the lighter, less varnished sound David would have made."],"mendelssohn-midsummer-night-s-dream-overture":["Four sustained wind chords open the piece and close it, like curtains. Between them, the strings scamper in feather-light staccato for the fairies; a loud, square court theme brings in the Athenian nobles; a warm string melody serves the lovers; and a lumbering figure with a braying descending leap gives us Bottom and the workmen, complete with donkey. Twelve minutes, and every character in Shakespeare's play is placed in sound before a word is spoken.","Mendelssohn wrote it in the summer of 1826, aged seventeen, in the garden house of the family's Berlin property on Leipziger Strasse, after he and Fanny had read Shakespeare in Schlegel and Tieck's German translation. It was first played publicly in Stettin in February 1827. There was no stage production attached: the overture is a concert piece about a play, which was itself a new kind of object.","The braying is scored partly for ophicleide, a keyed bass brass instrument now extinct and usually replaced by tuba — the original is coarser and funnier. The four opening chords return at the very end differently voiced, so the frame is not quite identical on the way out. Fanny, who followed the piece's composition day by day, said she knew it note for note.","Nothing else by a seventeen-year-old holds a comparable place in the repertoire, and the piece founded the concert overture as a genre — a self-contained orchestral drama attached to a literary subject, taken up by Berlioz, Brahms and Tchaikovsky. Seventeen years later Mendelssohn wrote a full set of incidental music around it for the Prussian court, matching the earlier style so closely that the join is inaudible.","Listen to the four chords, then to the strings that follow — the change of weight is the whole trick. Wait for the donkey. Then hear the 1843 incidental music and try to guess which numbers are seventeen years younger. A period orchestra with a real ophicleide is worth seeking out for the rustics alone."],"mendelssohn-hebrides-overture":["A ten-minute concert overture built from one short rocking figure, first heard low in violas, cellos and bassoons and then repeated at rising pitches, over and over, like a swell running under a boat. A broad cello melody rides above it. The orchestra gathers into spray and wind, subsides, and gathers again; near the end a pair of clarinets sings the second theme almost alone before the sea closes over it. No storm, no shipwreck, no narrative.","Mendelssohn toured Scotland in 1829 with his friend Karl Klingemann, visiting the basalt caves of Staffa in the Hebrides. He sent the opening bars home in a letter from Tobermory dated 7 August 1829, before he had written anything else. He then rewrote the score repeatedly, in Rome and afterwards, and only declared himself satisfied in 1832; the London premiere came that May.","Count the repetitions of the opening figure: it appears more than twenty times in the first minutes, transposed but essentially unchanged, which is how the piece produces motion without going anywhere. Mendelssohn's own complaint about an earlier draft was that the middle section tasted more of counterpoint than of blubber and seagulls — the revision cut exactly that.","Wagner, no admirer of Mendelssohn generally, called him a first-rate landscape painter and meant the Hebrides; Brahms is reported to have said he would give all his works to have written it. The overture's real consequence was formal: it showed that an orchestral piece could describe a place rather than tell a story, which is where the tone poem begins. Two versions survive and both are recorded.","Listen for the opening figure and then keep tracking it — the piece is a study in one idea refusing to develop. Wait for the clarinets near the end, the most exposed moment in the score. Then hear Debussy's La Mer or Sibelius for two later answers to the same question of how to put weather into an orchestra."],"mendelssohn-octet-scherzo":["Four and a half minutes of near-continuous whisper. Eight string players run a rapid, staccato figure in G minor with almost no sustained note anywhere; the parts hand fragments to one another so fast that the texture flickers rather than sings. There is one short fugal passage and no loud climax at all. At the end the music simply thins out and disappears in a single flick, the whole scherzo having stayed at the edge of audibility.","Mendelssohn wrote the Octet in October 1825, aged sixteen, as a birthday piece for his violin teacher Eduard Rietz, and directed that the eight instruments should play in the style of a symphony orchestra rather than as two quartets. The Berlin house on Leipziger Strasse had the players available on any Sunday, so an unprecedented combination was simply a matter of who was in the room.","Fanny recorded that the movement was inspired by the Walpurgis Night Dream stanzas in Goethe's Faust — cloud and mist brightening, and everything vanishing like foam at the close. Mendelssohn later orchestrated the scherzo and used it in London in 1829 to replace the minuet of his First Symphony, which is why an orchestral version exists of a movement written for eight strings.","The Octet is still the benchmark against which precocity is measured, and no comparable case exists in music. Its scherzo also fixed a manner — fast, quiet, staccato, fantastical — that Mendelssohn returned to all his life and that later composers treated as his signature. Performances argue mainly about tempo and about whether to use one player or several per part.","Turn it up rather than down; the piece lives at a whisper and gets lost at low volume. Listen for the moment near the end when the music evaporates instead of finishing. Then hear the Midsummer Night's Dream overture, written a year later, and recognise the same fairy machinery scaled up to an orchestra."],"mendelssohn-spring-song":["Three minutes of solo piano in A major. A rippling middle-register accompaniment runs throughout while a simple melody, decorated with a small curling turn each time it starts, sits above it. The shape is straightforward: tune, a slightly shadowed central passage, tune again with extra ornament, then a short coda that lifts and closes. The technical demand is light; the difficulty is entirely one of touch and balance between the hands.","One of forty-eight Songs Without Words published in eight books between 1829 and 1845, written for the middle-class parlour piano that had become standard equipment across Europe. Mendelssohn was at the height of his career in Leipzig and London, and these pieces were his most commercially successful publications by a wide margin — music designed to be bought and played rather than attended.","Mendelssohn did not supply most of the titles; publishers added them, and he was uneasy about the practice. In a letter of 1842 he argued the opposite of the usual view: that music is not too vague for words but too precise for them, and that words are what fail. Notice that the little turn at the head of the melody is the only thing that changes on each return.","Victorian ubiquity was followed by twentieth-century condescension, and for decades the Songs Without Words were treated as the definition of harmless. Recordings by Barenboim, Perahia and others have made the case that the best of them are exacting miniatures whose transparency leaves a pianist nowhere to hide. This one remains the most played and the most parodied.","Listen to the left hand alone if you can — its evenness is the whole performance. Notice the turn that opens the tune, and how it is decorated differently on the return. Then hear a few less famous Songs Without Words, the Duetto or the Venetian gondola songs, which show the same craft without the wallpaper associations."],"mendelssohn-venetian-boat-song-op-30-no":["A barcarolle in a rocking six-beat lilt. The left hand keeps a low repeated note under a swaying figure, like an oar stroke and the water it displaces; the melody sits in the middle of the texture rather than at the top, so the piece has a muffled, interior quality. Three minutes, quiet throughout, in F sharp minor. It ends by slowing to a stop rather than arriving anywhere, the boat drifting out of hearing.","Written in 1834, three years after Mendelssohn's Italian journey. He composed three Venetian gondola songs across the Songs Without Words books, and the type — the gondolier's rocking barcarolle — became one of the nineteenth century's favourite images, an urban working song reimagined as private reverie for northern Europeans who had made the trip once and remembered it.","The repeated low note is the piece's clock, and it barely varies for three minutes; everything expressive happens above it. Listen for where the melody actually lies — under the top voice, in the thumb of the right hand — which is why the piece sounds like something overheard from below deck. The title here is Mendelssohn's own, unlike most in the series.","Long dismissed with the rest of the set as salon goods, the gondola songs are now the pieces cited when the Songs Without Words are defended: they are unsentimental, formally taut and harmonically more restless than their reputation allows. Chopin's Barcarolle and Fauré's thirteen barcarolles descend directly from the type, and pianists increasingly programme them together.","Listen for the oar stroke in the bass and try to ignore everything else for one hearing; then listen for the melody in the middle. Compare it with Chopin's Barcarolle for the same rocking figure given ten minutes and far more harmony. A slightly faster tempo than tradition allows makes the piece a boat again rather than a mood."],"mendelssohn-wedding-march":["A trumpet fanfare, answered by the full orchestra in bright C major with the tune everyone knows. Two quieter contrasting episodes follow, one lyrical and one lighter-footed, each returning to the fanfare and the main march. Five minutes in total, though almost nobody hears more than the first thirty seconds. In the theatre it is an entr'acte, played between acts to carry the audience from the forest back to the Athenian court.","In 1842 Friedrich Wilhelm IV of Prussia commissioned Mendelssohn to write incidental music for a court production of A Midsummer Night's Dream, and he built it around his own overture of sixteen years earlier. The staged premiere took place at Potsdam on 14 October 1843. Its career as wedding music began in 1858, when Victoria, the Princess Royal, chose it for her marriage at St James's Palace.","In the play the march accompanies a comic triple wedding of characters who have spent the night bewitched, one of whom was recently in love with a donkey — the grandeur is a joke. Some churches later banned it precisely because it came from the theatre and not from the liturgy. Liszt made a piano paraphrase pairing it with the fairy music from the same score.","Paired with the bridal chorus from Wagner's Lohengrin, it became the standard processional across the English-speaking world, an unlikely alliance between two composers who despised each other's aesthetics. The Nazi regime banned Mendelssohn's music and commissioned replacement wedding marches, none of which survived the war. Performances of the complete incidental music remain much rarer than the march.","Listen past the first minute — the middle episodes are the part nobody knows and the best of the piece. Then hear it in the complete incidental music, where it follows the lovers' confusion and lands as comedy. Compare an orchestral performance with the organ version most weddings actually use."],"mendelssohn-symphony-no-4-italian-first-movement":["Pulsing repeated woodwind chords start immediately, and the violins leap in over them with one of the most headlong themes in the repertoire — the whole movement is a run in bright A major. A gentler clarinet-led second theme provides shade. Then, in the development, an entirely new theme arrives and is worked fugally, so the movement stacks three ideas rather than two. Ten minutes with almost no slack in them.","The material comes from Mendelssohn's grand tour of 1830 and 1831 — Rome, Naples, Holy Week at St Peter's — but the score was completed in Berlin in March 1833 to fulfil a commission from London's Philharmonic Society, which had asked for a symphony, an overture and a vocal work. He conducted the premiere himself in London on 13 May 1833.","The extra theme introduced in the development is genuinely new, not derived from anything heard earlier, which breaks the classical rule that development develops. Listen for the moment the strings begin a fugue on it, then for the recapitulation where all three ideas are folded together. The symphony also ends in the minor: its finale is a whirling saltarello in A minor, so the sunlight does not last.","Mendelssohn never published it. He revised the last three movements in 1834, remained dissatisfied, and the work appeared only in 1851, after his death, in a text he had not approved. Conductors must therefore choose between the 1833 original and the 1834 revision; both are recorded, and the differences in the inner movements are substantial. The nickname is not his either.","Listen for the woodwind pulse before the tune, which sets the whole movement's forward tilt. Then wait for the new theme in the development and notice you have not heard it before. Compare a fleet period-instrument reading with a large modern orchestra; the piece rewards lightness far more than weight."],"mendelssohn-elijah-he-watching-over-israel":["A quiet chorus near the middle of the oratorio, following the angels' trio that tells Elijah to lift up his eyes. The voices move in gently overlapping lines rather than blocks, the sopranos floating above, over an orchestral accompaniment that keeps a soft continuous motion. Four minutes, almost entirely hushed, with one small swell in the middle. It ends quieter than it began, the words \"slumbers not, nor sleeps\" repeated into near-silence.","The Birmingham Triennial Festival commissioned the oratorio, and Mendelssohn conducted the premiere on 26 August 1846 before an audience of some two thousand in Birmingham Town Hall, sung in English. Julius Schubring assembled the German biblical text; William Bartholomew made the English version. Britain in the 1840s had a mass amateur choral movement and an appetite for oratorio that Germany did not match.","The text splices two psalms together, so the chorus is a compilation rather than a quotation, assembled to give the exhausted prophet a lullaby. Structurally it is a resting point between two dramatic scenes: Mendelssohn puts the calmest music in the oratorio directly after its hero has asked to die. The model throughout is Bach's Passions crossed with Handel's crowd choruses.","Eight numbers were encored at the premiere and Elijah became, after Messiah, the most performed oratorio in Britain for a century. Mendelssohn revised heavily before the 1847 London performances and died months later. A twentieth-century reaction against Victorian religiosity — Shaw was merciless — pushed the work aside; period-instrument recordings and a return to the German text have restored it.","Listen for the overlapping entries rather than the words; the chorus works as texture before it works as text. Then hear the trio \"Lift thine eyes\" that precedes it, which sets it up. Compare a large amateur choral society performance with a small professional period-instrument one — the piece survives both, differently."],"mendelssohn-rondo-capriccioso":["Two pieces joined. A slow, songful E major Andante opens, unhurried and richly harmonised, and then hesitates on a single held chord before the music tips into E minor and takes off: a fast, feather-light rondo in which the fingers must stay detached and almost weightless for five minutes. The two moods alternate briefly near the end, and the piece finishes with a rush and a snap rather than a grand chord.","The fast rondo dates from the mid-1820s, the same years as the Octet and the Midsummer overture; the slow introduction was added in Munich in 1830, when Mendelssohn, twenty-one and touring, met the pianist Delphine von Schauroth, to whom the piece is dedicated. The concert piano was then rapidly gaining power and evenness, and a work built entirely on rapid detached playing was a demonstration of the new instrument.","The elfin staccato world here is exactly that of the Octet scherzo and the fairies of the Midsummer overture, transferred to two hands — Mendelssohn found this manner as a teenager and used it for life. The join between the Andante and the rondo is worth watching: the music does not modulate so much as flinch, from major to minor across one sustained chord.","It became a competition and conservatory staple, which cost it critical standing: for much of the twentieth century it was filed under salon display. Defenders point out that almost nothing else in the repertoire trains this particular touch, and that the Andante contains some of Mendelssohn's most searching harmony. Rachmaninoff and Cortot both recorded it, and both take it faster than modern pianists dare.","Listen for the hinge where the slow music tips into the fast, and note that nothing changes but the mood. In the rondo, follow the left hand, which does more than it seems to. Then hear the Octet scherzo and the Midsummer overture and recognise the same imaginary creatures on three different instruments."],"chopin-ballade-no-1-g":["Nine minutes for solo piano that behave like a told story. A slow rising introduction ends on a chord that sounds wrong and hangs unresolved. Then a hesitant, waltz-like first theme in a broad six-beat measure, and a second theme in a warm major key that returns later transformed into a blaze of sound. The two alternate and escalate. The end is a fast, violent coda with scales tearing down the keyboard in both hands and two bare octaves to close.","Chopin began it in Vienna around 1831, hearing news of the failed Polish uprising against Russian rule, and finished it in Paris; it was published in 1836. He was in his mid-twenties, established in the salons and unable to return home. Nothing called a ballade for solo piano existed before this: the word belonged to narrative poetry and to sung ballads, and Chopin borrowed it without supplying a story.","The recapitulation reverses the order of the two themes, so the piece comes back wrong way round — a small structural decision that makes the ending feel like a consequence rather than a return. The chord at the end of the introduction contains a note long argued over: editions and pianists disagree about whether one written pitch is a misprint, and the piece can be heard either way.","Schumann recalled Chopin saying that this was the work of his own he liked best. Chopin also told Schumann that the ballades owed something to the poems of Adam Mickiewicz, but never identified which, and scholars are sceptical of the detailed programmes later attached. The piece's twentieth-century fame owes something to film, notably The Pianist, which put it in front of audiences who knew no other Chopin.","Listen to the introduction's last chord and notice that it does not settle — everything after it is trying to fix that. Track the second, major-key theme and wait for its return in full force. Then compare Rubinstein's grandeur, Zimerman's control and Cortot's risk-taking; the coda exposes every pianist completely."],"chopin-nocturne-op-9-no-2":["A swaying accompaniment in the left hand, three beats to the bar, under a melody that returns three times, each time more elaborately decorated with small runs and turns. The piece is short and its harmony simple; nearly all its expressive weight is carried by ornament. Near the end an unmeasured flourish spills down the keyboard, a long trill gathers, and the music settles onto quiet closing chords. Under five minutes, and never loud.","Written around 1830 and 1831 when Chopin was barely twenty and published in Paris in 1832, in his first set of nocturnes. The genre was not his invention — the Irish pianist John Field had created it, and Chopin acknowledged the debt — but the Paris salon was, and Chopin built a career there playing to a few dozen listeners at a time rather than to concert halls, on the light-actioned Pleyel pianos he preferred.","The decorations are not fixed. Chopin wrote alternative versions of the ornamented returns into his pupils' copies, and several different readings survive in their hands, which means the score is a starting point rather than a text. The model for the whole approach is Italian opera: the right hand is a bel canto soprano, and the left hand is the orchestra keeping strict time beneath her.","Familiarity has done the piece no favours; it is the standard shorthand for tasteful background music. The performance argument concerns rubato: Chopin's own rule, as his pupils described it, was that the left hand keeps time while the right is free, a discipline that most sentimental performances abandon. Period Pleyel instruments make the ornament sound clearer and less syrupy.","Listen for the left hand's steadiness and judge the performance by whether it wavers. Follow the melody's three appearances and count the added decoration each time. Then hear Bellini's Casta diva and the comparison becomes obvious. A recording on an 1840s piano is a genuine revelation for anyone who thinks they know this piece."],"chopin-heroic-polonaise":["A rising, tightening introduction of chromatic chords, then the main polonaise theme: a striding processional in a firmly accented triple measure, with the left hand throwing chords like footfalls. The central section is the famous one — a long stretch over hammered repeating left-hand octaves, driving forward under a bugle-like figure above. The opening material returns, and the piece ends in a series of emphatic chords. Roughly seven minutes, and physically punishing throughout.","Composed in 1842 at Nohant, George Sand's country house in Berry, where Chopin spent his most productive summers. Poland had ceased to exist as a state; the polonaise, historically an aristocratic processional dance of the Polish court, had become for exiles a portable emblem of a nation under partition. Chopin wrote polonaises from childhood to his last years, and never wrote a note of programme music about them.","The introduction never comes back — a whole minute of gathering tension that the piece simply discards. The central octave passage is a physical problem as much as a musical one: repeated left-hand octaves at speed and volume for dozens of bars, which is why performances vary so widely in tempo. Chopin reportedly disliked the military readings that were attached to the piece almost at once.","The \"Heroic\" nickname is not Chopin's, and the cavalry-charge interpretation of the octave section is a nineteenth-century invention that has proved impossible to dislodge. Warsaw radio broadcast Chopin polonaises during the German siege of 1939, and Artur Rubinstein played them across the wartime years as an explicitly Polish statement. Modern pianists argue about whether to resist that history or play into it.","Listen for the introduction and then notice that it never returns. In the central section, follow the left hand alone and hear how relentless it is. Compare Rubinstein's broad, weighty reading with a faster modern one; the difference is essentially a disagreement about whether this is a dance or a demonstration."],"chopin-etude-op-10-no-3":["A study that begins slowly. The right hand must sing a melody on top while simultaneously playing moving inner notes underneath it with the same hand — that is the exercise, and it sounds like nothing more strenuous than a song. A stormy middle section then breaks in with hard double notes and grinding harmony, twice as fast and entirely different in character, before the opening tune returns quietly. Around four minutes.","From the first book of études, published in 1833 and dedicated to Liszt, written when Chopin was in his early twenties and newly settled in Paris. Before these, an étude was a technical exercise nobody performed in public. Chopin's twenty-four are exercises that are also concert pieces, each isolating one physical problem, and they redefined what piano teaching material could be.","Chopin reportedly told his pupil Adolf Gutmann that he had never in his life written another melody as beautiful — an unusually direct statement from a composer who almost never talked about his own music that way. The technical trap is hidden in plain hearing: what sounds like a simple tune requires one hand to hold a singing line steady while the other fingers of the same hand keep moving.","The tune has had a long afterlife in popular song under titles Chopin never authorised, including \"Tristesse\" and \"So Deep Is the Night\", which has coloured how it is played. The standard complaint among pianists is tempo: the marking is slow but not funereal, and the sentimental tradition drags it well below what the notation asks. The middle section is technically the hardest passage in the set for many hands.","Listen for the inner voices under the melody rather than the melody itself; once you hear them the piece changes. Then brace for the middle section, which is nobody's idea of a love song. Compare a straightforward tempo with a slow, indulgent one, and Chopin's other slow étude, Op. 25 No. 7, for the same idea taken further."],"chopin-fantaisie-impromptu":["Five minutes in three panels. The outer sections set the right hand running in groups of four notes against the left hand's groups of three, so the two hands never quite line up and the texture shimmers rather than clicks. Between them sits a broad, slow melody in a warm major key, the most quoted tune Chopin wrote. The fast music returns, and in the final bars the slow tune reappears deep in the bass while the running figure continues above it.","Written in 1834, when Chopin was twenty-four and at the centre of Parisian salon life, and then withheld: he never published it and left instructions that his unpublished manuscripts be destroyed. His friend and copyist Julian Fontana ignored the instruction and issued it in 1855, six years after Chopin's death, which is why a piece composed near the start of his career carries a high posthumous opus number.","Why he suppressed it is unsettled. One long-standing theory notes a resemblance to an impromptu by Moscheles, a composer Chopin respected, and suggests he did not want to appear to be borrowing. A manuscript he inscribed to a Baroness d'Este later surfaced with readings that differ from the published text, and a few pianists now play that version instead — the piece exists in two forms.","Fontana's editorial decisions on the posthumous works are still argued over, since he sometimes smoothed what he could not read. The slow melody was fitted with words in 1917 as \"I'm Always Chasing Rainbows\" and became a popular standard, which cemented the piece's reputation as Chopin's most sentimental. Scholarly opinion has warmed as the alternative manuscript has been studied.","Try to hear the two hands as separate rhythms rather than one blur; the effect is deliberate. Wait for the slow tune to come back in the bass at the very end, which most listeners miss entirely. Compare the published version with a recording of the d'Este manuscript to hear a familiar piece rewritten in small but audible ways."],"chopin-revolutionary-etude":["One dissonant chord, then a cascade down the whole keyboard, and the left hand begins a torrent of rapid notes that does not stop for nearly three minutes. Above it, the right hand hammers out a jagged dotted figure like a shouted slogan. The piece rises to two climaxes, sinks briefly to a hush, and finishes with both hands sweeping down the keyboard in unison into two hard closing chords. Under three minutes, and unrelenting.","Chopin left Warsaw in November 1830, weeks before the November Uprising against Russian rule. He was travelling through Stuttgart in September 1831 when news arrived that Warsaw had fallen and Russian troops had taken the city; the pages of his private notebook from those days are frantic. He never returned to Poland. The étude was published in 1833 as the last of his first book.","The virtuosity is entirely in the wrong hand: the study is for the left, which plays continuous fast figuration for the entire piece, while the right hand's part — the one that carries the tune and the drama — is comparatively easy. Chopin gave the melodic role to the hand that is not being trained, which is why the étude sounds like rage rather than practice.","The nickname came later, not from Chopin, and the Stuttgart story cannot be verified; sketches suggest material may predate the news. Scholars are wary of the whole heroic narrative, which was assembled in the nineteenth century by writers who wanted Chopin to be a national symbol. What is certain is that Polish audiences have heard it that way for nearly two centuries, and still do.","Listen only to the left hand for one hearing — it never rests, and that is the piece. Then listen to the right and notice how little it actually does. Compare a fast, dry reading with a broad, weighty one; the first sounds like panic, the second like defiance, and the notes are the same."],"chopin-raindrop-prelude":["The longest of the twenty-four preludes, at around five or six minutes. One repeated note beats steadily from the first bar to the last, never varying its pulse. The outer sections are serene, the note sitting inside gentle chords; in the middle the music darkens to the minor, the repeated note moves into the bass, and heavy chords pile above it until the effect is oppressive rather than soothing. Then the opening returns and the beating stops.","Written during the winter of 1838 and 1839 in a disused Carthusian monastery at Valldemossa on Mallorca, where Chopin had gone with George Sand and her children for his health. It went badly: the weather was foul, he was seriously ill with the tuberculosis that would kill him, the locals were hostile once his condition was known, and his Pleyel piano was stuck for weeks in customs at Palma.","Sand described returning from Palma through rain to find Chopin at the piano, saying he had seen himself drowned. She also recorded that he objected furiously to hearing his music as imitation of nature — he disliked the raindrop reading and the nickname is not his. The twenty-four preludes follow Bach in visiting every key, but Bach supplied fugues; Chopin supplies fragments and stops.","Schumann described the set as sketches, ruins and eagle feathers, and could not decide whether it was a work or a collection. That argument persists: some pianists play all twenty-four as one forty-minute arc, others treat them as an anthology to be raided. This prelude, the longest and most self-contained, is the one most often extracted, which is exactly what the cycle's defenders object to.","Fix on the repeated note and follow it through the whole piece; it is the same pulse in every section, only its surroundings change. Notice the moment it moves into the bass and the music turns menacing. Then hear all twenty-four preludes in order, which makes this one sound quite different — less a picture, more a breakdown."],"chopin-waltz-op-64-no-2":["Three kinds of music alternating. A wistful minor-key tune in a hesitant triple step opens; a faster whirling section follows, all sixteenth notes and forward motion; then a slower, warmer melody in the major arrives like a memory of a better evening. The three return in rotation, the fast music having the last word before a quiet close. Around four minutes, and never as simple rhythmically as it sounds — the phrases keep pulling against the beat.","Published in 1847, in the last set of waltzes to appear in Chopin's lifetime; he had two years left and was gravely ill. That year his long relationship with George Sand ended in bitterness, and the Paris salon world that had sustained him was disrupted by the revolutions of the following year. The waltz was by then the dominant social dance of Europe, and its music a mass commodity.","The fast section is not a new idea: it is the opening tune's shape, compressed and accelerated, which is why the piece feels obsessive rather than varied. Chopin's waltzes were never intended for dancing — the rhythmic irregularities and rubato make them unusable on a ballroom floor. What they portray is the dance seen from the edge of the room by someone who is not participating.","Over-recording has flattened it into a chocolate-box favourite, and the standard complaint is excessive rubato applied to a piece already built on rhythmic ambiguity. Nineteenth-century accounts of Chopin's own playing describe a flexibility that his pupils insisted was governed by a strict underlying pulse, a discipline most recordings ignore. Period-instrument performances tend to be brisker and less indulgent.","Listen for the relationship between the slow opening tune and the fast section — same shape, different speed. Try to conduct along and notice where the phrasing resists you. Then compare the Op. 64 No. 1, the so-called Minute Waltz, from the same set: two views of the same room, one delighted and one not."],"chopin-nocturne-c-op-posth":["Four minutes marked to be played slowly and with great expression. A rocking left hand supports an operatic melody in the right, which enters almost hesitantly and gradually accumulates decoration. Twice the music breaks into free, unmeasured runs that hang outside the pulse altogether. Near the end a quicker, brighter fragment appears and then dissolves, and the piece drifts to a stop on quiet repeated chords, more unfinished than concluded.","Written in 1830, when Chopin was twenty and about to leave Warsaw for good, and not published until 1870, twenty-one years after his death. He gave it to his older sister Ludwika, inscribing it as an exercise to be played before attempting his concerto — so it was conceived as private family music, never as a public piece, and carries no opus number of his own choosing.","It quotes him: the melody folds in material from the slow movement of his F minor piano concerto, written at the same time, and listeners also hear a phrase from one of his early Polish songs. So the piece functions as a private notebook of what he was writing that year, assembled for one player who would recognise all of it.","The pianist Władysław Szpilman played it in the final Polish Radio broadcast before German bombing silenced the station in September 1939, and again when Polish Radio resumed after the war — a coincidence of programming that gave the piece a second life as a national memory, and that Roman Polański's film The Pianist put in front of a world audience. Editions still disagree on its title and tempo.","Listen for the free runs that float outside the beat, and let them sound improvised. Then hear the slow movement of the F minor concerto and catch the borrowing. Compare a violin-and-piano arrangement, which is how many people first meet it, with the original — the piano version is stranger and less consoling."],"chopin-piano-concerto-no-1-romance":["The concerto's slow movement, about ten minutes long. Muted strings set a hushed background and the piano enters with a long, nocturne-like melody, elaborated with the same filigree Chopin used in his solo pieces. A central passage turns more agitated, the piano declaiming in free rhetorical phrases over tremolo strings, before the opening returns. The orchestra is deliberately kept quiet throughout; the bassoon and horn supply most of what colour there is.","Chopin, twenty, premiered the concerto at the National Theatre in Warsaw on 11 October 1830, in the last concert he gave in Poland; he left the country weeks later and never came back. He wrote to his friend Tytus Woyciechowski that the movement was meant to give the impression of a reverie in fine spring weather, but by moonlight — an unusually specific piece of self-description from him.","The two concertos were composed in the reverse order of their numbering, so this is the later of the pair despite being called the first. Listen for how the orchestra is used: it does almost nothing but hold the light, which is a deliberate strategy rather than incompetence. Chopin frequently performed the concertos in salons with only a string quintet accompanying him.","The orchestration has been criticised since the nineteenth century, and several composers — Klindworth, Tausig, Balakirev among them — rewrote it. That fashion has now largely reversed: scholars argue the thin scoring is a deliberate frame for the piano, and chamber versions with string quintet, which Chopin himself sanctioned, have become common in performance and on record.","Listen for the mutes on the strings at the start, which set the whole movement's light level. Follow the piano's decoration and notice how close it is to the nocturnes. Then try a chamber-sized version with string quintet, or a period-piano recording, and the orchestration problem largely disappears."],"chopin-scherzo-no-2":["It opens with a muttered three-note figure, barely audible, answered instantly by a huge fortissimo chord — a question and a slammed door, repeated. From there a soaring melody in a warm major key takes over, and the piece expands into a ten-minute structure with a long central section in a distant key, a written-out development, and a coda that accelerates to a headlong finish. The dynamic range is enormous, from whispered to violent, often within one bar.","Composed in 1837, five years into Chopin's Paris career. A scherzo had been, since Beethoven, the fast third movement of a symphony or sonata, and the word itself means joke. Chopin detached it, expanded it to ten minutes, and removed everything jocular, producing a large single-movement form with no precedent — one of four he wrote, none of them remotely comic.","Chopin told his pupil Wilhelm von Lenz that the opening figure had to sound like a question, and complained that nobody ever played it quietly enough; Lenz recorded the composer describing it in funereal terms. The whole piece hinges on that dynamic: if the first bars are audible enough to be comfortable, the answering chord loses its violence and the drama collapses in the first ten seconds.","Schumann, reviewing the earlier First Scherzo, asked how gravity should clothe itself if jest goes about in dark veils — a remark that has attached itself to the whole set. The scherzos were slower than the ballades to gain critical respect, being read as brilliant but structurally loose; twentieth-century analysis has shown how tightly the Second is built. It is now a competition warhorse.","Judge the performance in the first five seconds: the opening should be almost inaudible. Then wait for the big major-key melody and hear how much relief it brings. Rubinstein, Argerich and Cortot take three different views of the coda's speed, and the piece can survive being played faster than seems sane."],"chopin-mazurka-op-17-no-4":["Four bars of quiet chords that decline to say what key the piece is in, then a drooping melody in a limping triple step, its phrases falling and repeating like something half-remembered. A brighter major-key middle section offers a brief clearing. The opening returns, and at the close the same undeclared chords come back and simply stop, unresolved — one of the earliest pieces in Western music to end without settling on its home chord.","Published in 1833, part of the fifty-odd mazurkas Chopin wrote from adolescence until the last months of his life. The mazurka is not one dance but a family of them from the Mazovia region — the mazur, oberek and kujawiak, at different speeds — and Chopin folded all three into a single artistic form. He had left Poland in 1830, and the mazurkas became the running record of a country he could only remember.","The melody uses a raised fourth degree drawn from the scales of Polish highland music, which is why the tune keeps sounding slightly off-centre to ears trained on Western major and minor. Underneath, open fifths in the left hand imitate the drone of village bagpipes. The unresolved opening and closing frame is not a mistake: the piece begins and ends in the same state of not having arrived.","Schumann admired the mazurkas above almost everything else Chopin wrote. The performance problem is rhythmic: contemporaries reported that Chopin's mazurka playing lengthened the second or third beat in ways that could not be notated, and that visiting musicians accused him of playing in the wrong metre. Recordings by Rubinstein, Ignaz Friedman and Polish pianists preserve competing versions of that inflection.","Listen to the first four bars twice and notice you cannot tell where the music is. Then follow the limp in the rhythm — feel which beat is being leaned on. Friedman's old recording and Rubinstein's later ones disagree completely about that lean, and hearing both teaches more about rubato than any explanation."],"schumann-carnaval-excerpts":["Twenty-one linked miniatures, most of them under a minute, run past like a masked ball. Pierrot shuffles, Arlequin leaps, Chopin is caricatured in a nocturne of his own manner, Paganini in a scampering interlude. Schumann's two invented selves appear by name: the dreamy Eusebius in slow, blurred rhythm, the headlong Florestan broken off mid-phrase. It ends with a March of the League of David against the Philistines, in triple time, that sweeps the whole cast back on stage.","Schumann was twenty-four, a Leipzig critic building a magazine, the Neue Zeitschrift für Musik, as a weapon against what he called philistine taste. He was briefly engaged to Ernestine von Fricken, a fellow pupil of his piano teacher, whose Bohemian home town supplied the piece's letter-game. The League of David in the finale was his imaginary brotherhood of true artists; the Philistines they march against were the fashionable virtuosos and comfortable reviewers filling German concert rooms.","Between two of the numbers sits a page headed Sphinxes: three small groups of notes, printed without rhythm and generally not played, spelling A-S-C-H — Asch, Ernestine's town, and the only letters of his own surname that are also note-names. Nearly every piece is built from that cell, transposed and hidden inside the tunes. The closing march sets the Grossvatertanz, a seventeenth-century wedding tune, as the Philistines' plodding anthem.","Early listeners were baffled: a sequence of fragments where a sonata was expected. The cycle entered the repertoire slowly, carried in recital by Liszt, and only later became a pianist's proving ground. Arguments today are practical — whether to play all twenty-one numbers or a selection, whether to sound the Sphinxes in low octaves as a few pianists do, and how literally to take Schumann's whiplash changes of tempo.","Start with Eusebius and Florestan back to back: one composer heard as two people, first hesitant, then reckless. Then listen for the four-note cell surfacing at the head of almost every number. Rachmaninoff's 1929 recording is fast and hard-edged; Arrau and Michelangeli weigh the fragments more gravely. Follow it with Kinderszenen for the same imagination at rest."],"schumann-dichterliebe-im-wunderschonen-monat-mai":["Ninety seconds, and the opening of a cycle of sixteen Heine settings. The piano begins alone with a rocking figure whose top notes lean and sigh; the voice enters almost conversationally to say that in the loveliest month of May, when all the buds were bursting, love rose up in his heart. Two short verses, the same music twice. The piano is left holding an unresolved chord that simply stops, as if the sentence were unfinished.","1840 was Schumann's year of song: after a four-year court fight with Clara's father, Friedrich Wieck, he married her in September and wrote well over a hundred songs in twelve months. He chose twenty poems from Heine's Lyrisches Intermezzo and published sixteen, cutting four. Heine's verse suited him because it undercuts its own tenderness; the poems begin in blossom and end, sixteen songs later, with the poet sinking his love and his songs into the sea.","The song never states its home key plainly. It hovers between two chords, F sharp minor and A major, touching both and settling on neither, and the final chord is a dominant seventh that asks a question the next song answers. Schumann effectively writes a beginning with no beginning and an end with no end — a device singers and pianists must resist smoothing over by adding weight to the last note.","Judgment has shifted from the singer to the piano: nineteenth-century audiences heard a charming opening number, while modern criticism treats the piano's unresolved harmony as one of the hinge moments of Romantic song. Performance debates concern the original high keys against transposed ones, whether a tenor or a baritone owns the cycle, and how much of Heine's irony a singer should let show this early.","Play it twice: once for the voice, once listening only to the pianist's right hand, which sighs downward each time the singer rises. Then let the second song begin — the resolution arrives a bar into it. Fritz Wunderlich is warm and open, Dietrich Fischer-Dieskau (with Jörg Demus or Alfred Brendel) far more suspicious of the words. Compare with Schubert's Gretchen am Spinnrade for the older way of setting longing."],"schumann-piano-concerto":["One orchestral chord, then the piano tumbles down the keyboard in a cascade of chords, and the oboe states a falling theme that will govern everything. The first movement is long, discursive and full of duets between piano and single wind instruments rather than contests. A short Intermezzo follows, the piano trading two-note phrases with the cellos; it recalls the oboe theme and runs without a break into a finale in three-time whose main tune is the same theme turned dancing.","Schumann had abandoned several concertos before this one. He distrusted the touring virtuoso's concerto, with its display and its orchestral accompaniment, and wrote in his criticism that he wanted something between symphony and sonata. He composed the first movement in 1841 as a self-contained Phantasie for Clara; publishers would not take a one-movement piece, so in 1845 in Dresden he added the other two. Clara played the premiere in Dresden in December 1845.","Almost nothing here is new after the first minute. The oboe's falling four notes become the slow A flat major episode, the march-like passage, the clarinet solo, the Intermezzo's recollection and finally the finale's leaping tune — the same shape wearing different clothes. The finale's other trick is metrical: the second theme is written in triple time but accented so persistently across the bar that orchestras have come off the rails in it for a century and a half.","Critics who wanted fireworks found it undramatic; the Leipzig press was cool about the soloist's lack of opportunities to shine. Its reputation rose as taste turned against display, and it became the model for the lyrical concerto — Grieg's is a direct descendant. Current argument is mostly about tempo relations between the three movements and whether the finale's cross-accents should be pointed up or smoothed into an easy swing.","Listen for the oboe in the first fifteen seconds after the piano's crash, then track that shape through the piece; by the finale you are hearing it dance. The Intermezzo is the way in if the first movement feels rambling. Try Clara Haskil or Dinu Lipatti for restraint, Martha Argerich for heat, and compare with Grieg's concerto, which copies the opening gesture almost exactly."],"schumann-traumerei":["Twenty-four bars, the seventh of the thirteen Scenes from Childhood. A single melodic idea — a rising leap that then curls slowly downward — is stated four times over spread chords in the left hand, each time set against different harmony, each time ending in a different place. The texture is four voices moving like a small choir rather than tune-and-accompaniment. The last phrase hangs on a held chord before the softest possible close.","Schumann wrote the set in Leipzig in 1838, in the middle of the long separation from Clara, while planning a move to Vienna that failed. He drafted about thirty short pieces and selected thirteen. He was explicit that these were not pieces for children to play but an adult's backward glance, and told Clara she had once said he sometimes seemed like a child to her. Titles were added after the music, as labels rather than programmes.","The trick is harmonic. The same eight notes of melody appear in every phrase, but the chord underneath shifts each time, so the tune seems to be searching for a home it keeps missing; the second phrase reaches toward the flat side and darkens the whole piece for a moment. The written pauses matter as much as the notes — Schumann marks a fermata over silence, asking the player to stop time rather than fill it.","Sentimental performance nearly ruined it: for decades it was played with heavy rubato and treated as a drawing-room trifle, and serious pianists avoided it. Its standing recovered through players who took the four-part writing literally. It carries a second life outside music, played at Russian war memorials and chosen by Vladimir Horowitz as the encore at his 1986 return to Moscow, where the audience was audibly in tears.","Follow the inner voices rather than the top line — the piece is a quartet, and the alto part moves most. Notice how the fourth statement resolves differently from the first three. Horowitz's Moscow encore is the famous document; Martha Argerich and Clara Haskil play the whole set with less pathos. Then hear the neighbouring scenes, especially the frightened Curious Story and the closing Poet Speaks."],"schumann-fantasie-c-first-movement":["The instruction at the head of the score asks for playing that is fantastic and passionate throughout. It begins in mid-air: the left hand surges in continuous waves while the right sings a broad descending melody that never settles on the home chord for pages. The middle of the movement drops into a slower, stiff-jointed episode marked to be played in the tone of a legend, archaic and bare. Then the wave returns, and the ending is quiet, slow and resigned.","Schumann composed it in 1836, the year Friedrich Wieck forced Clara and him apart and intercepted their letters. It was offered as a contribution to the fund for a Beethoven monument in Bonn, and drafts carry grandiose titles — Ruins, Trophies, Palms — later dropped. He dedicated the published score to Liszt, who returned the compliment seventeen years later by dedicating his B minor Sonata to Schumann. The epigraph is four lines of Friedrich Schlegel about a quiet tone audible only to whoever listens in secret.","The last page quotes Beethoven. The melody that arrives in the closing bars is taken from the final song of An die ferne Geliebte, to the words asking the distant beloved to accept these songs — a message to Clara concealed inside a memorial to Beethoven. Schumann later admitted the whole movement was a lament for her. The famous falling motif that opens the movement is the same phrase in disguise, heard long before it is identified.","Clara found it hard to grasp on first acquaintance and said she could not always follow where it was going; the piece was slow to be played and slower to be understood, since it obeys sonata outlines while constantly refusing to confirm them. A manuscript surfaced in Budapest showing that the finale originally ended by recalling the Beethoven quotation, and some pianists now restore that ending, which changes the balance of the whole work.","Give the first minute your whole attention: the harmony under the opening tune is unstable, and that instability is the subject. Then wait for the tone to change at the legend-like middle section. Sviatoslav Richter and Murray Perahia are contrasting guides, one severe, the other singing. Compare with Liszt's B minor Sonata, its dedicatory twin, and with Beethoven's song cycle for the quotation."],"schumann-widmung":["Two and a half minutes in three panels. The outer sections are headlong: the piano throws up rolling chords and the voice declares that you are my soul, my heart, my rapture, my pain, the world in which I live. The middle turns to a slower, hymn-like calm — you are peace, you are given to me from heaven — before the opening rushes back. The piano finishes alone with a quiet coda that arrives from somewhere else entirely.","The song opens Myrthen, a set of twenty-six settings bound in leather and given to Clara on the eve of their wedding in September 1840; myrtle is the traditional German bridal flower. The poem is by Friedrich Rückert, a scholar-poet Schumann returned to often. The couple had married only after suing Clara's father for consent, and the songs were composed while the case was still running through the Leipzig courts.","The postlude is a quotation: as the singer stops, the piano plays the melody of Schubert's Ave Maria, so that the song ends by blessing its dedicatee in someone else's voice. The two halves are also keyed against each other, the ardent outer sections and the still centre a third apart, so that the return sounds like waking. Liszt's piano transcription, still widely played, adds octaves and loses the quotation's plainness.","Widmung became the most performed number of a set otherwise neglected, and its popularity as a wedding piece has sometimes obscured how strange the ending is. Singers argue about the middle section's tempo — whether to make the contrast extreme or to keep one pulse throughout — and about the transcription question: Liszt's arrangement is often heard by people who have never heard the song, which reverses Schumann's intent.","Listen for the change of light at the middle section, then for the piano's last twenty seconds — the Schubert tune is unmistakable once you know it is there. Barbara Bonney, Fischer-Dieskau and Jonas Kaufmann all record it; hear one singer, then Liszt's piano version, and decide what the words were doing. Clara Schumann's own Rückert setting makes a fine companion."],"schumann-symphony-no-3-rhenish-first-movement":["No slow introduction: the movement begins already in motion, a wide leaping theme in the strings and horns whose accents pull against the three-beat bar so insistently that the metre is hard to feel for the first minute. The material is broad and open-air rather than argumentative, with horn calls cutting through repeatedly. The development thins out into a hushed passage for strings alone before four horns announce the return of the theme, one of the loudest homecomings Schumann wrote.","Schumann took the post of municipal music director in Düsseldorf in 1850, his first secure job, and wrote the symphony within weeks of arriving. The move down the Rhine was a fresh start after Dresden and the failed revolutions of 1848–49. The family made an excursion to Cologne and saw the unfinished cathedral, then a symbol of German unity under construction; the symphony's five movements include a solemn fourth inspired by a ceremony there.","The rhythmic ambiguity is built in: the melody's accents fall a beat early against the notated bar, so the orchestra is effectively playing two metres at once, which is why the theme sounds airborne rather than square. Schumann removed his descriptive titles before publication, wanting the piece heard as music rather than a Rhine journey; the nickname Rhenish was attached by others and has shaped how audiences hear it ever since.","Schumann's orchestration has been the standing controversy: Mahler rescored his symphonies, and generations of conductors doubled winds and thinned strings to clear the textures. That practice has been reversed by conductors using smaller orchestras and period brass, who argue the supposed thickness is a matter of balance and tempo, not of writing. The symphony is now generally the most performed of the four, and the least revised.","Count three beats against the opening theme and feel it refuse to line up — that friction is the whole character. Then wait for the horns at the recapitulation. Compare a modern orchestra under Bernstein or Sawallisch with a period-instrument reading by Gardiner or Heras-Casado to hear what the rescoring debate is about, and continue into the solemn fourth movement with its trombones."],"schumann-cello-concerto-first-movement":["Three soft wind chords, and the cello enters immediately with a long, falling melody that seems already halfway through a thought. There is no orchestral exposition and no display: the solo line sings almost continuously, high in its register for long stretches, while the orchestra supplies a light, mostly quiet frame. The movement never fully stops, running instead into the slow movement, so the concerto plays as one twenty-four minute arc in three connected panels.","Schumann drafted it in about two weeks in October 1850, weeks after arriving in Düsseldorf and immediately after the Rhenish Symphony. He called it a concert piece rather than a concerto, avoiding the word's associations with bravura. No cellist would take it up: publishers hesitated, revisions dragged on, and it was never played in public in his lifetime. The premiere came in Leipzig in 1860, four years after his death in the Endenich asylum.","The cadenza, when it arrives late in the finale, is accompanied — the orchestra keeps playing underneath, which removes the point of a cadenza as a solo stunt and was close to unheard of in 1850. In the slow movement the soloist is joined by a second cello drawn from the orchestra, turning the concerto momentarily into a duet. Schumann also asked for a lighter, gut-strung sound than modern cellos give, which is why balance is a perpetual problem.","Long considered awkward — badly laid out for the instrument, thickly accompanied — it was rescued in the twentieth century by Casals, Rostropovich and Jacqueline du Pré, who treated its inwardness as the point. Shostakovich made an orchestration; several cellists play their own retouched versions. Older writing that read the piece as evidence of mental decline has been largely abandoned, since the manuscript shows careful, lucid work.","Notice that the soloist begins before you expect and barely rests: this is a singer's part, not an athlete's. Listen for the second cello emerging in the slow movement. Du Pré with Barenboim is passionate; Steven Isserlis and Queyras play it leaner, with lighter orchestras. Set it beside Schumann's Piano Concerto, written for the same reason — to make a concerto that argues quietly."],"schumann-arabeske":["A rondo of about seven minutes built on a light, curling refrain in which the melody is threaded through a rippling middle register — the ornamental line of the title, drawn rather than declaimed. Two minor-key episodes interrupt it, the first grave and the second more agitated, each time answered by the refrain's return. Instead of a bright ending, a short coda marked as an epilogue slows everything down and closes in a mood entirely unlike the rest.","Schumann spent the winter of 1838–39 in Vienna, hoping to move his magazine there and establish himself well enough to marry Clara over her father's objection. The plan failed; Austrian censorship and the Viennese music trade defeated him. He wrote several deliberately approachable pieces that season, describing this one and its companion, the Blumenstück, as delicate and aimed at the amateur market, and privately conceding they were written to sell.","The epilogue is the whole point. Everything before it is charm; the last page changes the harmony's centre of gravity and ends the piece in a tone of quiet regret that retrospectively colours the refrain. Schumann marks it apart from the rondo in the score, effectively appending a private confession to a public trifle — the same doubled personality that runs through Carnaval, here compressed into thirty bars.","Dismissed for decades as salon music, and Schumann's own marketing description has been used against it. Later pianists took the coda seriously and the piece has been reassessed as one of his most concentrated designs. The chief interpretive question is tempo: played briskly it is decorative, played slowly it becomes a study in melancholy, and recordings differ by nearly two minutes over seven.","Ignore the refrain the first time and wait for the last minute — then start again and hear the refrain differently. Listen for how the tune hides in the middle of the texture rather than sitting on top. Radu Lupu and Mitsuko Uchida take it slowly and inwardly; Horowitz is quicker and more decorative. Pair it with Träumerei for the same voice at the same scale."],"liszt-campanella":["Five minutes of bells. The right hand strikes single high notes over and over at the top of the keyboard while leaping down to catch the melody and back up again, jumps of two octaves at speed, over a light left-hand accompaniment. A middle section adds trills and rapid repeated notes played by alternating fingers on one key; the last page runs the tune in octaves and glittering scales, then vanishes upward. The bell-note is present in nearly every bar.","Liszt heard Paganini in Paris in 1832 and set out to do on the piano what the violinist did on four strings. His first Paganini studies of 1838 were so difficult they were barely playable; by 1851 he had retired from touring, settled in Weimar as court music director, and revised the whole set into the Grandes études de Paganini, thinning the textures so that a very good pianist, rather than only Liszt, could manage them.","The theme is not Liszt's: it is the rondo of Paganini's Second Violin Concerto, where a small bell on the orchestra's percussion table answers the soloist, which is what the piano's repeated high notes imitate. Liszt keeps the bell audible even when the hand is two octaves away, so the pianist must jump and land silently. The 1838 version, still occasionally recorded, is harder and less effective — proof that he learned to cut.","For a century it was treated as a stunt and programmed as an encore, and critics of the Weimar circle used it as evidence that Liszt was a showman rather than a composer. Recorded competition has changed its meaning: it became a benchmark of accuracy, played faster and cleaner each decade, which arguably obscures the piece's lightness. Scholars now emphasise the 1851 revision as an act of compositional editing, not dilution.","Listen for the single repeated high note and hear how rarely it stops — everything else moves around it. Watch a video if you can; the leaps are half the drama. Compare Paganini's original concerto finale first, then hear the piano version. Recordings by Cziffra and Kissin lean on brilliance, while Berman and Bolet keep the bell delicate."],"liszt-hungarian-rhapsody-no-2":["Two halves, following the shape of the Hungarian recruiting dance. The slow first part is heavy and declamatory, full of grace-note snaps and long pauses, imitating a band's cimbalom and clarinet. Then the fast half arrives and accelerates without ever quite stopping: repeated chords, hand-crossing, a whirling tune that returns faster each time, and a headlong final page. Many pianists insert a cadenza before the close, some of them written by other players.","Liszt collected the tunes on his tours through Hungary in the 1840s, mostly from urban Roma bands playing in cafés and at gentry gatherings rather than from villages. He was a national hero there — given a jewelled sword in 1840 — though he spoke little Hungarian and lived in Weimar. He published nineteen rhapsodies; this second one was dedicated to Count László Teleki, and later arranged for orchestra with his assistant Franz Doppler.","Liszt believed he was preserving Roma music and said so in a book that caused lasting offence; Bartók and Kodály later showed that these were composed popular songs in the verbunkos style, played by Roma musicians, not an ancient ethnic repertory. The cadenza slot near the end is genuinely open — Liszt left space, and Rachmaninoff, Horowitz and Cziffra all supplied their own, so no two performances end alike.","Nineteenth-century Hungary embraced it; twentieth-century folk scholarship dismantled its ethnographic claims, and Bartók's field recordings made Liszt's sources look like café repertory. The piece then survived as pure theatre, helped by cartoons — the Tom and Jerry short The Cat Concerto took an Academy Award in 1947 and Bugs Bunny's Rhapsody Rabbit used the same music — which is how most people first meet it, playing on a cartoon grand piano.","Feel the two-part shape: everything before the change of speed is preparation. Listen for the imitation of the cimbalom, a struck dulcimer, in the fast half's rattling repeated notes. Cziffra's recordings are the wildest, Horowitz's the most manipulated. Then hear Bartók's Romanian Folk Dances to understand what a different approach to the same region sounds like."],"liszt-liebestraum-no-3":["The melody sits in the middle of the keyboard and is passed between the hands while a continuous ripple of broken chords surrounds it above and below, so the tune seems to come from inside the sound rather than on top of it. It rises twice to a climax, and each time the flood is interrupted by a fast unaccompanied cadenza that hangs in the air. After the second, the music returns quietly and closes with a slow, spread final chord.","Liszt published three Liebesträume in 1850, all of them piano versions of songs he had written a few years earlier. This one sets a poem by Ferdinand Freiligrath that instructs the reader to love as long as you can love, because the hour will come when you stand at graves and mourn — the words are a warning about harsh speech to the living, not a serenade. He was by then living in Weimar with Princess Carolyne von Sayn-Wittgenstein.","The piece is a song without its words, and the words are grim. Knowing the poem changes where the climaxes point: the second peak lands on the line about the grave. Liszt also changes key twice as the melody passes hands, so that the same tune is heard through three different lights. In the song version the singer's line is simpler; the piano version's decoration is what listeners now remember as the tune.","Its ubiquity has cost it: taught to intermediate pianists, used as film shorthand for romance, and rarely programmed by major recitalists, it is usually filed under salon music. Defenders point out the harmonic scheme and the cadenzas' structural placement, and note that the original song is almost never performed, which leaves the piece stranded halfway between two genres and judged as neither.","Listen for the moment the melody moves from one hand to the other and the key shifts under it. Then find a recording of the song, O lieb, so lang du lieben kannst, and hear the poem — it reframes everything. Jorge Bolet and Claudio Arrau play it without sugar. Schumann's Widmung, which Liszt also transcribed, makes the natural companion."],"liszt-piano-sonata-b":["Thirty minutes without a break. It opens with two quiet descending scales low on the keyboard, then two ideas that will drive everything: a hammering repeated-note figure and a huge leaping octave phrase. What follows works as one sonata movement and as four linked movements at once — an allegro, a slow section with a broad singing theme, a fugue that starts almost comically dry, and a return. It ends with three soft chords and one very low, very quiet note.","Liszt finished the score in Weimar in February 1853. He had given up the touring life in 1847, was running the court's opera and orchestra, and was writing his symphonic poems in the same years. He dedicated it to Robert Schumann, repaying the dedication of the Fantasie in C; Schumann was by then in the asylum at Endenich and probably never saw it. Hans von Bülow gave the first public performance in Berlin in 1857.","Every theme in the sonata comes from the handful of ideas heard in the first ninety seconds. The hammering motif becomes, at different points, a threat, a fugue subject and a hymn; the leaping octaves turn into the great lyrical tune of the slow section. Liszt called this thematic transformation, and the sonata is its proof. The manuscript shows he first wrote a loud ending, crossed it out, and substituted the quiet close that now makes the piece.","Reception split along partisan lines. Eduard Hanslick attacked it, Clara Schumann called it merely noise in her diary, and Brahms is reported to have dozed while Liszt played it through; Wagner and Bülow championed it. It took thirty years to become standard repertoire and is now regarded as the central Romantic piano work after Beethoven. The old programmatic readings — Faust, Milton's Paradise Lost, the Fall — remain speculative, none confirmed by Liszt.","Memorise the two opening ideas, then simply notice where they come back wearing different clothes; you will hear the whole architecture without needing the score. The fugue, roughly two-thirds through, is the moment the piece turns sardonic. Try Martha Argerich for velocity, Krystian Zimerman for structure, Claudio Arrau for weight. Then hear Schumann's Fantasie, its dedicatory twin."],"liszt-un-sospiro":["A wide, unbroken wash of broken chords sweeps the whole keyboard while a slow melody is picked out inside it — and the melody is played by the hands crossing over each other, so that a single line emerges from two alternating arms. The effect is of three hands. The tune moves gradually into more remote keys, a short cadenza breaks the surface near the end, and the final bars settle back into the rippling texture and dissolve.","The three concert studies date from the late 1840s, written as Liszt was leaving the touring circuit for Weimar; they are teaching pieces in the sense that each isolates one technical problem and builds an entire poem out of it. This one takes hand-crossing. The Italian and French nicknames attached to the set — this one means a sigh — were probably supplied by a publisher rather than by Liszt, whose own title was simply a study.","The melody notes are almost all played by whichever hand is momentarily free, which means the pianist's arms are continuously swapping registers; the physical choreography is inseparable from the sound, and Liszt notates it precisely. Listen for the sighing two-note figure, falling a step, that gives the nickname its logic and recurs at every phrase end. Liszt later wrote an alternative, harder ending, printed in some editions and rarely used.","It has been treated mainly as a display item and an examination piece, which undersells the harmonic writing in the middle, where the key drifts a long way from home before finding its way back. Performance argument concerns pedalling: too much blurs the melody into the arpeggios, too little exposes the seams between hands. Recording engineers face the same problem, which is why some famous accounts sound washed out.","Try to hear which hand is carrying the tune at any moment — you generally cannot, which is the achievement. Then listen for the falling two-note sigh at the end of each phrase. Earl Wild and Jorge Bolet are unhurried and clear; Lang Lang is more theatrical. Compare with Ravel's Jeux d'eau to hear where this kind of keyboard writing went next."],"liszt-consolation-no-3":["Four minutes, marked to be played slowly and placidly. The left hand rocks a low bass note and a soft chord in steady triplets throughout, almost without variation; above it a simple melody unfolds in long notes, decorated only lightly. Halfway, the harmony darkens briefly and the melody climbs, then subsides. There is no climax and no display anywhere in the piece — the loudest marking is moderate, and it ends more quietly than it began.","Liszt assembled six Consolations in Weimar around 1849–50, taking the title from a collection of poems by Sainte-Beuve. He was living openly with Princess Carolyne von Sayn-Wittgenstein, whose divorce case would occupy Rome for a decade, and was writing music of two quite opposite kinds at once — the Sonata and the symphonic poems on one hand, and small consoling pieces like these on the other, meant for domestic pianos.","The model is unmistakable: the rocking left hand, the key and the shape of the tune are Chopin's D flat Nocturne, written a decade earlier by a friend who had died in 1849. Liszt published a book about Chopin the following year. Read that way the piece is an act of mourning rather than a study in style, which explains why it refuses the ornamental flourishes Chopin himself would have added.","Its simplicity has kept it in the shadow of the flashier Liszt and made it a staple of teaching anthologies. Critics who read Liszt as a showman tend to treat it as an anomaly; those who take the late piano music seriously hear it as an early sign of the stripped-back style of his final years. Performances split between the sentimental and the almost expressionless, and the piece survives both.","Listen to the left hand alone for a minute — it barely changes, and that steadiness is the consolation being offered. Then follow the melody's one climb and its retreat. Play Chopin's D flat Nocturne straight afterwards and the resemblance becomes a conversation. Recordings by Bolet, Arrau and Kempff are unhurried; avoid anything that pushes the tempo."],"liszt-mephisto-waltz-no-1":["It begins by tuning up: bare stacked fifths piled higher and higher, as if a fiddle were being screwed into pitch. Then a rough village waltz starts and gathers speed, interrupted by a slower, sweeter episode where a single melodic line spins out over trills — the seduction at the centre. The dance returns wilder each time, with leaps, glissandos and hammered chords, and after a final quiet passage the piece breaks off in a violent gesture rather than an ending.","Liszt took his programme not from Goethe but from Nikolaus Lenau's Faust, and printed the relevant passage in the score. Faust and Mephistopheles come upon a wedding party in a village inn; Mephistopheles seizes a fiddle from the players and strikes up a dance that maddens the crowd; Faust whirls a dark-eyed village girl out of the door and into the woods, where a nightingale is singing. Liszt scored it for orchestra first, then made this piano version.","The stacked fifths at the opening are a literal joke — a violin's four open strings tuned in fifths, built into a chord no earlier composer would have written as a beginning. Near the end the music stops for a long trill high on the keyboard: that is the nightingale in Lenau's poem, and the two lovers have vanished. Liszt's final crash is the poem's line about the lovers being swallowed in the roaring sea of lust.","Nineteenth-century critics found it indecent as well as noisy, which suited Liszt, who had long cultivated the diabolical as a public persona. Twentieth-century opinion has divided between hearing a piece of theatre and hearing an experiment: the harmony at the opening and in the closing pages is genuinely advanced for 1860, and the piano version's textures anticipate Bartók's percussive keyboard writing.","Wait for the tuning-up chords to become a dance. Then listen for the change of character at the seduction episode, and for the nightingale trill in the last two minutes. Horowitz's version, in his own reworking, is the most notorious; Cziffra and Yuja Wang are equally fearless. Compare with Saint-Saëns's Danse macabre for the same devil in an orchestra."],"liszt-preludes":["Sixteen unbroken minutes in one movement, moving through four moods without pausing. Two plucked notes and a questioning three-note figure in the strings open it; that figure becomes a broad theme for cellos and horns, then a pastoral episode with solo oboe and horn calls, then a storm of rushing strings and brass, and finally the same theme turned into a full-orchestra march with trumpets and cymbals. The last minute is the opening question answered at maximum volume.","Liszt directed the Weimar court orchestra from 1848 and used it as a laboratory. He invented the term symphonic poem for these single-movement orchestral works, of which he wrote thirteen; this, the third, became by far the most played. He conducted the premiere in Weimar in February 1854. The whole genre was a challenge to the symphony: music shaped by an idea or a poem rather than by inherited movement schemes.","The famous preface — quoting Lamartine on life as a series of preludes to an unknown song — was attached after the music existed. The score began life as an overture to a set of choruses on poems by Joseph Autran, and the Lamartine text was probably fitted to it later, most likely by Princess Carolyne von Sayn-Wittgenstein. Everything you hear grows from the three-note figure in the first bars, stretched, inverted and finally militarised.","It was the most popular orchestral piece of Liszt's lifetime and then acquired a poisonous second history: German radio used the march music as the signature fanfare for wartime bulletins from the Russian front, and the association kept it out of concert programmes for a generation. It has also been mined by Hollywood, from Flash Gordon serials onward. Its rehabilitation rests on hearing it as the model for every later tone poem.","Fix the three rising notes in your head from the opening and then count how many disguises they wear — pastoral, stormy, martial. The march at the end is the same tune as the quiet question at the start. Karajan is grand, Masur clearer and swifter. Then hear Smetana's Vltava or Strauss's Don Juan to see the form Liszt invented being used by others."],"liszt-totentanz":["A set of variations on the plainchant Dies irae for piano and orchestra, about fifteen minutes long. It opens with the chant hammered out by brass, low strings and timpani while the piano crashes down in bare octaves. The variations range from grinding chordal assaults played with the full weight of the arms to a skeletal fugue, a passage of glassy high filigree and a long cadenza. The final variation drives the chant to a violent close in rushing octaves.","Liszt began it in 1849 in Weimar and revised it twice, publishing the final form in 1865, when Hans von Bülow — the dedicatee, and by then his son-in-law — gave the first performance in The Hague. Liszt said the idea came from the Campo Santo in Pisa, where a fourteenth-century fresco of the Triumph of Death shows riders confronting three open coffins; the medieval dance-of-death woodcut tradition, notably Holbein's, lies behind it too.","The chant Liszt hammers out is the Dies irae from the Requiem Mass, the same nine notes Berlioz had used to summon a witches' sabbath in 1830 — this piece is the moment the melody became Western music's all-purpose sign for death. Liszt's piano writing includes clusters struck with the flat of the hand and long stretches where the instrument is used percussively, closer to Bartók than to anything else in 1849.","Early audiences found it grotesque, and the work stayed on the fringe of the repertoire well into the twentieth century, dismissed alongside the Mephisto Waltz as diabolism for effect. Its stock rose when pianists and critics noticed how far ahead the sonorities were. Two versions circulate, the 1859 revision and an earlier one with a different variation scheme, and there is a solo-piano arrangement that removes the orchestra's snarl.","Learn the Dies irae tune first — a few seconds of plainchant — then everything that follows is legible. Listen for the moment the piano stops being a melodic instrument and becomes percussion. Byron Janis with Reiner, and Krystian Zimerman with Ozawa, are the classic accounts. Compare with Rachmaninoff, who used the same chant obsessively half a century later."],"wagner-ride-valkyries":["The curtain-raiser to the third act of Die Walküre. A galloping dotted rhythm starts low in the strings and spreads upward while horns and Wagner tubas hurl out a rising three-note call; brass pile in over tremolo strings until the whole orchestra is airborne. Eight Valkyries then shout greetings to each other across the stage — a whooping cry on the syllables Hojotoho — as they land on a rock with dead heroes slung over their saddles. In concert the voices are usually cut.","Wagner drafted the music in 1856 during his political exile in Switzerland, more than a decade before any part of the Ring reached the stage. Die Walküre was premiered in Munich in 1870 at the insistence of King Ludwig II, against Wagner's wish to withhold the cycle until the whole thing could be given in his own theatre; that theatre opened at Bayreuth in 1876 with the complete Ring, twenty-six years after he began the text.","The gallop is written so that the rhythm is passed around the orchestra rather than played by everyone at once, which is why it sounds like many horses rather than one big pulse. The Valkyries' cry is not decorative: it is built from the same rising interval as the ride motif, so the women and their horses share a melodic identity. The passage is also a rare moment of eight female soloists singing simultaneously in the entire cycle.","Wagner was unhappy about the excerpt's detachable success, since the whole point of the Ring was that nothing should be extractable. It became his most recognised music anyway, and its later history is inseparable from film: D. W. Griffith used it in 1915 and Coppola bolted it to the helicopter attack in Apocalypse Now in 1979, an association that now colours every hearing and has been read as a comment on Wagner's own appetite for spectacle.","Hear it once in a purely orchestral concert version and once with the singers, in a staged Walküre — the voices change it from a fanfare into a scene. Listen for the rhythm being handed between sections rather than pounded. Solti's Vienna Ring is the reference recording. Then hear the end of the same act, the Magic Fire Music, for what this violence subsides into."],"wagner-tristan-und-isolde-prelude":["Cellos alone climb a wide interval and stop; woodwinds answer with a chord that leans and then slips sideways into another chord instead of resolving. That two-part gesture, four bars and a silence, is the entire material. Over eleven minutes it is repeated at rising pitches, the silences filled in, the orchestra thickening, until a long ascent reaches a shattering climax and collapses. In concert the Prelude is normally joined directly to the opera's closing scene.","Wagner wrote it in 1857–59 while exiled in Zurich, living in a cottage on the estate of the silk merchant Otto Wesendonck and in love with Wesendonck's wife, Mathilde. He had put the Ring aside at the end of the second opera to write something he thought could be staged easily. It could not: Vienna abandoned the production after seventy-seven rehearsals, and the premiere waited until 1865 in Munich, paid for by King Ludwig II.","The second chord of the opera is the most analysed sound in Western music. It has been read as a French sixth, as a half-diminished seventh, as several appoggiaturas at once — the argument is unsettled because Wagner deliberately made the chord's function ambiguous. What matters aurally is that it resolves onto another dissonance. The opera then withholds a full, settled cadence for roughly four hours, releasing it only at the very last bars.","Hanslick and the Viennese conservatives treated it as the end of tonal order; Nietzsche adored it and then attacked himself for adoring it; Debussy quoted the opening in Golliwog's Cakewalk in 1908 with a mocking snigger written into the score. Modern scholarship has moved from asking what the chord is to asking what its ambiguity does — and performance argument now centres on tempo, since Wagner's slowness is what makes the withheld resolution ache.","Listen for the silence after the first phrase, and count how many times the same gesture returns before anything is allowed to settle. Then go straight to the opera's last five minutes and hear the resolution arrive. Furtwängler's 1952 recording with Flagstad is the historic account; Carlos Kleiber's is faster and more electric. Debussy's cakewalk makes a good joke afterwards."],"wagner-lohengrin-bridal-chorus":["The opening of the third act's second scene. After a boisterous orchestral prelude the women of the court sing the newlyweds into the bridal chamber, faithfully guided, in a steady march-tempo hymn with a rocking four-square tune; men's voices answer, the two choruses combine, and the procession files out leaving Lohengrin and Elsa alone. The famous melody is sung, not played, and lasts only a couple of minutes before the drama resumes.","Wagner completed Lohengrin in 1848 and then fled Dresden with a warrant out for his part in the May uprising of 1849. Unable to enter Germany, he asked Liszt to stage it; Liszt conducted the premiere at Weimar in August 1850 while Wagner sat in Switzerland reading reviews. He did not hear his own opera until 1861, in Vienna, eleven years after everyone else. The exile lasted until an amnesty allowed his return in 1862.","The chorus is a trap. Within minutes of this serene procession the marriage disintegrates: Elsa asks the forbidden question about her husband's name and origin, and by the end of the act he has left her. Wagner wrote a wedding hymn whose dramatic function is to precede a catastrophe. The music also carries the opera's tonal symbolism — the bright key of the human court, against the shimmering high strings that represent the Grail.","Its afterlife is entirely detached from the opera. Princess Victoria, Queen Victoria's eldest daughter, used it at her wedding in 1858 and paired it with Mendelssohn's Wedding March for the exit, fixing a template that most of the English-speaking world still follows. Many churches now decline it, citing its theatrical origin, its unhappy plot, or Wagner's antisemitism; the tune survives all of it, largely unrecognised as Wagner.","Hear it in context, in a staged or recorded Lohengrin, and stay for the next ten minutes — the collapse is the point. Then listen to the opera's Prelude, high strings alone, for the sound Wagner really invented here. Rudolf Kempe's and Claudio Abbado's recordings both give the chorus its ceremonial weight without slowing it to a crawl."],"wagner-siegfried-s-funeral-march":["Siegfried has been speared in the back and dies on stage; the lights fail, mist rises, and the orchestra plays for eight minutes while his body is carried away. Two timpani strokes and a low tread begin it. Then the brass deliver a series of great slow melodies one after another, each rising higher, with cymbal and drum crashes between them, before the music sinks back to the opening tread and moves without a break into the next scene.","This is the third act of Götterdämmerung, the last of the four Ring operas, composed in the early 1870s at Wahnfried, the house in Bayreuth built with King Ludwig's money while the Festspielhaus was going up on the hill. The complete cycle was first given there in August 1876, twenty-eight years after Wagner began writing the poem, before an audience that included Kaiser Wilhelm I, Liszt, Tchaikovsky, Grieg and Bruckner.","It is an obituary told in motifs rather than words. The melodies are, in order, the ones associated with Siegfried's murdered parents and their love, the sword, Siegfried's horn call and finally Brünnhilde — so the orchestra recites his ancestry and his life to a listener who has learned the vocabulary over fifteen hours. Nothing is new; the power comes entirely from what the themes have already meant.","It became the standard music for state mourning in Germany and beyond, played at Wagner's own funeral and at countless others, which has embedded it in political ceremony of every kind. Critics have long argued whether the passage is genuinely tragic or an act of self-aggrandisement on the hero's behalf, and whether extracting it for concerts destroys its meaning, since without the preceding hours the motifs are only tunes.","Do not start here. Hear the horn call in the earlier operas first, then this, and the march becomes almost unbearable. Listen for the two drum strokes that frame the whole passage. Solti, Böhm and Karajan all recorded complete cycles; if you want it alone, an orchestral Ring compilation works, but the effect is a fraction of the real thing."],"wagner-tannhauser-overture":["Fourteen minutes with a clear three-part shape. Clarinets, bassoons and horns intone the pilgrims' hymn quietly; it grows to a full brass statement and recedes. Then the Venusberg music takes over — shivering high violins, chattering woodwind figures, a swooning theme for the goddess and a swirling bacchanal that keeps threatening to spill over. Finally the pilgrims' hymn returns in the brass while the violins keep the Venusberg's rushing figuration going on top, sacred and profane sounding simultaneously.","Wagner was court Kapellmeister in Dresden, still a salaried official four years from the barricades. The opera, premiered there in October 1845 with his niece Johanna as Elisabeth, sets a medieval poet-knight between the goddess Venus's mountain and the pilgrimage to Rome. He revised it repeatedly across forty years and never considered it finished; for Paris in 1861 he rewrote the Venusberg music in the language he had just developed in Tristan.","The two versions are stylistically decades apart, and it shows: in the Paris score the overture stops short and runs straight into an extended ballet whose harmony belongs to the 1860s, grafted onto a work from 1845. Wagner refused to move that ballet to the second act where Parisian subscribers expected it. The Dresden overture's neat return of the hymn — the tidy ending most concerts use — is the older, more conventional Wagner.","The Paris premiere in 1861 was destroyed by members of the Jockey Club, who arrived late by habit, found the ballet already over, and drowned three performances with hunting whistles; Wagner withdrew the production. The overture nonetheless became a concert staple worldwide. Modern debate is about which version to stage, since the Paris Venusberg is better music but sits oddly against the surrounding acts.","Track the pilgrims' hymn: quiet at the start, thunderous at the end, with the Venusberg's tremor still audible underneath. That superimposition in the last three minutes is the whole opera in miniature. Compare the Dresden and Paris versions back to back — Solti and Sinopoli recorded the Paris music — and you can hear Wagner's style change inside one piece."],"wagner-meistersinger-prelude":["Ten minutes of bright C major that begin with a broad brass march for the guild of mastersingers, deliberately stiff and ceremonious. A warmer, freer melody for the young knight follows in the strings, then a scampering miniature version of the march played by woodwinds — the apprentices mocking their elders. The last two minutes stack all three tunes on top of one another simultaneously, each keeping its own character, and drive to a blazing close.","Wagner's only mature comedy, and his longest opera. The idea dated back to 1845 as a light companion piece to Tannhäuser; he took it up seriously in 1861 and finished under the protection of King Ludwig II, who paid his debts and staged the premiere in Munich in June 1868 under Hans von Bülow, whose wife Cosima was by then living with Wagner. The setting is sixteenth-century Nuremberg and its guild of shoemaker-poets.","The triple combination at the end is a boast: Wagner, accused of being unable to write counterpoint, ends by running three independent themes at once in strict harmony. The prelude also states the opera's argument in advance — rules, freedom, and mockery — before a word is sung. Beckmesser, the pedantic town clerk whose music is deliberately deformed, has been read since Adorno as an antisemitic caricature, a reading that remains contested.","The opera's nationalism, and Hans Sachs's closing address about holy German art, made it the Nazi party's ceremonial work: it was performed at the Nuremberg rallies and at the 1933 Bayreuth reopening, and that history is now inseparable from staging decisions. Musically it has never been in doubt; the prelude entered the concert repertoire immediately and is used as a test of an orchestra's brass and its capacity for clean polyphony.","Learn the three tunes separately in the first six minutes, then listen for all three arriving together at the end — it is one of the most satisfying tricks in orchestral music. Compare Karajan's expansive reading with the swifter, lighter one of a period-instrument orchestra. Then hear the opera's final scene, where the same themes carry the argument in words."],"wagner-tristan-und-isolde-liebestod":["The last seven minutes of the opera. Tristan is dead; Isolde, standing over his body and ignoring everyone on stage, begins quietly — how softly and gently he smiles — and describes what she alone can see and hear. The orchestral line rises in slow, repeated waves, each higher than the last, the singer riding on top rather than leading, until the whole thing crests and then subsides through a long descent to a single sustained chord and silence.","Composed in 1859 at the end of Wagner's Swiss exile and first staged in Munich in June 1865 under Hans von Bülow, with Ludwig and Malvina Schnorr von Carolsfeld as the lovers; Ludwig Schnorr died three weeks after the fourth performance, aged twenty-nine, and the coincidence fed the opera's reputation as dangerous. Wagner had by then borrowed heavily against King Ludwig's favour and was expelled from Munich by the cabinet six months later.","The names are back to front. Wagner called the Prelude the Liebestod — love-death — and this closing scene the Verklärung, transfiguration; the common usage swapped them, largely through Liszt's piano transcription of 1867, which popularised the title now attached to the ending. Musically the scene is the answer to the Prelude: the same rising phrase, but here it is allowed at last to complete itself, and the full cadence the opera has withheld for four hours finally lands.","Nietzsche called the opera the true opus metaphysicum and later recanted publicly; Verdi kept the score and studied it warily. The scene has been read as a religious apotheosis, as a Schopenhauerian dissolution of the will, and, more recently, as a straightforwardly erotic climax notated in slow motion. Sopranos argue about whether to sing it as an outward address or an inward trance, and about how much orchestral volume to fight.","Listen to the Prelude first, then this — the two are one arch with four hours in between. Notice that the orchestra, not the singer, carries the melody; Isolde often doubles or floats above it. Kirsten Flagstad with Furtwängler is the monument, Nilsson with Böhm the more dramatic, Waltraud Meier the most conversational."],"wagner-walkure-magic-fire-music":["The end of Die Walküre. Wotan has kissed away his daughter's divinity and laid her asleep on the rock; he summons Loge, the fire god, and strikes the ground three times with his spear. The orchestra answers with a shimmer — divided strings flickering, harps rippling, glockenspiel glinting — that grows into a wall of flame while a horn sounds a call from far off. The god walks away through the fire and the curtain falls on quiet, glowing sound.","Wagner completed the score in 1856 in Zurich, where he had fled after the Dresden uprising, working with a piano in a rented room and no prospect of performance. He needed an orchestra of unprecedented size — six harps, Wagner tubas, a huge string section — and had no theatre that could supply it. Ludwig II ordered a Munich premiere in 1870 over Wagner's objections; the Bayreuth Festspielhaus, built for exactly this problem, opened six years later.","Listen for the horn call that cuts through the flames: it is Siegfried's motif, and the hero has not yet been born. Wotan is telling the audience who will one day walk through this fire, which is the seed of the next two operas. The fire itself is not a tune but a texture — the strings are split into many separate parts playing slightly different figures, so the sound flickers rather than shimmers evenly.","Nineteenth-century audiences found the stage effect risible as often as thrilling, and productions have struggled with literal flames ever since; Bayreuth's Patrice Chéreau centenary Ring of 1976 replaced fire with industrial light and provoked a riot before becoming the model for modern staging. As concert music the passage is usually heard in Wotan's Farewell and Magic Fire Music, an excerpt Wagner himself permitted, unusually for him.","Start at Wotan's Farewell a few minutes earlier so the fire arrives as consequence, not effect. Then listen for the distant horn call and remember it. Hans Hotter, in Solti's recording, is the classic Wotan; James Morris and Bryn Terfel are later benchmarks. Follow with the Ride of the Valkyries from the same act to hear the two extremes of Wagner's orchestra."],"wagner-parsifal-prelude":["It begins with a long melody played in bare unison — no harmony at all — by strings and woodwind together, drifting without a fixed pulse, then repeated a step higher in the minor and shadowed by trumpets. A brass figure follows, then a rising phrase for horns and trumpets. There is almost no counterpoint and very little volume for fourteen minutes; the piece ends unresolved, on a chord that leads straight into the first scene rather than closing.","Wagner's last work, first given at Bayreuth in July 1882, seven months before he died in Venice. He called it not an opera but a stage-consecration festival play and intended it never to be performed anywhere else; the family enforced that until copyright lapsed in 1913, and the Metropolitan Opera's unauthorised staging in 1903 caused an international row. The theatre's covered orchestra pit, invented for the Ring, gives the score its peculiar disembodied glow.","The rising phrase in the brass is the Dresden Amen, a short liturgical cadence used in Saxon churches, which Mendelssohn had already borrowed for his Reformation Symphony fifty years earlier. Wagner spreads the Prelude's harmony so slowly that the ear loses track of pulse — his character Gurnemanz says here time becomes space, and the music demonstrates it before the line is spoken. The unison opening is Wagner deliberately abandoning his own thick orchestral manner.","Nietzsche broke with Wagner over it, calling it a capitulation to the cross. Debate since has run three ways: whether Parsifal is Christian, Buddhist, or an argument for racial purity in line with the regeneration essays Wagner wrote alongside it; Gobineau's influence is documented, its presence in the score disputed. Musically its influence is undisputed — Debussy, hostile to Wagner, called the orchestral sound unique and stole from it.","Listen for how long the opening melody goes without any harmony under it, and notice that you cannot easily count a beat. Then hear the Dresden Amen and compare it with Mendelssohn's use. Knappertsbusch's Bayreuth recordings are slow and cavernous; Boulez's is faster by nearly half an hour and clarifies the lines. Follow the Prelude straight into Act I."],"wagner-siegfried-idyll":["Eighteen minutes for a chamber-sized group — strings, a handful of woodwinds, two horns and a trumpet — beginning with a quiet, wandering string melody that never raises its voice for the first several minutes. An oboe brings in a lullaby, a horn calls, and the textures gradually interlock until a single brief climax arrives and dissolves. There are no fast movements and no contrasts of the usual kind; the whole piece is one continuous, low-lit stretch of music.","Wagner had the players assemble on the staircase of his villa at Tribschen on Lake Lucerne on Christmas morning 1870 and play it as Cosima woke — a present for her birthday and for the birth of their son Siegfried the previous year. Hans Richter, later the conductor of the first Ring, learned the trumpet part for the occasion. Cosima wrote in her diary that she thought she was dreaming. It was meant to stay private.","The melodies are borrowed from the third act of Siegfried, which Wagner was composing at the time, so the piece is a domestic sketch of a scene between two mythological lovers; a German cradle song, Schlaf, Kindchen, schlaf, is woven in for the oboe. Wagner later needed money and sold the score to a publisher in 1878, which Cosima regarded as a small betrayal, and the thirteen-player original was expanded for concert use.","It is the piece critics reach for when arguing that Wagner could write with restraint, and conductors disagree sharply about size: the chamber original is intimate and rough-edged, the full orchestral version smoother and, some argue, false to the occasion. Recent performances have returned to thirteen players. Its emotional standing has also been complicated by the marriage it celebrates, which began in adultery with Hans von Bülow's wife.","Listen for the horn call about halfway through — the one moment the piece reaches outward — and for the oboe's cradle song. Then find the passage in Siegfried's third act where the same music serves the drama. Compare a thirteen-player recording, such as Boulez's or Barenboim's chamber version, with a full orchestral one; the difference is the whole argument."],"verdi-traviata-libiamo":["A drinking song at a Paris party, in waltz time, three minutes long. Alfredo raises his glass and sings the tune; Violetta answers with the same melody and her own words about seizing pleasure; then the guests take it up in chorus and the three strands combine over an offbeat accompaniment that never lets the momentum drop. It is deliberately shallow music — party music, written to sound like something the guests themselves might sing.","Verdi wrote La traviata in a few weeks in late 1852, immediately after Il trovatore, adapting a play Alexandre Dumas the younger had drawn from his own novel about a Parisian courtesan. He wanted it staged in modern dress and in the present day, which was close to unheard of in Italian opera; the Venice censors refused and pushed the setting back to about 1700. The premiere was at La Fenice in March 1853.","The waltz is a piece of characterisation, not decoration: everyone in this scene is performing gaiety, and the music's brittle regularity is the point. Verdi keeps the same rhythmic figure under every phrase so that nobody can escape it. Listen for the moment the chorus is silenced and Violetta hears coughing in her own chest — the party tune continues offstage, and the opera's real subject arrives while the waltz is still playing.","The premiere failed. The audience laughed at a consumptive heroine sung by a well-built soprano, Fanny Salvini-Donatelli, and Verdi wrote to a friend that it was a fiasco, though he blamed the performers rather than the score. A revival at another Venice theatre a year later, with a different cast and small revisions, succeeded, and the opera has since become the most performed in the world.","Notice how little the melody develops — that flatness is intentional. Then listen to what happens in the ten minutes after it, when Violetta is alone. Pair it with Sempre libera from the same act. Recordings with Maria Callas, Anna Netrebko or Renée Fleming show three ways of playing a woman who is acting cheerful for a room full of clients."],"verdi-requiem-dies-irae":["Four hammer-blows from the full chorus and orchestra, each punched home by a bass drum struck so hard that the sound is felt rather than heard, then chromatic scales tearing downwards through the strings while the choir shouts the day of wrath on repeated notes. It is the loudest passage Verdi ever wrote and among the loudest in nineteenth-century music. It subsides into a terrified whisper, and returns twice more across the ninety-minute work like an unwelcome visitor.","Verdi had effectively left the theatre after Aida in 1871. When Rossini died in 1868 he proposed a Requiem written jointly by thirteen Italian composers and contributed the final movement; the project collapsed unperformed. The death of the novelist Alessandro Manzoni in 1873 — for Verdi the moral figure of Italian unification — prompted him to write the whole Mass himself. He conducted the first performance in the church of San Marco in Milan on the anniversary of Manzoni's death.","Verdi splits the trumpets and places some of them offstage, calling to each other across the hall in the Tuba mirum, so the last trumpet arrives from outside the room. The Dies irae music is not confined to its own movement: it erupts again near the end, inside the Libera me, wrecking the calm that has been assembled. The work then closes not with a full chorus but with the soprano almost speaking the words on a monotone.","Hans von Bülow, who had not heard it, dismissed it in print as an opera in ecclesiastical robes; eighteen years later he wrote to Verdi to apologise. Brahms, more sensibly, said only a great master could have written such a thing. The theatrical charge has never quite gone away, and conductors still choose between an operatic reading with large forces and a more liturgical one with a leaner chorus.","Listen for the bass drum: Verdi asks for a single dry stroke, and the effect depends on the player. Then wait for the Dies irae to come back near the end, when you have stopped expecting it. Toscanini's recording is fierce, Giulini's grand and slow, John Eliot Gardiner's leaner. Compare with Fauré's Requiem, which leaves the Day of Wrath out altogether."],"verdi-va-pensiero":["The Hebrew captives sit by the Euphrates and tell their thoughts to fly on golden wings to the homeland they have lost. For most of its length the whole chorus sings the same melody together in unison, very quietly, with a rocking accompaniment underneath — no counterpoint, no soloists, one line of music sung by everyone. Only near the end does it open into harmony and swell, before falling back to the hush it began with.","Verdi was twenty-eight and close to giving up. His wife and both his small children had died within two years, and his second opera, a comedy, had been booed off at La Scala. Temistocle Solera's libretto about the Babylonian captivity was pressed on him by the impresario Bartolomeo Merelli; Verdi later said the page with these words fell open on his table. Nabucco's success at La Scala in March 1842 made him, at a stroke, Italy's leading composer.","The unison writing is the device. A whole chorus singing one line reads as a single people rather than a crowd of individuals, and audiences under Austrian rule in Lombardy heard it that way. The much-repeated story that this chorus was encored on the first night is probably wrong: contemporary accounts suggest the encored number was a different one, and the political reading, including the slogan spelling out the name of the future king, dates from the late 1850s.","Verdi never claimed the piece as a nationalist anthem, and Roger Parker and others have argued the Risorgimento legend was constructed retrospectively. That has not affected its function: it was sung by the crowd in the streets at his funeral in 1901, Toscanini conducted a massed performance at his reburial, and in 2009 the Italian parliament seriously debated replacing the national anthem with it. Conductors are still asked for encores, and sometimes give them.","Listen to how quietly it starts and how long the chorus stays on one melodic line — resist any recording that begins loud. Then hear the swell near the end and notice it lasts only a few bars. Muti's La Scala recordings treat it with maximum restraint. Compare with Sibelius's Finlandia for a nation finding a tune it did not know it needed."],"verdi-rigoletto-donna-e-mobile":["Two minutes of swagger. The Duke of Mantua, waiting in a riverside tavern for a girl he intends to seduce, sings that woman is fickle as a feather in the wind — over a bouncing accompaniment that stops dead at the end of each line so the tenor can hold a note and show off. It is a music-hall tune by design, with a repeated verse and an optional high note at the close. Then it returns, offstage, in the last minutes of the opera.","Verdi adapted Victor Hugo's play Le roi s'amuse, banned in France after one performance for showing a king as a predator. The Austrian censors in Venice objected too; the king became an unnamed Duke of Mantua, and the title changed from The Curse to the jester's name. Verdi refused to give up the sack containing a body in the final scene. The premiere was at La Fenice in March 1851 and the opera was an immediate success.","Verdi withheld the tune. He is said to have given the melody to the tenor Raffaele Mirate only a day or two before the premiere and made the cast swear not to hum it outside the theatre, knowing that once it escaped, Venice's gondoliers would be singing it before the curtain rose. Its dramatic function is crueller: Rigoletto, standing over a sack he believes contains the Duke, hears the same song drifting from the tavern and realises the body is his daughter's.","Its instant popularity — organ-grinders had it within days — has made it a byword for the cheap tune, and critics from Verdi's day onward have used it to accuse him of vulgarity. Defenders point out that the vulgarity is characterisation: the Duke has no inner life, and Verdi gives him music to match. The interpolated high B at the end is a tradition, not in the score, and tenors are judged on it anyway.","Hear it in the theatre's own order: once in the tavern, once through the storm at the end. The second hearing is one of the most brutal reversals in opera. Compare Caruso's 1908 recording with Pavarotti's or Jonas Kaufmann's for how much charm to allow the character. Then listen to the quartet that immediately follows the aria in Act III."],"verdi-il-trovatore-anvil-chorus":["Dawn in a Roma encampment in the Biscay mountains. Hammers strike real anvils on the offbeat, cutting across the orchestra's rhythm, while the men sing about work, wine and the gypsy girls who make labour bearable. The chorus is short, loud and relentlessly regular, and it opens the opera's second act cold, with no scene-setting: the curtain rises on the noise already happening. Verdi asks for the anvils to be tuned and placed for maximum clang.","Verdi composed it in Rome in the winter of 1852–53, working on La traviata at the same time, and premiered it at the Teatro Apollo in January 1853 — six weeks before La traviata opened in Venice. The libretto, from a Spanish play, was left unfinished when the librettist Salvadore Cammarano died, and was completed by Leone Emanuele Bardare. The plot's notorious complications turn on a Roma woman who threw the wrong baby on a fire.","The anvils are percussion instruments with a dramatic job: they establish that these people work for a living, in contrast to the aristocrats who occupy every other scene, and the offbeat placement makes the whole chorus feel physically laboured. Verdi reuses the same rhythmic snap in the gypsy music later in the act, binding the community together musically. The chorus's cheerfulness sits directly before Azucena's account of watching her mother burn.","Enrico Caruso is supposed to have said that all Il trovatore needs is the four greatest singers in the world, and the opera's reputation has swung between adoration for its melodic generosity and embarrassment at its plot. The Anvil Chorus itself became a fairground staple, parodied by the Marx Brothers in A Night at the Opera and played by brass bands everywhere, which has hardened the impression that the opera is crude.","Listen for where the anvils fall against the beat — they are early, not on it, which is what makes the chorus swing. Then go straight to Azucena's narration that follows and hear the same world turn horrifying. Karajan's recording with Price and Corelli is the plush option; Muti's is harder and faster. Compare with the triumphal crowd scenes of Aida."],"verdi-aida-triumphal-march":["The Egyptian army returns victorious and the stage fills: priests, dancers, prisoners, the entire chorus. Long straight trumpets on stage play a plain rising fanfare in unison; a second group answers it a semitone higher, in a different key, and the effect is of two armies calling across a square. Between the fanfares come a heavy processional march, a ballet, and finally the whole apparatus combined — chorus, band, orchestra — in the largest single number Verdi wrote.","The Khedive Ismail Pasha commissioned it for the opera house in Cairo, which had opened in 1869 with Rigoletto; the persistent story that Aida was written for the opening of the Suez Canal is false. Verdi resisted, then accepted a very large fee, and worked from a scenario developed with the Egyptologist Auguste Mariette. The premiere was delayed almost a year because the sets and costumes were stuck in Paris during the Prussian siege, and took place in December 1871.","The trumpets were purpose-built. Verdi had straight instruments made in two different keys so that the fanfare could be played, then repeated a semitone higher by the second group, a lurch upward that no ordinary orchestral trumpet section could produce so plainly. He also insisted the players be on stage, in costume, as part of the spectacle. The march's grandeur is deliberately hollow: the woman at the centre of the scene is watching her father led in as a prisoner.","Aida has carried a colonial charge since Edward Said's reading of it as European Egyptology staged for a European-run court in Cairo, and the Triumphal Scene is where that argument lands — its exoticism is invented, not researched. Musically it is also the scene productions most often inflate, with live horses and hundreds of extras, which Verdi's own staging notes do not require. Stadium performances have made it the most abused eight minutes in opera.","Listen for the second trumpet group entering a semitone above the first — the jolt is the composition, not the pageantry. Then watch Amneris and Aida rather than the parade. Karajan and Muti both recorded it with full weight; a video from a theatre rather than a stadium gives a truer sense of scale. Compare with Verdi's Requiem for the same forces used seriously."],"verdi-nabucco-overture":["A medley in the Italian manner, about eight minutes long, assembled from tunes the audience is about to hear. Solemn trombones and low brass open it with a chorale; a scurrying, military figure follows, brass-heavy and closer to a town band than an opera orchestra; the melody of the captives' chorus appears in the woodwinds, plainly stated; and the whole thing accelerates into a whirling final stretch of the kind Rossini had made obligatory, with a long crescendo and a hard finish.","Verdi assembled the overture last, as was normal practice, for the La Scala premiere in March 1842 — his third opera and the one that ended his intention to abandon composition after the deaths of his family and the failure of his previous work. Milan was under Austrian administration; the theatre was the city's chief public space, and its orchestra, with a full brass complement, encouraged the loud, banda-flavoured writing that critics north of the Alps found crude.","The overture is a trailer, and its ordering is dramatic rather than musical: the sacred brass chorale represents the Hebrews, the brash march the Babylonian court, and the two are set in collision before a word is sung. Listen for the captives' melody arriving quietly in the middle, without emphasis — audiences at the premiere heard it here first, in the pit, before the chorus made it famous an hour later.","Nineteenth-century German writers used pieces like this as evidence that Italian opera was theatrical rather than symphonic, and the overture was often cut or replaced with a shorter prelude. Its rehabilitation came through the concert hall and the wind-band repertoire, where it is a fixture. Conductors argue mainly about the brass balance, since a modern orchestra's trombones can obliterate everything Verdi wrote underneath them.","Treat it as a map: every tune returns later in the opera, so listen once now and once after hearing the whole work. Notice the trombone chorale at the start and how differently it functions on stage. Muti's La Scala recording keeps the brass in proportion; Toscanini's is faster and drier. Then go straight to Va, pensiero."],"verdi-traviata-sempre-libera":["Violetta, alone after her party, first wonders in a slow, halting arioso whether the young man who declared love could be the one she has waited for — then dismisses it and launches into a fast, glittering aria declaring she will always be free, moving from pleasure to pleasure. The vocal writing is all leaps, trills and scales at speed. Twice, from the street below, the tenor's voice floats in with the love theme, and twice she sings straight over it.","The scene closes the first act. Verdi's source, Dumas's play about the courtesan Marguerite Gautier, was drawn from the author's affair with Marie Duplessis, who died of tuberculosis in Paris in 1847 aged twenty-three; Verdi and his partner Giuseppina Strepponi were themselves living unmarried in Busseto and enduring the town's disapproval, which sharpened his interest in a woman judged by her past. The premiere was in Venice in March 1853.","The offstage tenor is the trap. Alfredo's melody, first heard minutes earlier, keeps interrupting her declaration of independence, and Verdi scores it so the audience hears both at once while Violetta pretends not to — a psychological split written into the staging rather than the words. The high E flat that ends most performances is not in Verdi's score; it is an interpolation that has become an expectation, and sopranos who omit it are noticed.","The aria became the standard test of the Italian soprano's agility, which distorted casting for a century: the role needs coloratura in Act I and dramatic weight in Act III, and few singers own both. Maria Callas's performances in the 1950s reframed the whole part around characterisation rather than display, and modern productions increasingly cast for the later acts and let the fireworks look effortful.","Listen for the offstage tenor cutting through twice and note what she does with the interruption. Then decide whether you want the high E flat. Callas is the reference; Ileana Cotrubas and Anna Netrebko are gentler; Diana Damrau is the most brilliant. Follow with the aria that opens Act III to hear the same voice hollowed out."],"gounod-ave-maria-bach-gounod":["Bach's Prelude in C from the first book of the Well-Tempered Clavier is played complete and unaltered — the same broken chords, in the same order — while a new melody is laid over the top. Gounod's tune moves slowly in long notes where Bach's ripple is continuous, rising to one sustained high note near the end and falling back. Three minutes, no development, no repeat. The whole effect depends on two pieces written 130 years apart fitting exactly.","Gounod improvised the melody at the piano in about 1852 in the house of Pierre-Joseph-Guillaume Zimmermann, a Paris Conservatoire piano professor whose daughter he later married; Zimmermann wrote it down. It was published in 1853 as a Meditation on Bach's first prelude, an instrumental piece with no text. The Latin Ave Maria was fitted to the melody in 1859. Gounod was then between a period as a would-be priest and the success of Faust.","The Bach that Gounod knew was not quite Bach's. An early nineteenth-century editor, Christian Friedrich Gottlieb Schwencke, had inserted an extra bar into the prelude, and that spurious bar survives in many editions of the Ave Maria, so the accompaniment is often one measure longer than Bach wrote. Gounod's melody also avoids the notes Bach's chords emphasise, which is why the two lines never collide even though nothing was composed to fit.","Purists have complained since the 1850s that Bach is being used as wallpaper, and the piece is a standing example in debates about arrangement and appropriation. It has nonetheless outlasted almost all of Gounod's church music, is performed at more weddings and funerals than the composer's operas combined, and exists in hundreds of arrangements. Scholars now treat it as evidence of how the nineteenth century rediscovered and reshaped Bach.","Listen to Bach's prelude alone first, twice, until the pattern is familiar; then play the Ave Maria and you will hear a duet rather than a song with accompaniment. Try a violin version and a sung one — the wordless version makes the counterpoint clearer. Compare with Schubert's Ave Maria, which sets a different text and owes nothing to Bach."],"gounod-faust-jewel-song":["Marguerite, alone in her garden, opens a casket of jewels left at her door and puts them on. The aria is a waltz: light, fast, decorated with trills and rising runs, punctuated by little orchestral glitters as she looks at herself in the glass and laughs at the reflection. It follows immediately after a plain, modal ballad she has just sung about a king of Thule, so a folk-song and a coloratura showpiece stand side by side, sung by the same girl.","Gounod worked on Faust for years and finally staged it at the Théâtre Lyrique in Paris in March 1859, with spoken dialogue rather than recitative; the recitatives and the ballet came later, for the Opéra in 1869. Goethe's poem was a French obsession, and Gounod's version, which keeps the seduction and drops most of the philosophy, was attacked in Germany for that reason and retitled Margarethe there.","The two adjacent songs are a portrait in two frames. The Thule ballad is bare, strophic and almost without accompaniment; the Jewel Song is glittering and unstable, and Marguerite keeps breaking off her own line as if surprised at herself. Gounod interleaves the two texts, so she returns to the old ballad between bursts of the waltz — the seduction happening inside the music before Faust says a word.","Faust was for decades the most performed opera in the world; it opened the Metropolitan Opera in 1883 and was played so often that George Bernard Shaw complained. The twentieth century turned on it, dismissing it as sentimental and unfaithful to Goethe, and it left the core repertory for a time. Recent productions have taken the sexual politics seriously and the score's reputation has partly recovered.","Listen to the king of Thule ballad first — the aria means little without it. Then follow the moments Marguerite interrupts her own laughter. Recordings by Victoria de los Ángeles and Renée Fleming take opposite views of her innocence. And Hergé's opera singer Bianca Castafiore sings nothing else, if you want a shortcut to how familiar this tune once was."],"gounod-funeral-march-marionette":["A funeral march performed by puppets: a stiff, tiptoeing minor-key tune played staccato in the low woodwinds and strings, with mock-solemn drum taps and comically abrupt stops. A gentler middle section in the major provides a moment of sentiment before the tiptoeing resumes and the procession shuffles off. Five minutes, scored lightly and full of small orchestral jokes — a bassoon grunt here, a sudden silence there — with nothing developed and nothing taken seriously.","Gounod spent the Franco-Prussian War and the Commune in London, from 1870 to 1874, conducting a choir and living in some social confusion. He wrote the piece there for piano, intending it for a suite of comic pieces he never finished, and orchestrated it in 1879. The Victorian appetite for the grotesque-genteel — puppet theatres, mock ceremonies, Punch — is the world it belongs to, rather than the Paris of Faust.","Gounod supplied a written scenario. The marionette has been killed in a duel; the mourners set off; part-way through they stop at an inn for refreshments, which is the major-key middle section, and then resume the procession rather less steadily than before. Once you know that the tender interlude is a drinks break, the whole piece reorganises itself, and the slight unevenness of the returning march becomes a joke about the mourners.","It was a popular encore and light-orchestral staple, then acquired a second identity entirely: chosen as the theme for Alfred Hitchcock Presents in 1955, it became, for two generations of television viewers, the sound of a raised eyebrow and a silhouette. Critics have generally treated it as a trifle, though it is a compact demonstration of orchestral wit from a composer usually discussed for his sacred music.","Listen for the pause and change of key where the mourners stop at the inn, then for the slightly tipsy return. Try the piano original alongside the orchestration to hear how much the woodwind colouring adds. Compare with Saint-Saëns's Danse macabre, written two years later, for a French orchestra playing the same kind of graveyard joke on a larger scale."],"gounod-romeo-et-juliette-je-veux-vivre":["Juliet's entrance aria at the Capulet ball, and a waltz. She sings that she wants to live in this dream, that spring is too short and she will not be told about marriage yet; the vocal line runs in long garlands of scales and trills, with two high notes held over the orchestra's turning accompaniment. Four minutes of pure momentum, ending with a rising flourish. Her nurse tries to interrupt and is brushed aside without the music breaking stride.","Gounod's fifth attempt at Shakespeare's play as an opera, staged at the Théâtre Lyrique in April 1867 during the Paris Exposition Universelle, when the city was full of visitors and every theatre was competing for them. Barbier and Carré's libretto reduces the feud and keeps four love duets, making the opera almost a chamber piece for two voices; this waltz is one of the few numbers where Juliet is alone and unshadowed.","The aria exists to be contradicted. It is the only music in the opera where Juliet is entirely carefree, and Gounod places it minutes before she meets Roméo, so that the audience's memory of the waltz colours everything that follows; the same rhythm returns, darkened, in the tomb scene's orchestral writing. The refusal-to-marry text is also Gounod's invention rather than Shakespeare's, added to give the soprano a display number.","French critics complained that the opera was a series of duets with a plot attached, and German ones repeated the charge levelled at Faust — that Gounod had sentimentalised a masterwork of another literature. The waltz survived independently as a soprano audition and recital piece, which has divorced it from the drama, and productions now sometimes stage it as forced gaiety rather than innocence.","Hear it as an entrance: this is how we first meet her. Then listen to the balcony duet immediately afterwards for what the same voice sounds like when it stops performing. Anna Netrebko, Nathalie Dessay and Elisabeth Schwarzkopf recorded very different Juliets. Compare with Tchaikovsky's orchestral treatment of the same play for a wholly wordless version."],"offenbach-orpheus-underworld-can-can":["The gods of Olympus, bored in hell, begin a stately minuet and abandon it after a few bars for the fastest music in the score. The galop that follows is two minutes of relentless two-beat drive: a scampering tune tossed between strings and woodwinds, cymbal crashes on the strong beats, and a build that simply gets louder and faster without changing shape. The chorus whoops over the top. It ends abruptly, at maximum speed, with everybody exhausted.","Offenbach was running his own theatre, the Bouffes-Parisiens, under licence restrictions that limited how many singers he could put on stage; Orphée aux enfers, in October 1858, was his first full-length work and a satire on the Second Empire under a classical disguise. Napoleon III's court, the Institut and the reverence for Gluck's Orpheus were the targets. The dance itself was already notorious in Paris dance halls, where the police watched for indecency.","The joke is that Offenbach quotes Gluck. Earlier in the score, when the figure of Public Opinion drags a reluctant Orpheus off to rescue a wife he is glad to be rid of, the orchestra plays the melody of Gluck's lament for Eurydice — the most solemn moment in the older opera — as a piece of mockery. The galop's minuet introduction sets up the same joke in miniature: dignity established, then kicked over.","The critic Jules Janin denounced it in the Journal des débats as a profanation of holy antiquity; Offenbach replied in print, the row filled the papers, and the run extended to well over two hundred performances. He revised it into a four-act spectacle in 1874. The dance's later association with the Moulin Rouge and Toulouse-Lautrec's posters came decades afterwards, and has largely erased the satire the music was written to carry.","Listen for the minuet first — the joke is the switch. Then notice the cymbal placement, which is what makes the galop feel like kicking. Find the Gluck lament and then its parody in Offenbach's first act. Recordings by Marc Minkowski and Michel Plasson take the score seriously as music, which paradoxically makes it funnier."],"offenbach-barcarolle":["A duet for two women's voices over a rocking six-beat accompaniment that never stops, imitating a gondola on the water; the two lines move mostly in parallel thirds so they sound like one voice split in two. The words address the beautiful night, the night of love, and ask time to stop. It opens the Venetian act of the opera, sung as guests arrive at a palazzo on the Grand Canal, and is repeated in the orchestra later as the act turns violent.","Offenbach spent his last years trying to be taken seriously, writing an opera on three tales by E. T. A. Hoffmann in which the poet loses three women in three cities. He died in October 1880 with the orchestration unfinished; Ernest Guiraud completed it and the premiere took place at the Opéra-Comique in February 1881. The composer who had spent thirty years mocking the Paris opera establishment did not live to see himself admitted to it.","The tune is recycled. Offenbach had written it in 1864 for a German romantic opera, Die Rheinnixen, where it was a chorus of elves on the Rhine; the melody was moved from a German river to a Venetian canal and became famous in its second life. The Venetian act itself was cut at the premiere for length, so the Barcarolle circulated for decades as a detached number before the act was restored.","There is no single authentic score. Offenbach left the work incomplete, Guiraud's version dominated for eighty years, the Opéra-Comique fire of 1887 destroyed material, and later editions by Fritz Oeser and Michael Kaye restored music from rediscovered manuscripts — with the acts in different orders. The Barcarolle's placement varies accordingly. Its afterlife in film, notably Life Is Beautiful, has made it shorthand for doomed romance.","Let the accompaniment establish itself before listening to the voices; the rocking figure is the piece. Then note how little the two lines diverge. Recordings by Régine Crespin, and complete sets under Sylvain Cambreling or Kent Nagano, differ in which edition they follow. Compare with the Flower Duet from Lakmé, written two years later on much the same principle."],"offenbach-tales-hoffmann-doll-song":["A mechanical doll is displayed to a party of guests and sings for them: a bright, jerky little song about the birds in the arbour, with staccato phrases, high leaps, trills and scales climbing to the top of the soprano range. Twice the machine runs down mid-phrase — the voice sags, the orchestra sags with it — and a servant winds her up again with an audible crank, whereupon she resumes exactly where she stopped and finishes faster than before.","The act comes from Hoffmann's story The Sandman, in which a student falls in love with an automaton built by two rival inventors and is driven mad when she is torn apart in front of him. Offenbach, dying while he wrote, was making a serious opera out of German fantastic literature after a career of topical satire. Paris in 1881 was full of mechanical novelties — automata, phonographs, clockwork displays — and the audience knew exactly what was being parodied.","The comedy is engineering. The winding-down passages are notated precisely: the tempo is marked to slacken and the pitch to sag, so the singer must perform a machine failing rather than improvise one. Hoffmann sees Olympia as a living woman only because he is wearing magic spectacles sold to him minutes earlier, so the audience is watching a man fall in love with something everyone else can see is a doll.","The aria became a coloratura vehicle detached from its context, sung in recitals as a party trick, which loses the satire on the display soprano that Offenbach built into it — the joke is that the fashionable virtuoso singer is literally a machine. Editions differ over whether the act comes second or third, changing the whole arc, and staging fashion has swung between charming clockwork and something considerably more sinister.","Watch a staged version rather than only listening; the winding-down is visual as well as vocal. Listen for the two collapses and how differently a singer plays them. Beverly Sills, Natalie Dessay and Diana Damrau each find different degrees of menace. Then hear Delibes's dancing doll in Coppélia, and Stravinsky's puppet in Petrushka, for the same anxiety in other forms."],"chant-viderunt-omnes-gradual":["The gradual sung between the readings at the third Mass of Christmas Day, about five minutes of unaccompanied unison singing. Its text is from Psalm 97: all the ends of the earth have seen the salvation of our God. The shape is a dialogue — full choir for the respond, one or two soloists for the verse Notum fecit Dominus, then the choir again. On the word omnes the syllable dissolves into a long garland of notes over a single vowel.","The melody belongs to the repertory the Carolingians called Gregorian and claimed Pope Gregory the Great had received from a dove. In truth it was assembled north of the Alps in the eighth and ninth centuries, as Pepin and Charlemagne pressed Frankish churches to abandon their local singing for Roman use. Singers carried the tunes in memory for generations; the earliest surviving notation, in the manuscripts of Laon and St Gall around 900, records shape and nuance but not pitch.","Listen closer: the very first syllable of the respond, and the word Notum that opens the verse, are the two places later composers seized on. At Notre-Dame around 1200, Léonin and then Pérotin took exactly these solo passages, stretched each note into a drone lasting half a minute, and built new voices above them. The choral sections between were left as plainchant, which is why the polyphony arrives in blocks.","Nineteenth-century monks at Solesmes reconstructed the melodies from medieval sources and gave the modern church a smooth, equal-note style of delivery. Since Eugène Cardine's work on the earliest neumes, scholars have argued that those signs encode sharp rhythmic distinctions the Solesmes editions flattened out. Comparison with the Old Roman version, notated later but probably preserving older Roman practice, shows how far the Frankish singers reshaped what they received.","Hear this first, then play Pérotin's four-voice setting immediately afterwards: the same notes, unrecognisably slowed. Notice how the melody circles a single reciting pitch and keeps returning to it, and how the soloist's verse is more florid than the choir's music. Seek a recording that uses the early neumes rather than the smoothed Solesmes edition — the difference in bite is startling."],"chant-alleluia-pascha-nostrum":["The Alleluia of Easter Sunday Mass, four minutes long. A soloist sings the word Alleluia; the choir repeats it and continues into the jubilus, a wordless cascade of perhaps forty notes on the final vowel. The soloist then takes the verse — Christ our Passover is sacrificed for us — and the Alleluia with its jubilus returns. Nothing accompanies the voices; the whole piece turns on one syllable given room to unfold.","Easter was the year's summit, and its Alleluia received the most elaborate music the liturgy allowed. The Frankish church had suppressed Alleluia singing in Lent precisely so that this moment would land. Around this melody grew the two great medieval inventions: the sequence, which fitted new words to the jubilus, and organum, which added voices to the soloist's phrases. Both begin here, in the space one exultant word opens up.","Listen closer: the choir and soloist never sing together. Every florid passage belongs to one or two trained singers; the choir's music is plainer. That division of labour, invisible on the page, decided the whole future of polyphony — for two centuries composers added extra voices only to the solo passages, leaving the choral ones untouched. The blocky alternation of chant and polyphony in Léonin comes straight from this rule.","The Alleluia was long treated as the freest, most improvisatory chant, and modern scholarship broadly agrees that its melodies were assembled from stock phrases rather than composed as wholes. Arguments persist over the jubilus: whether its length is a survival of pre-Christian Jewish practice, as Augustine's remarks about wordless joy have been taken to suggest, or a Frankish elaboration of something originally short.","Follow one thing only: the moment the words stop and the vowel keeps going. Count how long the singers stay on it. Then hear Léonin's organum on this same chant and notice that his added voice dances above notes taken from what you have just heard, held so long they stop sounding like melody at all."]});